window.CONTENT = {"chapters":[{"id":"start","title":"Start Here: Blueprint and Cram Sheet","part":"Start","summary":"What's on Exam 1, where to find each topic, your instructor's emphasis, and the one-page cram sheet.","sections":[{"id":"start--exam-1-blueprint","title":"Exam 1 blueprint"},{"id":"start--what-the-tags-mean","title":"What the tags mean"},{"id":"start--one-page-cram-sheet","title":"One-page cram sheet ★"}],"html":"<section id=\"start--exam-1-blueprint\"><h2>Exam 1 blueprint<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#start--exam-1-blueprint\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><p>Topics and question counts from your Exam 1 study guide and notes.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Topic</th>\n<th>Qs</th>\n<th>Where to study it</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Leukemia:</strong> risk factors, medications</td>\n<td>5 (notebook says 10)</td>\n<td>Heme Disorders</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Prostate cancer</strong> (post-op complications)</td>\n<td>5 (per notes)</td>\n<td>Male Reproductive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Dosage calculation</strong></td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Other Exam Topics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>End of life:</strong> hospice vs. palliative</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Other Exam Topics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Organ donation</strong></td>\n<td>not specified</td>\n<td>Other Exam Topics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Breast cancer</strong> (male + female) medications</td>\n<td>not specified</td>\n<td>Female Cancers, Male Reproductive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Labs:</strong> CBC, WBC, platelets (+ TIBC, diet, clotting)</td>\n<td>not specified</td>\n<td>Heme Assessment, Cheat Sheets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lung cancer</strong></td>\n<td>not specified</td>\n<td>Lung Cancer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Multiple myeloma:</strong> nursing interventions</td>\n<td>not specified</td>\n<td>Heme Disorders</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cancer risk factors</strong> (Table 13.2)</td>\n<td>not specified</td>\n<td>Cancer Overview</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hodgkin lymphoma:</strong> complications, symptoms</td>\n<td>not specified</td>\n<td>Heme Disorders</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cancer staging:</strong> tumor size, TNM (Davis table)</td>\n<td>not specified</td>\n<td>Cancer Overview</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cervical guidelines</strong></td>\n<td>not specified</td>\n<td>Female Cancers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Testicular cancer</strong></td>\n<td>not specified</td>\n<td>Male Reproductive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Biopsy procedures</strong></td>\n<td>not specified</td>\n<td>Cancer Overview</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Uterine cancer</strong></td>\n<td>not specified</td>\n<td>Female Cancers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"chead\"><span class=\"tag\">Class note</span> Housekeeping from your notes</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>The exam has questions on <strong>oncology staging</strong>. You need to <strong>know lab numbers</strong> (electrolytes, CBC). <strong>It takes questions from tables</strong>, so study every table.</p>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"chead\"><span class=\"tag\">Class note</span> Your notes also say to concentrate on Chapters 19 and 20</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Immune disorders (Ch 19) and connective tissue disorders (Ch 20: OA, RA, lupus) are in the Immune part. HIV (Ch 22) and bioterrorism are there too.</p>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"start--what-the-tags-mean\"><h2>What the tags mean<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#start--what-the-tags-mean\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><ul>\n<li><strong>Colored boxes</strong> on each disease: Why it matters, Patho, Signs and symptoms ★, Diagnosis, Treatment, Watch (side effects and danger signs), Nursing (with <em>→ why</em> reasons), Pearl.</li>\n<li><strong>Purple &quot;Class note&quot; boxes</strong> hold things your instructor emphasized or that were only in your NUR 291 notes.</li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Class vs book&quot;</strong> flags spots where your notes and the textbook disagree. Ask your instructor which one the exam uses.</li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Slide vs book&quot;</strong> flags where the slides and the textbook give different numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>★</strong> marks high-yield items (starred in your notebook or tested).</li>\n<li>Every chapter ends with your <strong>PowerPoint slides, word for word</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"start--one-page-cram-sheet\"><h2>One-page cram sheet ★<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#start--one-page-cram-sheet\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><p><em>If you only read one page.</em></p>\n<h3>Oncology</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Cancer cells: <strong>no apoptosis, no contact inhibition.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Carcinogens = #1 risk factor;</strong> BRCA1/2 → breast/ovarian.</li>\n<li><strong>TNM:</strong> T size · N nodes · M mets. <strong>M1 = not local.</strong></li>\n<li>Breast sizes: ≤2 · 2–5 · &gt;5 cm.</li>\n<li><strong>CAUTION &gt;2 weeks → evaluate.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Biopsy = only definitive diagnosis.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Neo = before · Adjuvant = after.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Chemo + fever ≥100.4°F = call now.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Palliative = from diagnosis; hospice = ≤6 months, no curative tx.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Lung:</strong> smoking 90%; NSCLC 85% good; SCLC 15% bad; <strong>never clamp chest tube.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Breast:</strong> age #1 risk; frozen shoulder; lumpectomy → radiation; ER+ → tamoxifen, stop OCPs.</li>\n<li><strong>Ovarian:</strong> bloating, early satiety, no screening, CA-125.</li>\n<li><strong>Uterine:</strong> unopposed estrogen + obesity; vaginal bleeding.</li>\n<li><strong>Cervical:</strong> HPV; Pap at 21, every 3 years; postcoital bleeding.</li>\n<li><strong>Prostate:</strong> PSA ≥4; Gleason max 10; impotence + incontinence post-op; brachytherapy: thicker PPE, no sex 2 weeks.</li>\n<li><strong>Testicular:</strong> age 33, cryptorchidism, painless mass, AFP/hCG, sperm bank.</li>\n<li><strong>Male breast:</strong> BRCA2, mastectomy, hormone tx only if receptor +.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Heme and immune</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Left shift</strong> = immature neutrophils (bands) = active infection.</li>\n<li><strong>Neutrophils:</strong> bacteria/fungi, first to arrive, 6 hours.</li>\n<li><strong>Intrinsic → PTT (heparin) · Extrinsic → PT/INR (warfarin).</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Ferritin = stores · transferrin = transport · TIBC ↑ in IDA</strong> = low O₂ sat.</li>\n<li><strong>IDA diet:</strong> red meat, spinach, beans + vitamin C, no dairy; iron on empty stomach.</li>\n<li><strong>Anemia:</strong> BP ↓, HR ↑, RR ↑; low albumin = nutrition.</li>\n<li><strong>PV:</strong> thick blood → hydrate, phlebotomy; stroke/DVT risk.</li>\n<li><strong>Platelets &lt;50k:</strong> no IM, no rectal, soft brush; <strong>Bactrim #1 drug cause.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Leukemia:</strong> immature WBCs → infection, anemia, bleeding; benzene, Down syndrome.</li>\n<li><strong>Neutropenic:</strong> no plants, no humidifiers, reverse isolation (you wear PPE).</li>\n<li><strong>Lymphoma:</strong> painless nodes + B symptoms; Reed–Sternberg; SVC syndrome.</li>\n<li><strong>Myeloma:</strong> CRAB, hypercalcemia (tingling), push fluids, bisphosphonates, ambulate.</li>\n<li><strong>IgG</strong> crosses placenta · <strong>IgM</strong> first · <strong>IgE</strong> allergy · <strong>IgA</strong> secretions.</li>\n<li><strong>Humoral</strong> = B cells/antibodies · <strong>Cellular</strong> = T cells.</li>\n<li><strong>XLA:</strong> boys, 6 months, BTK, IVIG every 3–4 weeks · <strong>DiGeorge:</strong> CATCH, ↓Ca, FISH.</li>\n<li><strong>Anaphylaxis:</strong> stop drug → IM epi → O₂; don&#39;t drop BP.</li>\n<li><strong>Only NS with blood · O donor · AB recipient.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Latex ↔ banana, avocado, kiwi, chestnut.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</section>"},{"id":"cancer","title":"Overview of Cancer Care","part":"Part 1 · Ch 13","summary":"Patho, spread, risk factors, CAUTION, TNM staging, oncologic emergencies, prevention, surgery, radiation, chemo toxicities, vesicants, transplant.","sections":[{"id":"cancer--key-terms-and-pathophysiology","title":"Key terms and pathophysiology"},{"id":"cancer--risk-factors-table-13-2","title":"Risk factors (Table 13.2)"},{"id":"cancer--clinical-presentation-caution","title":"Clinical presentation: CAUTION ★"},{"id":"cancer--staging-and-grading","title":"Staging and grading"},{"id":"cancer--diagnosis-goals-and-tools","title":"Diagnosis: goals and tools"},{"id":"cancer--oncologic-emergencies","title":"Oncologic emergencies"},{"id":"cancer--prevention-and-screening","title":"Prevention and screening"},{"id":"cancer--treatment-goals-and-the-4-modalities","title":"Treatment: goals and the 4 modalities"},{"id":"cancer--nursing-management-cancer-general","title":"Nursing management (cancer, general)"},{"id":"cancer--class-notes-add-on-nur-291","title":"Class notes add-on (NUR 291)"},{"id":"cancer--more-from-your-first-study-guide","title":"More from your first study guide"},{"id":"cancer--chapter-13-powerpoint-word-for-word","title":"Chapter 13 PowerPoint, word for word"}],"html":"<section id=\"cancer--overview-of-cancer-care\"><p>Cancer is a group of disorders with uncontrolled abnormal cell growth. It is the 2nd most common cause of death in the US (2nd leading cause worldwide, after heart disease). US death rates dropped 32% (1991–2019) because of less smoking, earlier diagnosis, and newer targeted drugs.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"cancer--key-terms-and-pathophysiology\"><h2>Key terms and pathophysiology<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#cancer--key-terms-and-pathophysiology\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Malignant</td>\n<td>Cancerous, unregulated, invasive cell growth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Non-malignant</td>\n<td>Not cancerous</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Benign</td>\n<td>Not cancerous, does not invade or metastasize, but can become cancerous</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Carcinogen</td>\n<td>An exposure that damages DNA (tobacco, ionizing radiation, benzene, HPV, sun). <strong>The most common risk factor.</strong> Promotes carcinogenesis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Carcinogenesis</td>\n<td>Initiation and promotion of cancer after carcinogen exposure plus genetic mutations that the immune system misses</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Apoptosis</td>\n<td>Programmed cell death. <strong>Cancer cells skip it</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Contact inhibition</td>\n<td>Normal cells stop growing when they touch other cells. Cancer cells lack it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dysplasia</td>\n<td>Deranged growth (cells vary in size, shape, organization). A <strong>precursor</strong> to cancer but not the same as cancer. No apoptosis results in dysplasia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Neoplasia</td>\n<td>Uncontrolled proliferation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Anaplasia</td>\n<td>Cell has lost its expected structure and function (no longer looks or acts like the parent cell)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metastasis</td>\n<td>Abnormal cells invade surrounding tissue and gain access to lymph and blood vessels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Carcinoma / Squamous cell / Sarcoma</td>\n<td>Carcinoma = epithelial cells. Squamous cell = squamous cells. Sarcoma = connective tissue</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Remission / Palliation</td>\n<td>Disease-free state / relieving symptoms when cure is not possible</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vesicant</td>\n<td>A drug that causes extensive tissue damage if it leaks out of the vein</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout patho\"><div class=\"chead\">Two hallmarks</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p><strong>1. Uncontrolled cell growth</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lack contact inhibition, so they don&#39;t stop growing when they touch other cells.</li>\n<li>No apoptosis (no programmed cell death).</li>\n<li>No anchorage dependence: they grow in atypical places, make their own blood vessels, and survive anoxia and acidosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. Altered differentiation</strong>: the cell no longer looks or acts like the parent cell. <em>Well differentiated</em> cells look like the parent cell. <em>Poorly differentiated</em> cells are harder to treat and more aggressive. This is the basis of <strong>grading</strong>.</p>\n</div></aside>\n<p><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-003-000.jpg\" alt=\"Mild dysplasia slide: the yellow cells are abnormal, sitting above the basement membrane\"><figcaption>Mild dysplasia slide: the yellow cells are abnormal, sitting above the basement membrane</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-003-001.jpg\" alt=\"Stages of metastasis: (1) cancer cells at the primary site → (2) invade surrounding tissue and vessels → (3) travel through the circulatory or lymphatic system → (4) reinvade and grow at a new site\"><figcaption>Stages of metastasis: (1) cancer cells at the primary site → (2) invade surrounding tissue and vessels → (3) travel through the circulatory or lymphatic system → (4) reinvade and grow at a new site</figcaption></figure></p>\n<h3>How cancer spreads</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cell-to-cell (direct invasion).</strong> Example: prostate spreads to the rectum, pelvic floor, lower spine, and hip.</li>\n<li><strong>Lymphatic.</strong> Travels lymph channels to nodes, where it can be destroyed, grow, lie dormant, or spread further.</li>\n<li><strong>Hematogenous (blood).</strong> Example: colorectal goes through the portal circulation to the liver.</li>\n<li>A metastatic tumor <strong>keeps the identity of its origin</strong>. Prostate cancer in the rectum is still prostate cancer (a secondary tumor).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Types and naming</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Solid tumors</strong> arise from a body organ and grow into masses (e.g., lung).</li>\n<li><strong>Hematologic (&quot;liquid&quot;) malignancies</strong> come from hematopoietic stem cells (myeloid and lymphoid lines, lymph nodes and spleen): <strong>leukemia</strong> (blood cells), <strong>lymphoma</strong> (lymph system), <strong>multiple myeloma</strong> (plasma cells and immunoglobulins).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Who gets cancer</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Most common cancers worldwide: lung, breast, prostate, colorectal, skin, stomach.</li>\n<li>US: men, prostate &gt; lung &gt; colorectal. Women, breast &gt; lung &gt; colorectal. Children, leukemia &gt; brain.</li>\n<li>78% of US cancers are diagnosed at age ≥55, partly because immune surveillance weakens with aging.</li>\n<li>Incidence, prevalence, and mortality vary by gender and socioeconomic indicators.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"cancer--risk-factors-table-13-2\"><h2>Risk factors (Table 13.2)<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#cancer--risk-factors-table-13-2\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><p>Carcinogens · environment · hormones · genetics · lifestyle · infectious disease · medications (e.g., <strong>beta blockers</strong> as an example from class) · immune status · nutrition. Risks are cumulative.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Non-modifiable:</strong> age and genetics. 5–10% of cancers come from known genetic syndromes such as BRCA1/2, p53 (Li-Fraumeni), FAP, and retinoblastoma.</li>\n<li><strong>Modifiable:</strong> sedentary lifestyle, poor diet, smoking.</li>\n<li><strong>Red flags for hereditary cancer:</strong> multiple primaries, bilateral tumors in paired organs, younger-than-usual age, a tumor in the sex not usually affected (e.g., male breast cancer), ≥2 first-degree relatives with the same cancer.</li>\n<li><strong>BRCA1</strong> raises the risk of breast and ovarian cancer. <strong>BRCA2</strong> also raises the risk of pancreatic, prostate, and melanoma.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"cancer--clinical-presentation-caution\"><h2>Clinical presentation: CAUTION ★<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#cancer--clinical-presentation-caution\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>C</strong></td>\n<td>Change in bowel or bladder habits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>A</strong></td>\n<td>A sore that does not heal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>U</strong></td>\n<td>Unusual bleeding or discharge</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>T</strong></td>\n<td>Thickening or lump (breast or anywhere)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>I</strong></td>\n<td>Indigestion or difficulty swallowing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>O</strong></td>\n<td>Obvious change in a wart or mole</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>N</strong></td>\n<td>Nagging cough or hoarseness</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p>If a sign lasts <strong>more than 2 weeks</strong>, the patient needs evaluation.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Constitutional signs:</strong> vague symptoms, fatigue, fever of unknown cause, unexplained weight loss, night sweats. In lymphoma these are called <strong>&quot;B symptoms&quot;</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Malignancy-specific signs:</strong> usually come from the tumor mass compressing or eroding nearby structures.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"cancer--staging-and-grading\"><h2>Staging and grading<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#cancer--staging-and-grading\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><aside class=\"callout dx\"><div class=\"chead\">TNM staging (exam topic, tables are tested)</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>T</strong> = tumor size · <strong>N</strong> = number of lymph nodes involved · <strong>M</strong> = metastasis (yes or no)</li>\n<li><strong>Stage I:</strong> small, no obvious spread outside the organ</li>\n<li><strong>Stage II:</strong> invades deeper tissues or local lymph nodes</li>\n<li><strong>Stage III:</strong> large or locally advanced, possibly in local nodes</li>\n<li><strong>Stage IV:</strong> metastasized</li>\n<li><strong>All hematologic malignancies are stage IV at diagnosis</strong> because they involve the whole body from the start.</li>\n<li><strong>Grading</strong> describes how differentiated the cells are. Poorly differentiated = more aggressive.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"chead\"><span class=\"tag\">Class note</span> TNM decode (from your class PLQ)</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>T = size of tumor · N = lymph nodes affected · M = metastasis? yes or no. A patient with <strong>T3, N2, M1</strong> who says &quot;I am relieved that the cancer is still local&quot; needs more teaching: <strong>M1 means it has spread.</strong></p>\n</div></aside>\n<p><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-004-002.jpg\" alt=\"Staging S0–S4: the tumor goes deeper through mucosa, submucosa, and muscle, then reaches lymph nodes. In S4 it has spread to other organs.\"><figcaption>Staging S0–S4: the tumor goes deeper through mucosa, submucosa, and muscle, then reaches lymph nodes. In S4 it has spread to other organs.</figcaption></figure></p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"cancer--diagnosis-goals-and-tools\"><h2>Diagnosis: goals and tools<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#cancer--diagnosis-goals-and-tools\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><p><strong>Goals:</strong> find whether a tumor is present and how far it extends, look for metastasis, check how involved and uninvolved organs are working, and get tissue for stage and grade.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Labs</strong> such as PSA, CBC, and tumor markers support the diagnosis and are tracked during treatment. <strong>PSA has high sensitivity but low specificity</strong> (false positives, e.g., prostatitis).</li>\n<li><strong>Imaging:</strong> CT shows mass location and vessel or organ involvement. PET lights up metabolically active &quot;hot spots&quot; (glucose must be controlled before PET). MRI is best for brain, joint, and breast.</li>\n<li><strong>Biopsy is the MOST SPECIFIC test</strong> and is required for a definitive (cytopathologic) diagnosis. <strong>Excisional</strong> biopsy (whole mass plus margin) is preferred over <strong>incisional</strong> (core) biopsy, which can track cells through normal tissue. <strong>FNA</strong> is less traumatic but yields fewer cells. A <strong>liquid biopsy</strong> is a blood test that looks for tumor DNA. Results take 3–10 days.</li>\n<li><strong>Endoscopic procedures</strong> (e.g., bronchoscopy) let the provider see inside hollow organs and take tissue.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-005-003.jpg\" alt=\"Fine-needle aspiration (FNA) of a breast mass\"><figcaption>Fine-needle aspiration (FNA) of a breast mass</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-005-004.jpg\" alt=\"Bronchoscopy lets the provider see the airways and biopsy the tumor\"><figcaption>Bronchoscopy lets the provider see the airways and biopsy the tumor</figcaption></figure></p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"cancer--oncologic-emergencies\"><h2>Oncologic emergencies<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#cancer--oncologic-emergencies\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><p>Acute complications that need urgent treatment, grouped as <strong>structural, metabolic, or hematologic</strong>. Slide examples: neutropenic fever, arrhythmia, DVT, hypercalcemia, spinal cord compression. Treat aggressively if the patient can recover. If the emergency signals progressive disease, the team may choose a palliative approach.</p>\n<h3>Table 13.3 Oncologic emergencies ★</h3>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Emergency / who gets it</th>\n<th>What&#39;s happening</th>\n<th>Look for</th>\n<th>Treatment</th>\n<th>Nursing</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Hypercalcemia</strong> (bone mets from breast, lung, renal; lung and pancreatic tumors that make PTH-like hormone)</td>\n<td>Bone breakdown releases calcium into the blood</td>\n<td>Delirium, somnolence, muscle weakness, <strong>polyuria</strong>, bradycardia, nausea, constipation</td>\n<td><strong>NS hydration</strong> (dilutes calcium, increases excretion), <strong>bisphosphonates</strong> (pamidronate, zoledronate), denosumab</td>\n<td>Monitor Ca, phosphorus, and renal function. Give fluids. <em>Why: bone mets mean calcium is a key lab.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Spinal cord compression</strong> (tumors near the spine)</td>\n<td>A mass compresses the epidural space or its blood supply</td>\n<td><strong>Back pain and sensory changes</strong> (numbness, tingling) come before motor weakness. Unsteady gait, can&#39;t tell hot from cold. Bowel and bladder dysfunction is late</td>\n<td><strong>Radiation</strong> (most common, rapid relief), rapid-onset corticosteroids, laminectomy, vertebroplasty or kyphoplasty</td>\n<td>Assess back pain, weakness, gait, constipation or incontinence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Superior vena cava syndrome</strong> (lung cancer, lymphoma, chest tumors)</td>\n<td>Tumor or nodes squeeze the soft-walled SVC, so blood can&#39;t return from the upper body</td>\n<td><strong>Dyspnea</strong> (most common). Edema of neck, face, eyes, <strong>worst in the morning</strong>. Prominent upper-body veins. Headache, visual changes, confusion (cerebral edema)</td>\n<td>Immediate chemo or radiation, corticosteroids, thrombolytics if a clot is present, stent</td>\n<td>Early: dyspnea and facial edema. Late: confusion, cyanosis, hypotension, tachycardia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Tumor lysis syndrome</strong> (leukemia, lymphoma, fast-responding tumors)</td>\n<td>Rapid cell lysis dumps contents faster than the kidneys can clear them</td>\n<td><strong>Hyperkalemia</strong> (peaked T, heart block), <strong>hypocalcemia</strong> (perioral and fingertip tingling, laryngospasm), <strong>hyperphosphatemia</strong>, <strong>hyperuricemia</strong>, renal failure (↑BUN and creatinine, ↓urine output)</td>\n<td>Large-volume IV fluids plus diuretics. <strong>Allopurinol</strong> to prevent hyperuricemia. <strong>Rasburicase</strong> if uric acid already high. Phosphate binders (Amphojel). Dialysis or CRRT</td>\n<td>ECG changes, signs of low calcium, renal labs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SIADH</strong> (brain and lung cancer)</td>\n<td>Excess ADH causes water retention</td>\n<td>Dilutional <strong>hyponatremia</strong> leading to confusion, seizures, coma. Fluid overload and hypertension</td>\n<td><strong>Fluid restriction first.</strong> Hypertonic saline (correct Na no faster than 0.5 mEq/hr). Vaptans</td>\n<td>Restrict fluids. Watch for overload and neuro changes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Leukostasis</strong> (AML with very high WBC)</td>\n<td>Immature WBCs sludge the capillaries, causing clots and ruptured vessels</td>\n<td>Kidney, lung, and brain signs: blurred vision, headache, TIA or stroke, dyspnea, oliguria</td>\n<td>IV fluids, emergency chemo, leukapheresis</td>\n<td>Monitor WBC. Assess for occluded microcirculation and bleeding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pericardial effusion / tamponade</strong> (lung and breast cancer, lymphoma)</td>\n<td>Excess fluid around the heart blocks venous return, so cardiac output drops</td>\n<td>Dyspnea, poor perfusion, right heart failure signs</td>\n<td>Pericardiocentesis or pericardial window. Large IV fluid volumes until then</td>\n<td>Vital signs, perfusion. Prepare for pericardiocentesis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pleural effusion</strong> (breast, lung, leukemia, lymphoma, ovarian)</td>\n<td>Fluid in the pleural space compresses the alveoli</td>\n<td>Dyspnea, accessory muscle use, unequal chest excursion, diminished breath sounds, hypoxemia (confusion, anxiety)</td>\n<td>Thoracentesis, chest tube or pleural catheter, pleurodesis</td>\n<td>Breath sounds, chest excursion, how often symptoms recur</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Tracheobronchial obstruction</strong> (lymphoma, lung cancer)</td>\n<td>Tumor blocks the major airways</td>\n<td>Dyspnea with <strong>stridor</strong>, wheezing. Absent breath sounds late. More hypoxic than hypercarbic</td>\n<td>Bronchodilators and steroids, then stent, laser, or brachytherapy</td>\n<td>Respiratory distress, stridor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Bowel obstruction</strong> (colon, pancreatic, ovarian, prostate)</td>\n<td>A mass blocks the bowel. Greatest danger is rupture or perforation</td>\n<td>Vomiting, distention. Bowel sounds ↑ before the obstruction and ↓ or absent after it. Thin, <strong>ribbon-like stools</strong></td>\n<td>Surgery. NPO or TPN. No peristaltic stimulants if the obstruction is complete</td>\n<td>Detailed GI assessment</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p>Other cancer complications that are not emergencies: anorexia and cachexia, hypercoagulation (DVT prophylaxis is common), effusions, electrolyte and glucose problems, depression. <strong>Constipation in a patient on opioids could also mean bowel obstruction, hypercalcemia, or spinal cord compression.</strong></p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"cancer--prevention-and-screening\"><h2>Prevention and screening<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#cancer--prevention-and-screening\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Primary: prevent it</th>\n<th>Secondary: find it early</th>\n<th>Tertiary: reduce harm</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Risk factor modification, immunization, chemoprevention, avoiding carcinogens. Quitting tobacco for 10 years lowers lung cancer risk 30–50%. PPE for asbestos, daily sunscreen, healthy weight, exercise, plant-based diet, limit alcohol (≤1 drink/day women, ≤2 men). <strong>Immunization:</strong> HPV (cervical, head and neck), hepatitis. <strong>Chemoprevention:</strong> hormone drugs for high-risk patients. Nutraceuticals are not proven</td>\n<td><strong>Screening for early detection:</strong> general check-up, breast, cervical, colon, prostate. A good screening test is <strong>sensitive</strong> (few false negatives) and <strong>specific</strong> (few false positives)</td>\n<td>Reduce morbidity and mortality after diagnosis: treat the cancer and manage side effects</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><h3>Table 13.4 Screening guidelines (ACS) ★</h3>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Cancer</th>\n<th>Guideline</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>General</td>\n<td>Age ≥20: discuss risk and screening at the annual physical. Share family history</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Breast</td>\n<td>Ages 40–44 may choose an annual mammogram. Ages 45–54: annual. Age ≥55: may switch to every 2 years, and continue while healthy with ≥10 years of life expectancy. Annual clinical breast exam. Ages 20–39: clinical breast exam every 3 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cervical</td>\n<td>Ages 25–65: Pap plus HPV every 5 years, OR Pap every 3 years plus HPV every 5 years. May stop at 65 if prior screens were normal. (See Chapter 65 for the ACOG start-at-21 schedule)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Endometrial</td>\n<td>At menopause, counsel all patients about the risk and to report any unexpected vaginal bleeding or spotting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Colon</td>\n<td>Age ≥45: stool test, OR flex sigmoidoscopy every 5 years, OR colonoscopy every 10 years, OR barium enema or CT colonography every 5 years. Stop over age 85</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prostate</td>\n<td>Age ≥50 (≥45 with risk factors): talk to the provider about PSA ± DRE. African American men have the highest incidence and 2× the mortality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lung</td>\n<td>No general guideline. Yearly low-dose helical CT for high-risk patients: current smokers or those who quit within 15 years, with a 20 pack-year history</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Melanoma; NHL, pancreatic, myeloma</td>\n<td>Melanoma: monitor your own skin. The others have no guideline (for myeloma, consider a urine Bence Jones protein test if family history is strong)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></section>\n<section id=\"cancer--treatment-goals-and-the-4-modalities\"><h2>Treatment: goals and the 4 modalities<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#cancer--treatment-goals-and-the-4-modalities\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><aside class=\"callout tx\"><div class=\"chead\">Goals of care</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Cure:</strong> often defined as 5 years disease-free. Possible with early resectable breast, cervix, colon, testicular, and uterine cancers.</li>\n<li><strong>Remission:</strong> disease-free, but unclear whether it&#39;s gone for good. The usual goal in heme malignancies.</li>\n<li><strong>Control:</strong> extend life. <strong>Palliation:</strong> relieve symptoms when cure isn&#39;t possible.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout pearl\"><div class=\"chead\">Timing terms ★ (testable)</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Primary therapy:</strong> the definitive treatment (often surgery).</li>\n<li><strong>Neoadjuvant</strong> = given <strong>BEFORE</strong> definitive treatment to shrink the tumor (e.g., chemo before mastectomy).</li>\n<li><strong>Adjuvant</strong> = given <strong>AFTER</strong>, when the patient is disease-free but at risk from leftover microscopic cells (e.g., radiation or oral hormonal therapy after surgery).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<h3>1 · Surgery</h3>\n<p>The preferred modality and primary treatment for solid tumors. Goal is total excision plus a margin. Other types: <strong>debulking</strong> (partial removal when the whole tumor can&#39;t come out), <strong>redirection of vital functions</strong> (e.g., an ostomy), and <strong>reconstruction</strong>. Cancer patients may come to surgery with chronic pain meds, weight loss and malnutrition, poor skin integrity, organ dysfunction (slower anesthetic clearance), and infection risk. Watch for <strong>phantom limb</strong> after limb surgery.</p>\n<h3>2 · Radiation</h3>\n<p>Localized ionizing radiation destroys the DNA of cancer cells.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>External beam</strong> is given in daily &quot;fractions&quot; (5 days on, 2 off). <strong>CyberKnife</strong> is stereotactic: one precise high-dose treatment.</li>\n<li><strong>Internal = brachytherapy:</strong> radioactive &quot;seeds&quot; placed in a body cavity (cervix, prostate, breast, others). Low-dose seeds stay in for days or are permanent. High-dose sources dwell for a set time and are then removed.</li>\n<li><strong>Systemic:</strong> radioactive substance given IV (e.g., radioactive iodine for thyroid cancer).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-009-005.jpg\" alt=\"Brachytherapy: radioactive seeds placed in the prostate near the tumor\"><figcaption>Brachytherapy: radioactive seeds placed in the prostate near the tumor</figcaption></figure></p>\n\n<aside class=\"callout watch\"><div class=\"chead\">Radiation side effects</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>Effects match the body area treated. Local injury peaks at <strong>weeks 2–3</strong>. Inflammation comes first, then <strong>fibrosis</strong> (a late effect: adhesions, skin changes, strictures, so a vaginal dilator may be needed).</li>\n<li><strong>Radiodermatitis:</strong> erythema, pruritus, dry or moist desquamation. Use mild pH-neutral soap. Calendula or hyaluronic acid cream can prevent it. <strong>No rubefacients (Icy Hot, Bengay)</strong> on radiated skin.</li>\n<li><strong>Fatigue</strong> is very common, builds throughout treatment, peaks at 3–6 weeks. Pace activities and get mild to moderate exercise. Reassure the patient that fatigue does not mean the cancer is spreading.</li>\n<li><strong>Chest wall radiation:</strong> watch skin, lungs (pneumonitis: dry cough), and heart (late cardiac effects).</li>\n<li><strong>Nurse safety:</strong> wear PPE. Brachytherapy patients may eliminate radioactive waste in body fluids, so teach the family too.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<h3>3 · Antineoplastic medications (Table 13.5)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chemotherapy</strong> (alkylating agents, antimetabolites, antitumor antibiotics) destroys DNA. It can be cell-cycle specific or nonspecific. It kills <strong>all rapidly dividing cells</strong>, including hair, skin, GI lining, and bone marrow, which explains its side effects. Many are hazardous drugs. Class example: <strong>methotrexate</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Immunotherapy</strong> (pembrolizumab/Keytruda) boosts immune recognition of the tumor. Assess for allergic reactions. Checkpoint inhibitors can cause immune-mediated adverse effects for up to a year after treatment.</li>\n<li><strong>Targeted agents</strong> (HER-2 blockers like trastuzumab; Bcr-Abl inhibitors like imatinib; EGFR agents) block a tumor&#39;s unique pathway. Many are oral, with drug–food interactions. Check insurance coverage.</li>\n<li><strong>Hormonal agents</strong> (tamoxifen, aromatase inhibitors, androgen suppression) change the hormone environment that feeds the tumor.</li>\n<li>Regimens combine classes and are given in <strong>cycles</strong>. Delays or dose reductions can compromise the response, so patients should not skip a treatment.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<aside class=\"callout watch\"><div class=\"chead\">Immune-mediated adverse effects (checkpoint inhibitors)</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Pro-inflammatory effects can hit any organ: myocarditis, diabetes, hypothyroidism, hypopituitarism, colitis and diarrhea (perforation), hepatitis, pneumonitis, nephritis, rashes, fatigue. They can be mistaken for infection, and missing treatment with corticosteroids or infliximab could be fatal. EHRs often flag patients who got these drugs in the past year.</p>\n</div></aside>\n<h3>Table 13.6 Chemo adverse effects and what the nurse does ★</h3>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Adverse effect</th>\n<th>Nursing implications</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Myelosuppression</strong> ⚠</td>\n<td>CBCs checked frequently. Teach the patient to <strong>call the provider IMMEDIATELY for a fever</strong>. Myelosuppression plus fever is treated aggressively to prevent sepsis and shock. <strong>Neutropenia is ANC &lt;1,500; fever is ≥38°C (100.4°F)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alopecia</td>\n<td>Starts soon after therapy begins and grows back weeks after it ends, often different (curly, thicker, a new color). Address body image, suggest wigs, cover the head in the sun. Scalp cooling (cap 20–30 min before to 2 hr after) works for solid tumors. <strong>Contraindicated in heme malignancies and cold agglutinin disease</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nausea / vomiting</td>\n<td>Prophylactic antiemetics. <strong>5-HT3 antagonists are the most effective</strong>; others are NK1 antagonists, steroids, phenothiazines, H1 blockers. <strong>Lorazepam</strong> is for anticipatory N/V. Assess fluid and nutrition status</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Oral mucositis</td>\n<td>Rinse every 2–4 hours and brush with a soft toothbrush. 2% viscous lidocaine rinse for pain. Cryotherapy (ice chips) with 5-FU or melphalan. Palifermin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hemorrhagic cystitis</td>\n<td>IV hydration before and after. <strong>Mesna</strong> protects the bladder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hypersensitivity</td>\n<td>Premedicate as ordered. Emergency equipment readily available (e.g., paclitaxel)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cardiotoxicity</td>\n<td>Echo or MUGA to monitor ejection fraction. Many drugs are held if EF drops. Troponin with doxorubicin. Keep fluids and electrolytes balanced (dysrhythmias)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pulmonary fibrosis</td>\n<td>Periodic PFTs (e.g., bleomycin)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hepatic / renal toxicity</td>\n<td>Check LFTs regularly. Review renal function before each dose and ask whether the dose needs changing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Clotting problems</td>\n<td>Some drugs are never given within 6–8 weeks of major surgery (bleeding). Prophylactic anticoagulation for clots. Bleeding precautions: avoid falls, sharps, razors; use stool softeners</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Peripheral neuropathy</td>\n<td>Fall precautions: remove scatter rugs and cords, use nonslip bath mats</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Diarrhea · skin (hand-foot)</td>\n<td>Antimotility drugs, fluids. Emollients. Report severe skin toxicity because therapy may need a break</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Infertility · menopause sx</td>\n<td>Offer sperm or egg banking before therapy. Teach about hot flashes, insomnia, fatigue</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Capillary permeability</td>\n<td>Fluid replacement for hypovolemia. Daily weights</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout watch\"><div class=\"chead\">Vesicants and extravasation</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Vesicant = a drug that destroys tissue on contact if it leaks out of the vein.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Give by IV push or a short infusion. A <strong>central line is ideal</strong>. A peripheral IV should be &lt;24 hours old, in a superficial vein you can watch.</li>\n<li>Check blood return <strong>before, every 2 mL or 2 minutes, and after</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>No blood return, pain, or swelling: STOP immediately.</strong> Put no more fluid into that vein. Call the pharmacist. An antidote goes through the catheter and into the tissue if one exists; otherwise use hot or cold compresses per policy. Mark the area with indelible marker, document, and follow up. A plastic surgery consult may be needed.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout watch\"><div class=\"chead\">Hazardous drug safety</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Most antineoplastics are Group 1 hazardous drugs: carcinogenic and teratogenic.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>PPE: impermeable gown plus <strong>TWO pairs of nitrile gloves</strong> (cuffs over the sleeves), a mask, and a face shield if there is a splash risk.</li>\n<li>Use closed-system transfer devices. Dispose of waste in hazardous-drug containers. <strong>Patient excreta are hazardous too</strong>, so teach the family.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<h3>4 · Cellular therapy: stem cell and bone marrow transplant</h3>\n<p>Two steps: (1) high-dose chemo ± radiation, which would normally destroy the marrow permanently, then (2) infusion of stem cells or marrow that &quot;rescues&quot; the patient.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Autologous</strong> = the patient&#39;s own cells (collected and frozen beforehand). <strong>Allogeneic</strong> = from a related or matched donor, with a possible graft-versus-tumor benefit but a risk of <strong>GVHD</strong>.</li>\n<li>Used mostly for heme malignancies and nonmalignant blood or immune disorders. Not routine for solid tumors.</li>\n<li>Other options: CAR T-cell therapy, donor lymphocyte infusion.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-012-006.jpg\" alt=\"Autologous transplant: (1) collect stem cells → (2) process → (3) cryopreserve → (4) high-dose chemo or radiation → (5) reinfuse the thawed cells\"><figcaption>Autologous transplant: (1) collect stem cells → (2) process → (3) cryopreserve → (4) high-dose chemo or radiation → (5) reinfuse the thawed cells</figcaption></figure></p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"cancer--nursing-management-cancer-general\"><h2>Nursing management (cancer, general)<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#cancer--nursing-management-cancer-general\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><p>Cancer care spans every med-surg specialty, across acute and chronic settings. <strong>Pain control is central.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bleeding risk (platelets &lt;50,000):</strong> monitor the CBC. Keep platelets at least 10,000–20,000 to prevent spontaneous bleeding, and transfuse below that threshold.</li>\n<li><strong>Infection risk (ANC &lt;1,500):</strong> hand hygiene, no sick visitors, no gardening or soil, no cat litter, flu vaccine, prophylactic antimicrobials.</li>\n<li><strong>Anorexia:</strong> corticosteroids or megestrol. <strong>Fatigue:</strong> rule out anemia and depression, encourage exercise, conserve energy.</li>\n<li><strong>Older adults:</strong> frailty, polypharmacy, renal dosing. Infection may show up as acute confusion without a fever. Fall risk.</li>\n<li>Cancer patients should not skip a treatment.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<aside class=\"callout pearl\"><div class=\"chead\">Case study: Mrs. Jones</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>She has a 2 cm immovable mass in the upper outer breast near the axilla. Her mother had ovarian cancer. The plan is chemo first (<strong>neoadjuvant</strong>) to shrink the tumor, then mastectomy, then <strong>adjuvant</strong> radiation and oral hormonal therapy. For <strong>paclitaxel</strong> (a hypersensitivity risk and irritant/vesicant): have emergency equipment ready and check blood return.</p>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"cancer--class-notes-add-on-nur-291\"><h2>Class notes add-on (NUR 291)<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#cancer--class-notes-add-on-nur-291\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><aside class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"chead\"><span class=\"tag\">Class note</span> Exam tips from class</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>The exam has questions on <strong>oncology staging</strong> (TNM and stages I–IV).</li>\n<li><strong>Know lab numbers</strong> (electrolytes, CBC). Both tables are in the Cheat Sheets part.</li>\n<li><strong>The exam takes questions from tables.</strong> Study every table (13.3 emergencies, 13.4 screening, 13.6 chemo effects, 33.7 labs).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"chead\"><span class=\"tag\">Class note</span> Your instructor emphasized</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>Apoptosis: no cell death with cancer cells.</li>\n<li>Stages of cancer cell metastasis (slide 7): primary site → invade tissue and vessels → travel → reinvade.</li>\n<li>Tumor staging and grading.</li>\n<li>CAUTION symptoms.</li>\n<li>Hematologic (&quot;liquid&quot;) malignancies arise from hematopoietic cells: lymphoid and myeloid lines.</li>\n<li>No apoptosis results in dysplasia, which can be a precursor to cancer.</li>\n<li>Health promotion: <strong>Primary</strong> = risk factor modification, immunization, chemoprevention, avoiding carcinogens. <strong>Secondary</strong> = screening for early detection (general check-up, breast, cervical, colon, prostate). <strong>Tertiary</strong> = reducing morbidity and mortality once disease is diagnosed.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"cancer--more-from-your-first-study-guide\"><h2>More from your first study guide<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#cancer--more-from-your-first-study-guide\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><h3>Table 13.2 · risk factors → signs → where it spreads ★ (tables are tested)</h3>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Cancer</th>\n<th>Risk factors</th>\n<th>Signs and symptoms</th>\n<th>Common mets</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Breast</strong></td>\n<td>Early menarche, late menopause, nulliparity, late 1st child; BRCA1/2, p53; obesity, high-fat diet</td>\n<td>Mass, axillary nodes, asymmetry, nipple discharge</td>\n<td>Bone, lung, nodes, liver, brain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lung</strong></td>\n<td>Tobacco, asbestos, coal/soot/tar, pollution, wood dust</td>\n<td>Chronic cough, hemoptysis, dyspnea, chest discomfort, weight loss, paraneoplastic syndromes</td>\n<td>Brain, bone, adrenal, other lung</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Colon</strong></td>\n<td>Alcohol, polyps, obesity/high-fat diet, Crohn&#39;s/UC</td>\n<td>Bowel change, ribbon stools, blood in stool, anemia, weight loss</td>\n<td>Liver, lung, muscle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Prostate</strong></td>\n<td>Age, family history, pesticides</td>\n<td>Pelvic/rectal pain, dysuria, nocturia, ED</td>\n<td>Bone, nodes, rectum, bladder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cervical</strong></td>\n<td>HPV, multiple partners, early sexual contact, tobacco, long-term OCPs</td>\n<td>Postcoital bleeding, malodorous discharge, dyspareunia</td>\n<td>Endometrium, vagina, pelvic wall, vulva</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ovarian</strong></td>\n<td>BRCA1/2, nulliparity, no breastfeeding, long-term OCPs*</td>\n<td>Abdominal fullness, bloating, fatigue</td>\n<td>Peritoneum, omentum, pleura</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Uterine</strong></td>\n<td>Early menarche/late menopause, estrogen therapy, PCOS, tamoxifen, DM, obesity</td>\n<td>Bleeding between cycles, lower abdominal cramping</td>\n<td>Nodes, vagina, peritoneum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Bladder</strong></td>\n<td>Tobacco</td>\n<td>Hematuria, abdominal discomfort, low-back pain</td>\n<td>Nodes, bone, lung, skin, liver</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Kidney</strong></td>\n<td>Pesticides, solvents, tobacco</td>\n<td>Flank pain, hematuria, weight loss</td>\n<td>Nodes, lung, liver, bone, adrenal, brain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pancreatic</strong></td>\n<td>BRCA2, tobacco, DM, pancreatitis, obesity</td>\n<td>N/V, early satiety, vague abdominal pain, jaundice</td>\n<td>Liver, peritoneum, lungs, bones</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Gastric</strong></td>\n<td>H. pylori, alcohol, ulcers, asbestos, smoking</td>\n<td>Indigestion, postprandial fullness, N/V</td>\n<td>Nodes, esophagus, liver</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Esophageal</strong></td>\n<td>Alcohol, smoking</td>\n<td>Dysphagia, reflux, chest discomfort</td>\n<td>Liver, lung, bones</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hepatic</strong></td>\n<td>Alcohol, hepatitis A/B/C, Budd-Chiari</td>\n<td>Jaundice, easy bruising, N/V</td>\n<td>Omentum, peritoneum, pleura</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Head and neck</strong></td>\n<td>Alcohol, tobacco, wood dust, HPV</td>\n<td>Oral lesions/bleeding, neck masses, jaw pain</td>\n<td>Nodes, lung</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Brain</strong></td>\n<td>Excessive alcohol</td>\n<td>Headaches, personality change, memory/visual deficits</td>\n<td>Spinal cord</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Leukemia</strong></td>\n<td>Alkylating agents, radiation, genetic mutations</td>\n<td>Fatigue, anemia, infections, bruising, petechiae, gum bleeding</td>\n<td>Diffuse at diagnosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lymphoma</strong></td>\n<td>Chronic immunosuppression, radiation, EBV, HIV</td>\n<td>Lymphadenopathy, subcutaneous masses</td>\n<td>Diffuse at diagnosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Multiple myeloma</strong></td>\n<td>Older age, Black race</td>\n<td>Fatigue, bone pain, dark infrequent urine</td>\n<td>Bone and kidney infiltration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Melanoma/skin</strong></td>\n<td>Sun/UV, ionizing radiation, family</td>\n<td>Irregular, new or growing lesions; palms/soles</td>\n<td>Nodes, lung, brain, liver, bone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Testicular</strong></td>\n<td>Undescended testes, HIV, age 20–34</td>\n<td>Painless scrotal lump, heavy sensation</td>\n<td>Nodes, lung, liver, bone, brain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Thyroid</strong></td>\n<td>Ionizing radiation</td>\n<td>Enlarged irregular thyroid, dysphagia</td>\n<td>Nodes, lung, bone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sarcoma</strong></td>\n<td>Arsenic, dioxins, herbicides, radiation</td>\n<td>Soft-tissue mass or organ dysfunction</td>\n<td>Lung, abdomen, local recurrence</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p>*Table 13.2 lists long-term OCPs for ovarian; Ch 65 says OCPs are <strong>protective</strong> for ovarian cancer. If asked about ovarian protection, OCPs protect.</p>\n<p><strong>Also:</strong> the 9 risk factors are carcinogens, environment, hormones, genetics, lifestyle, infectious disease, medications, immune status, nutrition. <strong>Carcinogen exposure = #1, but exposure alone rarely causes cancer.</strong> Risks are cumulative. You can&#39;t prevent it, so <strong>screen often (secondary prevention)</strong>. Disease-linked risks (IBD → colon, hep C → liver) are modifiable only as far as the disease is managed. Hereditary red flags also include rare histology, and ≥3 relatives in 2 generations. BRCA2 also raises male breast cancer risk. The BRCA1 and BRCA2 test detects breast cancer risk.</p>\n<h3>Metastasis: routes and examples</h3>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Route</th>\n<th>How</th>\n<th>Classic example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Cell-to-cell</strong></td>\n<td>Direct invasion of adjacent cells</td>\n<td>Prostate → rectum, pelvic floor, spine, hip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lymphatic</strong></td>\n<td>Through lymph channels of the organ</td>\n<td>Breast → axillary nodes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hematogenous</strong></td>\n<td>Into veins draining the organ</td>\n<td>Colon → liver (portal circulation)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p>Four steps: <strong>1 primary site → 2 invasion → 3 transport → 4 new growth.</strong> Solid tumors arise from organs and grow into masses that invade and erode tissue. Liquid (hematologic) tumors are all stage IV at diagnosis.</p>\n<h3>Stage definitions by cancer (all the size tables in your chapters)</h3>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Cancer</th>\n<th>Stage → size / spread</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>TNM stages</strong></td>\n<td><strong>0</strong> in situ (mucosa only; colon figure S0 stays in mucosa) · <strong>I</strong> small, no obvious spread outside the organ · <strong>II</strong> deeper tissues or local nodes · <strong>III</strong> large or locally invasive · <strong>IV</strong> metastasized</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Breast (female, Ch 65)</strong></td>\n<td>0 = in situ (DCIS) · IA ≤2 cm, no nodes · IB none or &lt;2 cm + node micromets &lt;2 mm · IIA none/&lt;2 cm with node &gt;2 mm, or 2–5 cm no nodes · IIB 2–5 cm + nodes, or &gt;5 cm no nodes · IIIA &gt;5 cm or any size + 4–8 nodes · IIIB any size to chest wall/skin (inflammatory), up to 9 nodes · IIIC any size to chest wall, 10+ nodes · IV any size + distant mets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Male breast (Table 66.6)</strong></td>\n<td>0 = DCIS, Paget&#39;s of nipple, LCIS · I &lt;2 cm, no nodes · II up to 5 cm ± nodes · III &gt;5 cm, nearby/supraclavicular nodes · IV mets to bone, brain, liver, lungs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Prostate (Table 66.4)</strong></td>\n<td>T1 impalpable, not on US · T2 confined to prostate · T3 locally extensive · T4 invades neighboring organs · N0 none → N1 single node &lt;2 cm → N2 2–5 cm → N3 &gt;5 cm · M1a nonregional nodes / M1b bone / M1c other</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lymphoma (Table 34.4)</strong></td>\n<td>I single node · II ≥2 nodes same side of diaphragm · III nodes on both sides · IV organs + nodes (&quot;E&quot; extranodal, &quot;S&quot; spleen)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ovarian (FIGO)</strong></td>\n<td>I ovaries · II pelvis · III peritoneal mets or nodes · IV distant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Uterine (FIGO)</strong></td>\n<td>I uterine body · II + cervix · III outside uterus in pelvis · IV bladder/bowel or distant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Prostate grade</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Gleason:</strong> 2 sites each graded 1–5, added; max 10 (higher = worse)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p><strong>Breast size breakpoints to memorize:</strong> ≤2 cm (stage I), 2–5 cm (stage II), &gt;5 cm (stage IIB/III). Nodes: 4–8 (IIIA), up to 9 (IIIB), 10+ (IIIC).</p>\n<p><em>Staging</em> = how big, how far (anatomy). <em>Grading</em> = how differentiated the cells look under the microscope (poorly differentiated = higher grade = more aggressive).</p>\n<h3>Biopsy procedures ★ (on your exam list)</h3>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Biopsy type</th>\n<th>Key facts</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Excisional</strong></td>\n<td>Removes whole mass + margin. <strong>Preferred</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Incisional (core)</strong></td>\n<td>Cuts into the mass; risk of tracking cancer cells through normal tissue</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Fine-needle aspiration (FNA)</strong></td>\n<td>Needle (by feel or imaging) suctions cells near the skin (breast). Less traumatic but fewer cells, limited molecular testing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Core needle</strong></td>\n<td>Wider needle with a cutter; larger sample</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Vacuum-assisted</strong></td>\n<td>Imaging-guided probe through small incision; vacuum + rotating cutter; local anesthesia; no sutures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Surgical excision (breast)</strong></td>\n<td>Mass &lt;2.5 cm removed entirely; nonpalpable mass marked with a hooked wire first; sutures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sentinel node biopsy</strong></td>\n<td>First node the cancer drains to; found with radioactive tracer/blue dye. Determines stage. Negative → no more nodes removed. Positive → axillary dissection (20–40 nodes)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Bone marrow aspiration/biopsy</strong></td>\n<td>For hematologic cancers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lymph node biopsy</strong></td>\n<td>Confirms lymphoma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Liquid biopsy</strong></td>\n<td>Blood test for cancer cells/tumor DNA (NSCLC, breast, colorectal, GI, ovarian)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Prostate needle biopsy</strong></td>\n<td>Office, local anesthetic, after abnormal DRE/PSA; tissue from 2 sites → Gleason score. Can hurt, bleed, get infected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Colposcopy + cervical biopsy</strong></td>\n<td>Cervix swabbed with acetic acid, viewed with lighted magnifier; abnormal areas biopsied. Cervical biopsy is diagnostic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Endometrial biopsy / D&amp;C</strong></td>\n<td>Uterine cancer: endometrial biopsy (99% sensitive for high grade); D&amp;C scrapes the lining if needed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ovarian</strong></td>\n<td>Biopsy <strong>NOT recommended</strong> (spreads cells). Definitive = surgical removal of mass + pathology</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Bronchoscopy / mediastinoscopy</strong></td>\n<td>Lung: scope through mouth/nose to view and biopsy airways; mediastinoscopy through chest incision checks nodes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p><strong>Imaging and labs:</strong> CT = mass location/organ involvement · PET = metabolically active &quot;hot spots&quot; (needs glucose control) · MRI = brain, joint, breast · contrast shows vessel involvement. Labs are supportive, not definitive. Tumor markers and blood counts track response. <strong>Nurse&#39;s role:</strong> explain purpose, prep, post-care, what to expect and when results come.</p>\n<h3>Treatment extras</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Neo = NEW = first.</strong> Neoadjuvant before, adjuvant after (kills microscopic cells).</li>\n<li><strong>External beam:</strong> machine (CyberKnife = one-time stereotactic). <strong>Patient is NOT radioactive.</strong> <strong>Internal (brachytherapy):</strong> body fluids may be radioactive: PPE, limit exposure. <strong>Systemic:</strong> IV radioactive substance (radioactive iodine).</li>\n<li><strong>Radiation skin care:</strong> mild pH-neutral soap; <strong>don&#39;t wash off markings</strong>; no menthol rubs on radiated skin. Fatigue = most common, peaks weeks 3–6.</li>\n<li><strong>Targeted:</strong> imatinib (Bcr-Abl, CML), trastuzumab (HER2), ibrutinib, sorafenib. Often oral: teach food/drug interactions. <strong>Hormonal:</strong> breast and prostate cancer.</li>\n<li><strong>Stem cell transplant:</strong> autologous = self · allogeneic = donor (graft-vs-tumor bonus, risk of GVHD) · matched unrelated · <strong>haploidentical</strong> (half match).</li>\n<li><strong>Constitutional signs:</strong> in lymphoma, fever + night sweats + weight loss = <strong>B symptoms (fatigue is NOT a B symptom).</strong> Malignancy-specific example: brain tumor → nerve pain and ↑ICP.</li>\n<li>Patients who get chemo do not drink a lot. <strong>Encourage lots of fluids beforehand</strong> or they can vomit.</li>\n<li><strong>Tumor lysis memory:</strong> cells burst and dump K⁺, phosphate, uric acid (all UP). Calcium binds phosphate, so Ca goes DOWN.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Table 13.7 · chemo priority nursing problems</h3>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Problem</th>\n<th>Cue</th>\n<th>Management</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Risk for infection</strong></td>\n<td>Neutropenia: WBC &lt;1,500; temp ≥38°C</td>\n<td>Monitor CBC, hand hygiene, no sick visitors, no gardening/soil, no cat litter, flu vaccine, prophylactic antimicrobials</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Risk for bleeding</strong></td>\n<td>Thrombocytopenia: platelets &lt;50,000</td>\n<td>Transfuse below 10–20k; bleeding and fall precautions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>N/V</strong></td>\n<td>Nausea, emesis</td>\n<td>5-HT3 (ondansetron) + NK1 + steroids on a schedule; lorazepam for anticipatory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Mucositis</strong></td>\n<td>Oral pain, sores</td>\n<td>Soft toothbrush, rinse q2–4h, viscous lidocaine, ice chips with 5-FU</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Anorexia/cachexia</strong></td>\n<td>↓BMI, ↓albumin/prealbumin</td>\n<td>Steroids, megestrol, dietary counseling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Alopecia</strong></td>\n<td>Starts soon after therapy</td>\n<td>Regrows (may differ); wigs; protect scalp from sun</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hemorrhagic cystitis</strong></td>\n<td>Hematuria</td>\n<td>IV hydration; mesna</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cardiotoxicity</strong></td>\n<td>↓EF (doxorubicin)</td>\n<td>Echo/MUGA scans</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Peripheral neuropathy</strong></td>\n<td>Numbness, tingling</td>\n<td>Fall precautions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Infertility</strong></td>\n<td></td>\n<td>Offer sperm/egg banking before therapy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p><strong>Geriatric:</strong> infection may show as confusion, not fever; renal dosing; fall risk; polypharmacy. <strong>Fever ≥100.4°F (≥38°C) in a chemo patient = call the provider NOW.</strong> Hair loss, dry skin, nausea are expected.</p>\n<h3>Screening extras (Table 13.4)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Breast: 40–44 may start yearly mammogram · 45–54 yearly · ≥55 every 2 years (or yearly) · <strong>self-breast exam NOT recommended.</strong></li>\n<li>Endometrial: at menopause report any unexpected bleeding/spotting; yearly biopsy if high risk.</li>\n<li>Melanoma: annual skin self-exam. Ovarian, NHL, pancreatic, myeloma: no general screening guideline.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"cancer--chapter-13-powerpoint-word-for-word\"><h2>Chapter 13 PowerPoint, word for word<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#cancer--chapter-13-powerpoint-word-for-word\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><p>Your instructor&#39;s slides for this chapter, kept in slide order.</p>\n\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 1 · Key definitions</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Cancer – group of disorders characterized by uncontrolled abnormal cell growth Malignant cells – are characteristic of cancers Non-malignant – non cancerous Benign – not cancerous, but can become cancerous Carcinogens – promotes carcinogenesis Metastasis – abnormal cells that invade the surrounding tissue &amp; gain access to lymph and blood vessels</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 2 · How a normal cell becomes cancer</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Carcinogenesis – multiple carcinogen exposures &amp; genetic mutations Uncontrolled cell growth – no apoptosis, results in dysplasia – can be precursor to cancer Altered cell differentiation – do not look like the parent cell Metastasis – spread of tumors</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 3 · WHO gets cancer</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Second leading cause of death worldwide · Expected to increase over time Most common types globally: lung, breast, prostate, colorectal, skin, stomach Incidence, prevalence &amp; mortality vary on gender, socioeconomic indicators; carcinogen exposure – most common risk factor</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 4 · What raises cancer risk</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Carcinogens · Environment · Hormones · Genetics · Lifestyle · Infectious disease · Medications · Immune status · Nutrition · Table 13.2 – common cancers with risk factors</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 5 · Solid vs. hematological</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Solid tumor malignancies – arise from specific body organs, grow into masses Hematological malignancies – arise from hematopoietic cells; give rise to myeloid &amp; lymphoid cells (lymph nodes, spleen)</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 6 · TNM and stages I–IV</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Staging determines the size of the tumor, the lymph node involvement &amp; metastasis Tumor, nodes, metastasis (TNM): T – tumor size · N – number of lymph nodes involved · M – presence of metastasis Stages 1 to 4</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 7 · CAUTION, constitutional &amp; specific signs</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>CAUTION symptoms · Constitutional signs – vague symptoms, fatigue, fever of unknown etiology, unexplained weight loss, night sweats · Malignancy-specific signs &amp; symptoms – often related to tumor- mass compression or erosion</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 8 · The ones that can kill fast</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Acute clinical complications – require urgent treatment secondary to tumor growth Categorized as structural, metabolic, or hematological Organ dysfunction may be present Common ex: neutropenic fever, arrhythmia, DVT, hypercalcemia, spinal cord compression</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 9 · Primary, secondary, tertiary</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Primary: risk factor modification, immunization, chemoprevention, avoiding carcinogens Secondary: screening for early detection – general check-up, breast, cervical, colon, prostate Tertiary: reducing morbidity and mortality once disease is diagnosed</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 10 · Goals and diagnostic tests</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Determine presence, extent of tumor · Identify possible metastasis · Evaluate functions of involved and uninvolved body systems · Obtain tissue and cells for analysis, including tumor stage &amp; grade Laboratory tests · Imaging · Biopsy · Endoscopic procedures</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 11 · Surgery</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Surgical resection of a tumor – solid tumor malignancies; goal: total excision · Debulking of tumor mass · Redirection of vital functions · Reconstruction</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 12 · External, internal, systemic</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Radiation therapy – localized delivery of ionizing radiation, inducing cell death · External – via external beam; CyberKnife · Internal – placement in a cavity (brachytherapy) · Systemic – vascular administration of a radioactive substance</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 13 · Chemo, immunotherapy, targeted, hormonal</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Antineoplastic medications · Chemotherapy · Immunological therapy · Targeted agents · Hormonal agents</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 14 · Stem cell &amp; bone marrow transplant</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Stem cell and bone marrow transplants · Administration of high-dose antineoplastic therapy · Infusion of stem cells or bone marrow</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 15 · Caring for the oncology patient</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>All subspecialties of med-surg – oncology; geriatric nursing · Variety of care settings – acute or chronic · Pain control</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slides · Image-only slides (written out)</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>[Image: Mild Dysplasia] Normal pink cells sit in orderly layers on the basement membrane; the yellow abnormal cells are irregular in size and shape and disorganized.</p>\n<p>[Image: Staging (colon wall)] S0 stays in mucosa → S1 into submucosa → S2 into muscle → S3 through the wall and to lymph nodes → S4 spread to other organs.</p>\n<p>[Image: Fine-needle aspiration] Needle drawing cells from a breast mass.</p>\n<p>[Image: Bronchoscopy] Scope used in the lung cancer workup.</p>\n<p>[Image: Brachytherapy] Radioactive seeds in the prostate.</p>\n<p>[Image: Autologous transplant] 1. Collection (stem cells taken from patient&#39;s marrow or blood) → 2. Processing (lab purifies and concentrates) → 3. Cryopreservation (frozen) → 4. High-dose chemo and/or radiation → 5. Reinfusion (thawed cells infused back).</p>\n</div></details></section>"},{"id":"lung","title":"Lung Cancer","part":"Part 2 · Ch 26","summary":"SCLC vs NSCLC, diagnostics, surgeries, chest tubes, nursing care, plus the asthma case.","sections":[{"id":"lung--why-it-matters-and-risk-factors","title":"Why it matters and risk factors"},{"id":"lung--sclc-vs-nsclc-know-this-difference","title":"SCLC vs NSCLC ★ (know this difference)"},{"id":"lung--nursing-care","title":"Nursing care"},{"id":"lung--from-your-slides-asthma-case-clifford-davis","title":"From your slides: asthma case (Clifford Davis)"},{"id":"lung--class-notes-add-on-nur-291","title":"Class notes add-on (NUR 291)"},{"id":"lung--more-from-your-first-study-guide","title":"More from your first study guide"},{"id":"lung--chapter-26-powerpoint-word-for-word","title":"Chapter 26 PowerPoint, word for word"}],"html":"<section id=\"lung--lung-cancer\"><p>Lung cancer is the <strong>#1 cancer killer in the US</strong> for both men and women. <strong>More than 90% is caused by smoking.</strong> It is often silent until a late stage and is most common after age 65.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"lung--why-it-matters-and-risk-factors\"><h2>Why it matters and risk factors<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#lung--why-it-matters-and-risk-factors\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><ul>\n<li>ACS 2022: lung cancer is the 2nd most common cancer in both men and women and the leading cause of cancer death (~130,180 deaths).</li>\n<li>Survival depends on stage at diagnosis. <strong>Stage IA has 49% survival; stage IV has 1%.</strong></li>\n<li>Black men have higher incidence than White men. White women have higher incidence than Black women.</li>\n<li><em>SLIDE vs BOOK:</em> one slide says &quot;leading cause of cancer,&quot; but the book (and another slide) says 2nd most common cancer and #1 cause of cancer death.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Risk factors:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Smoking (90%):</strong> risk rises with the number of cigarettes and the years smoked. The most preventable cause.</li>\n<li>Second-hand smoke</li>\n<li>Radon</li>\n<li>Occupational pollutants, asbestos (also coal, soot, tar, wood dust, pollution per Ch 13)</li>\n<li><strong>Screening:</strong> yearly low-dose CT for high-risk patients (≥20 pack-years, current smoker or quit within 15 years).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<aside class=\"callout patho\"><div class=\"chead\">Pathophysiology</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Carcinogens such as cigarette smoke cause dysplasia of the lung epithelium. Repeated exposure leads to genetic mutations: oncogenes switch on, tumor suppressors switch off, cells evade apoptosis, and DNA repair fails. The result is uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells (carcinogenesis).</p>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"lung--sclc-vs-nsclc-know-this-difference\"><h2>SCLC vs NSCLC ★ (know this difference)<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#lung--sclc-vs-nsclc-know-this-difference\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Small cell (&quot;oat cell&quot;)</th>\n<th>Non–small cell</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>% of lung cancers</td>\n<td><strong>15%</strong></td>\n<td><strong>85%</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cell types</td>\n<td>Small (oat) cells</td>\n<td><strong>3 types: adenocarcinoma, squamous cell carcinoma, large cell carcinoma</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Behavior</td>\n<td>Grows quickly and spreads to other organs</td>\n<td>Slower</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prognosis</td>\n<td><strong>Poor at any stage.</strong> Only 5–10% survive 5 years. <strong>Class: life expectancy 6 months–1 year</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Good</strong> 5-year survival if found at an early stage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Surgery?</td>\n<td>Not the main treatment</td>\n<td><strong>Surgery is used primarily for NSCLC when there is no metastasis</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"chead\"><span class=\"tag\">Class note</span> Class vs book</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Your notes say small cell life expectancy is <strong>6 months–1 year</strong>. The book says only 5–10% survive 5 years. These fit together, but if a question asks for a number, the class number is 6 months–1 year.</p>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout ss\"><div class=\"chead\">Clinical manifestations (\"recognizing cues\") ★</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Usually not evident early; show up as the disease advances.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>new cough that won&#39;t go away</strong>, or a change in a chronic cough</li>\n<li><strong>Hemoptysis</strong> (small vessels rupturing)</li>\n<li>Shortness of breath, wheezing, hoarseness</li>\n<li>Chest pain</li>\n<li><strong>Headache and bone pain</strong>, which suggest metastasis (brain, bone)</li>\n<li>Weight loss</li>\n<li>Frequent pneumonia or bronchitis</li>\n<li>Common metastasis sites (Ch 13): <strong>brain, bone, adrenal gland, the other lung</strong>. Lung tumors can also cause paraneoplastic syndromes such as <strong>SIADH and hypercalcemia</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout dx\"><div class=\"chead\">Diagnosis, and why each test is done</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Chest x-ray / CT of the chest</strong> find the pulmonary lesion.</li>\n<li><strong>Sputum cytology</strong> may identify the specific tumor cells.</li>\n<li><strong>Bronchoscopy</strong> (scope through the mouth or nose) shows the trachea and bronchi and allows biopsy.</li>\n<li><strong>Mediastinoscopy</strong> is a surgical incision in the chest to view the mediastinum and biopsy.</li>\n<li><strong>PET scan</strong> looks at <strong>metabolism, not structure</strong>. It detects areas of high activity (rapidly dividing cancer cells).</li>\n<li><strong>Bone and abdominal scans</strong> look for metastasis.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout tx\"><div class=\"chead\">Nonsurgical treatment</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Depends on the type, size, location, and stage of the tumor.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Chemotherapy:</strong> primary treatment for advanced cancer or a patient too ill for surgery. Can be neoadjuvant (shrink first) or adjuvant (kill leftover cells).</li>\n<li><strong>Radiation:</strong> used when surgery isn&#39;t an option, before or after surgery, or for palliation of symptoms and pain.</li>\n<li><strong>Pain management follows the WHO ladder.</strong> Step 1: nonopioid (acetaminophen, NSAIDs). Step 2: moderate opioid (codeine, hydrocodone, oxycodone) plus a nonopioid. Step 3: strong opioid (morphine, fentanyl) plus a nonopioid. <strong>Give around the clock, not on demand</strong>, to avoid pain peaks.</li>\n<li><strong>Palliative care should begin AT DIAGNOSIS.</strong> It manages pain, dyspnea, and nausea and improves quality of life.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout tx\"><div class=\"chead\">Surgery (preferred if no metastasis)</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Lobectomy:</strong> removes an entire lobe, when the cancer is localized to one lobe.</li>\n<li><strong>Pneumonectomy:</strong> removes the entire lung, when cancer is spread diffusely through it.</li>\n<li><strong>Wedge resection:</strong> removes a small section of a lobe, for cancer in one small area.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<p><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-015-007.jpg\" alt=\"Types of lung surgery: lobectomy, pneumonectomy, wedge resection\"><figcaption>Types of lung surgery: lobectomy, pneumonectomy, wedge resection</figcaption></figure></p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"lung--nursing-care\"><h2>Nursing care<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#lung--nursing-care\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Assessments, and why</th>\n<th>Actions, and why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>SpO₂:</strong> low because of poor gas exchange. &lt;90% = significant problem</td>\n<td>Give O₂ to keep SpO₂ ≥90%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Temperature:</strong> a fever means infection</td>\n<td><strong>Semi-Fowler&#39;s position</strong> <em>→ why: allows full lung expansion and better oxygenation</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Breath sounds:</strong> wheezes suggest airway obstruction. Rhonchi mean secretions in the upper airways</td>\n<td><strong>Small, frequent meals plus supplements</strong> <em>→ why: a large meal pushes on the diaphragm; supplements add calories for the work of breathing</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cough:</strong> irritation of the tracheobronchial tree. Hemoptysis means small vessels are rupturing</td>\n<td><strong>Give meds:</strong> pain and anti-anxiety <em>→ why: relaxation lets the lungs expand better</em>; bronchodilators <em>→ why: open the airway and decrease the work of breathing</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pain:</strong> increases respiratory rate and lowers quality of life</td>\n<td>Monitor temperature, cough, pain, and breath sounds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Appetite and weight:</strong> chemo side effects and the work of breathing both cause weight loss</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p><strong>Nursing diagnoses:</strong> ineffective gas exchange · anxiety (can&#39;t breathe) · activity intolerance (fatigue, dyspnea) · pain</p>\n\n<aside class=\"callout watch\"><div class=\"chead\">Post-op: lobectomy / pneumonectomy</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Vital signs:</strong> hypotension or tachycardia may mean excessive blood or fluid loss.</li>\n<li><strong>Breath sounds:</strong> diminished or absent sounds may mean atelectasis.</li>\n<li><strong>Suture line:</strong> red, warm, with thick yellow drainage means infection.</li>\n<li><strong>Chest tube:</strong> excess bloody drainage suggests a bleed. Cloudy drainage suggests infection.</li>\n<li><strong>Water-seal chamber:</strong> persistent bubbling = an air leak in the system.</li>\n<li>Keep the system <strong>CLOSED</strong> to prevent air leaks.</li>\n<li><strong>NEVER CLAMP THE CHEST TUBE!</strong> <em>→ why: air or fluid builds up in the pleural space, worsening the pneumothorax and possibly causing a TENSION pneumothorax.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout nursing\"><div class=\"chead\">Teaching, and why</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Breathing techniques / pursed-lip breathing</strong> <em>→ why: clear sputum, reduce breathlessness, remove trapped air</em></li>\n<li><strong>Pace activities</strong> <em>→ why: conserves energy and lowers the work of breathing</em></li>\n<li><strong>Smoking cessation</strong> <em>→ why: can change the course of the disease</em></li>\n<li>Small frequent meals · medication regimen (proper inhaler use)</li>\n<li><strong>Take pain meds around the clock</strong> <em>→ why: keeps a steady level of control instead of peaks of pain</em></li>\n<li><strong>Outcomes:</strong> pain controlled, enough appetite and weight, adequate oxygenation. Refer for pulmonary rehab (respiratory therapists) and palliative care.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"lung--from-your-slides-asthma-case-clifford-davis\"><h2>From your slides: asthma case (Clifford Davis)<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#lung--from-your-slides-asthma-case-clifford-davis\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><p>A 39-year-old with asthma, a 15-year one-pack-a-day smoker. He is in the <strong>tripod position</strong> using pursed-lip breathing and can&#39;t finish a conversation. HR 150, RR 40, bilateral wheezes, <strong>SpO₂ 88%</strong>. Treatment: O₂ (up to 96%), prednisone (he missed his daily anti-inflammatory), albuterol nebulizers ×3, peak flow below baseline, then IM prednisone. Discharged on a prednisone taper with an updated asthma action plan.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Most important part of management:</strong> disease management through <strong>education</strong>, using an <strong>asthma action plan built on peak flow readings</strong>.</li>\n<li>⚠ <strong>Wheezing goes away AND breath sounds are diminished</strong> means he is not moving air (worsening obstruction or exhaustion). <strong>Emergency:</strong> respiratory failure, possible need for ventilation. A <strong>rising PaCO₂</strong> signals impending failure.</li>\n<li><strong>Priority when a partially obstructed airway is suspected: airway management.</strong> A major part of his treatment program: smoking cessation.</li>\n<li><strong>Rescue med</strong> = short-acting β₂ agonist (albuterol). <strong>Controller</strong> = inhaled corticosteroid (rinse the mouth to prevent oral candidiasis). <strong>Never stop prednisone suddenly (taper).</strong> Asthma meds are not stopped when asthma is controlled. <strong>Peak flow: blow 3 times and record the highest reading.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"lung--class-notes-add-on-nur-291\"><h2>Class notes add-on (NUR 291)<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#lung--class-notes-add-on-nur-291\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>From class</th>\n<th>Small cell (bad)</th>\n<th>Non–small cell (good!!)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>% of lung cancer</td>\n<td>15%</td>\n<td>85%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Outlook</td>\n<td>Life expectancy 6 months–1 year</td>\n<td>5-year survival rate for those diagnosed early</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Types</td>\n<td>Small (oat) cells</td>\n<td>3 types: adenocarcinoma · squamous cell carcinoma · large cell</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><ul>\n<li>90% of lung cancer is from smoking. No apoptosis in these cells.</li>\n<li>2 major types: small cell and non–small cell.</li>\n<li>Signs and symptoms (recognizing cues): new or changed cough, hemoptysis, SOB, wheezing, hoarseness, chest pain, headache and bone pain (mets), weight loss, frequent pneumonia.</li>\n<li>Diagnosis and medical management (nonsurgical vs surgical).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<aside class=\"callout pearl\"><div class=\"chead\">Connection check</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p><strong>When should palliative care start?</strong> Upon diagnosis. It manages pain, dyspnea, and nausea and improves quality of life, so it starts at diagnosis, not at the end of life.</p>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"lung--more-from-your-first-study-guide\"><h2>More from your first study guide<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#lung--more-from-your-first-study-guide\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><ul>\n<li><strong>Memory:</strong> small cell = <strong>S</strong>peedy, <strong>S</strong>preads, <strong>S</strong>ad survival. NSCLC types = &quot;All Smokers Lose&quot; (<strong>A</strong>deno, <strong>S</strong>quamous, <strong>L</strong>arge).</li>\n<li><strong>Headache = brain mets, the #1 site.</strong> Bone pain (bone mets) → check calcium. Lung cancer can also cause SIADH, hypercalcemia, and <strong>SVC syndrome.</strong> &quot;New cough that doesn&#39;t go away&quot; is the <strong>N</strong> in CAUTION.</li>\n<li>Lung cancer risk factors also include age &gt;65, air pollution, coal/soot/tar, wood dust.</li>\n<li><strong>Slide wording:</strong> the nursing diagnoses are <em>ineffective gas exchange related to disruption of pulmonary cells</em>, <em>anxiety related to inability to breathe effectively</em>, and <em>activity intolerance related to fatigue and dyspnea</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Day 1:</strong> palliative care starts upon diagnosis (Connection Check 26.4). It&#39;s <strong>not the same as hospice.</strong></li>\n<li>Asthma case reminder: a quiet chest (no wheezes + diminished breath sounds) = obstruction/emergency → <strong>airway first.</strong> Controller meds daily even when well; don&#39;t stop prednisone suddenly.</li>\n<li><strong>Only biopsy confirms</strong> lung cancer.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"lung--chapter-26-powerpoint-word-for-word\"><h2>Chapter 26 PowerPoint, word for word<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#lung--chapter-26-powerpoint-word-for-word\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><p>Your instructor&#39;s slides for this chapter, kept in slide order.</p>\n\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 1 · WHO gets lung cancer</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Leading cause of cancer in US for both men and women 2nd most common cancer in both men and women as per ACS statistics, 2022 &gt; 90% caused by smoking · Often asymptomatic until late stage · Most prevalent in persons over age 65</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 2 · How lung cancer develops</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells in the lungs; repeated exposure leads to genetic mutations, promoting carcinogenesis 2 major types: Non-small cell – 85% · Small cell or oat-cell cancer – 15%</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 3 · Small cell vs. non-small cell</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Small cell or oat cell: represents 15% of LC; grows quickly &amp; spreads to other organs; survival rate only 5–10% in 5 years Non-small cell: represents 85% of LC; 3 types of cell: adenocarcinoma, squamous cell carcinoma &amp; large cell; 5-yr survival rate for those diagnosed in early stage is good</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 4 · What causes lung cancer</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Smoking (90% of cases) · Second-hand smoke · Radon · Occupational pollutants · Asbestos</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 5 · Signs and symptoms</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>New cough that does not go away · Changes to chronic cough · Hemoptysis · Shortness of breath · Wheezing · Hoarseness · Chest pain · Headache · Bone pain · Weight loss</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 6 · How lung cancer is diagnosed</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Chest x-ray · CT scan of the chest · PET scans · Sputum for cytology · Bronchoscopy and mediastinoscopy · Bone scan and abdominal scans – to look for metastasis</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 7 · Chemo, radiation, pain &amp; palliative care</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Nonsurgical: depends on size, location, and stage of tumor · Radiation · Chemotherapy · Pain management</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 8 · Lobectomy, pneumonectomy, wedge</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Surgical intervention: lobectomy · pneumonectomy · wedge resection</p>\n<p>Figure: three lungs showing a lobectomy, a pneumonectomy, and a wedge resection</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 9 · What to assess and nursing diagnoses</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Assessment and analysis: persistent cough · dyspnea · wheezing · hemoptysis · chest pain · frequent pneumonia or bronchitis Problem list Nursing diagnoses: ineffective gas exchange related to disruption of pulmonary cells · anxiety related to inability to breathe effectively · activity intolerance related to fatigue and dyspnea</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 10 · What the nurse does</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Provide oxygen · Monitor temperature · Assess breath sounds · Monitor for cough, pain · Position – semi-Fowler · Monitor appetite/weight – provide small, frequent meals with supplements Provide O₂ – increase SpO₂ to ≥ 90% · Position semi-Fowler · Small frequent meals with dietary supplements · Administer meds as ordered: pain medications, antianxiety, bronchodilators</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 11 · After lung surgery &amp; chest tubes</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Post-op care, monitor: vital signs · breath sounds · suture line · chest tube – maintain a closed tube system · monitor water-seal chamber · NEVER CLAMP THE CHEST TUBE!</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 12 · Patient education</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Teaching: breathing techniques · pace activities · smoking cessation · nutritional needs · medication regimen · pain control Evaluating care outcomes: pain control · sufficient appetite and weight</p>\n</div></details></section>"},{"id":"heme-assess","title":"Assessment of Hematologic Function","part":"Part 3 · Ch 33","summary":"Blood cells, left shift, clotting cascade, assessment, lab values (Table 33.7), iron studies, bone marrow biopsy.","sections":[{"id":"heme-assess--anatomy-and-physiology","title":"Anatomy and physiology"},{"id":"heme-assess--hematologic-assessment","title":"Hematologic assessment"},{"id":"heme-assess--table-33-7-lab-profile-normal-values-and-what-abnormal-means","title":"Table 33.7 Lab profile, normal values, and what abnormal means ★"},{"id":"heme-assess--bone-marrow-aspiration-and-biopsy","title":"Bone marrow aspiration and biopsy"},{"id":"heme-assess--class-only-nursing-tips","title":"Class-only nursing tips"},{"id":"heme-assess--case-marge-starr-25","title":"Case: Marge Starr, 25"},{"id":"heme-assess--more-from-your-first-study-guide","title":"More from your first study guide"},{"id":"heme-assess--chapter-33-powerpoint-word-for-word","title":"Chapter 33 PowerPoint, word for word"}],"html":"<section id=\"heme-assess--assessment-of-hematologic-function\"><p>Blood makes up <strong>7–10% of body weight</strong>, and an adult has <strong>5–6 L</strong>. Its jobs: deliver oxygen, form clots, and fight infection through inflammation and immunity.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"heme-assess--anatomy-and-physiology\"><h2>Anatomy and physiology<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#heme-assess--anatomy-and-physiology\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><ul>\n<li><strong>Bone marrow</strong> is the main site of blood formation (hematopoiesis), about 4–5% of body weight. Its stem cells become either the <strong>myeloid line</strong> (RBCs, WBCs, platelets) or the <strong>lymphoid line</strong> (T and B lymphocytes).</li>\n<li><strong>RBCs (erythrocytes)</strong> are the most abundant cells: small biconcave discs with no nucleus. They carry O₂ from lungs to tissues and CO₂ back. They live about <strong>120 days</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Hemoglobin</strong> is the O₂-carrying part of the RBC. Its pigment (heme) contains <strong>iron</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>WBCs (leukocytes)</strong> defend against infection. Normal is <strong>4,500–11,000</strong>. Granulocytes protect during acute inflammation. <strong>Neutrophils</strong> fight bacteria and fungi, are phagocytic, and are the <strong>first cells to arrive</strong> at a site. <strong>Eosinophils</strong> destroy foreign cells and respond in allergy.</li>\n<li><strong>Platelets (thrombocytes)</strong> activate clotting factors and form a stable <strong>fibrin</strong> clot.</li>\n<li><strong>Plasma</strong> is about 90% water, 6.5–8% protein, and 2% other substances.</li>\n<li><strong>Spleen (LUQ)</strong> filters blood and removes abnormal WBCs, platelets, and old RBCs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-018-008.jpg\" alt=\"Left shift: more immature neutrophils (bands) than normal are in the blood, meaning the marrow is pumping out cells to fight infection or inflammation\"><figcaption>Left shift: more immature neutrophils (bands) than normal are in the blood, meaning the marrow is pumping out cells to fight infection or inflammation</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-018-009.jpg\" alt=\"Blood composition: plasma 55%, buffy coat (WBCs and platelets) &amp;lt;1%, RBCs 45%\"><figcaption>Blood composition: plasma 55%, buffy coat (WBCs and platelets) &amp;lt;1%, RBCs 45%</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-019-010.jpg\" alt=\"RBC life cycle: the marrow makes RBCs (needs iron, B₁₂, folic acid). They live 120 days. The spleen breaks down old cells: globin becomes amino acids, heme becomes iron (recycled) plus bilirubin (excreted in bile)\"><figcaption>RBC life cycle: the marrow makes RBCs (needs iron, B₁₂, folic acid). They live 120 days. The spleen breaks down old cells: globin becomes amino acids, heme becomes iron (recycled) plus bilirubin (excreted in bile)</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-019-011.jpg\" alt=\"Clotting cascade: the intrinsic pathway is monitored by PTT (heparin). The extrinsic pathway is monitored by PT/INR (warfarin). Both join the common pathway: prothrombin → thrombin, fibrinogen → fibrin clot\"><figcaption>Clotting cascade: the intrinsic pathway is monitored by PTT (heparin). The extrinsic pathway is monitored by PT/INR (warfarin). Both join the common pathway: prothrombin → thrombin, fibrinogen → fibrin clot</figcaption></figure></p>\n\n<aside class=\"callout pearl\"><div class=\"chead\">Too many RBCs is also bad</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Excess RBCs increase blood viscosity, which decreases O₂ transport. Polycythemia vera works this way.</p>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"chead\"><span class=\"tag\">Class note</span> Clotting cascade from class</div><div class=\"cbody\"><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pathway</th>\n<th>Drug</th>\n<th>Healing</th>\n<th>Lab</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Intrinsic</td>\n<td>Heparin</td>\n<td>Healing the mucosa lining</td>\n<td>Check PT and PTT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Extrinsic</td>\n<td>Warfarin</td>\n<td>Healed with good coagulation factor</td>\n<td>Check PT/INR</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p><em>Class vs book:</em> your notes list PT and PTT for the intrinsic pathway. The book and slide picture pair <strong>heparin with PTT</strong> and <strong>warfarin with PT/INR</strong>. If a question asks which lab monitors heparin, <strong>PTT</strong> is the safe answer.</p>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"heme-assess--hematologic-assessment\"><h2>Hematologic assessment<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#heme-assess--hematologic-assessment\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><p><strong>History</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Any change in energy level? Trouble keeping up daily activities?</li>\n<li>Pain, burning, or tingling? Skin color or temperature changes? Edema?</li>\n<li>Bleeding or easy bruising, dizziness, fatigue, swollen or tender lymph nodes?</li>\n<li>PMH: hepatitis, cirrhosis, cancer, clotting factor problems. Pregnancy can cause gestational thrombocytopenia.</li>\n<li><strong>Meds:</strong> anticoagulants, aspirin and salicylates, herbs, supplements (goldenseal and green tea can reduce anticoagulant effect). Long-term antibiotics can cause coagulopathy and neutropenia. <strong>Aspirin irreversibly blocks platelets for their whole life span (4–7 days).</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Drugs that predispose to bleeding:</strong> aspirin, NSAIDs, heparin and LMWH, warfarin, DOACs, clopidogrel, antineoplastics, penicillins and cephalosporins, sulfonamides, thiazides, and antibiotics (they lower vitamin K).</li>\n<li><strong>Ginger and garlic promote bleeding</strong> (ask about herbs in the med history).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Physical exam</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Skin and mucosa:</strong> color, temperature, cap refill, edema. Look for petechiae, purpura, bruising.</li>\n<li><strong>Tongue:</strong> red, swollen, smooth, shiny, tender (<strong>glossitis</strong>) points to IDA or pernicious anemia.</li>\n<li><strong>Respiratory:</strong> are the lungs working harder to deliver O₂? (RR &lt;12 or &gt;20 is abnormal.)</li>\n<li><strong>Cardiovascular:</strong> vital signs, murmurs, gallops. Systolic BP is lower in anemia and higher with too many RBCs.</li>\n<li><strong>Renal:</strong> check the meatus for bleeding and look for hematuria.</li>\n<li><strong>Musculoskeletal:</strong> bone pain. In leukemia, packed WBCs raise pressure inside the bone. In sickle cell, sickled RBCs cause infarction and swelling.</li>\n<li><strong>Abdomen: palpate the LUQ LIGHTLY.</strong> <em>→ why: an enlarged spleen is tender and can RUPTURE with deep palpation.</em> Stand on the opposite (right) side of the patient for the spleen assessment.</li>\n<li><strong>Normal lymph nodes should not be palpable nor painful.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-021-013.jpg\" alt=\"Light palpation of the LUQ for splenomegaly. Never palpate deeply because of the rupture risk.\"><figcaption>Light palpation of the LUQ for splenomegaly. Never palpate deeply because of the rupture risk.</figcaption></figure></p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"heme-assess--table-33-7-lab-profile-normal-values-and-what-abnormal-means\"><h2>Table 33.7 Lab profile, normal values, and what abnormal means ★<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#heme-assess--table-33-7-lab-profile-normal-values-and-what-abnormal-means\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Test</th>\n<th>Normal (adult)</th>\n<th>Significance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>RBC</strong></td>\n<td>F 4.01–5.51 · M 4.51–6.01 million/mm³</td>\n<td>↑ high altitude, chronic anoxia, polycythemia · ↓ anemia, blood loss, destruction, missing nutrients, marrow suppression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hgb</strong></td>\n<td>F 11.7–15.5 · M 14–17.3 g/dL</td>\n<td>Measures the severity of anemia or polycythemia and the response to therapy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hct</strong></td>\n<td>F 36–48% · M 42–52%</td>\n<td>↓ anemia, hemodilution, massive blood loss · ↑ polycythemia, dehydration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>MCV</strong> (cell size)</td>\n<td>78–100/102 μm³</td>\n<td>↑ <strong>macrocytic</strong>: pernicious anemia, folic acid deficiency · ↓ <strong>microcytic</strong>: IDA, lead poisoning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>MCHC</strong> (color)</td>\n<td>31–37%</td>\n<td>↓ hypochromic: IDA or a hemoglobinopathy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>WBC</strong></td>\n<td>4.5–11.1 ×10³/mm³</td>\n<td>↑ infection, inflammation, leukemia · ↓ prolonged infection, marrow depression. Get a differential</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Reticulocytes</strong></td>\n<td>0.5–2.5% of RBCs</td>\n<td>Immature RBCs. ↑ = marrow responding to hemolysis or blood loss (or to therapy). ↓ = hypoproliferative marrow (aplastic, pernicious anemia)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Platelets</strong></td>\n<td>150,000–450,000/mm³</td>\n<td>↓ thrombocytopenia. <strong>&lt;50,000: spontaneous bleeding possible. &lt;5,000: fatal CNS or GI bleed possible.</strong> ↑ PV, malignancy, after splenectomy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Serum iron</strong></td>\n<td>30–160 mcg/dL</td>\n<td>↓ IDA, hemorrhage. <strong>Draw in the MORNING</strong> (lower in the evening). <strong>No iron supplements for 24 hours before.</strong> An overnight fast is recommended</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ferritin</strong> (iron stores)</td>\n<td>20–400 ng/mL</td>\n<td>↓ iron deficiency, chronic GI bleed, heavy menses. Infection and inflammation can falsely raise it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>TIBC</strong></td>\n<td>250–410 mcg/dL</td>\n<td><strong>↑ in IDA</strong> (many empty binding spots). ↓ in hemochromatosis or chronic disease</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Transferrin sat.</strong></td>\n<td>20–50%</td>\n<td>↓ in IDA (always low)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hgb electrophoresis</strong></td>\n<td>Hgb A 95–100%</td>\n<td>Separates normal from abnormal Hgb. Diagnoses thalassemias and sickle cell (Hgb S 70–98%). Ask about transfusions in the past 4 months</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Coombs</strong></td>\n<td>Negative</td>\n<td>Positive means antibodies on RBCs: hemolytic disease of the newborn, transfusion reaction, autoimmune hemolytic anemia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>LAP</strong></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Enzyme from mature neutrophils. ↑ with infection, stress, inflammation, pregnancy, steroids, leukemia. <strong>High neutrophils WITHOUT high LAP suggests CML</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>PT</strong></td>\n<td>10–13 sec</td>\n<td><strong>Extrinsic</strong> pathway. Monitors warfarin. Prolonged with low factors or vitamin K deficiency</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>INR</strong></td>\n<td>0.9–1.1 (no anticoag)</td>\n<td><strong>Warfarin goal 2–3. Mechanical valve 2.5–3.5.</strong> ↑ with DIC, cirrhosis, vitamin K deficiency</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>PTT / aPTT</strong></td>\n<td>PTT 60–70 · aPTT 30–40 sec</td>\n<td><strong>Intrinsic</strong> pathway. Monitors <strong>heparin</strong> (and argatroban)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Bleeding time</strong></td>\n<td>3–8 min</td>\n<td>Platelet function. Not done if platelets &lt;75,000. Stop the test if bleeding lasts more than 15 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Platelet aggregation</strong></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Checks abnormal platelet function. Impaired in von Willebrand disease and with aspirin or NSAIDs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p><strong>Tube colors:</strong> CBC uses a <strong>lavender (EDTA)</strong> tube. PT, INR, and PTT use <strong>sodium citrate</strong>. Remove the tourniquet as soon as blood flows (to prevent hemolysis), invert tubes gently, and hold pressure until bleeding stops (longer if on an anticoagulant).</p>\n\n<aside class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"chead\"><span class=\"tag\">Class note</span> Iron studies emphasized in class (Diagnostic Studies slide 31)</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>Avoid iron supplements for at least 24 h before the test.</li>\n<li><strong>Serum ferritin</strong> = ability to store iron.</li>\n<li><strong>Transferrin</strong> = iron-transporting capacity.</li>\n<li><strong>TIBC</strong> measures the amount of iron in the plasma. <strong>High TIBC = low O₂ saturation.</strong></li>\n<li>Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase: elevated, &quot;thing about the bones&quot; (as written in your notes).</li>\n<li>With hematological disorders, the bone marrow needs to be examined for diagnosis.</li>\n<li>Know reticulocyte count, thalassemia, Coombs, clotting studies, bone marrow aspiration vs biopsy.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"heme-assess--bone-marrow-aspiration-and-biopsy\"><h2>Bone marrow aspiration and biopsy<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#heme-assess--bone-marrow-aspiration-and-biopsy\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><ul>\n<li><strong>Why:</strong> to diagnose thrombocytopenia, leukemias, anemias, granulomas, and primary or metastatic tumors, to stage Hodgkin&#39;s, to evaluate chemo, and to monitor myelosuppression.</li>\n<li><strong>Aspiration</strong> removes fluid. <strong>Needle biopsy</strong> removes a core of marrow. Usually done together.</li>\n<li><strong>Most common site: posterior iliac crest</strong>, patient prone or side-lying. Sternum or anterior iliac crest: supine.</li>\n<li><strong>Before:</strong> informed consent, allergies, bleeding risk (platelets, coags, anticoagulants). Explain they will feel pressure on insertion and a brief pulling pain on aspiration, and may hear crunching. Takes 5–10 minutes. A mild sedative is OK.</li>\n<li><strong>After:</strong> direct pressure 5–10 minutes and a sterile dressing. Rest several hours. Leave the dressing on ≥24 hours. Report bleeding that soaks the dressing or lasts more than 24 hours. Watch for infection and give analgesia.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-021-012.jpg\" alt=\"Bone marrow aspiration from the posterior iliac crest\"><figcaption>Bone marrow aspiration from the posterior iliac crest</figcaption></figure></p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"heme-assess--class-only-nursing-tips\"><h2>Class-only nursing tips<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#heme-assess--class-only-nursing-tips\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><ul>\n<li><strong>Multiple myeloma:</strong> tingling and burning sensation due to hypercalcemia.</li>\n<li>Patients who get chemo do not drink a lot. <strong>Encourage a lot of fluids beforehand</strong> or they can vomit.</li>\n<li>Most common site of <strong>oral cancer</strong> is the <strong>bottom of the tongue</strong>. <strong>Xerostomia</strong> = dry mouth.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"heme-assess--case-marge-starr-25\"><h2>Case: Marge Starr, 25<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#heme-assess--case-marge-starr-25\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><p>Fatigue, SOB, needs afternoon naps. HR 118, BP 90/72, RR 26. Pale skin and nailbeds, red swollen tongue. Labs: RBC 3 million, Hct 31%, Hgb 9, ↓ ferritin. This is <strong>iron deficiency anemia</strong>. The ↑HR and ↑RR are <strong>compensation</strong>, as the heart and lungs work harder to deliver O₂.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"heme-assess--more-from-your-first-study-guide\"><h2>More from your first study guide<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#heme-assess--more-from-your-first-study-guide\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><h3>White blood cells: the differential ★ (&quot;know neutrophils&quot;)</h3>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Cell</th>\n<th>%</th>\n<th>Job</th>\n<th>↑ / ↓</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Neutrophils</strong></td>\n<td>50–70</td>\n<td>Bacteria, fungi; first to arrive</td>\n<td>↑ bacterial infection · ↓ chemo, radiation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Eosinophils</strong></td>\n<td>1–3</td>\n<td>Destroy invading foreign cells; allergies, parasites</td>\n<td>↑ allergies, parasites · ↓ steroids</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Basophils</strong></td>\n<td>0.4–1</td>\n<td>Release histamine and heparin</td>\n<td>↑ allergy, inflammation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lymphocytes</strong></td>\n<td>20–35</td>\n<td>B cells → antibodies; T cells kill foreign/tumor cells</td>\n<td>↑ viral infections · ↓ HIV, cancer, radiation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Monocytes</strong></td>\n<td>3–8</td>\n<td>Largest WBC → macrophages</td>\n<td>↑ viral, chronic inflammation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><ul>\n<li><strong>Neutrophils</strong> remain about <strong>6 hours</strong> in circulation; mature = &quot;segs,&quot; immature = &quot;bands.&quot; ↑ in bacterial infection; ↓ with chemo/radiation → infection risk.</li>\n<li><strong>Left shift</strong> = immature neutrophils (bands) = active infection. The marrow rushes out immature cells (normal bands 3–5%). Bands aren&#39;t very effective at fighting infection.</li>\n<li>WBCs are the least abundant formed element (~1% of blood) but required for life. Normal 4,500–11,000/mm³.</li>\n<li><strong>WBC differential</strong> = the percentage (proportion) of each type of WBC.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Plasma proteins and other facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Albumin (60%):</strong> made in the liver; keeps fluid in vessels (osmotic pressure). <strong>Low albumin = problems with nutrition</strong> (also liver disease, burns, hemorrhage) ★ notebook. <strong>Globulins</strong> transport and help clotting; <strong>gamma globulins = antibodies</strong>. <strong>Fibrinogen → fibrin</strong> (clot framework). Plasma = water 90%, proteins 8% (albumin, globulin, fibrinogen), other solutes 2%.</li>\n<li><strong>Bone marrow</strong> = primary site of hematopoiesis; 4–5% of body weight; <strong>red marrow in flat bones</strong> (sternum, ribs, pelvis, vertebrae). Myeloid → RBCs, WBCs (granulocytes, monocytes), platelets. Lymphoid → T and B lymphocytes.</li>\n<li><strong>RBCs:</strong> made when the kidneys release <strong>erythropoietin (EPO)</strong>; need iron, B₁₂, folic acid, B6, protein. Each Hgb carries 4 O₂.</li>\n<li><strong>Platelets:</strong> from <strong>megakaryocytes</strong> (large marrow cells); 25–40% stored in spleen; live 7–10 days; normal 150,000–450,000 (lecture: 150–400k).</li>\n<li><strong>Spleen:</strong> LUQ &quot;coffee-bean.&quot; Filters blood; removes old RBCs and abnormal WBCs and platelets; recycles iron; stores platelets. <strong>Splenomegaly causes:</strong> anemia, PV, leukemia, cirrhosis, mono. Spleen is normally not palpable.</li>\n<li><strong>Assessment table:</strong> pallor = anemia · petechiae, purpura, bruising = bleeding · glossitis = iron deficiency or pernicious anemia · anemia → ↑HR, ↓ systolic BP, SOB · too many RBCs → ↑BP · hematuria · bone pain.</li>\n<li>Anemia vital signs (notebook ★): <strong>BP↓ HR↑</strong> (and ↑RR). Gestational thrombocytopenia 5–11%.</li>\n<li>Hematology functions: oxygen delivery · clotting cascade · inflammation and immunity (eliminate cause of injury, remove damaged tissue, generate new tissue).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Clotting: 5 steps, pathways, and factors</h3>\n<p><strong>1 Vasospasm</strong> (injured vessel constricts, ↓ flow) → <strong>2 Platelet plug</strong> (platelets stick; need von Willebrand factor) → <strong>3 Clot formation</strong> (cascade → fibrin mesh) → <strong>4 Clot retraction</strong> (platelets contract, pull edges together) → <strong>5 Dissolution</strong> (fibrinolysis removes clot after repair).</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pathway</th>\n<th>Trigger</th>\n<th>Factors</th>\n<th>Lab</th>\n<th>Drug</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Intrinsic</strong> (slower)</td>\n<td>Vessel injury, factors inside the blood</td>\n<td>XII → XI → IX + VIII → X</td>\n<td><strong>PTT / aPTT</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Heparin</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Extrinsic</strong> (faster)</td>\n<td>Tissue trauma; tissue factor (III)</td>\n<td>III + VII → X</td>\n<td><strong>PT / INR</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Warfarin</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Common</strong></td>\n<td>Both meet at factor X</td>\n<td>X + V → prothrombin (II) → thrombin → fibrinogen (I) → fibrin; XIII cross-links</td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><ul>\n<li><strong>Memory:</strong> <strong>PITT</strong> = PTT-Intrinsic (heparin). <strong>PET</strong> = PT-Extrinsic-Tissue (warfarin, &quot;War&quot;). <strong>Vitamin K</strong> makes factors II, VII, IX, X. <strong>Calcium (factor IV)</strong> is needed in all pathways.</li>\n<li><strong>Factors:</strong> I fibrinogen · II prothrombin · III tissue factor (starts extrinsic) · VIII/IX missing in hemophilia A/B (Christmas factor) · XII Hageman (starts intrinsic).</li>\n<li>Notebook ★: Intrinsic → check <strong>PTT</strong>. Extrinsic → check <strong>PT</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Lab extras</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>tourniquet &gt;1 minute</strong> falsely ↑ RBC/Hgb/Hct. <strong>Lavender (EDTA)</strong> tube for CBC; <strong>light blue (citrate)</strong> tube for coag studies, filled completely.</li>\n<li><strong>ANC ≤500</strong> = severe neutropenia → neutropenic precautions. Platelets: &lt;100k precautions · &lt;50k spontaneous bleed risk · &lt;20k transfuse · &lt;5k fatal CNS/GI bleed.</li>\n<li><strong>MCHC ↑:</strong> spherocytosis. <strong>Ferritin ↑:</strong> liver disease, inflammation. <strong>Serum iron ↑:</strong> hemochromatosis. <strong>INR ↑</strong> = bleeding risk. <strong>aPTT ↑:</strong> heparin, hemophilia.</li>\n<li><strong>Coombs</strong> also diagnoses transfusion reaction and autoimmune hemolytic anemia. <strong>LAP:</strong> high neutrophils without high LAP → CML.</li>\n<li><strong>Ferritin ≤100 indicates IDA</strong> (slide wording). <strong>Transferrin:</strong> low–normal in IDA. <strong>TIBC ↑</strong> and <strong>transferrin saturation ↓</strong> in IDA.</li>\n<li><strong>Bus analogy:</strong> transferrin is the bus, iron is the passengers. In IDA the bus has lots of empty seats (high TIBC) and few passengers (low saturation). Ferritin is the bus garage, and it&#39;s empty.</li>\n<li><strong>Patient prep for iron studies:</strong> avoid iron supplements ≥24 h before · draw serum iron in the morning (levels drop in the evening) · overnight fast recommended.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<aside class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"chead\"><span class=\"tag\">Class note</span> Class vs class</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Your notes&#39; PLQ answer for &quot;which result confirms IDA&quot; is <strong>C, increased TIBC</strong> (first guide). The newer guide&#39;s worked answer was <strong>A, decreased transferrin saturation</strong>. Both are true of IDA (TIBC ↑, transferrin saturation ↓). If only one is offered, pick it; if both are offered, ask your instructor.</p>\n</div></aside>\n<h3>Standard chemistry values (ranges vary slightly by lab)</h3>\n<p>Sodium 135–145 mEq/L · Potassium 3.5–5.0 mEq/L · Chloride 98–106 mEq/L · Calcium 9.0–10.5 mg/dL (≈8.6–10.2) · Magnesium 1.3–2.1 mEq/L · Phosphorus 3.0–4.5 mg/dL · BUN 10–20 mg/dL · Creatinine 0.6–1.2 mg/dL · Glucose (fasting) 70–100 mg/dL · Albumin 3.5–5.0 g/dL · Uric acid ≈2.5–7.0 mg/dL. Myeloma hypercalcemia criterion: Ca &gt;11.5 mg/dL; lymphoma hypercalcemia emergency: Ca &gt;11. <em>(Your class electrolyte table is in the Cheat Sheets part.)</em></p>\n<h3>Connection checks</h3>\n<p>Blood is formed mostly in <strong>flat bones</strong> · <strong>EPO</strong> stimulates RBC production · low Hgb → pallor and fatigue · clotting factor deficiency → easy bruising, gum bleeding · inspect mouth/tongue (never deep-palpate the spleen) · differential = proportion of each WBC · thrombocytopenia priority = <strong>prevent injury</strong>.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"heme-assess--chapter-33-powerpoint-word-for-word\"><h2>Chapter 33 PowerPoint, word for word<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#heme-assess--chapter-33-powerpoint-word-for-word\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><p>Your instructor&#39;s slides for this chapter, kept in slide order.</p>\n\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 1 · What the hematological system is</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Structure: hematological system – blood, blood cells, lymph &amp; organs · Plasma (clear yellow, protein- rich) · Solutes (proteins, electrolytes, organic elements)</p>\n<p>Function: blood accounts for 7–10% of body weight; average adult has 5–6 L in the circulatory system</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 2 · Where blood is made</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Primary site for blood formation and maturation (hematopoiesis) Involved in immune response &amp; makes up 4–5% of total body weight Produces stem cells that can become any type of blood cell: Myeloid – RBC, WBC, platelets · Lymphoid – T or B lymphocytes</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 3 · Erythrocytes &amp; hemoglobin</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Erythrocytes – most abundant, small biconcave disk with no nucleus; pick up O₂ from lungs &amp; transport; pick up CO₂ from tissues &amp; deliver to lungs Hemoglobin – oxygen-carrying component of an RBC; contains thousands of Hgb molecules with a pigment (heme) that contains iron</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 4 · Leukocytes &amp; the left shift</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Leukocytes – defense against infection; least abundant (4,500–11,000 WBC) – 1% Granulocytes – protect against harmful microorganisms during acute inflammation/infection Neutrophils – fight bacteria &amp; fungi, cell debris, foreign substances; phagocytic; first cell to arrive at a site; remain 6 hours in circulation Eosinophils – destroy invading foreign cells Diagram of left shift: more immature cells are present in the blood than normal</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 5 · Thrombocytes</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Thrombocytes – from large cells in the bone marrow · Activate coagulation factors in the blood plasma · Initiate stable clot formation – fibrin</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 6 · The liquid part of blood</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Plasma (clear yellow): complex mixture of water, proteins, nutrients, electrolytes, nitrogenous wastes, hormones, and gases · Extracellular fluid · 90% water; 6.5–8% proteins; 2% other molecular substances</p>\n<p>Figure: centrifuged blood tube = plasma (55%), buffy coat of WBCs and platelets (&lt; 1%), RBCs (45%). Plasma = water 90%, proteins 8% (albumin, globulin, fibrinogen), other solutes 2% (nutrients, electrolytes, waste products, hormones, gases)</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 7 · The blood filter &amp; RBC life cycle</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Spleen: filter · removal of abnormal WBC and platelets</p>\n<p>Figure (continued_12): RBC life cycle. Red marrow makes erythrocytes → RBCs circulate 120 days → old/damaged RBCs broken down in spleen → hemoglobin splits into heme and globin → globin becomes amino acids; heme becomes iron (recycled to marrow) and bilirubin (to liver → bile) → nutrients, iron, B12, and folic acid from the intestine return to the marrow</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 8 · Carrying oxygen</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Hemoglobin transports oxygen (respiration) · Sufficient &amp; balanced RBCs · Excessive increase in RBCs can raise blood viscosity, decreasing O₂ transport</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 9 · Hemostasis &amp; coagulation pathways</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Clotting cascade · Coagulation process: intrinsic pathway, extrinsic pathway</p>\n<p>Figure (continued_2): intrinsic pathway (blood vessel injury; XII → XI → IX + VIII → monitored by PTT) and extrinsic pathway (trauma; tissue factor III + VII → monitored by PT) join at factor X → common pathway: prothrombin (II) → thrombin → fibrinogen (I) → fibrin → XIII cross-links → stable fibrin clot</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 10 · Blood's defense role</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Inflammation and immunity: eliminate cause of injury · remove damaged tissue · generate new tissue</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 11 · Nutrition, PMH &amp; medications</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>History – Nutrition: Any recent changes in energy level? Difficulty maintaining daily activities, recreation, sports? Pain, burning, or tingling? Skin color or temperature changes? Swelling (edema)? Bleeding or easy bruising, dizziness, fatigue, or lymph node changes (swelling, pain/tenderness, warmth)? Past medical history: chronic disease – hepatitis, cirrhosis, cancer, clotting factor · Pregnancy – gestational thrombocytopenia Medication history: anticoagulants, aspirin, salicylates, herbs, nutritional supplements, nutraceuticals</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 12 · Head-to-toe hematology exam</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Skin, head and neck: color of skin and mucous membranes; skin temperature, capillary refill, edema; palpate skin, lymph nodes; inspect oral mucous membranes and skin; inspect tongue Respiratory: rate, rhythm, depth, sound; do the lungs work harder to increase O₂ delivery? Cardiovascular: vital signs – BP, HR, R, SaO₂ abnormal? Auscultation – murmurs, gallops, irregular rhythms Renal and urinary: urinary meatus for bleeding; urine specimen – hematuria? Musculoskeletal: abdominal pain – hyper/hypoproliferative disorders (sickle cell, anemia, leukemia); bone pain – leukemia (WBC) increases pressure in bone; sickle cell (RBC) – pain, tissue infarctions &amp; swelling; ROM – joint pain, swelling Abdominal: inspection – visible masses, abdominal distention, change in bowel &amp; bladder pattern; palpation – light palpation LUQ</p>\n<p>Figure (continued_9): nurse lightly palpating the left upper abdomen for the spleen</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 13 · The CBC</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Complete blood count: hemoglobin and hematocrit · red blood cells · white blood cells · platelets</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 14 · Reticulocytes, electrophoresis, LAP, Coombs</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Reticulocyte count (retic cells are larger than mature RBCs) – useful in evaluating anemia · Increase – response to hemolysis or blood loss for more RBCs · Decrease – hypoproliferative bone marrow (anemias)</p>\n<p>Hemoglobin electrophoresis: separates and measures normal and abnormal Hgb · helps diagnose thalassemias – absent or reduced production of Hgb alpha or beta Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase – elevated from infection, stress, inflammation, pregnancy, steroid use &amp; leukemia · enzyme made by normal mature neutrophils · venipuncture Coombs test: presence of immunoglobulins on RBCs – antibodies · used to diagnose hemolytic disease of the newborn</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 15 · Iron studies</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Iron (instruct patient to avoid taking iron supplements at least 24 h) · Serum ferritin – ability to store iron · Transferrin – iron-transporting capacity · Total iron-binding capacity – measures the amount of iron in the plasma – high TIBC = low O₂ saturation</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 16 · PT, INR, PTT, bleeding time, aggregation</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Prothrombin time – fibrin to clot · Bleeding time · International Normalized Ratio – oral anticoagulants · Partial thromboplastin time · Platelet aggregation – tests abnormal platelet function</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 17 · Bone marrow aspiration &amp; biopsy</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Examination of bone marrow (to diagnose thrombocytopenia, leukemias, granulomas, anemias, primary and metastatic tumors) · Bone marrow aspiration – removal of fluid · Bone marrow biopsy – specimen; soft tissue · Needle biopsy – removal of a core of marrow cells</p>\n<p>Figure (continued_8): patient lying on side; needle and syringe inserted through skin and cortical bone into spongy bone and marrow at the posterior iliac crest of the hip bone</p>\n</div></details></section>"},{"id":"heme-disorders","title":"Hematologic Disorders","part":"Part 4 · Ch 34","summary":"IDA, B12, folate, sickle cell, aplastic, G6PD, PV, thrombocytopenia (ITP, hemophilia, DIC, HIT), leukemia, lymphoma, multiple myeloma.","sections":[{"id":"heme-disorders--iron-deficiency-anemia-ida-rbc-disorder","title":"Iron deficiency anemia (IDA) · RBC disorder"},{"id":"heme-disorders--other-rbc-disorders-tell-them-apart","title":"Other RBC disorders: tell them apart ★"},{"id":"heme-disorders--polycythemia-vera-pv-too-many-rbcs","title":"Polycythemia vera (PV) · too many RBCs"},{"id":"heme-disorders--thrombocytopenia-and-bleeding-disorders-platelets-150-000","title":"Thrombocytopenia and bleeding disorders · platelets <150,000"},{"id":"heme-disorders--leukemia-wbc-malignancy","title":"Leukemia · WBC malignancy"},{"id":"heme-disorders--malignant-lymphomas-hodgkin-s-vs-non-hodgkin-s","title":"Malignant lymphomas · Hodgkin's vs non-Hodgkin's"},{"id":"heme-disorders--multiple-myeloma-plasma-cell-cancer-incurable","title":"Multiple myeloma · plasma cell cancer, incurable"},{"id":"heme-disorders--leukemia-vs-lymphoma-vs-myeloma","title":"Leukemia vs lymphoma vs myeloma ★"},{"id":"heme-disorders--case-mr-owens-62-aml","title":"Case: Mr. Owens, 62, AML"},{"id":"heme-disorders--class-notes-add-on-nur-291","title":"Class notes add-on (NUR 291)"},{"id":"heme-disorders--more-from-your-first-study-guide","title":"More from your first study guide"},{"id":"heme-disorders--chapter-34-powerpoint-word-for-word","title":"Chapter 34 PowerPoint, word for word"}],"html":"<section id=\"heme-disorders--hematologic-disorders\"><p>The four components are RBCs, WBCs, platelets, and plasma. Most disorders come from having too many or too few of one cell type. <strong>Anemia</strong> means reduced O₂-carrying capacity (fewer RBCs or less Hgb).</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"heme-disorders--iron-deficiency-anemia-ida-rbc-disorder\"><h2>Iron deficiency anemia (IDA) · RBC disorder<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#heme-disorders--iron-deficiency-anemia-ida-rbc-disorder\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><aside class=\"callout matters\"><div class=\"chead\">Why it matters / risk factors</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>The <strong>most prevalent nutritional deficiency in the world</strong>. Seen most in infants, children, pregnant people, and lower socioeconomic groups. More common in females. About 10% more anemia after age 65.</li>\n<li><strong>Inadequate dietary iron is the #1 cause.</strong> Needs rise during pregnancy and growth.</li>\n<li><strong>Hemorrhage and chronic blood loss:</strong> heavy menses, GI cancers (esophagus, colon, stomach), peptic ulcer disease.</li>\n<li><strong>Poor absorption:</strong> celiac, Crohn&#39;s, gastric bypass or gastrectomy, long-term PPIs, H2 blockers, antacids, aspirin, NSAIDs.</li>\n<li><strong>Socioeconomic:</strong> lead paint. <strong>Pica</strong> (craving ice, dirt, clay, paint) usually stops with iron replacement.</li>\n<li>IDA is often one of the earliest signs of another disease (such as GI cancer), so the cause must be worked up. Normal menses should not cause IDA.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout patho\"><div class=\"chead\">Patho</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Iron is needed to make hemoglobin. Without it, Hgb can&#39;t form, so not enough O₂ is carried. Iron is stored in the liver as <strong>ferritin</strong> and transported by <strong>transferrin</strong>. IDA means the iron stores are exhausted. It develops slowly in stages: negative iron balance → depletion → deficient RBC production → IDA. <strong>Anemia is a LATE sign</strong> of iron deficiency (except with hemorrhage).</p>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout ss\"><div class=\"chead\">Clinical manifestations ★</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>Hypoxia from low Hgb causes <strong>fatigue, pallor, SOB</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Tachycardia and tachypnea</strong> <em>→ why: the heart and lungs try to compensate for hypoxemia.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Glossitis:</strong> painful, swollen, smooth, shiny, bright red tongue from flattened papillae. Also cracks at the corners of the mouth.</li>\n<li><strong>Koilonychia:</strong> spoon-shaped nails after severe, prolonged deficiency.</li>\n<li>Changes in LOC, cognitive impairment (in children it can be permanent), pica.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinguishing features:</strong> glossitis plus spoon nails, microcytic and hypochromic cells, ↓ferritin with ↑TIBC. <strong>No neuro symptoms</strong> (which separates it from B12 deficiency).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<p><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-023-014.jpg\" alt=\"Glossitis (red, swollen, shiny tongue) in IDA\"><figcaption>Glossitis (red, swollen, shiny tongue) in IDA</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-023-015.jpg\" alt=\"Koilonychia (spoon-shaped, thin nails) in IDA\"><figcaption>Koilonychia (spoon-shaped, thin nails) in IDA</figcaption></figure></p>\n\n<aside class=\"callout dx\"><div class=\"chead\">Diagnosis</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>CBC:</strong> ↓Hgb and Hct, ↓MCV (microcytic), ↓MCHC (hypochromic). Often the first sign.</li>\n<li><strong>Iron panel (needed to confirm):</strong> ↓iron, ↓ferritin (stores), transferrin low or normal, <strong>transferrin saturation ALWAYS low, TIBC HIGH</strong> (empty binding spots).</li>\n<li>No single lab makes the diagnosis; it takes the whole picture. Iron and ferritin can fluctuate with inflammation.</li>\n<li><em>SLIDE vs BOOK:</em> slide says ferritin &quot;&lt;100 ug/dL indicates IDA.&quot; Book says &quot;serum ferritin &lt;10 mcg/L indicates IDA.&quot; Ask which number your instructor wants.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout tx\"><div class=\"chead\">Treatment</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Diet first.</strong> Heme iron: red meat, eggs. Non-heme iron: dark green leafy vegetables (spinach, broccoli, peas), beets, dried beans and legumes, nuts, fortified cereals and breads, Cream of Wheat.</li>\n<li><strong>Vitamin C</strong> (oranges, grapefruit, citrus juice) increases absorption.</li>\n<li><strong>Oral iron is first line.</strong> Take with citrus juice and <strong>NOT with milk or dairy</strong> <em>→ why: dairy binds iron and blocks absorption.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Parenteral iron</strong> is for severe GI distress, malabsorption, or when levels must rise fast. <strong>IM iron uses the Z-track method</strong> <em>→ why: prevents leakage and skin staining.</em> Old iron dextran was cardiotoxic. Newer IV forms are safer, but still monitor vital signs during the infusion.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout watch\"><div class=\"chead\">Side effects and complications</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Oral iron:</strong> nausea, abdominal discomfort, constipation and/or diarrhea. The patient should report these.</li>\n<li><strong>Complications:</strong> heart failure, renal failure, impaired thermoregulation and immunity, psychomotor and cognitive impairment in children. In pregnancy: low birth weight, preterm labor, maternal or infant death.</li>\n<li><strong>Iron overload</strong> (from pica or many transfusions, not usually oral pills): fatigue, palpitations, joint pain, GI upset, heart and artery damage.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout nursing\"><div class=\"chead\">Nursing</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Dx:</strong> inadequate tissue perfusion · fatigue · activity intolerance</li>\n<li><strong>Assess:</strong> vital signs (tachycardia and tachypnea are compensation), Hgb and ferritin, fatigue, pallor, SOB, LOC, blood loss, and the cause (diet, IBD, malabsorption).</li>\n<li><strong>Actions:</strong> ↑dietary iron and vitamin C, give iron supplements, minimize blood loss (control any hemorrhage; hormones for heavy menses).</li>\n<li><strong>Teach:</strong> iron food sources, take daily supplements as prescribed, report GI side effects, dangers of lead, prenatal iron.</li>\n<li><strong>Outcomes:</strong> normal Hgb and ferritin, better perfusion, HR and RR back to normal.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"heme-disorders--other-rbc-disorders-tell-them-apart\"><h2>Other RBC disorders: tell them apart ★<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#heme-disorders--other-rbc-disorders-tell-them-apart\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Disorder</th>\n<th>Patho / cause</th>\n<th>Signature manifestations (beyond fatigue, pallor, ↑HR, ↑RR)</th>\n<th>Key labs</th>\n<th>Treatment and watch-outs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Iron deficiency</strong></td>\n<td>Iron stores exhausted, so Hgb can&#39;t be made</td>\n<td>Glossitis, spoon nails, pica</td>\n<td>Microcytic, ↓ferritin, ↑TIBC</td>\n<td>Iron plus vitamin C. Avoid dairy with doses. Z-track for IM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Vitamin B₁₂ (pernicious)</strong></td>\n<td>B₁₂ needs <strong>intrinsic factor</strong> (from stomach parietal cells) to be absorbed in the <strong>ileum</strong>. Pernicious anemia = autoimmune loss of intrinsic factor. Risks: older adults (gastric atrophy), GI resection, PPIs and H2 blockers, alcohol, vegans and vegetarians (B₁₂ only in animal foods)</td>\n<td><strong>NEURO:</strong> symmetric paresthesias of hands and feet (first neuro sign), <strong>Lhermitte&#39;s sign</strong> (electric shock with neck flexion), confusion, depression, dementia, poor balance (especially in the dark), yellow-blue color blindness, tinnitus, glossitis. Pernicious anemia: vitiligo, early gray hair</td>\n<td><strong>Macrocytic (↑MCV).</strong> Serum B₁₂ (200–1,000 pg/mL). <strong>↑MMA</strong> (more sensitive) with normal homocysteine. Schilling test (rare)</td>\n<td>Diet (meat, seafood, eggs, dairy) or oral supplement. Severe deficiency needs <strong>weekly B₁₂ injections</strong>. ⚠ Neuro damage can become irreversible. ⚠ <strong>Folic acid can mask B₁₂ deficiency</strong> (fixes the anemia while the nerves keep degenerating). ⚠ Nitrous oxide can cause profound, possibly fatal postop anemia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Folic acid</strong></td>\n<td>Absorbed in the <strong>jejunum</strong>. Alcohol, malnutrition, gastric bypass or Whipple, meds (OCPs, metformin, antineoplastics) interfere</td>\n<td>Mood changes, bleeding risk (pancytopenia). <strong>NO lasting neuro deficits</strong>; confusion is reversible</td>\n<td>Macrocytic. <strong>↑Homocysteine with NORMAL MMA</strong> (the reverse of B₁₂). Fasting serum folate. RBC folate reflects the last 3 months</td>\n<td>Supplement. <strong>≥400 mcg daily in pregnancy prevents neural tube defects.</strong> ⚠ Folic acid speeds phenytoin metabolism and can cause seizures, so correct the deficiency before starting phenytoin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sickle cell disease</strong></td>\n<td>Genetic <strong>Hgb S</strong>. RBCs sickle with low O₂, becoming stiff and fragile, lodging in capillaries and forming thrombi. RBC life is 15–20 days. <strong>Triggers:</strong> hypoxemia, dehydration, cold, infection, high altitude</td>\n<td>Pain (joints, bones, chest, abdomen), swelling, jaundice, splenomegaly, fever, poor wound healing, hand-foot syndrome, <strong>priapism</strong>, infections (pneumococcus, H. influenzae). Symptoms start after 6 months of age (fetal Hgb protects until then)</td>\n<td><strong>Hgb electrophoresis (Hgb S).</strong> ↑bilirubin from hemolysis. ↓retics in aplastic crisis</td>\n<td><strong>Hydroxyurea</strong> (makes fetal Hgb; ⚠ myelosuppression, teratogenic, so counsel on contraception). Transfusions (⚠ iron overload, so give chelation such as desferrioxamine). <strong>Aggressive hydration</strong>, analgesia (often opioids at higher doses), O₂ only if critically low. <strong>Stem cell transplant is the only cure.</strong> Vaccines: pneumococcal and flu. ⚠ <strong>Crises:</strong> vaso-occlusive (most common), aplastic, sequestration (shock), hemolytic. ⚠ Acute chest syndrome, stroke</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Aplastic anemia</strong></td>\n<td>Marrow fails (infection, chemicals, myelotoxins), so <strong>ALL 3 cell lines drop (pancytopenia)</strong></td>\n<td>Anemia signs plus frequent infections (↓WBC) plus bruising, nosebleeds, petechiae, gum bleeding (↓platelets). Headache</td>\n<td>CBC shows pancytopenia with ↓retics. Bone marrow is mostly fat cells. ↑Hgb F</td>\n<td>Transfusions, growth factors (filgrastim, epoetin), immunosuppressants. Stem cell transplant for severe disease (keep transfusions to a minimum to avoid sensitization). ⚠ Mortality &gt;70% from hemorrhage and infection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>G6PD deficiency</strong></td>\n<td>Missing enzyme causes RBCs to hemolyze early</td>\n<td>Jaundice, dark urine (bilirubin), splenomegaly. Neonatal jaundice and kernicterus</td>\n<td>↑retics, <strong>Heinz bodies</strong> on the smear, DNA testing</td>\n<td>Avoid triggering foods and drugs. Transfuse for severe hemolysis. Folic acid. ⚠ Kidney damage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Polycythemia</strong></td>\n<td>See next section</td>\n<td>Pruritus (after a warm bath), facial flushing, headache, splenomegaly</td>\n<td>↑RBC, Hgb, Hct</td>\n<td>Phlebotomy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout pearl\"><div class=\"chead\">Size clue</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p><strong>Microcytic = iron deficiency (and lead). Macrocytic = B₁₂ or folate.</strong> To tell B₁₂ from folate, check MMA: <strong>↑MMA = B₁₂</strong>, while <strong>↑homocysteine with normal MMA = folate</strong>. <strong>Neuro symptoms mean B₁₂.</strong></p>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"heme-disorders--polycythemia-vera-pv-too-many-rbcs\"><h2>Polycythemia vera (PV) · too many RBCs<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#heme-disorders--polycythemia-vera-pv-too-many-rbcs\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><aside class=\"callout matters\"><div class=\"chead\">Why it matters</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>Rare: 5 per 1 million per year. More common in males, usually after age 60. <strong>JAK2 gene mutation in 90%</strong> (acquired after birth). Radiation exposure also raises risk.</li>\n<li><strong>Primary (PV):</strong> a bone marrow disorder that overproduces <strong>RBCs AND WBCs AND platelets</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary:</strong> a response to hypoxia (high altitude, smoking, COPD, heart failure) through ↑erythropoietin, or an &quot;inappropriate&quot; response from kidney disease or EPO-secreting tumors. <strong>WBCs and platelets stay NORMAL.</strong> Treat the cause (e.g., stop smoking).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout patho\"><div class=\"chead\">Patho</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Too many RBCs make the blood thick (<strong>hyperviscosity</strong>). Circulation slows and the heart labors, so even with more RBCs, tissue perfusion and O₂ delivery FALL (hypoxemia). The slow flow causes <strong>clots</strong>, and the abnormal platelets mean <strong>bleeding</strong> too.\n<strong>Lab definition:</strong> Hct &gt;48% (women) or &gt;52% (men). Hgb &gt;16.5 (women) or &gt;18.5 (men) g/dL. RBC &gt;6 million. WHO diagnosis: Hgb &gt;16.5 (men) or &gt;16.0 (women).</p>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout ss\"><div class=\"chead\">Clinical manifestations ★</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Slowly progressive, often found on routine labs. Caused by hyperviscosity, hypervolemia, and capillary engorgement.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>SOB, difficulty breathing lying flat</li>\n<li>Headache, dizziness, weakness, fatigue</li>\n<li>Blurred vision or blind spots</li>\n<li><strong>Splenomegaly</strong> (about 75%), with fullness in the LUQ, early satiety, and weight loss</li>\n<li><strong>Generalized pruritus, especially after a WARM bath</strong> (aquagenic)</li>\n<li><strong>Flushing of the face</strong> (ruddy &quot;plethora&quot;)</li>\n<li>Nosebleeds and bleeding gums. Angina and claudication.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout dx\"><div class=\"chead\">Diagnosis</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>CBC:</strong> RBC &gt;6 million, Hct can exceed 60%. ↑WBCs and platelets in PV (normal in secondary).</li>\n<li><strong>Bone marrow biopsy:</strong> panmyelosis (overproduction of all 3 lines).</li>\n<li><strong>Genetic testing: JAK2.</strong> <strong>Erythropoietin level:</strong> ↑ in secondary, <strong>low in PV</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Uric acid:</strong> ↑ from rapid cell turnover, which can cause gout and kidney stones.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout tx\"><div class=\"chead\">Treatment: reduce viscosity, prevent hemorrhage</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Therapeutic phlebotomy:</strong> remove 350–500 mL weekly until Hct &lt;45%, then as needed. Patients feel better right away as organ congestion drops.</li>\n<li><strong>Hydroxyurea or interferon</strong> (or radiation) suppress the marrow when WBCs and platelets are high. Phlebotomy does not lower those.</li>\n<li><strong>Low-dose aspirin or anagrelide</strong> reduce thrombosis.</li>\n<li>Medications for itching.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout watch\"><div class=\"chead\">Watch</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>THROMBOSIS (the main killer):</strong> MI, stroke, PE, DVT. Sudden chest pain, headache, or SOB needs immediate attention.</li>\n<li><strong>Hemorrhage (GI)</strong> from abnormal platelets.</li>\n<li><strong>Phlebotomy:</strong> going too fast causes hypovolemia (↓BP, ↑HR, nausea), so check vital signs. Repeated phlebotomy causes IDA, so monitor ferritin.</li>\n<li><strong>Late:</strong> myelofibrosis, transformation to AML, heart failure. Chemo and radiation carry a small risk of secondary cancer.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout nursing\"><div class=\"chead\">Nursing actions, and why</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Dx:</strong> ineffective tissue perfusion · activity intolerance · risk for ineffective coping</li>\n<li>Routine CBCs: keep <strong>Hct &lt;45% (men), &lt;42% (women)</strong> during phlebotomy, and monitor myelosuppression if on chemo.</li>\n<li><strong>Increase fluid intake</strong> <em>→ why: dilutes the thick blood.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Adequate rest</strong> <em>→ why: the heart is working hard.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Elevate legs when sitting</strong> <em>→ why: helps venous return and prevents clots from stasis.</em></li>\n<li>Modify CV risk factors: stop smoking, no crossed legs or restrictive clothing, DVT prophylaxis.</li>\n<li><strong>Teach:</strong> signs of MI, stroke, and PE and to seek help immediately; why aggressive daily hydration matters; small frequent meals (abdominal fullness); avoid tight clothing and extreme temperatures; tepid baths ≤30 minutes, mild soap, pat dry, lotion, colloidal oatmeal; bleeding precautions; regular moderate exercise; regular labs; genetic testing.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"heme-disorders--thrombocytopenia-and-bleeding-disorders-platelets-150-000\"><h2>Thrombocytopenia and bleeding disorders · platelets &lt;150,000<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#heme-disorders--thrombocytopenia-and-bleeding-disorders-platelets-150-000\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><aside class=\"callout matters\"><div class=\"chead\">Why it matters</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>A complication or syndrome, not a disease by itself. It comes from ↓platelet production, ↑platelet use, or ↑destruction. Low platelets are the <strong>most rapidly developing life-threatening cytopenia</strong> because of the bleeding risk.</li>\n<li><strong>Risk factors:</strong> malignancy, infection, meds (sulfa), autoimmune disease (SLE), DIC, African descent, obesity. ITP female:male is 2:1, peaking at ages 20–30. Gestational thrombocytopenia occurs in 6–15% of pregnancies (mild, resolves postpartum).</li>\n<li><strong>Acute ITP</strong> (children 2–6): rapid onset, resolves in weeks to 6 months. <strong>Chronic ITP</strong> (women 15–40): insidious and rarely remits.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout ss\"><div class=\"chead\">Clinical manifestations (all types) ★</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Easy bruising (ecchymosis) and PETECHIAE are the FIRST signs.</strong> Petechiae do not blanch.</li>\n<li>Nosebleeds, bleeding gums, GI bleeding (black tarry stools), hematuria.</li>\n<li><strong>Wet purpura</strong> (bruising of the buccal mucosa) is an early sign of worsening and usually needs treatment.</li>\n<li>Severe: bleeding into vital organs such as the brain can be fatal.</li>\n<li><strong>Thresholds:</strong> start bleeding precautions &lt;100,000, especially &lt;50,000. Spontaneous bleeding &lt;20,000, and platelets are usually transfused below 20,000 (or before procedures).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<h3>The 4 causes: tell them apart ★</h3>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Patho</th>\n<th>Diagnosis</th>\n<th>Treatment and ⚠</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>ITP</strong> (immune / idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura)</td>\n<td><strong>Antiplatelet antibodies (IgG)</strong> cause the spleen to destroy platelets in hours instead of their normal 7–10 days. In children it follows a viral infection or immunization (70% have a virus 1–3 weeks before). In adults it can be drug-induced or autoimmune</td>\n<td>CBC (&lt;150,000). Usually a clinical diagnosis after ruling out other causes. Bone marrow biopsy if needed. Platelets &gt;100,000: just observe</td>\n<td><strong>Corticosteroids</strong> (↓antibodies, ↓phagocytosis). <strong>IVIG</strong> (near-immediate rise). <strong>Rituximab</strong> (↓B cells). <strong>Splenectomy</strong> in extreme cases (the spleen is where platelets are destroyed)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hemophilia</strong></td>\n<td><strong>X-linked</strong>, so it shows up in males. A female carrier has a 50% chance of passing it to each son. <strong>A = factor VIII, B = factor IX</strong> (Christmas disease). Minor injuries bleed a lot. <strong>Hemarthrosis</strong> (bleeding into joints and muscles) causes deformity</td>\n<td>Factor VIII and IX levels</td>\n<td><strong>Replace the missing factor IV</strong> (recombinant, no human blood) as soon as possible after injury: up to 3 daily doses for minor injuries, 7–10 days for major trauma or surgery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>DIC</strong></td>\n<td>Secondary to severe trauma (crush, burns, sepsis, shock), OB complications (abruption, retained products), extensive surgery, cancers (leukemia, widespread mets), chemo. <strong>Platelets and clotting factors are used up forming clots everywhere, then the patient bleeds from every orifice.</strong> Microclots cause organ necrosis</td>\n<td><strong>↑PT, PTT, and thrombin time, ↓fibrinogen, ↑fibrin split products and D-dimer</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Treat the cause</strong> (e.g., antibiotics for sepsis). Give blood, platelets, FFP, and <strong>cryoprecipitate</strong> (rich in factor VIII). ICU care. ⚠ High mortality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HIT</strong> (heparin-induced thrombocytopenia)</td>\n<td>Immune system makes antibodies against heparin, usually <strong>5–14 days</strong> after it is started. Platelets are consumed making NEW CLOTS, so the patient usually <strong>clots rather than bleeds</strong>. Heparin flushes of central lines may contribute</td>\n<td>Falling platelets while on heparin, with a new or enlarging thrombus</td>\n<td><strong>STOP heparin first.</strong> Give a different anticoagulant (<strong>argatroban</strong>, lepirudin). ⚠ <strong>No warfarin</strong> (causes skin gangrene). ⚠ <strong>No platelet transfusion</strong> (feeds more clots)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout nursing\"><div class=\"chead\">Nursing actions, and why</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Dx:</strong> risk of injury (bleeding) · fear · ineffective regimen management</li>\n<li>Assess platelet count, ecchymosis, petechiae and purpura, frank bleeding. In pregnancy, check LFTs and platelets and watch for postpartum hemorrhage.</li>\n<li><strong>Minimize blood loss:</strong> direct pressure and/or ice for ≥5 minutes <em>→ why: vasoconstriction stops bleeding.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Avoid IM injections</strong> <em>→ why: bleeding into the muscle is hard to control.</em></li>\n<li><strong>No rectal temps, enemas, suppositories, or douches</strong> <em>→ why: they can tear thin mucosa.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Safe, clutter-free room</strong> <em>→ why: prevents bruising.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Minimal BP cuff inflation</strong>, only until the pulse disappears, and avoid automatic cuffs <em>→ why: overinflation bruises.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Minimize blood draws</strong> (consider a venous access device).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout nursing\"><div class=\"chead\">Box 34.1 Bleeding precautions ★</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>Soft-bristle toothbrush or gauze. <strong>No flossing.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Electric razor only.</strong></li>\n<li>Shoes or slippers when out of bed (no bare feet). Clutter-free environment.</li>\n<li><strong>Daily stool softener</strong> (no straining).</li>\n<li>No rectal thermometers, suppositories, enemas, or douches.</li>\n<li>No sexual intercourse when platelets are very low (slide: &lt;50,000).</li>\n<li><strong>Do not blow your nose.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Use a sharp knife</strong> (a dull one needs more force).</li>\n<li>For a cut: direct pressure and/or ice ≥5 minutes.</li>\n<li>Keep IM injections, arterial sticks, and blood draws to a minimum.</li>\n<li>Avoid dental cleanings while platelets are low (case study). Know the emergency signs to report.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"heme-disorders--leukemia-wbc-malignancy\"><h2>Leukemia · WBC malignancy<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#heme-disorders--leukemia-wbc-malignancy\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><aside class=\"callout matters\"><div class=\"chead\">Why it matters</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>A malignant bone marrow disease in which leukocytes multiply uncontrollably. <strong>Fatal if untreated.</strong> Named by stem cell line and speed of onset:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>AML and ALL</strong> have an acute, sudden onset. <strong>ALL is the most common childhood leukemia.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>CML and CLL</strong> can be present for years but eventually turn acute.</li>\n<li>Most common in White males over 60.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Risk factors:</strong> genetic anomalies (<strong>Philadelphia chromosome</strong>, a 9;22 swap, in &gt;90% of CML), <strong>Down syndrome</strong> (10–30× risk), <strong>radiation or benzene</strong> (the oil refinery in the case study), prior chemo or radiation, smoking.</p>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout patho\"><div class=\"chead\">Patho</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>An unknown stimulus mutates a myeloid or lymphoid stem cell. It clones itself into immature <strong>blasts</strong> that never mature and don&#39;t undergo apoptosis, producing <strong>leukocytosis</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Marrow, spleen, and lymph tissue become congested with blasts: lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, mucosal infiltration.</li>\n<li>The crowded marrow can&#39;t make enough RBCs and platelets, causing <strong>ANEMIA and THROMBOCYTOPENIA</strong>.</li>\n<li>Blasts can&#39;t fight infection, causing <strong>neutropenia</strong> and infections.</li>\n<li>Only cells that escape into the blood show up on a CBC, so early diagnosis is hard.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout ss\"><div class=\"chead\">Clinical manifestations ★</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>Swollen lymph glands (neck, axillae, groin) and a swollen, painful LUQ (spleen)</li>\n<li>Increased infections and frequent low-grade fevers (from cytokines)</li>\n<li><strong>Anemia:</strong> fatigue, pallor, weakness, SOB (worse on exertion or lying flat), dizziness</li>\n<li><strong>Bleeding:</strong> bruising, petechiae, nosebleeds, bleeding gums</li>\n<li><strong>Bone pain</strong> (marrow swelling in vertebrae, ribs, pelvis, long bones), gait changes</li>\n<li>Unintentional weight loss (high metabolic demand)</li>\n<li>It often presents as anemia or thrombocytopenia and may be found on a routine CBC in a patient with no symptoms. It can look like the flu.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout dx\"><div class=\"chead\">Diagnosis</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>CBC:</strong> leukocytosis plus anemia plus thrombocytopenia.</li>\n<li>History and physical.</li>\n<li><strong>Flow cytometry</strong> identifies the WBC markers and type. Can be run on blood if blasts are high; otherwise done on marrow.</li>\n<li><strong>Bone marrow aspirate and biopsy</strong> confirm the type and extent.</li>\n<li><strong>Genetic testing</strong> finds chromosome abnormalities that guide treatment.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout tx\"><div class=\"chead\">Treatment</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Chemotherapy</strong> (IV, intrathecal, oral, in combinations) in phases: <strong>induction</strong> (get remission) → <strong>consolidation</strong> (treat hidden disease) → <strong>maintenance</strong> (prevent relapse). <strong>Remission ≠ cure.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>CNS involvement</strong> is treated with radiation to the brain and spine <em>→ why: chemo can&#39;t cross the blood–brain barrier.</em></li>\n<li>Supportive transfusions of RBCs and platelets.</li>\n<li><strong>Bone marrow / stem cell transplant</strong> for refractory or relapsed disease. Donor cells come from a pelvic marrow harvest or peripheral pheresis after growth factor. ⚠ GVHD.</li>\n<li><strong>Immunotherapy:</strong> monoclonal antibodies, and <strong>CAR-T</strong> (the patient&#39;s T cells are engineered to kill leukemia cells; the effect is permanent, and patients may live without B cells).</li>\n<li><strong>G-CSF growth factors (filgrastim) are generally not indicated in leukemia</strong> <em>→ why: they may fuel the disease, since it comes from hematopoietic stem cells</em> (although the slide case did give filgrastim).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout watch\"><div class=\"chead\">Complications (mostly from treatment)</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>MYELOSUPPRESSION</strong> from chemo or radiation leads to neutropenia, which brings a high risk of life-threatening infection. <strong>Neutropenic precautions when ANC ≤500.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Fever in a patient on chemo means broad-spectrum antibiotics IMMEDIATELY.</strong> Fever is often the first and only sign of infection, and infection is the most common cause of death besides the disease itself.</li>\n<li><strong>Hemorrhage</strong> from thrombocytopenia. Report bruising, petechiae, nosebleeds, conjunctival hemorrhage, bleeding gums, hemoptysis, hematuria, or GI bleeding right away.</li>\n<li>Anemia needs frequent RBC transfusions.</li>\n<li>Long-term organ damage: renal failure, cardiomyopathy, pulmonary fibrosis, neurocognitive and liver damage. Sterility.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout nursing\"><div class=\"chead\">Nursing</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Dx:</strong> risk for infection · risk for impaired tissue perfusion · risk for bleeding · risk for compromised family coping</li>\n<li><strong>Assess:</strong> vital signs (fever), anemia signs, bruising and petechiae (non-blanching), CBC and WBC.</li>\n<li><strong>Actions:</strong> give chemo as prescribed. Neutropenic precautions. Prophylactic antibiotics, antivirals, and antifungals. <strong>IV antibiotics immediately if ANC &lt;500 with infection signs.</strong> Symptom management (antiemetics, antidiarrheals, mouth sores). Blood products.</li>\n<li><strong>Teach:</strong> neutropenic and bleeding precautions, signs of anemia and thrombocytopenia, the disease course, chemo and radiation reactions, <strong>sterility risk (bank sperm or eggs before therapy)</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Outcomes:</strong> remission, fewer complications, less anemia, infection, and bleeding.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout nursing\"><div class=\"chead\">Box 34.2 Neutropenic precautions ★</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>Frequent hand washing or alcohol sanitizer, for everyone.</li>\n<li>Avoid crowds. Wear a mask if going out. Avoid sick people, small children, and pets.</li>\n<li>Wash raw fruits and vegetables well. <strong>Avoid raspberries and blackberries</strong> (bumpy and hard to wash).</li>\n<li>Check temperature daily and <strong>call for &gt;100.4°F (38°C)</strong>. For fever, rigors, or illness, seek care immediately (IV antibiotics prevent sepsis).</li>\n<li><strong>No live plants or cut flowers</strong> (bacteria, mold), at home or in the hospital.</li>\n<li>No standing water (humidifiers).</li>\n<li>Take prophylactic antimicrobials daily and finish the whole course.</li>\n<li>Private room if hospitalized.</li>\n<li><strong>No rectal temps, suppositories, or enemas</strong> <em>→ why: rectal bacteria can enter the blood.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"heme-disorders--malignant-lymphomas-hodgkin-s-vs-non-hodgkin-s\"><h2>Malignant lymphomas · Hodgkin&#39;s vs non-Hodgkin&#39;s<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#heme-disorders--malignant-lymphomas-hodgkin-s-vs-non-hodgkin-s\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><aside class=\"callout matters\"><div class=\"chead\">Why it matters</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Hodgkin&#39;s lymphoma</strong> is about 14% of lymphomas. Defined by <strong>Reed–Sternberg cells</strong> (giant lymphocytes) and has a <strong>better survival rate</strong>. Peaks in young adulthood and again after 55. Risks: Epstein–Barr virus, a first-degree relative with it.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-Hodgkin&#39;s lymphoma</strong> has dozens of B- and T-cell subtypes and causes about 4% of cancers and 4% of cancer deaths (1 in 50 lifetime). More common in males. Risks: pesticides (farming), autoimmune disease, organ transplant (immunosuppressants), HIV, EBV, HHV-8, H. pylori, prior chemo or radiation.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout patho\"><div class=\"chead\">Patho and staging</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>A single progenitor cell in a lymph node becomes a solid tumor that evades apoptosis. It collects where lymphocytes gather (nodes, mediastinum), spreads through lymph tissue and the spleen, and then reaches other organs. Spread beyond nodes is called <strong>extranodal</strong>.\n<strong>Staging (WHO):</strong> I = one node · II = ≥2 nodes on the same side of the diaphragm · III = both sides · IV = organs plus nodes. &quot;E&quot; = extranodal, &quot;S&quot; = spleen, plus bulky disease.</p>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout ss\"><div class=\"chead\">Clinical manifestations ★</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>PAINLESS swelling of lymph nodes</strong> (cervical, axillary, inguinal, breast)</li>\n<li><strong>&quot;B symptoms&quot;:</strong> low-grade fevers with no clear cause, <strong>DRENCHING NIGHT SWEATS</strong>, unintentional weight loss. These signal a heavier disease burden.</li>\n<li>Fatigue, anemia, neutropenia, thrombocytopenia</li>\n<li><strong>Generalized pruritus with excoriation, common in Hodgkin&#39;s</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Dry, persistent cough that is worse lying flat</strong> from a mediastinal mass, which can compress the trachea (airway risk)</li>\n<li>SOB and fullness when the spleen or liver is involved, or the vena cava or spine is compressed</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout dx\"><div class=\"chead\">Diagnosis</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>History and physical. CBC (marrow involvement). <strong>Uric acid and LDH are ↑</strong> from high cell turnover. Viral testing rules out infection.</li>\n<li>Imaging: chest x-ray, CT, PET for extent and staging.</li>\n<li><strong>Lymph node biopsy is definitive.</strong></li>\n<li>Bone marrow biopsy if marrow involvement is suspected. Lumbar puncture if CNS involvement is suspected.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout tx\"><div class=\"chead\">Treatment</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Chemotherapy is the primary treatment</strong>, plus radiation and/or immunotherapy. Lymphoma is sensitive and responds quickly.</li>\n<li>Surgery to remove the tumor is not typically done. Splenectomy is now rare.</li>\n<li>Indolent (slow) lymphoma may get a wait-and-see approach.</li>\n<li>Stem cell transplant for relapse or poor response. Autologous if the marrow is not involved (&quot;rescue&quot; after high-dose therapy).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout watch\"><div class=\"chead\">Complications</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Superior vena cava syndrome ⚠ EMERGENCY:</strong> edema of the face and arms, SOB, chest pain, dysphagia.</li>\n<li><strong>Spinal cord compression ⚠ EMERGENCY:</strong> neuro deficits, paralysis.</li>\n<li><strong>Hypercalcemia (&gt;11 mg/dL) ⚠ EMERGENCY:</strong> from bone mets, causing dehydration, polyuria, confusion, heart block.</li>\n<li>Myelodysplasia, hepatic or renal failure, hyperviscosity, venous thrombosis (leg pain or swelling).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout nursing\"><div class=\"chead\">Nursing</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Dx:</strong> risk for infection · activity intolerance · risk for ineffective coping</li>\n<li><strong>Assess:</strong> vital signs and B symptoms · pain, breathing, face and neck (SVC) · neuro status (cord compression) · nodes · skin (excoriation from itching) · CBC · lower extremities (clot) · serum calcium · activity tolerance.</li>\n<li><strong>Actions:</strong> blood products, chemo, IV fluids <em>→ why: replace losses from fevers and sweats and dilute calcium</em>, ease itching and night sweats, routine CBCs, emotional support.</li>\n<li><strong>Teach:</strong> disease, infection risk, keep the treatment schedule, rest often, nutrition, report SOB, facial or arm swelling, chest pain, fever, or neuro changes, infertility and fertility preservation.</li>\n<li><strong>Outcomes:</strong> quality of life, no bleeding, no infection.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"heme-disorders--multiple-myeloma-plasma-cell-cancer-incurable\"><h2>Multiple myeloma · plasma cell cancer, incurable<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#heme-disorders--multiple-myeloma-plasma-cell-cancer-incurable\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><aside class=\"callout matters\"><div class=\"chead\">Why it matters</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>13% of hematologic cancers, about 5–7 per 100,000 per year. <strong>Incurable</strong> B-cell malignancy. Average age 62. 2× more common in people of African descent, and more common in males. Mean survival about 3 years (46.6% alive at 5 years).\n<strong>Risk factors:</strong> herbicides, insecticides, petroleum, heavy metals, plastics, radiation. <strong>Precursors:</strong> MGUS (monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance) and solitary plasmacytoma.</p>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout patho\"><div class=\"chead\">Patho</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>One clone of plasma cells (mature B cells that make antibodies) overgrows the marrow and pumps out monoclonal immunoglobulin (IgG or IgA), called the <strong>&quot;M protein&quot;</strong> (a monoclonal gammopathy).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>An osteoclast and osteoblast imbalance causes <strong>lytic bone lesions</strong>, which release calcium (hypercalcemia).</li>\n<li>Excess light chains (<strong>Bence Jones protein</strong> in urine) are directly toxic to the kidney.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout ss\"><div class=\"chead\">Clinical manifestations ★</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>BONE PAIN, especially the BACK (and hips)</strong>, from lytic destruction</li>\n<li>Fatigue, weakness, pallor (normocytic anemia)</li>\n<li>Recurrent infections</li>\n<li>Weight loss</li>\n<li>Paresthesia, numbness and tingling (nerve root compression)</li>\n<li>Hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, renal stones</li>\n<li><strong>6 cardinal features:</strong> lytic bone disease · M protein · anemia · renal insufficiency · hypercalcemia · infection tendency</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout dx\"><div class=\"chead\">Diagnosis: CRAB ★</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>C</strong> hyperCalcemia (&gt;11.5 mg/dL)</li>\n<li><strong>R</strong> Renal insufficiency (creatinine &gt;2 mg/dL)</li>\n<li><strong>A</strong> Anemia (Hgb &lt;10 g/dL)</li>\n<li><strong>B</strong> Bone disease (lytic lesions, osteopenia, pathologic fractures)</li>\n<li>Plasma cells ≥10% in marrow, M protein in serum or urine.</li>\n<li>Serum and urine protein electrophoresis, free light chains, Bence Jones protein in urine, β2-microglobulin (tumor burden), CBC, Ca, creatinine, uric acid.</li>\n<li>Bone marrow aspiration, skeletal survey, bone density. <strong>MRI if there is back pain</strong> (rule out cord compression).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout tx\"><div class=\"chead\">Treatment</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Bisphosphonates</strong> <em>→ why: reduce bone breakdown, so fewer fractures and lower calcium.</em></li>\n<li>Radiation</li>\n<li><strong>DVT prophylaxis</strong> (thrombus prophylaxis)</li>\n<li><strong>Dexamethasone</strong> (corticosteroid)</li>\n<li>High-dose chemo: <strong>thalidomide, lenalidomide, bortezomib.</strong> ⚠ Thalidomide and lenalidomide are <strong>teratogenic</strong>.</li>\n<li>Autologous stem cell transplant</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout watch\"><div class=\"chead\">Complications</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Pathologic fractures.</strong> Sudden severe pain, or pain in a new spot, especially the back, may mean a fracture or vertebral collapse.</li>\n<li>Spinal cord compression causing numbness, tingling, and motor changes.</li>\n<li>Hypercalcemia. Renal failure (Bence Jones protein, stones). Anemia, marrow failure, thrombocytopenia and bleeding, infection. Chemo side effects.</li>\n<li>Bisphosphonates and denosumab can cause <strong>osteonecrosis of the jaw</strong> with long-term use (Ch 13).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout nursing\"><div class=\"chead\">Nursing</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Dx:</strong> acute or chronic pain · impaired mobility · risk for infection · risk for injury (fractures) · impaired urinary output · fear</li>\n<li><strong>Assess:</strong> vital signs, fatigue, pain, paresthesias, I&amp;O, serum and urine calcium, BUN and creatinine <em>→ why: kidney damage from light chains and calcium.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Actions:</strong> chemo, bisphosphonates, pain meds. <strong>Encourage fluids</strong> <em>→ why: dilute calcium and protect the kidneys.</em> Position for comfort (eases nerve compression). <strong>Encourage ambulation</strong> <em>→ why: weight-bearing builds bone and prevents DVT and deconditioning.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Teach:</strong> report fatigue, SOB, dizziness, or confusion · sudden severe pain · changes in sensation or motor function · nonpharmacologic pain management.</li>\n<li><strong>Outcomes:</strong> pain controlled, optimal renal function, no infection.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"heme-disorders--leukemia-vs-lymphoma-vs-myeloma\"><h2>Leukemia vs lymphoma vs myeloma ★<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#heme-disorders--leukemia-vs-lymphoma-vs-myeloma\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Leukemia</th>\n<th>Lymphoma</th>\n<th>Multiple myeloma</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Cell / site</strong></td>\n<td>WBC stem cell in the bone marrow (blasts)</td>\n<td>Lymphocyte in a lymph node (solid tumor)</td>\n<td>Plasma cell (mature B) in the marrow, making M protein</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hallmark sign</strong></td>\n<td>Anemia, bleeding, and infection from a crowded marrow. Bone pain</td>\n<td>Painless nodes plus B symptoms (fever, drenching night sweats, weight loss), itching (Hodgkin&#39;s)</td>\n<td>Back and bone pain, CRAB, Bence Jones protein</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Key risk</strong></td>\n<td>Benzene and radiation, Down syndrome, prior chemo, smoking</td>\n<td>EBV (Hodgkin&#39;s), pesticides, HIV, transplant (NHL)</td>\n<td>Herbicides, petroleum, heavy metals, MGUS, African descent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Definitive dx</strong></td>\n<td>Flow cytometry, bone marrow biopsy</td>\n<td>Lymph node biopsy</td>\n<td>Marrow plasma cells ≥10%, M protein, CRAB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Main treatment</strong></td>\n<td>Chemo (induction → consolidation → maintenance), transplant, CAR-T</td>\n<td>Chemo ± radiation and immunotherapy (surgery not typical)</td>\n<td>Bisphosphonates, dexamethasone, chemo, autologous transplant, DVT prophylaxis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Big danger</strong></td>\n<td>Infection (neutropenia), hemorrhage</td>\n<td>SVC syndrome, cord compression, hypercalcemia</td>\n<td>Fractures, renal failure, hypercalcemia, cord compression</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></section>\n<section id=\"heme-disorders--case-mr-owens-62-aml\"><h2>Case: Mr. Owens, 62, AML<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#heme-disorders--case-mr-owens-62-aml\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><p>He has worked in an oil refinery since his teens (<strong>benzene, his highest risk factor</strong>) and smoked for 40+ years. Fatigue, SOB on stairs, bleeding gums. CBC: RBC 3.2, Hgb 9.4, Hct 28.1, WBC 6.2, platelets 97,000. A bone marrow biopsy confirms AML, treated with <strong>daunorubicin + cytarabine (Ara-C)</strong>. Two weeks later: Hgb 7.9, <strong>ANC 0.6</strong> (severe neutropenia), platelets 19,000. He gets PRBCs, platelets, filgrastim, and prophylactic antibiotics, antivirals, and antifungals. Remission follows.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Priority after chemo: INFECTION</strong> (ANC 0.6).</li>\n<li><strong>Go to the ED for:</strong> temperature 102°F, chills, a nosebleed lasting more than 10 minutes.</li>\n<li><strong>Teaching gap:</strong> &quot;I have an appointment to get my teeth cleaned.&quot;</li>\n<li><em>SLIDE vs BOOK:</em> platelets were 19,000 on the slide and 12,000 in the book.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"heme-disorders--class-notes-add-on-nur-291\"><h2>Class notes add-on (NUR 291)<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#heme-disorders--class-notes-add-on-nur-291\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><aside class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"chead\"><span class=\"tag\">Class note</span> Iron deficiency anemia</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>Most prevalent nutritional deficiency in the world; risks include poor diet, hemorrhage, chronic blood loss, GI problems, lead paint.</li>\n<li><strong>Ferritin less than 100 indicates IDA (slide).</strong></li>\n<li>Know the diet: red meat, green leafy vegetables, vitamin C, oranges, grapefruit.</li>\n<li><strong>Iron is taken with vitamin C on an empty stomach for best absorption.</strong> <em>(class only)</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"chead\"><span class=\"tag\">Class note</span> Polycythemia vera (all purple in your notes)</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Hyperviscosity:</strong> the blood is thick, increased production of RBCs.</li>\n<li>Risk for blood clot and stroke.</li>\n<li>Presents with SOB, <strong>orthopnea</strong> (difficulty breathing lying flat), swollen spleen / splenomegaly, headache, dizziness, blurred vision.</li>\n<li>Diagnosis: labs, bone marrow testing, genetic testing.</li>\n<li>Treatment from class: reduce hyperviscosity, prevent hemorrhage, <strong>aggressive IV fluid hydration</strong>. <em>(class only: IV)</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"chead\"><span class=\"tag\">Class note</span> Thrombocytopenia</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Number 1 side effect is bleeding.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Most common medication cause: Bactrim</strong> <em>(class only; the guide says &quot;sulfa,&quot; and Bactrim is a sulfa drug).</em></li>\n<li>Higher risk for women.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"chead\"><span class=\"tag\">Class note</span> Leukemia and lymphoma</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>Leukemia: teach neutropenic precautions (Box 34.2).</li>\n<li>Lymphoma manifestations: painless swelling of lymph nodes, low-grade fever.</li>\n<li>Lymphoma complication: superior vena cava syndrome.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"chead\"><span class=\"tag\">Class note</span> Multiple myeloma and pain</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Think calcium</strong> (hypercalcemia, CRAB).</li>\n<li>Medical management: bisphosphonates, e.g. <strong>Boniva (ibandronate)</strong> <em>(class only: Boniva by name).</em></li>\n<li>Nursing: <strong>encourage fluids</strong>, because the extra calcium can cause stones.</li>\n<li>Pain: know your pain medications. <strong>Know slide 22:</strong> why we use opioids vs nonopioids. Nonopioids (acetaminophen, NSAIDs) for mild pain and anti-inflammation; opioids for moderate to severe pain; in cancer, use the WHO ladder and dose around the clock.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"heme-disorders--more-from-your-first-study-guide\"><h2>More from your first study guide<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#heme-disorders--more-from-your-first-study-guide\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><h3>Iron deficiency anemia</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Iron-rich foods:</strong> heme iron: red meat, <strong>liver, eggs, clams, oysters</strong>. Nonheme: dark green leafy vegetables (spinach, broccoli, peas), beets, dried beans/legumes, lima beans, nuts, seeds, fortified cereals/breads, Cream of Wheat.</li>\n<li><strong>Boost absorption:</strong> vitamin C: oranges, grapefruits, citrus juice, <strong>peppers, strawberries, tomatoes</strong>. <strong>Avoid with iron: dairy (blocks absorption), antacids.</strong> Iron is taken with vitamin C <strong>on an empty stomach</strong> for best absorption (your notes). Not with milk.</li>\n<li>Oral iron side effects: nausea, abdominal pain, constipation, diarrhea, <strong>dark stools</strong>. IM iron = Z-track (stains skin). IV iron: monitor vital signs.</li>\n<li><strong>Diet question on the test:</strong> the menu that shows teaching worked is <strong>roast beef, steamed spinach, tomato soup, and orange juice</strong> (heme iron + nonheme iron + vitamin C).</li>\n<li>Greatest IDA risk: a <strong>15-year-old pregnant female</strong> (growth + pregnancy).</li>\n<li>Pregnancy complications: low birth weight, preterm labor.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Polycythemia vera: the chain</h3>\n<p><strong>Too many cells</strong> (blood hyperviscous) → <strong>slow flow</strong> (heart labors; less O₂ to tissues) → <strong>clots</strong> (stasis → thrombosis: stroke, MI, DVT, PE) → <strong>bleeding</strong> (platelets used up/abnormal → nose, gum, GI bleeds).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lecture interventions:</strong> aggressive IV hydration (oral fluids if tolerated) · phlebotomy and monitor Hct/Hgb · oxygen for SOB · reduce stress, provide rest, elevate head · avoid tight clothing, extreme temps · small frequent meals · modify CV risk (stroke prevention) · regular blood tests and genetic testing. <strong>Prioritize with ABCs:</strong> airway/breathing first, then hydration and oxygen based on assessment.</li>\n<li><strong>Assess:</strong> sudden severe headache = stroke. Watch vitals during phlebotomy. Notebook: high risk for stroke and DVT. Lecture: clots, aneurysm rupture, hemorrhage; engorged capillaries.</li>\n<li><strong>Hydrate!</strong> Rapid / aggressive IV hydration to dilute the blood (starred).</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary polycythemia</strong> = from hypoxia (smoking, COPD, altitude) → ↑EPO. <strong>Connection check: smoking raises the risk.</strong></li>\n<li>Slide epidemiology: 5 out of 1 million persons diagnosed annually.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Thrombocytopenia: extras</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Affects 3.8 million patients in the US. <strong>#1 side effect = bleeding. Most common medication cause = Bactrim (sulfa).</strong> Higher risk for women (F:M 2:1). Risk factors: malignancy (chemo), infection, medications (sulfa/Bactrim), autoimmune (SLE), DIC, African American descent, obese.</li>\n<li>Diagnosis: <strong>CBC with manual differential</strong> (low platelets), coagulation studies, bone marrow biopsy. Treatment: treat underlying cause · replace clotting factors · discontinue heparin (HIT) · transfuse platelets.</li>\n<li><strong>ITP:</strong> antibodies destroy platelets; kids after virus/immunization (acute); women 15–40 (chronic). Observe if &gt;100k; steroids, IVIG, splenectomy.</li>\n<li><strong>Platelet thresholds:</strong> &lt;100,000 start bleeding precautions · &lt;50,000 precautions especially important; avoid intercourse; minimize invasive procedures (lecture) · &lt;20,000 spontaneous bleeding; platelets usually transfused.</li>\n<li><strong>Bleeding precautions, split:</strong> <em>The nurse does:</em> avoid IM injections · no rectal temps, suppositories, enemas · minimal BP cuff inflation (no automatic cuffs) · minimize blood draws/sticks; pressure after every stick · safe, clutter-free environment · transfuse platelets/factors as ordered. <em>Teach the patient:</em> soft toothbrush, no flossing · electric razor · shoes when up · daily stool softener, no straining · don&#39;t blow nose · no intercourse if platelets &lt;50k · direct pressure ≥5 min for cuts · report bleeding signs · frequent labs needed. Gentle oral care and bowel care to avoid mucosal trauma.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Leukemia: types and medications ★ (most-tested topic: 5 Qs; notebook says 10)</h3>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Onset</th>\n<th>Key facts</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>AML</strong></td>\n<td>Acute</td>\n<td>Myeloid. Linked to radiation, <strong>benzene (oil refinery)</strong>, prior chemo, smoking</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ALL</strong></td>\n<td>Acute</td>\n<td>Lymphoid. <strong>Most common childhood leukemia</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CML</strong></td>\n<td>Chronic → acute</td>\n<td><strong>Philadelphia chromosome (BCR/ABL) in &gt;90%</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CLL</strong></td>\n<td>Chronic → acute</td>\n<td>Lymphoid; can be present for years</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p><strong>Process:</strong> 1 Mutation (myeloid or lymphoid stem cell) → 2 Clones (immature &quot;blasts&quot; never mature) → 3 No apoptosis (blasts don&#39;t die → leukocytosis) → 4 Crowding (marrow packed → ↓RBCs, ↓platelets, ↓working WBCs). <strong>The WBC count may be high, but the cells are immature and useless → infections + anemia + bleeding.</strong> Early on WBC may look normal. All types are fatal if untreated. Chronic can degenerate into an acute phase.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Treatment</th>\n<th>Key points</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Chemotherapy</strong> (primary)</td>\n<td>Phases: induction (get remission) → consolidation (kill hidden disease) → maintenance (prevent relapse). IV, intrathecal, oral. <strong>AML example: daunorubicin + cytarabine (Ara-C)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Targeted therapy</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Imatinib</strong> (Bcr-Abl → CML); <strong>ibrutinib</strong> (CLL)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Immunotherapy</strong></td>\n<td>Monoclonal antibodies; CAR-T (patient&#39;s T cells engineered to kill leukemia)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Prophylactic antimicrobials</strong></td>\n<td>Antibiotics (levofloxacin/moxifloxacin), antivirals (acyclovir), antifungals (fluconazole/Diflucan)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Growth factor</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Filgrastim (G-CSF)</strong> ↑ neutrophils; bone pain side effect. Textbook: not generally indicated in leukemia (could fuel it), but the case patient got it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Blood products</strong></td>\n<td>PRBCs, platelets (supportive care)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Tumor lysis prevention</strong></td>\n<td>IV fluids, allopurinol (prevent), rasburicase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Bone marrow / stem cell transplant</strong></td>\n<td>Refractory or relapsed disease. Risk: graft-versus-host disease</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p><strong>Remission</strong> = no detectable leukemia cells in marrow (not a cure). Lecture: remission possible, but recurrence risk when immune suppression occurs.\n<strong>Clinical manifestations explained:</strong> swollen lymph glands/LUQ pain = blasts congest nodes and spleen · increased infections, low-grade fever = few mature neutrophils · anemia = RBCs crowded out · bleeding = thrombocytopenia · weight loss, bone pain = high metabolism and swollen marrow ↑ pressure in bone. Diagnosis includes CBC, <strong>peripheral smear</strong>, H&amp;P, bone marrow biopsy, genetic testing, flow cytometry. Complications: infection (top cause of death outside disease progression), hemorrhage, myelosuppression.\n<strong>Neutropenic precautions (notebook ★):</strong> no humidifiers · no plants · <strong>reverse isolation (YOU wear PPE) to protect the patient.</strong> Staff/visitors wear PPE when required; restrict visitors with communicable diseases.\n<strong>Connection check:</strong> which result raises infection risk? <strong>B. ANC 400/mm³.</strong></p>\n<h3>Lymphoma: extras</h3>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Hodgkin</th>\n<th>Non-Hodgkin</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Hallmark</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Reed–Sternberg cells</strong></td>\n<td>Dozens of B- and T-cell subtypes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Frequency</strong></td>\n<td>14% of lymphomas; better survival</td>\n<td>4% of all cancers and cancer deaths</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ages</strong></td>\n<td>Childhood–young adult, then after 55</td>\n<td>Preadolescence, late adulthood</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Risk</strong></td>\n<td>Epstein–Barr virus; first-degree relative</td>\n<td>Pesticides, autoimmune, transplant immunosuppression, HIV, EBV, H. pylori</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p>Lymphoma complications: SVC syndrome (edema of face and arms, SOB, chest pain, dysphagia; lecture: tachycardia, stress) · spinal cord compression (neuro deficits, paralysis → immediate radiation/chemo) · hypercalcemia (Ca &gt;11) · <strong>myelodysplasia</strong> (anemia, neutropenia, thrombocytopenia) · hepatic/renal dysfunction, hyperviscosity, DVT. <strong>Exam focus: Hodgkin complications and symptoms.</strong></p>\n<h3>Multiple myeloma: extras ★ (exam: nursing interventions)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypercalcemia signs:</strong> tingling and burning sensation (your notes) · confusion, lethargy · muscle weakness · polyuria, dehydration · constipation, nausea · bradycardia/heart block · kidney stones.</li>\n<li>Risk: oil refinery (petroleum), average age 62, men, 2× in people of African descent. Also dark, infrequent urine.</li>\n<li>Bisphosphonates slow bone breakdown. Notes: <strong>&quot;Boniva&quot; = ibandronate</strong>; also pamidronate, zoledronate.</li>\n<li>Teach: report fatigue, SOB, dizziness, confusion · sudden severe pain (fracture) · new numbness/tingling (cord compression) · use nonpharm pain management (<strong>guided imagery</strong>) · <strong>energy conservation.</strong></li>\n<li>Lecture: more common in men; nursing = pain management (pharm + nonpharm), high fluid intake to prevent stones and clear calcium, positioning, safe ambulation and weight bearing, monitor Ca/BUN/creatinine.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Heme disorders at a glance (lecture summary)</h3>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Disorder</th>\n<th>Key tests / diagnosis</th>\n<th>Primary nursing actions</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Iron-deficiency anemia</strong></td>\n<td>CBC (↓Hgb, ↓MCV), ↓ferritin, ↑TIBC</td>\n<td>Iron + vitamin C diet, supplements, find bleeding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Polycythemia vera</strong></td>\n<td>CBC (↑RBC), bone marrow biopsy, genetic testing</td>\n<td>Hydration, phlebotomy, oxygen, elevate head, avoid tight clothing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Thrombocytopenia</strong></td>\n<td>CBC (↓platelets), coag studies, bone marrow biopsy</td>\n<td>Bleeding precautions, no IM injections, minimize draws, platelet transfusion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Leukemia</strong></td>\n<td>CBC, bone marrow biopsy, genetic testing</td>\n<td>Chemo, neutropenic precautions, infection monitoring</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lymphoma</strong></td>\n<td>Imaging, lymph node biopsy, bone marrow biopsy</td>\n<td>Symptom management, chemo/radiation, watch cord compression/SVC, emotional support</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Multiple myeloma</strong></td>\n<td>CBC, serum/urine proteins, bone marrow biopsy, skeletal survey</td>\n<td>Pain control, bisphosphonates, fluids, monitor Ca/BUN/creatinine</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p><strong>Lecture key terms:</strong> hyperviscosity = thick blood from ↑ cells · splenomegaly = enlarged spleen · myelosuppression = marrow suppression → cytopenias · neutropenic precautions = protect immunocompromised from infection · bleeding precautions = minimize bleeding with thrombocytopenia · hypercalcemia = high serum Ca from bone breakdown.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"heme-disorders--chapter-34-powerpoint-word-for-word\"><h2>Chapter 34 PowerPoint, word for word<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#heme-disorders--chapter-34-powerpoint-word-for-word\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><p>Your instructor&#39;s slides for this chapter, kept in slide order.</p>\n\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 1 · The 4 components &amp; normal values</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Hematology – 4 components: red blood cells · white blood cells · platelets · plasma</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 2 · Epidemiology to complications</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Epidemiology: most prevalent nutritional deficiency in the world Risk factors: inadequate iron in the diet · hemorrhage · chronic blood loss · GI problems · socioeconomic – lead paint Pathophysiology: iron deficiency · insufficient hemoglobin to carry adequate oxygen Clinical manifestations: hypoxia · fatigue · pallor · tachycardia · tachypnea</p>\n<p>Figure (continued_4): glossitis (red, swollen, smooth tongue) and koilonychia (spoon-shaped nails)</p>\n<p>Diagnosis: CBC – Hgb &amp; Hct · serum iron tests – ferritin (body&#39;s iron stores) &lt; 100 indicates IDA; TIBC; MCV Treatment: increased iron intake – red meat, green leafy vegetables, vitamin C, oranges &amp; grapefruits · iron supplements Complications: heart failure · renal failure · impaired thermoregulation · impaired immune function · psychomotor and cognitive impairment</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 3 · Assess, act, teach, evaluate</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Assessment and analysis: signs/symptoms related to decreased oxygenation Nursing diagnoses: inadequate tissue perfusion · fatigue · activity intolerance Assessments: vital signs · serum hemoglobin · fatigue · level of consciousness · blood loss Actions: increase dietary iron · increase intake of vitamin C · minimize blood loss Teaching: dietary sources of iron · signs/symptoms of bleeding · daily iron supplements · dangers of lead exposure · prenatal teaching Evaluating outcomes: adequate iron levels · adequate production of hemoglobin · improved tissue perfusion</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 4 · Too many cells, thick blood</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Epidemiology: 5 out of 1 million persons diagnosed annually Pathophysiology: increased production of RBCs · hyperviscosity – thick blood Clinical manifestations: shortness of breath · difficulty breathing when lying flat · headache · dizziness · weakness · splenomegaly · blurred vision Diagnosis: laboratory tests · bone marrow biopsy · genetic testing Treatment: reducing hyperviscosity · preventing hemorrhage Complications: thrombosis · hemorrhage</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 5 · Assess, act, teach, evaluate</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Assessment: signs and symptoms related to hyperviscosity, hypervolemia, engorgement of capillaries Nursing diagnoses: ineffective tissue perfusion · activity intolerance · risk for ineffective coping Assessments: shortness of breath · headache · dizziness · generalized pruritus · flushing of the face · fullness in left upper abdomen · fatigue · vision problems · weight loss · lab tests · bone marrow biopsy · vital signs Actions: obtain routine CBCs · increase fluid intake · ensure adequate rest · elevate lower extremities when sitting · modify cardiovascular risk factors Teaching: manifestations of severe thromboembolic events · rationale for aggressive hydration · small, frequent meals · avoid tight clothing · avoid extreme temperatures · bleeding precautions · routine moderate exercise · regular lab tests · genetic testing Outcomes: prevented or delayed complications · prevention of major thromboembolic events · prevention of hemorrhage</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 6 · ITP, hemophilia, DIC &amp; HIT</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Epidemiology: 3.8 million patients in the US · Acute – presents rapidly and resolves within a few weeks to 6 months · Chronic – more insidious onset and rarely results in remission Risk factors: malignancy · infection · medications – sulfa · autoimmune conditions – SLE · DIC · African American descent, obese · female-to-male ratio 2:1 Pathophysiology: idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura – following viral infection or immunization in children · hemophilia – deficiency of factor VIII or IX, genetic · heparin-induced thrombocytopenia – immune response to heparin Clinical manifestations: decreased platelets – easy bruising, petechiae, bleeding Diagnosis: CBC with manual differential · coagulation studies · bone marrow biopsy Treatment: resolution of underlying condition · replacement of clotting factors · discontinue heparin Complications: hemorrhage</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 7 · Bleeding precautions</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Assessment: signs of low platelets – ecchymosis, petechiae, nosebleeds, black tarry stools, bleeding gums, hematuria Nursing diagnoses: risk of injury · risk for ineffective therapeutic regimen · fear Assessments: platelet count · ecchymosis · petechiae/purpura · frank bleeding Actions: implement bleeding precautions · minimize blood loss · avoid IM injections · avoid rectal temperatures · provide safe environment · minimal BP cuff inflation · minimize blood draws Teaching: bleeding precautions · avoid sexual intercourse when platelets &lt; 50,000 · necessity of frequent lab tests Outcomes: prevention of hemorrhage · minimize risk of bleeding</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 8 · Uncontrolled immature white cells</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Epidemiology: acute or chronic · chronic may degenerate into acute phase Risk factors: genetic anomalies · Down syndrome Pathophysiology: unknown stimulus mutates myeloid or lymphoid stem cell · uncontrolled proliferation of leukemic cells (leukocytosis) · apoptosis doesn&#39;t occur · bone marrow, spleen, lymph congested with blasts Clinical manifestations: swollen lymph glands · increased infections · anemia · bleeding Diagnosis: lab tests · history and physical · bone marrow biopsy · genetic testing Treatment: chemotherapy · bone marrow transplant Complications: infection · hemorrhage · myelosuppression – result of chemo or radiation · bleeding</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 9 · Neutropenic precautions</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Assessment: disease may present as anemia or thrombocytopenia Nursing diagnoses: risk for infection · risk for impaired tissue perfusion · risk for bleeding · risk for compromised family coping Assessments: vital signs – low-grade fever response to infection · fatigue, pallor, dizziness, SOB · excessive bruising, petechiae · labs – CBC, WBC Actions: administer chemo as prescribed · institute neutropenic precautions · prophylactic antibiotics, antivirals, antifungals · symptom management Teaching: neutropenic precautions · manifestations of anemia · manifestations of thrombocytopenia · bleeding precautions · diagnosis of leukemia · adverse reactions of chemo/radiation · possibility of sterility Outcomes: remission · minimize/prevent complications · decreased manifestations of anemia, infection, bleeding</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 10 · Hodgkin's &amp; non-Hodgkin's</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Epidemiology: Hodgkin&#39;s – 14% of lymphomas · Non-Hodgkin&#39;s – 4% of total cancer incidence, 4% of cancer- related deaths Pathophysiology: single progenitor in lymph node develops into solid tumor · spreads to other lymph tissue and spleen · involves other major organs Clinical manifestations: painless swelling of lymph nodes · low-grade fevers · drenching night sweats · unexplained weight loss Diagnosis: H&amp;P · lab testing · radiological testing · lymph node biopsy · bone marrow biopsy · lumbar puncture Treatment: chemotherapy · radiation · surgical removal of tumor · stem cell transplant Surgical management: surgical removal of solid tumor · splenectomy Complications: SVC syndrome · spinal cord compression · hypercalcemia · myelodysplasia · hepatic or renal dysfunction · hyperviscosity · venous thrombotic events</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 11 · Assess, act, teach, evaluate</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Assessment: manifestations of systemic B lymphocyte involvement · painless swelling of cervical, axillary, inguinal nodes · low-grade fevers of unknown origin · drenching night sweats · fatigue, anemia, neutropenia, thrombocytopenia Nursing diagnoses: risk for infection · activity intolerance · risk for ineffective coping Assessments: vital signs · pain · neurological status · enlarged lymph nodes · skin · lab tests · lower extremities · activity tolerance · spinal cord &amp; nerve root compression Actions: administer blood products · medications · IV fluids · symptom management · obtain labs · emotional support · primary treatment – chemotherapy Teaching: disease process · risk of infection · maintaining treatment schedules · frequent rest periods · dietary intake · manifestations of complications · potential for sterility Outcomes: optimized quality of life · minimized adverse reactions · no bleeding · no infections</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 12 · Plasma cell cancer</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Epidemiology: 13% of hematologic cancers · 5–7 cases per 100,000 per year Risk factors: exposure to herbicides, insecticides, petroleum, heavy metals, plastics, radiation · comorbid plasma cell disease – MGUS, solitary plasmacytoma Pathophysiology: asymptomatic premalignant proliferation of monoclonal plasma cells – less than 10% of bone marrow is made up of abnormal plasma cells Clinical manifestations: fatigue · weakness · bone pain · recurrent infections · weight loss · paresthesia Diagnosis: lab tests · bone marrow aspiration · skeletal survey · bone-density measurement Treatment: bisphosphonates · radiation therapy · DVT prophylaxis · dexamethasone · autologous stem cell transplant · chemotherapy Complications: chemo side effects · anemia · bone marrow failure · bleeding · infection · pathologic fractures · spinal cord compression · hypercalcemia</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 13 · Assess, act, teach, evaluate</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Assessment: multisystem effects · bone pain – back · numbness and tingling · anemia · weakness, pallor, fatigue · increased susceptibility to infection Nursing diagnoses: acute/chronic pain · impaired physical mobility · risk for infection · risk for injury · impaired urinary output · fear Assessments: vital signs · fatigue · pain · paresthesias · intake and output · serum and urine calcium · serum BUN Actions: chemo as ordered · bisphosphonate therapy · pain meds · encourage fluid intake as tolerated · position for comfort · encourage ambulation as tolerated Teaching: report fatigue, SOB, dizziness, confusion · report sudden severe pain · report changes in sensation · nonpharmacological pain management Outcomes: pain is controlled · optimal renal function</p>\n</div></details></section>"},{"id":"immune","title":"Immune Function and Disorders","part":"Part 5 · Ch 18–22","summary":"Immune system, immunodeficiencies, hypersensitivity types I–V, anaphylaxis, transfusion reactions, latex, connective tissue disorders, HIV, bioterrorism.","sections":[{"id":"immune--ch-18-immune-function","title":"Ch 18 · Immune function"},{"id":"immune--ch-19-immune-deficiencies","title":"Ch 19 · Immune deficiencies"},{"id":"immune--hypersensitivity-reactions-overactive-immune-response","title":"Hypersensitivity reactions (overactive immune response) ★"},{"id":"immune--anaphylaxis-type-i-emergency","title":"Anaphylaxis (Type I) · EMERGENCY"},{"id":"immune--ch-20-connective-tissue-disorders","title":"Ch 20 · Connective tissue disorders"},{"id":"immune--ch-22-hiv-aids","title":"Ch 22 · HIV / AIDS"},{"id":"immune--bioterrorism","title":"Bioterrorism"},{"id":"immune--more-from-your-first-study-guide","title":"More from your first study guide"},{"id":"immune--bioterrorism-powerpoint-your-uploaded-slides","title":"Bioterrorism PowerPoint (your uploaded slides)"},{"id":"immune--chapter-18-powerpoint-word-for-word","title":"Chapter 18 PowerPoint, word for word"},{"id":"immune--chapter-19-powerpoint-word-for-word","title":"Chapter 19 PowerPoint, word for word"}],"html":"<section id=\"immune--immune-function-and-disorders\"><p>Immune disorders come from either too little immunity (deficiency, which leads to infections) or too much (hypersensitivity, anaphylaxis, autoimmunity).</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"immune--ch-18-immune-function\"><h2>Ch 18 · Immune function<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#immune--ch-18-immune-function\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><h3>3 lines of defense</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>1st, innate:</strong> physical, mechanical, and chemical barriers (skin, mucosa, coughing, tears, saliva). Nonspecific.</li>\n<li><strong>2nd, innate:</strong> inflammation. <strong>Histamine</strong> causes vasodilation and ↑capillary permeability, so WBCs move in (chemotaxis) and phagocytize. Immediate and nonspecific, with no memory.</li>\n<li><strong>3rd, adaptive:</strong> delayed but <strong>specific</strong>, and it has <strong>memory</strong>.<ul>\n<li><strong>Humoral (B cells):</strong> B cells become plasma cells that make antibodies against extracellular bacteria and viruses. Memory cells remember.</li>\n<li><strong>Cell-mediated (T cells):</strong> cell-to-cell contact against intracellular organisms (viruses, fungi), tumors, and transplants. Helper (<strong>CD4</strong>), cytotoxic (<strong>CD8</strong>), and regulatory T cells.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Active immunity</strong> comes from infection or a vaccine. <strong>Passive immunity</strong> is preformed antibodies (placenta, IVIG).</li>\n<li>Class: <strong>Agglutination</strong> traps antigens and makes them like a paste. <strong>Opsonization</strong> coats antigens (marks them for phagocytes).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Organs and immunoglobulins</h3>\n<p><strong>Central lymphoid organs</strong> = thymus and bone marrow. B cells mature in the marrow. T cells mature in the thymus, which shrinks with age. <strong>Peripheral organs:</strong> spleen, lymph nodes, tonsils, adenoids, Peyer&#39;s patches. After splenectomy, high-risk patients may need lifelong antibiotics.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Ig</th>\n<th>Remember it as</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>IgG</strong></td>\n<td>Most abundant. Crosses the placenta</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>IgM</strong></td>\n<td>Largest. First antibody made in a primary response</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>IgA</strong></td>\n<td>In secretions (breast milk, saliva, tears, mucus)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>IgE</strong></td>\n<td>Least concentrated. Drives allergy and fights parasites</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>IgD</strong></td>\n<td>On developing B cells (activation)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><h3>Assessment (Ch 18)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>History:</strong> infections (how often, how severe, response to treatment), immunizations, TB test, STIs, surgeries, meds (antibiotics, steroids, immunosuppressants, antineoplastics), nutrition (protein deficiency weakens immunity).</li>\n<li><strong>Risk factors:</strong> liver, renal, and pulmonary disease, malnutrition, radiation, splenectomy, surgery, trauma.</li>\n<li><strong>Lymph nodes:</strong> normally not easily felt. Enlarged, tender, or fixed nodes are a concern. <strong>Cancerous nodes are usually LESS tender than infected ones.</strong> Palpate for hepatosplenomegaly. Check joints for swelling and warmth.</li>\n<li><strong>Labs:</strong> CBC with differential, CRP and ESR, HIV test, cultures, skin tests, bone marrow biopsy, chest x-ray.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Aging (immunosenescence)</h3>\n<p>↓T-cell function and antibody response, plus ↑autoantibodies, lead to more infection (pneumonia and flu are top-10 causes of death after 65; shingles), more cancer, and more autoimmune disease. <strong>Infection may present as confusion.</strong> Malnutrition makes it worse.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"immune--ch-19-immune-deficiencies\"><h2>Ch 19 · Immune deficiencies<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#immune--ch-19-immune-deficiencies\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><p><strong>Primary</strong> = born with it (genetic or congenital), usually found in childhood. <strong>Secondary</strong> = acquired from an outside cause: HIV, chemo (leukopenia), corticosteroids, radiation (pancytopenia), malnutrition, burns, removal of the spleen or thymus. <strong>Secondary is the most common type in adults.</strong> Immune deficiency is the opposite of autoimmune disorder.</p>\n\n<aside class=\"callout watch\"><div class=\"chead\">Warning signs of primary immune deficiency</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>≥8 new infections a year · ≥2 serious sinus infections a year · ≥2 pneumonias a year · ≥2 months on antibiotics with little effect · an infant not gaining weight · recurrent deep abscesses · persistent thrush after age 1 · needing IV antibiotics · ≥2 deep-seated infections · family history.</p>\n</div></aside>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Disorder</th>\n<th>Patho</th>\n<th>Manifestations</th>\n<th>Treatment / ⚠</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>X-linked agammaglobulinemia (XLA)</strong> · B cell</td>\n<td>X-linked recessive, so it shows in males and females are carriers. <strong>BTK gene mutation</strong> means B cells can&#39;t mature, so no antibodies are made. About 1 in 250,000 male births</td>\n<td>Starts at <strong>6–12 months</strong> (when maternal antibodies wear off). Recurrent <strong>BACTERIAL</strong> infections: ears (most common before diagnosis), lungs, sinuses, skin, conjunctiva, meningitis, sepsis (S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae). Handles short viral infections, but chronic viruses are dangerous</td>\n<td><strong>IVIG</strong> every 3–4 weeks (or weekly subcut). Prophylactic antibiotics, courses twice as long as usual. ⚠ <strong>NO LIVE VACCINES</strong> (5–10% got vaccine-associated polio), so give inactivated polio to the patient and siblings. Older adults on IVIG risk renal failure, fluid overload, clots, hemolysis. Safe food handling and handwashing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CVID</strong> · B cell</td>\n<td>B cells normal in number but don&#39;t become plasma cells, so antibodies are low</td>\n<td>Like XLA but milder. Diagnosed in adulthood (class: <strong>age 15–35</strong>), both sexes. Autoimmune disease, GI symptoms, granulomas, cancers</td>\n<td>Same as XLA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Selective IgA deficiency</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Most common Ig deficiency.</strong> Low IgA with other Igs normal</td>\n<td>Often asymptomatic (class: age 14). Recurrent sinus and lung infections. 20–30% have autoimmune disease</td>\n<td>Treat infections</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>DiGeorge syndrome</strong> · T cell</td>\n<td><strong>Chromosome 22 deletion</strong> (&quot;CATCH-22&quot;), autosomal dominant. Pharyngeal pouches don&#39;t develop in weeks 6–10, so the thymus is small or absent (no T cells) and the parathyroid is affected</td>\n<td><strong>CATCH:</strong> <strong>C</strong>ardiac (tetralogy of Fallot), <strong>A</strong>bnormal face, <strong>T</strong>hymic aplasia, <strong>C</strong>left palate, <strong>H</strong>ypocalcemia (hypoparathyroidism, which causes tetany and twitching). Opportunistic infections: Candida, viral, fungal. Feeding problems, developmental delays</td>\n<td><strong>Calcium supplements</strong> (prevent tetany, seizures, bronchospasm). IVIG (lasts 3–4 weeks), prophylactic antibiotics, aggressive infection treatment. <strong>Thymus transplant</strong> (ideally by 3–6 months). Bone marrow transplant. ⚠ GVHD from transfused T cells. Monitor calcium and WBC. Class: mother needs genetic testing while pregnant; <strong>FISH testing</strong> detects the chromosome 22 deletion; <strong>puree food</strong> (feeding problems, cleft palate)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hyper-IgM</strong> · T cell</td>\n<td>X-linked. T cells can&#39;t signal B cells to switch, so only IgM is made (low IgG and IgA)</td>\n<td>Infections in the first 2 years. Pneumocystis jiroveci</td>\n<td>Autoimmune blood disorders</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SCID</strong> · both T and B</td>\n<td>No T- or B-cell function at all (the &quot;boy in the bubble&quot;)</td>\n<td>Potentially fatal infections</td>\n<td>Stem cell transplant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Wiskott–Aldrich</strong> · both</td>\n<td>X-linked recessive</td>\n<td>Infections, <strong>eczema, thrombocytopenia</strong> (small platelets)</td>\n<td>Stem cell transplant</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout nursing\"><div class=\"chead\">Nursing: any immunodeficiency</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Assess:</strong> fever, behavior change (irritable, lethargic), appetite, RR and work of breathing, SpO₂, lung sounds, ears, eyes, skin and wounds. Findings may be subtle and nonspecific.</li>\n<li><strong>Actions:</strong> protective isolation, limit visitors, assign a nurse who is not caring for infectious patients, gamma globulin, prophylactic antibiotics, treat infection promptly.</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary (therapy-induced):</strong> consider changing or stopping the immunosuppressant if the infection risk outweighs the benefit. Signs of infection: fever, hypotension and tachycardia, cloudy or foul urine, CVA tenderness, rash.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout nursing\"><div class=\"chead\">Teaching: protect from infection</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>Hand washing. Avoid crowds and anyone who is ill.</li>\n<li><strong>Low-bacteria diet:</strong> no salads, raw fruit or vegetables, undercooked meat, fish, or eggs, or sushi. Safe food handling. Avoid untested well water.</li>\n<li><strong>No changing cat litter</strong> (toxoplasmosis). <strong>No turtles or reptiles</strong> (Salmonella).</li>\n<li>Report temperature &gt;100°F (37.8°C) (Ch 19 secondary) or ≥100.5°F (38°C) (XLA), cough or URI symptoms, cloudy urine, or a wound that is slow to heal or warm.</li>\n<li>Finish the full course of antibiotics or antivirals.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"immune--hypersensitivity-reactions-overactive-immune-response\"><h2>Hypersensitivity reactions (overactive immune response) ★<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#immune--hypersensitivity-reactions-overactive-immune-response\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Mechanism</th>\n<th>Antibody / cell</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>I · Immediate (allergic)</strong></td>\n<td>IgE on mast cells binds the allergen and the mast cell degranulates, releasing histamine, leukotrienes, and prostaglandins</td>\n<td>IgE, mast cells</td>\n<td><strong>Allergic rhinitis (most common)</strong>, asthma, <strong>anaphylaxis (most severe)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>II · Cytotoxic</strong></td>\n<td>IgG or IgM attack antigens on the cell surface, causing complement lysis</td>\n<td>IgG, IgM</td>\n<td><strong>Transfusion reaction</strong>, Rh disease of the newborn (erythroblastosis fetalis), <strong>Goodpasture&#39;s</strong>, autoimmune ITP, myasthenia gravis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>III · Immune complex</strong></td>\n<td>Antigen–antibody complexes lodge in vessel walls, joints, and kidneys, triggering complement and inflammation</td>\n<td>IgG, IgM, neutrophils</td>\n<td><strong>SLE, RA</strong>, serum sickness (7–12 days after exposure), Arthus reaction (local, at an injection site)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>IV · Delayed (cell-mediated)</strong></td>\n<td>Sensitized T cells release cytokines. Takes <strong>24–72 hours. No antibody</strong></td>\n<td>T cells, macrophages</td>\n<td><strong>Poison ivy</strong> contact dermatitis, <strong>positive TB skin test (Mantoux)</strong>, latex allergy (can also be type I)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>V · Stimulated</strong></td>\n<td>Autoantibody keeps a receptor switched ON</td>\n<td>Autoantibodies</td>\n<td><strong>Graves&#39; disease</strong> (TSH receptor leads to hyperthyroidism)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-044-016.jpg\" alt=\"Slide 49: type I hypersensitivity. First exposure: B cell → plasma cell releases IgE → IgE sits on mast cells. Second exposure: allergen binds → mast cell releases histamine → allergic reaction\"><figcaption>Slide 49: type I hypersensitivity. First exposure: B cell → plasma cell releases IgE → IgE sits on mast cells. Second exposure: allergen binds → mast cell releases histamine → allergic reaction</figcaption></figure></p>\n<p><strong>Type I patho (slide 49, learn it):</strong> first exposure: B cell → plasma cell releases IgE → IgE sits on mast cells. Second exposure: allergen binds → mast cell releases histamine → allergic reaction. <strong>IgE fights allergies.</strong></p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"immune--anaphylaxis-type-i-emergency\"><h2>Anaphylaxis (Type I) · EMERGENCY<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#immune--anaphylaxis-type-i-emergency\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><aside class=\"callout matters\"><div class=\"chead\">Why it matters</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>Lifetime prevalence 1–2%, and up to 1,500 people die of it each year. <strong>Penicillin causes about 400 deaths a year.</strong> Other triggers: foods (peanuts, tree nuts, seafood), insect stings, contrast media, latex, blood. 20% of people are atopic. If both parents have allergies, a child has an 80% chance of allergies.</li>\n<li><strong>Patho:</strong> the first exposure makes IgE, which attaches to mast cells. The second exposure causes degranulation. <strong>Histamine</strong> causes vasodilation, ↑permeability, bronchoconstriction, and edema. <strong>Leukotrienes</strong> cause bronchoconstriction (wheezing).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout ss\"><div class=\"chead\">Clinical manifestations ★</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Skin first:</strong> urticaria (hives), itching, rash, flushing or heat. Angioedema of the lips, mouth, and eyes. Skin signs occur in 90%.</li>\n<li>Dyspnea, wheezing, hoarseness, <strong>STRIDOR</strong> (airway narrowing), crackles</li>\n<li>N/V/D, abdominal cramps</li>\n<li>Anxiety, a <strong>sense of impending doom</strong>, dizziness</li>\n<li><strong>Anaphylactic shock: HYPOTENSION + TACHYCARDIA</strong> from vasodilation and capillary leak (distributive shock). Circulatory failure means it is progressing.</li>\n<li><strong>Local (allergic rhinitis):</strong> sneezing, nasal discharge, itchy watery eyes, ↑eosinophils. <strong>Diagnosis by skin scratch test: hold steroids, antihistamines (and NSAIDs per class) for 5 days beforehand.</strong> Results appear in 15–20 minutes.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout watch\"><div class=\"chead\">Priority actions, in order</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ol>\n<li><strong>STOP the offending agent.</strong> If it&#39;s an IV drug: stop the infusion, <strong>change the IV tubing</strong>, and hang normal saline.</li>\n<li><strong>IM EPINEPHRINE STAT</strong>, the first-line drug. It dilates the bronchioles, constricts blood vessels (↑BP), and strengthens cardiac contraction. Repeat every 5 minutes, and go to IV if IM isn&#39;t working. <strong>Glucagon</strong> if the patient is on beta blockers and not responding to epinephrine.</li>\n<li><strong>100% O₂ by non-rebreather.</strong> Raise the head of the bed, unless BP is low.</li>\n<li><strong>Second line, only AFTER epinephrine:</strong> diphenhydramine (H1), famotidine (H2), corticosteroids, albuterol, vasopressors, IV fluid bolus.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Have intubation or tracheostomy equipment ready. Monitor continuously and stay with the patient. Watch for a <strong>rebound reaction</strong> as the drugs wear off.</li>\n<li>Diphenhydramine is sedating (first generation). Second-generation antihistamines (cetirizine, loratadine, fexofenadine) are for daily use. Steroids: watch for infection risk, ↑glucose, ↑appetite, irritability, impaired skin integrity.</li>\n<li><strong>Priority assessment: respiratory status.</strong> Nursing diagnosis: risk for ineffective gas exchange.</li>\n<li><strong>DO NOT COMPROMISE BP</strong> (class): semi-Fowler&#39;s if having difficulty breathing; legs up if no breathing issues; do not move people with a hip fracture.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout nursing\"><div class=\"chead\">Teaching</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>Avoid triggers. Know the early signs (rash, itching).</li>\n<li><strong>EpiPen use is the teaching priority after a bee-sting anaphylaxis.</strong></li>\n<li>Medic Alert bracelet. Keep the allergy list updated.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<h3>Transfusion reaction (Type II) ★</h3>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Has</th>\n<th>Can&#39;t receive</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>A antigen, anti-B</td>\n<td>B, AB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>B antigen, anti-A</td>\n<td>A, AB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AB</td>\n<td>Both antigens, no antibodies</td>\n<td><strong>Universal recipient</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>O</td>\n<td>No antigens, anti-A and anti-B</td>\n<td>A, B, AB (<strong>universal donor</strong>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p>Incompatible blood causes chills, fever, pain and heat at the site, rash. Agglutination leads to hemoglobinuria, acute renal failure, and then DIC-like bleeding. <strong>Treatment: stop the transfusion.</strong> Plasmapheresis for type II reactions. <strong>Saline is the only compatible solution with blood</strong> (class).</p>\n<h3>Latex allergy (Type IV, can be Type I)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>About 2% of people. <strong>High risk:</strong> healthcare workers, multiple surgeries (spina bifida), atopic or hand dermatitis, females.</li>\n<li>Ranges from contact dermatitis on the hands, rhinitis, and conjunctivitis to pharyngeal edema and anaphylaxis (worse with repeat exposure).</li>\n<li>⚠ <strong>Cross-reacting foods:</strong> avocado, banana, kiwi, chestnut, mango, papaya, passion fruit, tomato, raw potato, peach.</li>\n<li><strong>Prevention is the best treatment:</strong> vinyl or neoprene gloves, cloth under the BP cuff, keep stethoscope tubing off the skin, don&#39;t inject through latex ports.</li>\n<li><strong>Latex items:</strong> tourniquets, airways, ET tubes, electrode pads, rubber bands, rubber balls, baby bottle nipples. Medic Alert bracelet. Carry an EpiPen.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<aside class=\"callout pearl\"><div class=\"chead\">Other type II and III disorders</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Goodpasture&#39;s (type II):</strong> antibodies attack the glomerular and alveolar basement membranes, causing glomerulonephritis plus lung hemorrhage (attacks lungs and kidneys). Treated with steroids, immunosuppressants, and plasmapheresis (± IVIG, dialysis).</li>\n<li><strong>Serum sickness (type III):</strong> fever, arthralgia, lymphadenopathy, urticaria, nephritis 7–12 days after exposure (1–4 days on re-exposure). Treated by removing the agent, aspirin, antihistamines, ± steroids or epinephrine.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"immune--ch-20-connective-tissue-disorders\"><h2>Ch 20 · Connective tissue disorders<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#immune--ch-20-connective-tissue-disorders\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><p>Connective tissue binds the body together (bones, cartilage, tendons, ligaments, blood, lymph). Your notes said to concentrate on <strong>Chapters 19 and 20</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Osteoarthritis (OA) · degenerative, &quot;wear and tear&quot;</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Most common form of arthritis (DJD);</strong> leading cause of chronic disability.</li>\n<li>Hits the <strong>weight-bearing joints:</strong> knees, hips, feet, cervical and lumbar spine, DIP and PIP joints of the hands. Knees bear most of the body&#39;s weight.</li>\n<li><strong>Patho:</strong> articular cartilage and synovial fluid break down → bone is unprotected → <strong>osteophytes (bone spurs)</strong> → narrowed joint space, pain, ↓ROM.</li>\n<li><strong>Risk:</strong> female, over 55, obesity, prior injury, repetitive-motion jobs and sports.</li>\n<li><strong>Manifestations:</strong> pain that <strong>worsens with activity and improves with rest</strong>. <strong>Morning stiffness lasting less than 30 minutes.</strong> ↓ROM, bony swelling, deformity, crepitus.</li>\n<li><strong>Heberden&#39;s vs Bouchard&#39;s nodes:</strong> &quot;B (more proximal) before H.&quot; <strong>Bouchard&#39;s = PIP</strong> (middle knuckle); <strong>Heberden&#39;s = DIP</strong> (closest to the fingertip).</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnosis:</strong> history (45+, pain, stiffness under 30 min); x-ray shows joint-space narrowing and spurs.</li>\n<li><strong>Meds:</strong> acetaminophen (mild to moderate), NSAIDs, intra-articular steroid injections, opioids with caution. <strong>3 g of Tylenol max a day</strong> → check LFTs and kidney function (NSAIDs stress the kidneys).</li>\n<li><strong>Heat 15 min on and off; cold 20 min on and off. DO NOT put directly on the skin.</strong></li>\n<li>Weight loss, exercise, PT/OT, assistive devices. Surgery: debridement, synovectomy, fusion, total joint replacement. <strong>90° hip angle after surgery</strong> (don&#39;t flex the new hip past 90°).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-046-017.jpg\" alt=\"OA of the hand: Bouchard&amp;#39;s node at the PIP, Heberden&amp;#39;s node at the DIP\"><figcaption>OA of the hand: Bouchard&amp;#39;s node at the PIP, Heberden&amp;#39;s node at the DIP</figcaption></figure></p>\n<h3>Rheumatoid arthritis (RA) · autoimmune, systemic</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Chronic, systemic, autoimmune inflammatory disease. 1% of people; women &gt; men; ages 30–50s. Risks: genetics, smoking.</li>\n<li><strong>Joint pain is symmetrical; hands are mainly affected.</strong> Synovitis, swelling, erythema, fatigue, <strong>morning stiffness over 30 minutes</strong>, irreversible deformity (swan-neck, boutonnière, ulnar deviation).</li>\n<li><strong>Dx:</strong> <strong>RF and anti-CCP2</strong> tests; CRP and ESR show inflammation; renal, hepatic, CBC. <strong>Check PPD</strong> (TB test before immunosuppressants).</li>\n<li>Proper nutrition is controversial; class: avoid night(shade) plants d/t water content.</li>\n<li><strong>METHOTREXATE and DMARDs:</strong> methotrexate is the <strong>first-line DMARD</strong> (others: leflunomide, hydroxychloroquine, sulfasalazine, Xeljanz). Also NSAIDs and glucocorticoids.</li>\n<li>Immunocompromised d/t steroids → report infection. Stay up to date with vaccines. Rest periods, ROM, aerobic exercise, PT/OT. Surgery: joint replacement, fusion.</li>\n<li><strong>Methotrexate teaching (case study):</strong> weekly dose, no alcohol, <strong>two forms of birth control (teratogenic)</strong>, watch liver enzymes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-047-018.jpg\" alt=\"RA deformities: swan-neck, boutonnière, ulnar deviation\"><figcaption>RA deformities: swan-neck, boutonnière, ulnar deviation</figcaption></figure></p>\n<h3>Lupus (SLE) · autoimmune, flares and remission</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Autoimmune, chronic inflammatory disease: <strong>immune complexes deposit in tissues (type III)</strong>. Most common in <strong>African American women of childbearing age</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Triggers:</strong> pregnancy, sunlight, illness, surgery, silica dust, drug allergies.</li>\n<li>No set pattern; flares and remissions. Fatigue, joint pain, rash, photosensitivity, oral and nasal ulcers, alopecia. <strong>Dx: ANA (anti-dsDNA, anti-Sm).</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Treatment:</strong> hydroxychloroquine (antimalarial), NSAIDs, glucocorticoids, belimumab. <strong>Sunscreen daily (SPF 50+)</strong>, rest, energy conservation. <strong>No live vaccines</strong>; keep other immunizations current. <strong>Avoid oral contraceptives</strong> (clot risk).</li>\n<li><strong>Complications:</strong> renal failure, heart and lung disease, clots, stroke, infection.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Quick reference (Ch 20 slides, not in your notes)</h3>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Disorder</th>\n<th>Key patho</th>\n<th>Signature signs</th>\n<th>Treatment / teaching</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Scleroderma</strong></td>\n<td>Excess collagen → hardened, fibrotic skin and organs</td>\n<td>Thick skin, Raynaud&#39;s, renal crisis (sudden HTN), pulmonary HTN; lung disease is the leading cause of death</td>\n<td>Steroids, immunosuppressants, vasodilators, skin care, ROM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Gout</strong></td>\n<td>Uric acid (urate) crystals in joints</td>\n<td>Sudden red, hot, swollen single joint (great toe); tophi in chronic gout; kidney stones</td>\n<td>Colchicine, indomethacin, steroids; allopurinol for chronic; avoid alcohol (beer), high-purine foods, aspirin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Fibromyalgia</strong></td>\n<td>CNS amplifies normal pain signals</td>\n<td>Widespread pain, 11+ of 18 tender points, non-restorative sleep, fatigue, &quot;fibro fog&quot;</td>\n<td>Symptom management; labs only rule out other causes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></section>\n<section id=\"immune--ch-22-hiv-aids\"><h2>Ch 22 · HIV / AIDS<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#immune--ch-22-hiv-aids\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><p>A <strong>retrovirus</strong> that carries genetic RNA and targets <strong>CD4 T cells</strong>. PLWHA = person living with HIV/AIDS.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Transmission:</strong> through fluids: blood and blood products, semen, vaginal secretions, mother to child (amniotic fluid, breast milk). <strong>Not casual contact.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Risk factors:</strong> unprotected sex, IV drug use.</li>\n<li><strong>Prevention:</strong> testing, PrEP, condoms every time, male circumcision, harm reduction (don&#39;t share needles).</li>\n<li><strong>Healthcare worker exposure:</strong> standard precautions; <strong>post-exposure prophylaxis within 72 hours</strong> (2–3 drugs for 28 days).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Stages of HIV ★</h3>\n<p><strong>Acute infection</strong> (1–2 weeks) → <strong>Seroconversion</strong> (antibodies appear) → <strong>Asymptomatic</strong> (3–10 years) → <strong>Symptomatic (AIDS)</strong> → <strong>Death</strong> (about 2 years).</p>\n<p><strong>CDC staging by CD4:</strong> Stage 1 = 500+ · Stage 2 = 200–499 · <strong>Stage 3 = less than 200 is AIDS.</strong></p>\n<h3>Labs: what you want</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Targets CD4: want <strong>CD4 HIGH, viral load DOWN.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>CD4 less than 200 = AIDS.</strong> Prophylaxis for opportunistic infections starts at a CD4 of 200.</li>\n<li>Diagnostics: HIV antibodies, CD4+ count, viral load.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-048-019.jpg\" alt=\"Slide 21: opportunistic infections by CD4 count\"><figcaption>Slide 21: opportunistic infections by CD4 count</figcaption></figure></p>\n<h3>Opportunistic infections by CD4 count (slide 21)</h3>\n<p>Herpes zoster and TB as CD4 starts to fall · oral candidiasis near 300 · <strong>PCP at 200</strong> · toxoplasmosis, cryptococcosis, MAC, CMV near 100 · cryptosporidiosis, PML below 50.</p>\n<h3>Treatment and nursing</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Antiretroviral therapy (ART)</strong>, Table 22.3 classes. &quot;<strong>-vir</strong>&quot; = antiviral. <strong>Give ART on time; adherence is everything.</strong> Tie doses to a daily routine. Immunizations. With adherence, people with HIV can live a normal life span.</li>\n<li><strong>Dx:</strong> risk for infection · risk for fluid volume deficit · anxiety · knowledge deficit.</li>\n<li><strong>Assess:</strong> vital signs and SpO₂, weight trends, CD4, viral load, ART adherence, TB status.</li>\n<li><strong>Actions:</strong> universal/standard precautions, ART on time, nutrient-dense small frequent meals, emotional support.</li>\n<li><strong>Teach:</strong> avoid high-risk behaviors, condoms with every encounter, infection control at home, adherence, what to report.</li>\n<li><strong>Complications:</strong> PCP, Kaposi sarcoma, candida esophagitis, toxoplasmosis, CMV, lymphoma, herpes zoster, IRIS, encephalopathy.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<aside class=\"callout watch\"><div class=\"chead\">Report right away (slides)</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>New cough, increased fatigue, night sweats · fever below 97°F (36°C) or above 102°F (39°C) · new headache, new blurred vision, mental status change · new skin lesions, new diarrhea, weight loss over 10%.</p>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"immune--bioterrorism\"><h2>Bioterrorism<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#immune--bioterrorism\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><p>The intentional release of bacteria, viruses, or toxins. An attack is often first noticed as a pattern of unusual illness.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Category</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n<th>Agents</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>A · highest priority</strong></td>\n<td>Easily spread person to person, high death rate, can cause panic, needs special preparedness</td>\n<td><strong>Anthrax · botulism · plague · smallpox (variola) · tularemia · viral hemorrhagic fevers</strong> (Ebola, Marburg, Lassa)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>B · second priority</strong></td>\n<td>Moderately easy to spread, moderate illness, low death rate</td>\n<td>Staph (enterotoxin B) · viral encephalitis · E. coli (food safety threats with Salmonella, Shigella) · <strong>glanders</strong> (from horses) · <strong>psittacosis</strong> (from birds) · brucellosis · Q fever · ricin · typhus · melioidosis · water threats (cholera)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>C · emerging</strong></td>\n<td>Could be engineered for mass spread in the future</td>\n<td>Viral encephalitis · novel influenza · Nipah virus · typhus · water threats. <em>Viral encephalitis is on both the B and C lists</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout pearl\"><div class=\"chead\">Anthrax (Category A)</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><em>Bacillus anthracis</em> spores; contact with animal products, or mailed spores (2001).</li>\n<li><strong>Cutaneous:</strong> painless papule → vesicle → <strong>painless black eschar</strong> on the hands, arms, face, neck; 7–14 day incubation.</li>\n<li><strong>Inhalational:</strong> 1–7 days, then rapid respiratory failure, hemorrhagic mediastinitis, shock. <strong>GI:</strong> from undercooked infected meat.</li>\n<li><strong>Dx:</strong> culture (blood, sputum, CSF, skin). <strong>Treatment: Cipro and doxy</strong> (ciprofloxacin, doxycycline).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout pearl\"><div class=\"chead\">Smallpox (Category A)</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>Also called <strong>variola</strong>. Eradicated in 1977, but contagious by aerosol, so it&#39;s a top threat.</li>\n<li>Lesions on the face, skin, and torso: macule → papule → vesicle → pustule.</li>\n<li>Diagnosis is clinical (PCR only at the CDC). Treatment is supportive (cidofovir shows some activity).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Other agents</th>\n<th>Key points</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Plague</strong></td>\n<td>Flea bites; painful swollen lymph node (<strong>bubo</strong>); pneumonic form → ARDS. Tx: <strong>streptomycin</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Tularemia</strong></td>\n<td>Ticks, flies, animals; no person-to-person spread. Tx: <strong>gentamicin</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Q fever</strong></td>\n<td>Cattle, sheep, goats; fever over 104°F. Tx: <strong>doxycycline</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Botulism</strong></td>\n<td><em>Clostridium botulinum</em> toxin, Category A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Glanders</strong></td>\n<td>From horses (<em>Burkholderia mallei</em>), Category B. Skin infection; droplets cause pneumonia and sepsis. Tx: <strong>imipenem</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Psittacosis</strong></td>\n<td>From birds (parrots, cockatiels), Category B. &quot;Atypical pneumonia&quot;: cough, fever, headache. Tx: macrolide, doxycycline, or cipro</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p><strong>Public health response:</strong> detect early (surveillance), confirm the agent in the lab, alert ERs and labs, start prophylaxis, consider quarantine, and keep the public informed.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"immune--more-from-your-first-study-guide\"><h2>More from your first study guide<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#immune--more-from-your-first-study-guide\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><h3>Ch 18 · Immune function</h3>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Innate (natural)</th>\n<th>Adaptive (acquired)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>When</strong></td>\n<td>Present at birth</td>\n<td>Acquired after exposure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Speed</strong></td>\n<td>Immediate</td>\n<td>Delayed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Specific?</strong></td>\n<td>Nonspecific: same response to any invader</td>\n<td>Specific to one antigen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Memory</strong></td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lines of defense</strong></td>\n<td>1st (barriers) + 2nd (inflammation)</td>\n<td>3rd (T cells = cellular, B cells = humoral)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><ul>\n<li><strong>Physical barriers and mechanisms:</strong> intact skin · mucous membranes · protective lining of organs · sloughing, coughing, sneezing, vomiting, urination · chemical: acidic secretions, enzymes in tears and saliva.</li>\n<li><strong>Acquired immunity:</strong> <strong>active</strong> (your body makes it; lasts years): natural infection or vaccine. <strong>Passive</strong> (borrowed, short): maternal IgG across placenta, IgA in breast milk, immune globulin.</li>\n<li><strong>Phagocytosis:</strong> neutrophils, monocytes/macrophages engulf and destroy foreign bodies and toxins. Functions of the immune system: remove foreign antigens · phagocytosis · inflammatory response.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Organ</th>\n<th>Key fact</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Thymus</strong> (central)</td>\n<td>In the chest; where T cells mature; shrinks with age</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Bone marrow</strong> (central)</td>\n<td>T and B cells form; B cells mature here</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Spleen</strong> (peripheral)</td>\n<td>LUQ; filters old/damaged RBCs; splenectomy → immunocompromised</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lymph nodes, tonsils, adenoids, Peyer&#39;s patches</strong></td>\n<td>Peripheral; filter lymph, trap inhaled germs, intestinal immune sensors</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p><em>Review bone anatomy and physiology (class note). Thymic hypoplasia = underdeveloped thymus (DiGeorge).</em></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Cell</th>\n<th>Job</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Neutrophils</strong> (granulocyte)</td>\n<td>Phagocytes of early inflammation; destroy bacteria; most numerous</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Basophils / mast cells</strong></td>\n<td>Release histamine and heparin; allergic reactions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Eosinophils</strong></td>\n<td>Destroy allergens; fight parasites</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Monocytes → macrophages</strong></td>\n<td>Digest microbes; present antigens</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>B lymphocytes</strong></td>\n<td>Become plasma cells → antibodies (humoral)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>T lymphocytes</strong></td>\n<td>CD4 helper (activate B cells and others) · CD8 cytotoxic (kill infected/cancer cells) · suppressor · <strong>NK cells kill cancer cells</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p><strong>Memory:</strong> granulocytes = &quot;<strong>NEB</strong>&quot; (Neutrophils, Eosinophils, Basophils).\n<strong>Other slide items:</strong> dendritic cells (macrophage-type, aid T and B cell recognition) · mast cells (skin, respiratory and GI linings; release heparin and histamine early) · <strong>cytokines</strong> (interleukins stimulate leukocyte production and maturation; <strong>interferon</strong> protects against viral and tumor growth; <strong>TNF-alpha</strong>) · <strong>complement system</strong> (proteins that help antibodies destroy microbes; antibody binds pathogen → cascade of enzymes kills it).</p>\n<h3>Immunoglobulin memory hooks ★</h3>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Ig</th>\n<th>Function</th>\n<th>Remember</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>IgA</strong></td>\n<td>In secretions: breast milk, sweat, saliva, mucus, tears</td>\n<td><strong>A = All secretions</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>IgD</strong></td>\n<td>B-cell activation</td>\n<td><strong>D = Develop B cells</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>IgE</strong></td>\n<td>Allergic responses; parasites</td>\n<td><strong>E = Exaggerated allergy</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>IgG</strong></td>\n<td>Most abundant; crosses the placenta; bacteria, viruses, toxins</td>\n<td><strong>G = Gestation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>IgM</strong></td>\n<td>First antibody in a primary response; largest</td>\n<td><strong>M = Made first</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><h3>Inflammation, humoral vs cellular</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Local (cardinal) signs:</strong> redness · heat · swelling (edema) · pain · drainage (serous or pus) ± loss of function. <strong>Systemic:</strong> fever · chills · malaise · leukocytosis (↑WBC).</li>\n<li>Acute inflammatory response: tissue damage → pathogen invasion → vasodilation (↑blood flow, chemotaxis of leukocytes) + ↑capillary permeability → invaders engulfed and phagocytized + fibrinogen clot border → cardinal signs → systemic response.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Humoral</th>\n<th>Cellular</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Cell</strong></td>\n<td>B cells → antibodies</td>\n<td>T cells</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>How</strong></td>\n<td>Circulating antibodies</td>\n<td>Direct cell-to-cell destruction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Fights</strong></td>\n<td>Extracellular bacteria, viruses</td>\n<td>Intracellular germs, tumors, transplants</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><ul>\n<li><strong>Agglutination</strong> = antibodies clump antigens together, &quot;traps and makes like a paste&quot; → easier phagocytosis. <strong>Opsonization</strong> = antigen–antibody complex &quot;coated with paste&quot; → easier phagocytosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Variables affecting immune function:</strong> age and gender · nutrition and lifestyle · history of infection or immunization · allergies · conditions (autoimmune, cancer, chronic illness, surgery) · medications and transfusions · psychoneuroimmunologic factors.</li>\n<li><strong>Labs:</strong> WBC 4.5–11.1; left shift = bands = infection; low ANC → reverse isolation. Aging (immunosenescence) → ↑infection, cancer, autoimmunity; <strong>Shingrix at 50+.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Diagnostic studies:</strong> WBC and differential · skin tests; hypersensitivity test · bone marrow aspiration and biopsy · radiological imaging · specific antibody-antigen tests · HIV tests.</li>\n<li><strong>Case: Grace Hall, 60 (shingles).</strong> 4 days of low-grade fever, malaise, headache, and itching/painful sensation on the right chest; erythematous maculopapular rash with vesicles. <strong>Herpes zoster</strong> = reactivation of the varicella-zoster virus (chickenpox) that can result in <strong>post-herpetic neuralgia.</strong> Increase fluids, avoid immunosuppressed people, report increasing fever or pain, follow up every 1–2 weeks.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Ch 19 · Immune deficiency extras</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>XLA:</strong> the most common type of primary immune deficiency per the slide (<strong>50% of PID cases</strong>); 1 in 250,000 male newborns. <strong>BTK (Bruton tyrosine kinase)</strong> on the X chromosome. <strong>Hallmark: no circulating B cells or plasma cells; IgG &lt;200 mg/dL.</strong> Each son of a carrier mother has a 50% chance of being affected. First indicator: recurrent bacterial infections of the respiratory tract in childhood. <strong>Diagnosis:</strong> detailed family history · B-lymphocyte surface marker assays · <strong>Western blot</strong> (BTK protein expressed?) · genetic testing · chest/sinus x-ray. <strong>IVIG</strong> = passive immunity (IgG, gammaglobulin) every 3–4 weeks or weekly subQ + low-dose prophylactic antibiotics. <strong>Complications:</strong> pneumonia, empyema, meningitis, sepsis, cellulitis, septic arthritis (60% in children); chronic enterovirus, enteroviral encephalitis, neurologic deterioration. <strong>Nursing diagnosis:</strong> risk for infection related to compromised host defenses secondary to B-cell immunodeficiency. Assess behavior (fever, change in eating, irritability, decreased activity, inconsolable crying in children). Start antibody replacement early → good prognosis. Teach: warning signs to parents, safe food handling and storage, handwashing, avoid contagious people, <strong>seek care for temp ≥100.5°F</strong>, genetic information and support groups. <strong>IVIG = immune globulin (mostly IgG) from human plasma; short-term passive immunity, lasts 3–4 weeks.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>CVID:</strong> low circulating B cells, like XLA but appears in adolescents/young adults (<strong>15–35</strong>). <strong>Selective IgA deficiency:</strong> 50% overcome it by age 14; can develop <strong>anaphylactic reactions when IgA is administered.</strong> <strong>IgG subclass deficiency:</strong> sinusitis, otitis media, pneumonia; prophylactic antibiotics, IVIG.</li>\n<li><strong>Wiskott–Aldrich:</strong> B and T cells affected; X-linked; always the triad: <strong>infection, thrombocytopenia, eczema.</strong> <strong>SCID:</strong> 13 different genetic defects; &quot;boy in the bubble&quot;; cure = stem cell transplant. <strong>CMC (chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis):</strong> thymus and endocrine glands; recurrent Candida of skin, nails, mucous membranes; adrenal cortex hypofunction is the major cause of death.</li>\n<li><strong>T-cell deficiency:</strong> CD4 helper T cells activate B cells, cytotoxic T cells, NK cells, and macrophages. Deficiency → fungal, viral, protozoan infections (Candida).</li>\n<li><strong>DiGeorge:</strong> gestational diabetes implicated; pharyngeal pouches, 6–10 weeks. Recurrent infections: yeast, fungal, protozoa, viral (measles, chickenpox, rubella), Candida. <strong>CATCH:</strong> Cardiac (tetralogy of Fallot, murmur) · Abnormal face (low-set ears, wide-set eyes) · Thymic aplasia → infections · Cleft palate · Hypocalcemia. Hypocalcemia: twitching, tingling of lips/around mouth → tetany, seizures, laryngospasm → give calcium, monitor Ca. <strong>Nursing diagnoses:</strong> risk for opportunistic infections · risk for infection · high risk for ineffective airway clearance (laryngospasm related to hypocalcemia). Complications: delayed brain development, speech delays, learning disability, ADHD, autism spectrum, psychiatric and autoimmune disorders, GVHD. Coordinate cardiology, speech, infectious disease. <strong>DiGeorge finding to report immediately: rhinorrhea</strong> (early infection sign in T-cell deficiency).</li>\n<li><strong>Therapy-induced (secondary):</strong> cytotoxic meds destroy WBCs. <strong>Corticosteroids</strong> keep T cells in marrow → lymphopenia, ↓IgG, suppress cell-mediated immunity. Assess temp &gt;100°F, hypotension, tachycardia, breath sounds, cloudy/foul urine, skin rashes.</li>\n<li><strong>Autoimmunity</strong> = body launches an immune response against self (SLE, RA, MS, type 1 DM, Graves&#39;). <strong>Hypersensitivity</strong> = overactive immune response.</li>\n<li><strong>Memory: ACID-S</strong> = Allergic · Cytotoxic · Immune complex · Delayed · Stimulated.</li>\n<li><strong>Type II epidemiology:</strong> erythroblastosis fetalis (Rh incompatibility; 1 in 1,000 births to Rh-negative women) · Goodpasture&#39;s (1 case per million per year) · <strong>myasthenia gravis</strong> (autoantibodies block acetylcholine receptors; 2 per 100,000). Treatment: remove offending drug/blood product, plasmapheresis, corticosteroids, immunosuppressants, IVIG, dialysis, renal transplant.</li>\n<li><strong>Type III:</strong> SLE (53 per 100,000; women 9× more than men), RA (women 2.5×), serum sickness (allergic reaction to meds with protein), <strong>Arthus reaction</strong> (local vasculitis after intradermal/subQ injection; raised red lesion after 4–10 hours, center ulcer). Treat: remove agent, symptom management, epinephrine or corticosteroids.</li>\n<li><strong>Type IV:</strong> antibody independent; 24–72 h; sensitized T lymphocytes release cytokines; macrophages cause destruction; range from local dermatitis to anaphylactic shock. Prevention, antihistamines, steroidal creams. <strong>Latex nursing diagnoses:</strong> risk for localized tissue damage · risk for impaired oxygenation. Ask about history of repeated surgeries.</li>\n<li><strong>Type V:</strong> Graves&#39; disease: antibody binds to the TSH receptor on the thyroid.</li>\n<li><strong>Most common food allergies:</strong> milk, eggs, peanuts, tree nuts, fish, shellfish, wheat, soy, sesame. The latex link is through fruits (latex-fruit syndrome).</li>\n<li><strong>Type I treatment:</strong> antihistamines/decongestants (diphenhydramine), steroids, beta-agonist bronchodilators. Diagnosis: WBC differential shows ↑eosinophils; scratch test with glucocorticoids, antihistamines, NSAIDs stopped 5 days prior. <strong>Teaching:</strong> identify causes and avoid; signs of initial reaction; EpiPen use; Medic Alert bracelet. <strong>Outcomes:</strong> free of symptoms, normal oxygen, no rash or itching.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Case: Samuel Smith, 50 (anaphylaxis to penicillin)</h3>\n<p>Presents with pneumonia and gets <strong>IV penicillin</strong>. Then: short of breath, anxious, itching, dizzy; rapid pulse, blue-tinged lips, rash on chest and arms, wheezing, sense of impending doom. <strong>BP 85/50, HR 130, SpO₂ 89%</strong>. Hoarseness, hives, <strong>angioedema</strong> of lips and mouth.\n<strong>What the nurse did:</strong> immediately <strong>discontinued the IV penicillin</strong>, changed the IV tubing, started normal saline; <strong>epinephrine 0.5 mg IM stat</strong>; <strong>100% O₂ by non-rebreather</strong>; cardiac monitor (HR 130 with PVCs); <strong>diphenhydramine 50 mg IV push</strong>; IV fluids; <strong>albuterol</strong> nebulizer for bronchospasm; intubation/tracheotomy equipment ready. Improved: SpO₂ 95%, BP 110/60, HR 90.</p>\n<h3>Ch 20/22 · standard additions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>OA / hip replacement:</strong> don&#39;t flex the hip past 90°, no leg crossing, abduction pillow, raised toilet seat.</li>\n<li><strong>RA:</strong> methotrexate monitoring: LFTs, CBC, folic acid, no alcohol, teratogenic.</li>\n<li><strong>Lupus (standard):</strong> butterfly rash, photosensitivity (sunscreen), joint pain, lupus nephritis (watch kidneys), hydroxychloroquine (yearly eye exams), steroids. Women 9:1.</li>\n<li><strong>HIV (standard):</strong> retrovirus (RNA) uses reverse transcriptase to insert into CD4 T-helper cells and destroys them. Normal CD4 ≈500–1,500 cells/mm³. AIDS = CD4 &lt;200 or an AIDS-defining opportunistic infection. ART is lifelong with strict adherence to prevent resistance; goal = undetectable viral load. Not spread by saliva, sweat, tears.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Opportunistic infection (standard)</th>\n<th>Usual CD4</th>\n<th>Note</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Pneumocystis pneumonia (PCP)</strong></td>\n<td>&lt;200</td>\n<td>Dry cough, dyspnea; prophylaxis TMP-SMX (Bactrim)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Oral/esophageal candidiasis</strong></td>\n<td>&lt;200–250</td>\n<td>White plaques; dysphagia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Toxoplasmosis</strong></td>\n<td>&lt;100</td>\n<td>Brain lesions; avoid cat litter, undercooked meat</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>MAC, CMV retinitis</strong></td>\n<td>&lt;50</td>\n<td>CMV → vision loss</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Kaposi sarcoma, TB</strong></td>\n<td>Any</td>\n<td>Purple skin lesions; TB at any CD4</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><h3>Bioterrorism extras</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anthrax:</strong> cutaneous = painless sore → black eschar; <strong>inhalation is deadliest.</strong> Not spread person-to-person (standard precautions). Ciprofloxacin or doxycycline.</li>\n<li><strong>Smallpox:</strong> airborne + contact precautions; lesions start on face/extremities and are <strong>all the same stage</strong> (chickenpox = mixed stages, mostly trunk).</li>\n<li><strong>Botulism:</strong> toxin → <strong>descending flaccid paralysis</strong> starting with cranial nerves (blurred/double vision, dysphagia, slurred speech) → respiratory failure. Antitoxin, ventilator support. <strong>Not contagious.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>CDC categories:</strong> A = highest risk. B = staph enterotoxin B, viral encephalitis, food safety threats (E. coli O157:H7, Salmonella, Shigella), glanders (horses), psittacosis (birds), brucellosis, Q fever, ricin, typhus, cholera. C = emerging (Nipah virus, hantavirus; pandemic flu often listed). <strong>Know category B: staph, viral encephalitis, E. coli. Cat C: viral encephalitis, flu.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"immune--bioterrorism-powerpoint-your-uploaded-slides\"><h2>Bioterrorism PowerPoint (your uploaded slides)<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#immune--bioterrorism-powerpoint-your-uploaded-slides\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><p><strong>Definition:</strong> terrorism involving the intentional release or dissemination of biological agents (bacteria, viruses, insects, fungi, or toxins), natural or human-modified. An attack may not be obvious: usually local health care workers report a <strong>pattern of unusual illness</strong> or a wave of sick people seeking emergency care. The first evidence may be symptoms of the disease. Officials may not immediately know what the illness is, how to treat it, or who is in danger.</p>\n<h3>CDC categories (A, B, C)</h3>\n\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Category A · highest priority</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Pathogens that pose a risk to national security because they (1) can be easily disseminated or transmitted person to person, (2) cause high mortality and major public health impact, (3) might cause public panic and social disruption, and (4) require special action for public health preparedness.</p>\n<p><strong>Agents:</strong> Anthrax (<em>Bacillus anthracis</em>) · Botulism (<em>Clostridium botulinum</em> toxin) · Plague (<em>Yersinia pestis</em>) · Smallpox (variola major) · Tularemia (<em>Francisella tularensis</em>) · Viral hemorrhagic fevers, including filoviruses (Ebola, Marburg) and arenaviruses (Lassa, Machupo).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Category B · second highest priority</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Moderately easy to disseminate; moderate morbidity and low mortality; require specific enhancements of CDC&#39;s diagnostic capacity and enhanced disease surveillance.</p>\n<p><strong>Agents:</strong> Brucellosis · Epsilon toxin of <em>Clostridium perfringens</em> · Food safety threats (<em>Salmonella</em>, <em>E. coli</em> O157:H7, <em>Shigella</em>) · Glanders (<em>Burkholderia mallei</em>) · Melioidosis (<em>Burkholderia pseudomallei</em>) · Psittacosis (<em>Chlamydia psittaci</em>) · Q fever (<em>Coxiella burnetii</em>) · Ricin toxin (castor beans) · Staphylococcal enterotoxin B · Typhus fever (<em>Rickettsia prowazekii</em>) · Viral encephalitis (alphaviruses: eastern, Venezuelan, western equine encephalitis) · Water safety threats (<em>Vibrio cholerae</em>, <em>Cryptosporidium parvum</em>).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Category C · third priority</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Emerging pathogens that could be engineered for mass dissemination in the future because of availability, ease of production and dissemination, and potential for high morbidity and mortality.</p>\n<p><strong>Agents:</strong> Influenza (novel strain) · Nipah virus · Typhus disease (<em>Rickettsia prowazekii</em>) · Viral encephalitis (alphaviruses) · Water safety threats (<em>Vibrio cholerae</em>, <em>Cryptosporidium parvum</em>).</p>\n</div></details>\n<h3>Anthrax (Category A)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Sporulating gram-positive rod. Human disease mostly through <strong>contact with animal products</strong> (skins). In <strong>2001, 22 cases</strong> occurred in the US through the postal system.</li>\n<li>Spores enter subcutaneously or by inhalation, become the vegetative form, and replicate. A thick capsule avoids phagocytosis; edema factor and lethal factor toxins drive edema, hemorrhage, and necrosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Inhalational:</strong> incubation 1–7 days; within 24 hours respiratory failure, <strong>hemorrhagic mediastinitis</strong>, necrotizing pneumonia, shock, multiorgan failure, death. Shock plus hemorrhagic mediastinitis is the clinical hallmark.</li>\n<li><strong>Cutaneous:</strong> incubation 7–14 days. Painless, possibly pruritic papule → central vesicle → <strong>painless black eschar.</strong> Edema, regional lymphadenopathy, fever, malaise. Hands, arms, face, neck.</li>\n<li><strong>GI:</strong> from undercooked meat of infected animals; bowel edema, mesenteric lymphadenitis and necrosis, rapid shock and death.</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnosis:</strong> culture of blood, sputum, pleural fluid, CSF, or skin. <strong>Treatment: ciprofloxacin, doxycycline.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Smallpox (variola, Category A)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Poxviridae. <strong>Eradicated 1977</strong>, now a bioterrorism concern: unvaccinated population, contagious, spreads by <strong>aerosol.</strong> Only two stockpiles remain (CDC and Russian State Research Center).</li>\n<li><strong>Five clinical categories:</strong> ordinary (macule → papule → vesicle → pustule) · modified (more rapid but less severe) · flat · <strong>hemorrhagic (more common in pregnant females)</strong> · variola sine eruptione.</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnosis primarily clinical;</strong> RT-PCR only at the CDC. <strong>Treatment supportive;</strong> some evidence cidofovir has activity.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Plague (Category A)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Yersinia pestis</em>. Transmitted by rodent-infected flea bites, animal scratches/bites, exposure to infected humans, bioterrorism.</li>\n<li><strong>Bubonic:</strong> sudden fever, chills, headache, then painful swollen regional lymph node (<strong>bubo</strong>, intensely tender, erythema, edema; fluctuation test negative). <strong>Septicemic:</strong> sepsis without a bubo. <strong>Pneumonic:</strong> short incubation, rapid <strong>ARDS.</strong></li>\n<li>Diagnosis: culture of sputum or blood; ELISA/PCR mainly field testing. <strong>Treatment: streptomycin</strong> (drug of choice); gentamicin or doxycycline.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Tularemia (Category A)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Francisella tularensis</em>, gram-negative, zoonotic. Spread by ticks and flies, infected animals, improperly prepared meat, animal bites, contaminated water, aerosol. <strong>No human-to-human transmission.</strong></li>\n<li>Patterns: ulceroglandular, pneumonic, glandular, oropharyngeal, oculoglandular. Hard to culture; diagnosis by ELISA or histology. X-ray: patchy bilateral opacities, hilar adenopathy.</li>\n<li><strong>Treatment: gentamicin</strong> (drug of choice); doxycycline and ciprofloxacin second choice.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Category B agents</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Glanders</strong> (<em>Burkholderia mallei</em>): disease of <strong>horses</strong>; humans infected by contact or droplet. Acute or chronic skin infection; droplets cause pneumonia and sepsis rapidly. Often misclassified on culture. <strong>Imipenem</strong> (3rd-gen cephalosporin, ciprofloxacin alternatives).</li>\n<li><strong>Melioidosis</strong> (<em>B. pseudomallei</em>): soil and water reservoir; contact or inhalation; aerosol → pneumonia; sepsis and MODS rapidly. Imipenem or 3rd-gen cephalosporin, then 20 days of doxycycline or cotrimoxazole.</li>\n<li><strong>Psittacosis</strong> (<em>Chlamydophila psittaci</em>): birds (parrots, cockatiels, canaries). Aerosol → &quot;atypical pneumonia&quot; (cough, fever, headache). ELISA. <strong>Macrolide, doxycycline, or ciprofloxacin.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Q fever</strong> (<em>Coxiella burnetii</em>): cattle, sheep, goats (bacteria in milk, urine, feces, amniotic fluid, placenta); droplet spread. <strong>Fever &gt;104°F</strong>, severe headache, myalgia, sore throat, nonproductive cough, N/V/D, chest pain; lasts 1–2 weeks; 30–50% develop pneumonia; endocarditis and chronic Q fever (&gt;6 months) rare. <strong>Doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 15–21 days.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Role of the public health system</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Being prepared:</strong> educate medical community and public · train special response teams · exercises for different scenarios · communication systems · interagency protocols.</li>\n<li><strong>Initial response:</strong> early detection through surveillance/rapid assessment · mobilize laboratory · rapid confirmation of agent, site, at-risk population, prophylaxis/treatment · alert medical community, ERs, labs · implement disease-specific plans (e.g., smallpox) · determine resource needs and possible quarantine · coordinate with partner agencies.</li>\n<li><strong>Continued response:</strong> monitor communication network · provide accurate, timely information to the public · continue epidemiologic investigation · assess environmental contamination · provide testing/prophylaxis/treatment to at-risk population · access biological stockpiles as necessary.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"immune--chapter-18-powerpoint-word-for-word\"><h2>Chapter 18 PowerPoint, word for word<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#immune--chapter-18-powerpoint-word-for-word\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><p>Your instructor&#39;s slides for this chapter, kept in slide order.</p>\n\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 1 · Innate vs. adaptive immunity</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Immunity – refers to the body&#39;s ability to resist infection and disease Innate – natural immunity – first line of defense – present at birth, protects the host without remembering prior contact; response without memory Adaptive – is acquired – it is a natural exposure; examples are infection, transfer of maternal antibodies, vaccination, infusion of immune globulin. Both cellular &amp; humoral mediated Components of the immune system: Bone marrow · Lymphoid tissue. Bone marrow: T cells &amp; B cells. T lymphocytes mature in the thymus. B lymphocytes mature in bone marrow. Lymphoid tissue: Spleen &amp; lymph nodes</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 2 · What the immune system does</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Function of the Immune system: Removes foreign antigens such as viruses and bacteria to maintain homeostasis · Phagocytosis – monocytes are responsible for engulfing and destroying foreign bodies and toxins · Inflammatory response – to injury or invading organisms; minimize blood loss; activate phagocytosis, formation of scar tissue and regeneration of injured tissue Immunity – Natural immunity: nonspecific response to any foreign invader. WBC action: release cell mediators such as histamine, bradykinin and prostaglandins – engulf foreign substance. Inflammatory response. Physical barriers: intact skin, chemical barriers, acidic secretions, enzymes – tears &amp; saliva Physical, Mechanical, and Biochemical Barriers: Skin · Mucous membranes · Protective lining of organs · Mechanisms – sloughing, sneezing, vomiting, urination Acquired Immunity: is specific against a foreign antigen – result of prior exposure to an antigen – active or passive. Active immunity – develops by person&#39;s own body; lasts many years; maybe lifetime</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 3 · The immune response map</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>[Image: The Immune Response] Flowchart: Immune responses split into innate (natural) immunity (nonspecific) and acquired (adaptive) immunity (specific). Innate → first line of defense (physical, biochemical, mechanical barriers) and second line of defense (inflammatory response). Acquired → third line of defense → naturally acquired (active: infection; passive: maternal antibodies) and artificially acquired (active: vaccination; passive: immune serum).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 4 · Where immunity lives</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>[Image: Lymph Nodes and the Lymphatic System] Left: a lymph node cut open, with lymph flowing in through incoming lymphatic vessels, filtered past clusters of lymphocytes and macrophages, and flowing out. Right: whole body with lymph nodes (cervical, axillary, inguinal), the thymus, spleen, bone marrow, thoracic duct, and right lymphatic duct emptying into the subclavian veins. A small inset shows the right upper body drains to the right lymphatic duct and the rest drains to the thoracic duct.</p>\n<p>Lymph nodes and the Lymphatic system: Thymus – located in the chest cavity; stimulates T cells &amp; where they develop · Bone Marrow – B &amp; T lymphocytes · Spleen – part of the lymphatic system; serves as a blood filter for old and damaged RBC Additional Lymphoid tissues: Tonsils – traps bacteria &amp; viruses inhaled · Adenoids – located in nasopharynx, also traps inhaled bacteria &amp; viruses · Peyer&#39;s Patches – lymphoid follicles located in the small intestines, known as the intestinal immune sensors against pathogens</p>\n<p>[Image: Central and Peripheral Organs of the Immune System] Body figure labeling central organs (thymus, bone marrow) and peripheral organs (adenoids, tonsils, lymph nodes, lymphatic vessels, spleen, Peyer&#39;s patches in the small intestine, lymph nodes in the jejunum).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 5 · Leukocytes (WBCs)</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Leukocytes – are the mobile units, travel through the bloodstream to fight infection · Neutrophils – phagocytes – destroy bacteria at early inflammation · Basophils – release heparin as anticoag &amp; histamine during early inflammation · Eosinophils – destroy allergens &amp; combat parasitic infections · Macrophages – digest &amp; destroy microorganisms</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 6 · B cells, t cells &amp; immunoglobulins</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Lymphocytes: B Lymphocytes – found in lymph nodes, spleen &amp; thymus – Immunoglobulins (Igs) produce antibodies (IgA, IgD, IgE, IgG and IgM) · T Lymphocytes – Natural killer cells – target and kill cancer cells</p>\n<p>Table 18.2 – Immunoglobulins that B cells produce: IgA – found in secretory gland secretions – breast milk, sweat, saliva, mucus and tears · IgD – plays a role in B cell activation · IgE – acts as a defender against parasitic infections; mediator of allergic responses · IgG – transported across placenta &amp; effective against bacteria, viruses &amp; toxins · IgM – first antibody produced during primary response to an antigen</p>\n<p>[Image: Process of Differentiation of T-Cell and B-Cell Lymphocytes] Flowchart from bone marrow. Left (humoral immunity): B cells → bone marrow maturation → B lymphocytes → memory cells and plasma cells → antibodies. Right (cellular, cell-mediated immunity): T cells → thymus → regulatory T cells (→ suppressor T cells) and effector T cells (→ helper T cells and cytotoxic T cells).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 7 · Messengers &amp; helpers</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Cont with Cells: Dendritic cells – macrophage cell, aids in T cell &amp; B cell recognition · Mast cells – found in the skin and lining of the respiratory and GI tracts; similar to basophils they release heparin and histamine during early inflammatory response · Cytokines – are small proteins that regulate immune response – Interleukins – suppresses inflammation; stimulates leukocyte production &amp; maturation · Interferon – proteins that protect against viral and tumor growth · Tumor necrosis factor alpha – produce macrophages, involved in production of immune cells</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 8 · The antibody's backup crew</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Anatomy of the Immune System: Complement system – is a system of proteins that complement or help the actions of antibodies and other cells in destroying bacteria or microbes · Whenever a pathogen enters the body, it gets recognized by antibodies present in our blood, then the binding of the antibody &amp; pathogen occurs activating the complement system, by different cascades of enzymes killing the microbe</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 9 · The acute inflammatory response</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>[Image: Acute Inflammatory Response] Flowchart: tissue damage → pathogen invasion → inflammatory response → splits into vasodilation (↑ blood flow, facilitating leukocyte movement [chemotaxis] to the inflamed area) and increased capillary permeability (leukocytes move into the tissue) → invading organisms engulfed and phagocytized + fibrinogen clot border forms → cardinal signs of inflammation: localized redness (erythema), warmth, edema, drainage (serous exudate or pus), pain → systemic response: fever, malaise, chills, elevated WBC count (leukocytosis).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 10 · Cellular vs. humoral immunity</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Adaptive Immune Response: Cellular – mediated by T lymphocytes · Humoral – mediated by the B lymphocytes Cellular Mediated Response: T cells · Direct destruction of foreign or abnormal cells Humoral-Mediated Response: Agglutination – when 2 antigens react – clump – facilitates phagocytosis and enables the body to clear itself of the invading organism · Opsonization – process in which the antigen-antibody binding is coated with a pasty substance</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 11 · Variables, advances &amp; the nurse's role</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Variables affecting Immune Function: Age &amp; gender · Nutrition &amp; lifestyle · Hx of infection or immunization · Allergies · Presence of conditions or disorders: autoimmune; cancer; chronic illness; surgery · Medications &amp; transfusions · Psychoneuroimmunologic factors Advances in Immunology: Genetic engineering: DNA technology · Stem cells – research shows that stem cells can restore an immune system that has been destroyed · Clinical trials using stem cells – RA, SLE, scleroderma and multiple sclerosis Nurse&#39;s Role in Evaluation of the Immune System: Offer support · Reduce anxiety · Provide patient education and counseling</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 12 · Assessment of the immune system</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>History – genetics? chem induced? · Age · Current medications – abx, anti-inflammatory · Family history; thyroid suppressive tx · Social history · Nutritional status – adequate vits/min · Infection history · Immunizations – childhood &amp; adult · Chronic illnesses – allergies · Autoimmune disorders – immunosuppressants · Cancer – antineoplastic agents Physical examination: Vital signs, LOC · Inspection – skin (rashes, dermatitis-type lesions, hematoma, petechiae, purpura), mucous membranes, edema, gait, vision, hearing, cardio, respiratory, GI, GU · Auscultation – listen for adventitious sounds, heart sounds, bowel sounds · Palpation and percussion – lymph nodes, hepatosplenomegaly, masses, fluid in abd</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 13 · Testing immune function</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Diagnostic studies: Blood tests – WBC &amp; differential · Skin tests; hypersensitivity test · Bone marrow aspiration and biopsy · Radiological imaging · Specific antibody-antigen tests · HIV infection tests</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 14 · Immunosenescence</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>[Image: Age-Related Changes in the Immune System] Flowchart: The Aging Immune System → three branches. (1) Decreased immunologic function; less efficient immune responses → increased infections, especially secondary infections. (2) Increased production of autoantibodies → increased autoimmune disorders. (3) Decline in immune response; malignant cells not destroyed as quickly → increased cancer incidence.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 15 · Grace Hall, 60: herpes zoster (shingles)</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Episode 1: Grace Hall is a 60-year-old female who presents to an urgent care clinic with a 4-day history of low- grade fever, malaise, headache, and an itching and painful sensation on the right side of her chest. She states that she has stopped exercising due to the discomfort and finds tight clothing to be irritating. Her maximum oral temperature has been 100.4°F (38°C). Ms. Hall denies weakness, dizziness, confusion, difficulty swallowing or breathing, cough, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and weight loss. She also denies exposure to ill individuals or recent travel.</p>\n<p>Episode 2: The nurse practitioner at the clinic obtains a comprehensive health history and performs a physical examination on Ms. Hall. Her vital signs show her to have a low-grade fever, with a temperature of 100.2°F (37.8°C), a heart rate of 88 bpm, and a blood pressure reading of 128/82 mm Hg. Her examination is significant for an erythematous maculopapular rash with several vesicles on the right upper chest.</p>\n<p>Wrap-up: Based on Ms. Hall&#39;s history of presenting symptoms and physical examination, she was diagnosed with herpes zoster (shingles). Herpes zoster is a reactivation of the chickenpox or varicella- zoster virus (VZV) that can result in post-herpetic neuralgia. The nurse practitioner instructs Ms. Hall to increase her fluid intake and avoid exposure to individuals who may be immunosuppressed. She is instructed to immediately report if she develops increasing fever or pain. She will follow up every 1 to 2 weeks until resolution of her symptoms.</p>\n</div></details></section>\n<section id=\"immune--chapter-19-powerpoint-word-for-word\"><h2>Chapter 19 PowerPoint, word for word<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#immune--chapter-19-powerpoint-word-for-word\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><p>Your instructor&#39;s slides for this chapter, kept in slide order.</p>\n\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 1 · Primary vs. secondary immunodeficiency</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Learning outcomes in Chapter 19: There are 8 learning outcomes to assist you in understanding the pathophysiological process of the immune dysfunction and correlation of clinical manifestations in order to develop a comprehensive plan of care and teaching plan for patients with immune dysfunction.</p>\n<p>Introduction: Immune deficiency disorder is the lack or absence of adequate immune response · Opposite of autoimmune disorder · Affected people are more susceptible to infections and have no fully functioning immune system. There are 2 immune deficiency disorders: Primary · Secondary Primary &amp; Secondary deficiency: Primary immunodeficiency – occurs from genetic or developmental abnormality. Defects from T &amp; B cells, natural killer cells, phagocytic cells or the complement system · Secondary immunodeficiencies occur after birth secondary to disease process (malnutrition, cancer, drugs/radiation therapy, loss of spleen, exposure to chemicals, infection or stress)</p>\n<p>Table 19.1 – Warning signs of PID: Eight or more new infections within 1 year · Two or more serious sinus infections within 1 year · Two or more months on antibiotics · Two or more pneumonias within 1 year · Failure of an infant to gain weight · Recurrent deep skin or organ abscess · Persistent thrush in the mouth or elsewhere on skin after age 1 · Need for IV antibiotics to clear infections · A family history of immune deficiency</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 2 · XLA: the disease</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Epidemiology: XLA – an inherited or congenital immune disorder · The most common type of Primary Immune Deficiency · Accounts for 50% of PID cases · 1 in 250,000 male newborns · XLA manifests approx 6 months of age Pathophysiology: Deficiency of BTK (Bruton Tyrosine Kinase) – gene essential in development of B lymphocytes – present in the X chromosome · BTK protein alerts B cells to mature – unable to mature, no antibody response · Hallmark: No circulating B cells or plasma cells · Lab studies show markedly reduced immunoglobulins (IgG &lt; 200 mg/dL) · Prone to severe bacterial infections</p>\n<p>[Image: X-Linked Recessive Genetic Inheritance of Disease] &quot;X-Linked Recessive, Carrier Mother.&quot; An unaffected father (XY) and a carrier mother (one altered X) have four possible children: unaffected son, unaffected daughter, carrier daughter (one altered X, no disease), and affected son (his only X is altered). Each son has a 50% chance of being affected.</p>\n<p>Clinical Manifestations: Infections of the ears, lungs, skin, conjunctiva, and central nervous system · Bacterial infections such as pharyngitis, sinusitis, otitis media, bronchitis and pneumonia · Presence of recurrent bacterial infections of the respiratory tract in childhood is the first indicator Medical Management – Diagnosis: Detailed family history – to detect abnormal immune response · B- lymphocyte surface marker assays – identifies specific cells involved in immune response · Western blot test – determines if the BTK protein is being expressed · Genetic testing – women with XLA · Chest or sinus X-ray Medical Management – Treatment: IV immune globulin (IVIG) – provides passive immunity – 1980&#39;s – Immunoglobulin G (IgG) – Gammaglobulin – protects against infection from various diseases; given every 3–4 weeks or weekly thru subQ injections (serum IgG) · Prophylactic antibiotics – low dose Complications: Severe, life-threatening bacterial infection – pneumonia, empyema, meningitis, sepsis, cellulitis, septic arthritis – 60% in children · Chronic viral enterovirus · Enteroviral encephalitis · Neurological deterioration</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 3 · Assess, act, teach, evaluate</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Assessment and analysis: Related to infection of the respiratory tract, skin, conjunctiva, or ears · Common findings – fever, change in eating patterns, irritability, decreased activity, changes in behaviour, in children – inconsolable crying Nursing diagnosis: Risk for infection related to compromised host defenses secondary to B-cell immunodeficiency Assessments: Vital signs – T? RR? · Assess lung sounds – adventitious? · Inspect eyes and ears, skin and nails, rashes and lesions – assess the color of the skin &amp; texture · Take note of current meds · Evaluate the infant or child&#39;s behavior and eating patterns Actions: Administer IV gamma globulin as ordered – assess any problems post infusion · Administer prophylactic antibiotic therapy as ordered · Anticipate prompt treatment with antibiotics when infection is present Teaching: Teach parents of at-risk children about the warning signs · Institute precautions to prevent infection · Protect the patient from direct contact with anyone with a contagious illness · Safe food handling and storage practices · Signs and symptoms of infection and when to seek medical attention · Good hand washing · Information regarding genetic disorders and community support groups Evaluating care outcomes: Infection prevention · When to contact healthcare provider · Start antibody replacement early – have good prognosis</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 4 · CVID, IgA &amp; IgG subclass deficiency</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Other B-Cell Deficiencies: Common Variable Immune Deficiency (CVID) – low levels of circulating B cells, similar to XLA except that it appears in adolescents or young adults (15–35 y/o) · Selective Immunoglobulin A – 50% affected overcome by age 14; develop anaphylactic reactions when IgA is administered · Immunoglobulin G Subclass Deficiency – risk for sinusitis, otitis media, PNA – Tx prophylactic abx; IVIG Type of Antibodies – 5 types of antibodies &amp; function: IgG – triggers autoimmune disease · IgA – found in mucosal tissues · IgM – helps B cell remember pathogen · IgE – responsible for allergic response · IgD – signals the release of front-line IgM to fight disease and infection. Kick starts the immune response</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 5 · DiGeorge: the disease</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Primary Immune Dysfunction: T-cell Deficiencies: CD4 helper cells · CD8 cytotoxic T cells · Cell- mediated immune response – protects against fungal, protozoan, viral, and intracellular bacterial infections · Control malignant cell proliferation Epidemiology: Autosomal-dominant genetic condition – presence of one altered gene · Arises from 22nd chromosome · Spontaneously present at birth · DiGeorge syndrome is a congenital thymic hypoplasia, present at birth; gestational diabetes is implicated</p>\n<p>[Image: Autosomal-Dominant Genetic Inheritance of DiGeorge&#39;s Syndrome] An affected father and an unaffected mother have four possible children: affected son, unaffected daughter, unaffected son, affected daughter. Each child has a 50% chance; boys and girls are equally affected.</p>\n<p>Pathophysiology: Disturbance of normal development of pharyngeal pouches – occurs during 6–10 weeks gestation · Effects depend on which pharyngeal pouch is affected – dysfunction of thymus and parathyroid – facial deformities (cleft palate) – heart anomalies Clinical Manifestations: Recurrent infections – yeast, fungal, protozoa; viral – measles, chicken pox, rubella, candida · Cardiac abnormality – heart murmur · Abnormal faces – low set ears, wide set eyes · Thymic aplasia – atrophy of the thymus · Cleft palate · Hypocalcemia – hypoparathyroidism – twitching, tingling in the lips, around the mouth Diagnosis: Genetic testing – finding of submicroscopic deletion of chromosome 22, detected by fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH) – molecular cytogenetic technique that allows the localization of specific DNA Treatment: Pharmacologic therapy – calcium supplements – prevents tetany &amp; seizures · Treatment of infections · IVIG – provides ST passive immunity (3–4 weeks) · Prophylactic antibiotics · Bone marrow transplant in severe cases Surgical Management: Thymus tissue transplant (3–6 months after birth) · Cardiac surgery Complications: Delayed brain development · Speech delays · Learning disability · Attention deficit- hyperactivity disorder · Autism spectrum disorder · Psychiatric disorders · Autoimmune disorders · Graft-versus-host disease – T cells attack</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 6 · Assess, act, teach, evaluate</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Assessment and analysis: Disturbance of normal embryonic development – thymus – parathyroid gland – facial structures – cardiac structures Nursing diagnoses: Risk for complications – opportunistic infections · Risk for infection – compromised host defense · High risk for ineffective airway clearance – laryngospasm related to hypocalcemia Assessments: Vital signs – T · Lung sounds · Calcium levels – presence of hypoparathyroidism · Tetany · Monitor WBCs Actions: Administer calcium supplements as ordered · Infection control precautions and standard precautions · Strategies for addressing feeding difficulties · Coordinate care for cardiac, infectious disease specialists, and speech pathologists Teaching: Overview of the disease process · Growth and development milestones – early assessment for learning disabilities · Emotional support and networking Evaluating care outcomes: Limited infections · No seizures · Growth and development within normal limits · Support from interprofessional team</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 7 · CMC, Hyper-IgM, SCID, Wiskott–Aldrich</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Other T-Cell Immune Deficiencies: Chronic Mucocutaneous Candidiasis – involves thymus and other endocrine glands. Recurrent Candida infections of the skin, nails, mucous membranes. High morbidity due to endocrine dysfunction. Hypofunction of the adrenal cortex is the major cause of death · Hyper- IgM Syndrome – X-linked immunodeficiency with low IgG and IgA serum levels Primary Immune Deficiency of Both T Cells and B Cells: Severe combined immune deficiency (SCID) – there are 13 different genetic defects · Wiskott–Aldrich syndrome – combined B &amp; T cell defect, characteristics include recurrent infection, eczema, and thrombocytopenia</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 8 · Therapy-induced immunodeficiency</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Epidemiology: Caused by medication-induced immunosuppression, radiation, or surgery Pathophysiology: Cytotoxic medications destroy WBCs – remaining lymphocytes cannot release antibodies and lymphokines · Corticosteroids interfere with cell-mediated immunity or production of antibodies – decreased T cells (keep T cells in bone marrow) – suppression of cell-mediated immunity and lymphopenia – decreased IgG production – decreased antibody-antigen binding Medical Management: Prevention · Hand washing · Avoiding contact with known infectious persons · Regular provider follow up · Prompt action when signs of infection develop Assessment and analysis: Protect patient from infection · Assess for signs/symptoms of infection Nursing diagnoses: Risk for complications · Risk for infection Assessments: Vital signs – T, hypotension, tachycardia · Breath sounds – may be decreased or adventitious · Urine – cloudy, foul smell – UTI · Skin rashes or lesions Actions: Practice good hand washing · Treat infections as ordered · Anticipate changes in immunosuppressive medications Teaching: Avoid crowds or large gatherings · Do not change cat litter boxes – avoid toxoplasmosis exposure · Avoid turtles or reptiles as pets – salmonella exposure · Eat a low bacteria diet · Report any signs/symptoms of infection Evaluating care outcomes: Takes precautions to prevent infection · Practices good hand washing · Knows when to contact provider · Treats infections promptly</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 9 · When the immune system overreacts</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Excessive Immune Response: Autoimmunity – body initiates an immune response against self; body fails to recognize normal cells and causes immune reaction against the perceived antigen · Hypersensitivity reaction – immune response is overactive. An antigen stimulates an immune response in a susceptible individual</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 10 · Immediate reactions &amp; anaphylaxis</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Epidemiology: Genetic predisposition – 80% chance if both parents have allergies · Allergic rhinitis most common type I hypersensitivity reaction · Incidence of anaphylaxis not known Pathophysiology [image]: Allergic reaction cycle. (1) First exposure: initial contact with the allergen → (2) B cell becomes a plasma cell and releases IgE antibodies → (3) IgE attaches to receptors on mast cells → (4) Second exposure: the allergen binds the IgE on the mast cell → (5) mast cell degranulates, releasing histamine and other chemicals → allergic reaction.</p>\n<p>Clinical Manifestations: Nasal discharge · Sneezing · Pruritus of upper airways · Headache · Sinus pressure · Itchy, watery eyes · Anaphylaxis: systemic response – dyspnea, wheezes/crackles, skin reaction, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, anxiety, flush of heat, angioedema, bronchospasm, hoarseness, and stridor Diagnosis: WBC with differential – reveals increase in eosinophils · Skin testing – scratch test · Glucocorticoids, antihistamines and NSAIDs are discontinued 5 days prior to testing Treatment: Identification, treatment, prevention of allergic responses · Antihistamines and decongestants – diphenhydramine hydrochloride · Steroids · Beta-agonist bronchodilators</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 11 · Assess, act, teach, evaluate</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Assessment and analysis: Bronchoconstriction · Increased capillary permeability · Runny nose, itchy, red eyes, rash · Dyspnea · Wheezes Nursing diagnosis: Risk for complications of allergic reaction Assessments: Monitor respiratory rate and depth, lung sounds, pulse ox, and ABG · Monitor blood pressure and pulse · General systems assessment Actions: Discontinue offending agent · Administer oxygen as ordered · Elevate head of bed for ventilation; do not compromise BP · Administer medications as ordered Teaching: Identification of potential causes and avoidance · Signs and symptoms of initial reaction · EpiPen use · Medic Alert bracelet Evaluating care outcomes: Free of symptoms · Normal oxygen level · No evidence of rash, itching, or difficulty breathing</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 12 · Cytotoxic reactions</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Type II Hypersensitivity Reaction: Cytotoxic: Complement and antibody-mediated cell destruction · Complement and antibody receptor-mediated inflammation · Antibody-mediated cellular dysfunction Epidemiology: Erythroblastosis fetalis – Rh desensitization; 1 in 1,000 births to Rh-neg women – due to incompatibility · Goodpasture&#39;s syndrome – produced antibodies attack the lungs &amp; kidneys – 1 case per one million people per year · Myasthenia gravis – 2 out of 100,000 people – cell dysfunction in the transmission of nerve impulse Subtype 1: Complement is not working · Susceptible to autoimmune diseases · Examples: blood transfusion reactions · hemolytic disease due to blood type or Rh incompatibility · certain medication reactions</p>\n<p>[Image: Compatibility of Blood Types] Diagram: type O (top) has no antigens and both A and B antibodies; type AB (bottom) has A and B antigens and no antibodies. Green arrows (compatible) run from O to every type and from A and B to AB. Red crossed arrows (incompatible) run between A and B.</p>\n<p>Subtype 2: Goodpasture&#39;s syndrome: immune system attacks Goodpasture&#39;s antigen in the glomerular basement membrane · Deposits of IgG form along basement membrane of lungs or kidneys · Glomerulonephritis and hemorrhage of lung Subtype 3: Antibodies bind to cell-surface receptors · Myasthenia gravis – the autoantibodies either block the action of acetylcholine or destroy the receptors Medical Management – Treatment: Removal of medication or blood product causing reaction · Plasmapheresis · Corticosteroids · Immunosuppressive medications · IVIG · Dialysis · Renal transplant Complications: Renal failure · Hemolytic reaction · Lung damage · Anemia, leukopenia, or thrombocytopenia · Hemorrhage</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 13 · Immune complex reactions</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Immune complex-mediated reactions: Categories: systemic or local immune complex reactions · Examples: Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) · Rheumatoid arthritis · Serum sickness – allergic reaction to meds with protein · Arthus reaction – local immune complex – acute inflammatory response after vaccination Epidemiology: SLE – genetic, environmental, or unknown factors – 53 per 100,000 people – women 9× more than men · Rheumatoid arthritis – women 2.5× more than men · Arthus reaction – rare, no specific prevalence Local Immune Complex Reactions: Deposit of immune complexes in dermal vessels · Arthus reaction is a localized vasculitis due to deposit of immune complexes in dermal vessels after intradermal or subcut injections – raised red lesion develops after 4–10 hours, an ulcer forms in the center Medical Management: Removal of offending agent · Symptom management · Epinephrine or systemic corticosteroids</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 14 · Delayed reactions &amp; latex allergy</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Epidemiology: Delayed hypersensitivity – poison ivy – Mantoux test for tuberculosis – latex allergy Pathophysiology and Clinical Manifestations: Antibody independent · Occurs 24–72 hours after exposure · Sensitized T lymphocytes attack antigens and release cytokines · Macrophages responsible for tissue destruction · Range from local dermatitis to anaphylactic shock Medical management: Prevention · Antihistamines · Steroidal skin creams Assessment and analysis: Tissue damage related to inflammatory response – local skin reactions – conjunctivitis – rhinitis – respiratory distress Nursing diagnoses: Risk for localized tissue damage · Risk for impaired oxygenation Assessments: Vital signs – respiratory distress, hypotension, tachycardia · Skin · Previous history of latex allergy · History of repeated surgical procedures · Allergies – avocado, chestnut, mango, papaya, peach, banana, kiwi, raw potato, tomato, passion fruit Actions: Eliminate exposure Teaching: Avoid exposure to items commonly made of latex · Medic Alert bracelet · EpiPen use Evaluating Care Outcomes: Heightened awareness of products that contain latex · EpiPen · Medic Alert bracelet</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 15 · Stimulatory reactions</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Type V Hypersensitivity: Stimulated: Antibody-mediated cellular dysfunction · Excess stimulation of normal cell- surface receptor by antibody (stimulatory reaction) · Example: Graves&#39; disease – antibody binds to the TSH receptor sites on the thyroid gland</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 16 · Samuel Smith, 50: anaphylaxis to penicillin</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Episode 1: Samuel Smith is a 50-year-old male patient who presents in the emergency department with a temperature of 101°F (38.3°C), a productive cough, fatigue, and difficulty breathing. He has a history of seasonal allergic rhinitis and mild asthma but no other significant health history. He does not report any known medication allergies. Initial orders include: chest radiography · complete blood count · complete metabolic profile · pulse oximetry · sputum for culture and sensitivity.</p>\n<p>Episode 2: Mr. Smith&#39;s chest radiograph reveals pneumonia. He is placed on IV penicillin. The nurse returns to check on the patient and finds he is short of breath and extremely anxious about his symptoms and states that his skin is &quot;itching&quot; and he feels dizzy. Upon initial assessment, his pulse is rapid, his lips are blue tinged, a rash is apparent on his chest and arms, and wheezing is heard on auscultation of his lungs. The patient expresses a sense of impending doom. Vital signs: BP 85/50 mmHg · HR 130 bpm · pulse oximetry 89%.</p>\n<p>Wrap-up: On auscultation, audible wheezing with decreased breath sounds in the lower lobes is noted. Mr. Smith complains of severe dyspnea and shortness of breath. Hoarseness is evident. Itchy skin wheals or hives have spread over his body, forming large, red blotches. Angioedema of his lips and mouth is visible. The nurse immediately discontinues the IV penicillin. The IV tubing is changed, and normal saline is initiated. Epinephrine 0.5 mg is administered IM stat. Oxygen therapy is provided via a 100% high-flow nonrebreather face mask. Oxygen saturation is monitored via pulse oximetry. Mr. Smith is placed on a cardiac monitor, which reveals a heart rate of 130 bpm with occasional premature ventricular contractions (PVCs). His BP is 85/50 mm Hg. His oxygen saturation improves to 90% after the initiation of oxygen. Diphenhydramine 50 mg is administered via IV push, and IV fluids are initiated. Albuterol, a beta agonist, is administered via nebulizer to relieve the bronchospasm. Emergency equipment for endotracheal intubation and possible tracheotomy is readied. Mr. Smith&#39;s condition begins to improve. His shortness of breath lessens, and pulse oximetry indicates improved oxygenation at 95%. Vital signs: BP 110/60 · HR 90.</p>\n</div></details></section>"},{"id":"repro-assess","title":"Assessment of Reproductive Function","part":"Part 6 · Ch 64","summary":"History, physical exam, Pap/HPV, PSA, and diagnostic study prep and nursing implications (Table 64.3).","sections":[{"id":"repro-assess--key-assessment-points","title":"Key assessment points"},{"id":"repro-assess--labs","title":"Labs"},{"id":"repro-assess--table-64-3-diagnostic-studies-prep-and-nursing-implications","title":"Table 64.3 Diagnostic studies: prep and nursing implications ★"}],"html":"<section id=\"repro-assess--assessment-of-reproductive-function\"><p>History, the physical exam, and the diagnostic tests you&#39;ll see used for the reproductive cancers.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"repro-assess--key-assessment-points\"><h2>Key assessment points<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#repro-assess--key-assessment-points\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><ul>\n<li><strong>Gravida</strong> = total number of pregnancies. <strong>Parity (TPAL)</strong> = Term, Preterm, Abortions, Living. Example: one baby at 40 weeks plus one miscarriage = <strong>G2 P1011</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Normal cycle:</strong> 21–35 days, lasting 2–8 days, blood loss 20–80 mL. Ask about the LMP, flow, painful periods, and bleeding between periods or after sex.</li>\n<li>Family history of breast, ovarian, uterine, cervical, or prostate cancer.</li>\n<li><strong>Speculum exam:</strong> warm and lubricate the speculum, announce each step, and the patient may stop at any time. <strong>Cervical motion tenderness suggests PID.</strong> Change gloves between the vaginal and rectal exam.</li>\n<li><strong>Breast exam:</strong> inspect sitting with arms in different positions. Palpate the clavicles and axillae for nodes, then palpate supine in light, medium, and firm circles down to the chest wall, including the nipple and areola. Note any discharge.</li>\n<li><strong>Male exam:</strong> done standing. <strong>Transilluminate scrotal masses in a dark room</strong> (benign masses let light through). Bear down to check for a hernia. <strong>DRE for all men &gt;50</strong> and any man with trouble starting urination or frequency.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"repro-assess--labs\"><h2>Labs<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#repro-assess--labs\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><ul>\n<li><strong>Pap test</strong> samples the <strong>squamocolumnar junction</strong>, where cervical cancer develops.</li>\n<li><strong>Wet prep:</strong> Trichomonas, Candida, bacterial vaginosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Hormones:</strong> FSH, LH, estrogen, progesterone, testosterone. <strong>hCG</strong> confirms pregnancy.</li>\n<li><strong>PSA</strong> is a protein made by the prostate, used as a tumor marker. ↑PSA raises prostate cancer risk 25–60%, but there are false positives AND false negatives, so testing is a shared decision with the provider.</li>\n<li><strong>Menopause:</strong> estrogen and progesterone ↓, <strong>FSH ↑</strong>. Hot flashes, vaginal atrophy, dyspareunia, ↑CVD risk, osteoporosis. With aging, men have more ED, BPH, and prostate cancer, and all ED and urinary complaints should be investigated.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"repro-assess--table-64-3-diagnostic-studies-prep-and-nursing-implications\"><h2>Table 64.3 Diagnostic studies: prep and nursing implications ★<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#repro-assess--table-64-3-diagnostic-studies-prep-and-nursing-implications\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Study</th>\n<th>Why / abnormal findings</th>\n<th>Prep and nursing</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Mammography</strong></td>\n<td>Finds tumors &lt;5 mm that can&#39;t be felt on exam</td>\n<td><strong>No cream, deodorant, or lotion</strong> on the day. Results take a few days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ultrasound</strong></td>\n<td>First line for the pelvis (inexpensive). Shows tumors, abscesses, and a thickened endometrial stripe. Guides breast biopsy. Shows scrotal masses</td>\n<td><strong>Full bladder</strong> for a pelvic scan. Explain the transvaginal probe if used</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>MRI</strong></td>\n<td>Soft tissue: tumors, abscess, bleeding</td>\n<td>Remove jewelry. Ask about implanted metal, pregnancy, and claustrophobia. Check allergies before contrast</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Colposcopy</strong></td>\n<td>After an abnormal Pap. Magnifies the cervix. Acetic acid and Lugol&#39;s iodine are applied, and abnormal tissue does not stain brown. Biopsy is taken</td>\n<td>Empty the bladder. <strong>Nothing in the vagina for 24 hours.</strong> Reschedule if menstruating heavily. Pregnancy test, consent. ⚠ <strong>Lugol&#39;s is contraindicated with iodine or shellfish allergy</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Laparoscopy</strong></td>\n<td>Ovarian cysts, fibroids, endometriosis (CO₂ distends the abdomen)</td>\n<td>NPO the night before, consent, pregnancy test. Rest 2 days. Bloating and shoulder or abdominal pain from CO₂</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hysteroscopy</strong></td>\n<td>Looks inside the uterus and can remove polyps and fibroids</td>\n<td>NPO, consent. Rest 2 days. Nothing in the vagina for 4 weeks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>D&amp;C</strong></td>\n<td>Endometrial sampling: molar pregnancy, endometrial cancer</td>\n<td>NPO, consent. Normal postop bleeding (count pads). Cramping relieved by NSAIDs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Endometrial biopsy</strong></td>\n<td>Endometrial cancer, endometritis. Done for abnormal uterine bleeding</td>\n<td>Pregnancy test, consent. Cramping and spotting afterward (NSAIDs)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hysterosalpingogram</strong></td>\n<td>Blocked fallopian tubes, uterine abnormalities (x-ray with contrast)</td>\n<td>Pregnancy test. Iodine or contrast allergy. Cramping afterward</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></section>"},{"id":"female","title":"Female Reproductive and Breast Cancers","part":"Part 7 · Ch 65","summary":"Breast, ovarian, uterine (endometrial), cervical: staging, treatment, tamoxifen and aromatase inhibitors, tell-them-apart table.","sections":[{"id":"female--breast-cancer-2nd-leading-cause-of-cancer-death-in-women","title":"Breast cancer · 2nd leading cause of cancer death in women"},{"id":"female--ovarian-cancer-most-deadly-gyn-cancer-silent","title":"Ovarian cancer · most deadly gyn cancer, \"silent\""},{"id":"female--uterine-endometrial-cancer-most-common-gyn-cancer-in-the-us","title":"Uterine (endometrial) cancer · most common gyn cancer in the US"},{"id":"female--cervical-cancer-almost-completely-preventable","title":"Cervical cancer · almost completely preventable"},{"id":"female--female-cancers-tell-them-apart","title":"Female cancers: tell them apart ★"},{"id":"female--more-from-your-first-study-guide","title":"More from your first study guide"},{"id":"female--chapter-65-powerpoint-word-for-word","title":"Chapter 65 PowerPoint, word for word"}],"html":"<section id=\"female--female-reproductive-and-breast-cancers\"><p>Breast · Ovarian · Uterine (endometrial) · Cervical. The &quot;tell them apart&quot; table is at the end.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"female--breast-cancer-2nd-leading-cause-of-cancer-death-in-women\"><h2>Breast cancer · 2nd leading cause of cancer death in women<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#female--breast-cancer-2nd-leading-cause-of-cancer-death-in-women\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><aside class=\"callout matters\"><div class=\"chead\">Why it matters and risk factors</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>The 2nd most common cause of cancer death in females. About 2.3 million new cases worldwide (2022). In the US, about 321,910 invasive and 60,730 in situ cases. About 2,670 cases in men.</li>\n<li><strong>THE single greatest risk factor = AGE.</strong> People aged 80–85 have 15× the risk of those aged 30–35. Black women &lt;50 have a higher risk. Black women have higher breast cancer mortality.</li>\n<li><strong>More lifetime ovulatory cycles:</strong> early menarche and late menopause raise risk 30–50%.</li>\n<li><strong>No pregnancy or live birth before 30</strong> nearly doubles the risk. Only a live birth lowers it.</li>\n<li><strong>BRCA1 and BRCA2</strong> cause 5–10% of breast cancers. Carriers have a 50–85% lifetime breast risk and a 45% ovarian risk by age 70. 70–80% of breast cancers occur by chance.</li>\n<li>Proliferative benign disease (hyperplasia) raises risk up to 70%. Other risks: excessive alcohol, inactivity, obesity, exogenous hormones.</li>\n<li><strong>BRCA testing</strong> is only for high-risk patients: breast cancer before 50, triple-negative cancer, ovarian cancer, bilateral disease, a relative diagnosed before 45.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout patho\"><div class=\"chead\">Patho and in-situ lesions</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Cells grow and divide abnormally and rapidly, invade nearby tissue, and spread through blood and lymph. Staged by size, nodes, and metastasis. The 2018 AJCC system also uses grade, hormone receptor (ER/PR) status, HER2 status, and Oncotype DX.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>DCIS</strong> (ductal carcinoma in situ) is <strong>stage 0</strong>, confined to the lining of the milk ducts. It <strong>requires SURGERY</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>LCIS</strong> (lobular carcinoma in situ) is in the milk-producing lobules. It is <strong>no longer considered cancer</strong> and has <strong>NO treatment</strong>. The focus is risk reduction and surveillance (mammogram).</li>\n<li>Both DCIS and LCIS increase the risk of later invasive cancer.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<h3>Breast cancer staging</h3>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Stage</th>\n<th>Tumor / nodes / mets</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>In situ (DCIS). No nodes, no mets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IA</td>\n<td>Tumor up to 2 cm. No nodes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IB</td>\n<td>No tumor or &lt;2 cm, with node involvement &lt;2 mm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IIA</td>\n<td>No tumor with nodes &gt;2 mm, OR &lt;2 cm with nodes, OR 2–5 cm with no nodes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IIB</td>\n<td>2–5 cm with nodes, OR &gt;5 cm with no nodes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IIIA</td>\n<td>Any size with 4–8 nodes, or &gt;5 cm with nodes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IIIB</td>\n<td>Spread to the chest wall, 9 nodes, or inflammatory breast cancer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IIIC</td>\n<td>Chest wall with ≥10 nodes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IV</td>\n<td>Any size, nodes, AND metastasis</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-053-020.jpg\" alt=\"Four stages of breast cancer. I: ≤2 cm, no node spread · II: larger, may reach nearby nodes · III: may involve nodes, chest wall, or skin · IV: any size with distant metastasis\"><figcaption>Four stages of breast cancer. I: ≤2 cm, no node spread · II: larger, may reach nearby nodes · III: may involve nodes, chest wall, or skin · IV: any size with distant metastasis</figcaption></figure></p>\n\n<aside class=\"callout ss\"><div class=\"chead\">Clinical manifestations ★</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>New mass or lump:</strong> cancerous masses are typically <strong>HARD, IRREGULAR, PAINLESS</strong> and often immovable, but they may be round, soft, and tender.</li>\n<li>Change in breast shape or swelling</li>\n<li><strong>Skin changes:</strong> peeling, flaking, pitting, <strong>DIMPLING</strong>, redness</li>\n<li><strong>Nipple changes:</strong> inversion, thickening, drainage</li>\n<li>Axillary node enlargement. <strong>Mets go to bone, lung, nodes, liver, and brain.</strong> (Class: &quot;soft tissue such as abdomen and lung are most impacted.&quot;)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout dx\"><div class=\"chead\">Diagnosis, and why</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Mammography</strong> (screening). Mammogram plus MRI for high-risk patients.</li>\n<li><strong>Ultrasound</strong> further defines an area seen on mammogram.</li>\n<li><strong>BIOPSY = DEFINITIVE:</strong> FNA, core needle, vacuum-assisted, or surgical excision (a wire marks a nonpalpable mass).</li>\n<li><strong>Sentinel node biopsy</strong> (blue dye or radioactive tracer) finds the first node cancer would reach, which determines stage. If it&#39;s negative, the full axillary dissection (20–40 nodes) can be skipped.</li>\n<li>Labs: CBC, platelets, LFTs, chest x-ray. Bone and liver scans if skeletal symptoms or abnormal LFTs. CT of the abdomen plus bone scan for stage II or higher.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout tx\"><div class=\"chead\">Treatment depends on stage</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Surgery:</strong> <strong>lumpectomy</strong> (breast conservation, removes only the involved tissue plus a margin) or <strong>mastectomy</strong> (modified radical removes the whole breast plus axillary nodes). For early disease the two are equivalent, so the patient chooses.</li>\n<li><strong>Lumpectomy is ALWAYS followed by radiation.</strong> Order: surgery → chemo → THEN radiation (case study).</li>\n<li><strong>Chemotherapy:</strong> for tumors ≥1 cm or positive nodes, and offered to all premenopausal patients. <strong>Neoadjuvant</strong> chemo is first line for intermediate and advanced operable cancer and makes breast conservation more likely.</li>\n<li><strong>Radiation:</strong> after lumpectomy, after mastectomy if ≥4 nodes are positive, for recurrence, for palliation.</li>\n<li><strong>Targeted therapy:</strong> <strong>HER2+ → trastuzumab (Herceptin).</strong> Also used for hormone-receptor+, BRCA, and triple-negative tumors.</li>\n<li><strong>Immunotherapy:</strong> pembrolizumab with chemo before surgery for high-risk triple-negative cancer.</li>\n<li><strong>Endocrine (hormone) therapy</strong> for ER/PR+ tumors, given 5–10 years after chemo: <strong>tamoxifen or aromatase inhibitors.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Locally advanced or inoperable:</strong> neoadjuvant chemo, then modified radical mastectomy, then chemo plus radiation. No immediate reconstruction.</li>\n<li><strong>Metastatic:</strong> focus on quality and quantity of life; weigh benefit against toxicity.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<p><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-054-021.jpg\" alt=\"Lumpectomy: only the involved tissue is removed\"><figcaption>Lumpectomy: only the involved tissue is removed</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-054-022.jpg\" alt=\"Modified radical mastectomy: all breast tissue plus axillary nodes are removed\"><figcaption>Modified radical mastectomy: all breast tissue plus axillary nodes are removed</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-054-023.jpg\" alt=\"Chest after mastectomy\"><figcaption>Chest after mastectomy</figcaption></figure></p>\n\n<aside class=\"callout watch\"><div class=\"chead\">Complications and treatment side effects</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Immediately post-op:</strong> infection, wound dehiscence, PE, pneumonia (immobility). <strong>Bloody drainage SATURATING the dressing is the most concerning wound finding.</strong> Serosanguineous drainage is expected.</li>\n<li><strong>Long term:</strong> pain, scarring, <strong>LYMPHEDEMA</strong> (arm swelling after axillary nodes are removed), and <strong>shoulder immobility (the most common long-term effect; &quot;frozen shoulder&quot;)</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Chemo:</strong> hair loss, N/V, mouth pain, diarrhea or constipation, peripheral neuropathy, rash, nail changes, ↓WBC (fever and infection), premature ovarian failure (bone loss, infertility), later MDS or leukemia.</li>\n<li><strong>Immunotherapy:</strong> skin rashes, flu-like symptoms, thyroid problems, diarrhea, autoimmune organ damage.</li>\n<li><strong>Endocrine therapy:</strong> hot flashes, vaginal dryness, ↑osteoporosis, rarely endometrial cancer.</li>\n<li><strong>Radiation:</strong> redness, patchy desquamation, itching, irritation of the skin (the key thing to monitor). Breast swelling for 6–18 months. <strong>Pneumonitis</strong> (nonproductive cough with an infiltrate, 6 weeks to 6 months later). Late cardiac risk.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout watch\"><div class=\"chead\">Hormone drugs (Table 65.3) ★</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p><strong>Tamoxifen (SERM)</strong> blocks estrogen at its receptor. Taken for 5–10 years.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Common:</strong> hot flashes, night sweats, irregular bleeding or spotting, ↓libido, vaginal discharge or dryness.</li>\n<li><strong>Rare but serious:</strong> DVT and PE, uterine/endometrial cancer, stroke, cataracts, bone loss (premenopausal).</li>\n<li>⚠ <strong>Avoid SSRIs</strong> (they reduce tamoxifen metabolism). <strong>Monitor for endometrial hyperplasia.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Aromatase inhibitors (anastrozole, letrozole, exemestane)</strong> block the conversion of androgens to estrogen. Taken for 5–10 years. ⚠ <strong>Osteoporosis and fractures.</strong></p>\n<p>⚠ <strong>Question an order for ORAL CONTRACEPTIVES in a patient with ER+ breast cancer</strong> (case study).</p>\n<p><strong>Trastuzumab:</strong> fatigue, joint and muscle pain, diarrhea, infusion reactions, rash, fever. <strong>Pembrolizumab:</strong> fatigue, muscle pain, itching, rash, diarrhea.</p>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout nursing\"><div class=\"chead\">Nursing, and why</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Dx:</strong> pain · risk for infection · altered nutrition · body image disturbance</li>\n<li><strong>Vital signs:</strong> ↑BP and HR suggest pain or anxiety. ↑Temperature and HR suggest infection.</li>\n<li>Pain scale. Daily weight (nutrition deficit). I&amp;O (tolerance, vomiting).</li>\n<li><strong>Wound:</strong> redness, warmth, purulent drainage. Radiated skin checks.</li>\n<li>CBC and metabolic panel: chemo lowers WBC, Hgb, and Hct and raises LFTs.</li>\n<li>Mood and nonverbal cues: high risk of depression.</li>\n<li>Give chemo, targeted therapy, pain meds, and antiemetics. <strong>Cough and deep breathe post-op</strong> <em>→ why: prevents atelectasis and pneumonia.</em> Keep the wound clean and dry. Encourage verbalization.</li>\n<li><strong>Teaching:</strong> treatment course · medication and radiation side effects · wound care (report infection or dehiscence) · nutrition · support groups. <strong>Mammography screening:</strong> 40–44 optional annual · 45–54 annual · ≥55 annual or every 2 years while healthy. <strong>Breast self-exam is NOT recommended</strong> (per Ch 65 and the slides). Lifelong follow-up mammograms.</li>\n<li><strong>Outcomes:</strong> N/V controlled, weight maintained, wound healed, minimal scarring and lymphedema, anxiety and depression managed.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"female--ovarian-cancer-most-deadly-gyn-cancer-silent\"><h2>Ovarian cancer · most deadly gyn cancer, &quot;silent&quot;<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#female--ovarian-cancer-most-deadly-gyn-cancer-silent\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><aside class=\"callout matters\"><div class=\"chead\">Why it matters</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>5th most common cancer in females (5th cause of cancer death per the slides). <strong>70–75% are not found until stage III or IV</strong>, which makes it the most deadly gynecologic cancer. Stage I has &gt;73% 5-year survival; stage III is 21% and stage IV is &lt;5%. Uncommon before 40, and most cases occur in the 70s. <strong>Advanced age is the most common risk.</strong></p>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout patho\"><div class=\"chead\">Risk vs protection ★</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>PROTECTIVE:</strong> breastfeeding, pregnancy, oral contraceptives, progesterone-only HRT.</li>\n<li><strong>RISK:</strong> long-term estrogen or estrogen/progesterone HRT, advanced age, family history and BRCA1/2, HNPCC (Lynch), obesity and PCOS (androgens), possibly infertility and ovulation-stimulating drugs.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<p><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-056-024.jpg\" alt=\"Ovarian cancer: cancer tissue on the left ovary\"><figcaption>Ovarian cancer: cancer tissue on the left ovary</figcaption></figure></p>\n\n<aside class=\"callout ss\"><div class=\"chead\">Clinical manifestations (vague) ★</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Early (often dismissed as nothing):</strong> bloating · pelvic or abdominal pain · early satiety or trouble eating · urinary urgency or frequency. Nonspecific, not noticed until the disease has progressed.</li>\n<li><strong>Late: ASCITES</strong> (fluid in the abdomen), so <strong>measure abdominal girth.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Priority call-back:</strong> &quot;I have trouble eating because I feel full and bloated.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout dx\"><div class=\"chead\">Diagnosis</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>NO recommended screening test</strong> for the general population.</li>\n<li><strong>CA-125</strong> (blood marker) rises in ovarian cancer, BUT is normal in 50% of early cases and also rises with diverticulitis, endometriosis, cirrhosis, and fibroids.</li>\n<li>Pelvic ultrasound or CT to see the ovaries. CA-125 plus ultrasound is used to screen only high-risk patients (BRCA, HNPCC).</li>\n<li>⚠ <strong>Biopsy of the mass is NOT recommended</strong> <em>→ why: it could spread the cancer.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Definitive diagnosis = surgical removal of the mass plus pathology.</strong> FIGO staging is based on surgical findings: <strong>I = ovaries · II = pelvis · III = peritoneum and/or nodes · IV = distant.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout tx\"><div class=\"chead\">Treatment</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Surgical staging and DEBULKING</strong> (remove as much tumor as possible) plus peritoneal washings, biopsies, omentectomy, lymphadenectomy, and usually hysterectomy with both ovaries and tubes. Fertility-sparing surgery only in rare early cases.</li>\n<li><strong>Chemotherapy AFTER debulking</strong> is the standard of care. Given IV, oral, or intraperitoneal. Neoadjuvant chemo is used in select patients.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout nursing\"><div class=\"chead\">Nursing</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Dx:</strong> ineffective coping · acute pain (surgery) · altered nutrition</li>\n<li><strong>Assess:</strong> symptoms, vital signs, <strong>abdominal girth (ascites)</strong>, weight (loss = nutrition; gain = fluid), CA-125 and imaging, chemo side effects (N/V/D, constipation, hair loss, ↓WBC and RBC), anxiety and depression.</li>\n<li><strong>Actions:</strong> pain meds, chemo, antiemetics (for nutrition). <strong>Teach ALL female patients the symptoms.</strong> Ease chemo side effects. Support groups and counselors.</li>\n<li><strong>Outcomes:</strong> nausea controlled, weight maintained, body image addressed, end-of-life issues addressed.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"female--uterine-endometrial-cancer-most-common-gyn-cancer-in-the-us\"><h2>Uterine (endometrial) cancer · most common gyn cancer in the US<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#female--uterine-endometrial-cancer-most-common-gyn-cancer-in-the-us\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><aside class=\"callout matters\"><div class=\"chead\">Why it matters and risk</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>The <strong>most common gynecologic cancer in the US</strong>, with a 2.6% lifetime risk. Postmenopausal, ages 55–65 (median 60). Rare under 45. 2–5% are hereditary (HNPCC). More common and more fatal in Black women.</li>\n<li><strong>#1 risk factors: UNOPPOSED ESTROGEN and OBESITY.</strong> Unopposed estrogen means estrogen therapy without progesterone, tamoxifen, obesity, cirrhosis, estrogen-producing tumors, or anovulation. (Ch 13 adds early menarche, late menopause, PCOS, infertility, and diabetes.)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout patho\"><div class=\"chead\">Patho</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Usually arises in the endometrium. ~80% are <strong>endometrioid adenocarcinoma (type I):</strong> estrogen-driven, usually early, 88% survival. <strong>Type II (serous or clear cell)</strong> is aggressive and occurs in older patients with a poor prognosis. <strong>Sarcoma (muscle)</strong> is 3–5%. Metastatic or recurrent disease is incurable (about 1-year median survival).</p>\n</div></aside>\n<p><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-057-025.jpg\" alt=\"Uterine cancer: endometrioid adenocarcinoma (lining) vs uterine sarcoma (muscle)\"><figcaption>Uterine cancer: endometrioid adenocarcinoma (lining) vs uterine sarcoma (muscle)</figcaption></figure></p>\n\n<aside class=\"callout ss\"><div class=\"chead\">Clinical manifestations ★</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>ABNORMAL UTERINE / VAGINAL BLEEDING is the MOST COMMON symptom.</strong></li>\n<li>Any postmenopausal bleeding needs evaluation. Premenopausal irregular or heavy bleeding with risk factors, or that doesn&#39;t respond to treatment.</li>\n<li>Ch 13 also lists lower abdominal pain and cramping.</li>\n<li><strong>Most concerning call from a 60-year-old: &quot;I have vaginal bleeding.&quot;</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout dx\"><div class=\"chead\">Diagnosis</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Endometrial biopsy:</strong> 99% sensitive for high-grade cancer.</li>\n<li><strong>Pelvic (transvaginal) ultrasound:</strong> shows a thickened endometrium.</li>\n<li><strong>D&amp;C</strong> (scrape the lining) if the biopsy is negative but bleeding continues.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout tx\"><div class=\"chead\">Treatment</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Surgery:</strong> remove the uterus, fallopian tubes, and ovaries, plus washings and lymphadenectomy. Early stage I is often cured by surgery alone.</li>\n<li><strong>Progestin therapy</strong> for stage I if surgery isn&#39;t possible.</li>\n<li><strong>Radiation</strong> is the usual adjuvant. Chemo for high-risk or higher-stage disease, with only a modest response.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout nursing\"><div class=\"chead\">Nursing</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Dx:</strong> acute pain (surgery) · nausea (chemo) · ineffective coping</li>\n<li><strong>Assess:</strong> vital signs, daily weight, I&amp;O, pain, abnormal bleeding, age 55–65.</li>\n<li><strong>Actions:</strong> pain meds, chemo, comfort (antiemetics, salt-water rinses for stomatitis).</li>\n<li><strong>Teach prevention:</strong> healthy weight and exercise. <strong>Anyone on estrogen who has a uterus needs progesterone too.</strong> Tamoxifen users need monitoring for endometrial hyperplasia. For stomatitis, avoid hot or spicy food.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"female--cervical-cancer-almost-completely-preventable\"><h2>Cervical cancer · almost completely preventable<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#female--cervical-cancer-almost-completely-preventable\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><aside class=\"callout matters\"><div class=\"chead\">Why it matters and risk</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>Rates have dropped since the Pap smear (mid-20th century) and HPV screening. Most often diagnosed at ages <strong>35–44</strong> (average 50). Rare under 20. More common in rural areas.</li>\n<li>90% of new cases and deaths are in low- and middle-income countries. It is the 4th most common cause of cancer death in females worldwide.</li>\n<li><strong>HPV (high-risk subtypes) is THE CAUSE.</strong> Nearly 100% of cervical cancers test positive. HPV risks: early sexual activity, multiple partners, low socioeconomic status, immunosuppression.</li>\n<li>Contributors: HSV-2, smoking, douching, poor nutrition, long-term oral contraceptive use.</li>\n<li><strong>HPV vaccine at age 11–12 (can start at 9).</strong> It does not treat an existing infection.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout patho\"><div class=\"chead\">Patho</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>A slow-developing disease: cervical dysplasia (low grade, then high grade) becomes invasive only if untreated. Catching dysplasia early prevents cancer.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>90% are squamous cell carcinomas</strong>, arising in the <strong>transformation zone</strong> where the endocervix meets the ectocervix.</li>\n<li>Most of the rest are adenocarcinomas from the glandular cells of the endocervix.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout ss\"><div class=\"chead\">Clinical manifestations ★</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Dysplasia has NO symptoms</strong> and usually can&#39;t be seen.</li>\n<li><strong>Vaginal bleeding is the most common symptom</strong> of invasive cancer. <strong>Postcoital bleeding</strong> (especially).</li>\n<li>Ch 13 adds vaginal discomfort, malodorous discharge, and pain with intercourse.</li>\n<li>Women who are not sexually active may have no symptoms until very advanced.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout dx\"><div class=\"chead\">Screening and diagnosis ★ (ACOG / ASCCP / SGO)</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>No screening before age 21. First Pap is at age 21.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Ages 21–29:</strong> Pap every 3 years. HPV testing is not a screen in this age group, only a reflex test if atypical cells are found. Primary HPV screening may start at 25 and repeat every 5 years. (ACS prefers starting at 25 with primary HPV.)</li>\n<li><strong>Ages 30–65:</strong> primary HPV or Pap plus HPV cotest every 5 years, OR Pap alone every 3 years.</li>\n<li>HIV, immunosuppression, DES exposure, or prior dysplasia may need more frequent screening.</li>\n<li>Hysterectomy for a benign cause with no CIN 2 history: stop. Over 65 with normal results: may stop.</li>\n<li><strong>Abnormal result plus high-risk HPV leads to colposcopy with biopsy</strong> (acetic acid or Lugol&#39;s). <strong>Biopsy is diagnostic.</strong></li>\n<li>Before treatment: MRI (best for tumor volume), PET (distant mets), CT, chest x-ray, IV pyelogram. FIGO staging I–IV.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<p><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-058-026.jpg\" alt=\"Colposcopy: the scope looks through a speculum at the cervix, which has been swabbed with acetic acid, and abnormal areas are biopsied\"><figcaption>Colposcopy: the scope looks through a speculum at the cervix, which has been swabbed with acetic acid, and abnormal areas are biopsied</figcaption></figure></p>\n\n<aside class=\"callout tx\"><div class=\"chead\">Treatment depends on stage</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Early:</strong> cone biopsy, or <strong>radical trachelectomy</strong> (removes the cervix only) for a patient who wants to keep her fertility. If fertility isn&#39;t desired: simple or radical hysterectomy with lymph node dissection.</li>\n<li><strong>Chemoradiation for higher stages:</strong> chemo makes the cells more sensitive to radiation. Radiation is usually <strong>brachytherapy</strong> (internal, placed in or next to the tumor) ± external pelvic beam.</li>\n<li><strong>Recurrence confined to the pelvis: pelvic exenteration</strong> (all pelvic organs removed) can be curative. Mortality about 5% and morbidity about 70%, leaving a colostomy and urinary diversion. Already radiated? Then chemo is the only option.</li>\n<li><strong>5-year survival:</strong> localized 92%, regional 58%, distant 17%.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout nursing\"><div class=\"chead\">Nursing</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Dx:</strong> ineffective health maintenance (no Pap screening) · ineffective coping · acute pain · nausea (chemoradiation)</li>\n<li><strong>Focus = PREVENTION</strong> (screening plus HPV vaccine) because there are no symptoms until it&#39;s advanced.</li>\n<li><strong>Assess:</strong> vital signs, Pap and HPV results, I&amp;O, daily weight, pain.</li>\n<li><strong>Actions:</strong> refer for colposcopy and biopsy, give meds and antiemetics, support and resources.</li>\n<li><strong>Teach:</strong> routine screening, HPV vaccination for eligible patients, support groups. <strong>Transgender men still need breast, Pap, and pelvic screening.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"female--female-cancers-tell-them-apart\"><h2>Female cancers: tell them apart ★<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#female--female-cancers-tell-them-apart\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Breast</th>\n<th>Ovarian</th>\n<th>Uterine / endometrial</th>\n<th>Cervical</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Typical age</strong></td>\n<td>Risk rises with age (Black women &lt;50 at higher risk)</td>\n<td>70s (rare &lt;40)</td>\n<td>55–65, postmenopausal</td>\n<td>35–44</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Biggest risk</strong></td>\n<td>Age. More ovulatory cycles, BRCA, no live birth before 30</td>\n<td>BRCA, family history, HNPCC, HRT, age</td>\n<td>Unopposed estrogen and OBESITY (tamoxifen too)</td>\n<td>HPV</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Protective</strong></td>\n<td>Live birth</td>\n<td>Pregnancy, breastfeeding, OCPs</td>\n<td>Progesterone with estrogen, healthy weight</td>\n<td>HPV vaccine, screening</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Key symptom</strong></td>\n<td>Hard, irregular, painless, fixed lump, dimpling, nipple inversion or discharge</td>\n<td>Vague: bloating, early satiety, pelvic pain, urinary frequency, late ascites</td>\n<td>Abnormal or postmenopausal bleeding</td>\n<td>Often none. Vaginal and postcoital bleeding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Screening</strong></td>\n<td>Mammogram (BSE not recommended)</td>\n<td>NONE (CA-125 plus ultrasound only if high risk)</td>\n<td>None. Counsel at menopause to report bleeding</td>\n<td>Pap ± HPV from 21</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Definitive dx</strong></td>\n<td>Biopsy. Sentinel node for staging</td>\n<td>Surgical removal plus pathology (no biopsy)</td>\n<td>Endometrial biopsy, ultrasound, D&amp;C</td>\n<td>Colposcopy plus biopsy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Main treatment</strong></td>\n<td>Lumpectomy plus radiation, or mastectomy. Chemo, targeted therapy, hormones</td>\n<td>Debulking, then chemo</td>\n<td>Remove the uterus, tubes, and ovaries, then radiation ± chemo</td>\n<td>Trachelectomy or hysterectomy, chemoradiation (brachytherapy)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Signature watch</strong></td>\n<td>Lymphedema, shoulder immobility, radiation skin</td>\n<td>Ascites (girth), chemo toxicity, end of life</td>\n<td>Bleeding, nutrition</td>\n<td>Prevention, nausea</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></section>\n<section id=\"female--more-from-your-first-study-guide\"><h2>More from your first study guide<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#female--more-from-your-first-study-guide\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><h3>Table 65.3 · medications for breast cancer and female reproductive disorders ★ (exam: medications)</h3>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Class</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n<th>Key nursing points</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>SERM</strong> (endocrine)</td>\n<td><strong>Tamoxifen (Nolvadex)</strong></td>\n<td>Blocks estrogen receptor; 5–10 yrs. Common: hot flashes, night sweats, irregular bleeding, vaginal dryness, ↓libido. Rare: DVT/PE, stroke, uterine cancer, cataracts. <strong>Avoid SSRIs</strong> (reduce tamoxifen metabolism)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Aromatase inhibitor</strong></td>\n<td>Anastrozole, exemestane, letrozole</td>\n<td>Block androgen → estrogen conversion; 5–10 yrs. Osteoporosis, fractures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Chemotherapy</strong></td>\n<td>Doxorubicin, paclitaxel, docetaxel, 5-FU, cyclophosphamide, carboplatin</td>\n<td>Hair loss, N/V, mouth sores, ↓WBC (infection), neuropathy, premature ovarian failure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Immunotherapy</strong></td>\n<td>Pembrolizumab (Keytruda)</td>\n<td>Given with chemo before surgery. Fatigue, rash, diarrhea, thyroid problems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Targeted</strong></td>\n<td>Trastuzumab (Herceptin)</td>\n<td>For HER2+. Fatigue, joint pain, diarrhea, fever; <strong>cardiotoxicity</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Combined OCPs</strong></td>\n<td>Sprintec, Yaz, patch, NuvaRing</td>\n<td>↑DVT risk. <strong>Avoid with estrogen-positive breast cancer</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Progestin injection</strong></td>\n<td>Depo-Provera</td>\n<td>IM every 3 months; weight gain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Levonorgestrel IUD</strong></td>\n<td>Mirena, Kyleena</td>\n<td>Bleeding changes; rare perforation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>GnRH agonist</strong></td>\n<td>Leuprolide (Lupron)</td>\n<td>Hot flashes, ↓bone density</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Progestins / estrogens</strong></td>\n<td>Provera / Premarin</td>\n<td><strong>Estrogen without progestin (intact uterus) ↑ endometrial cancer</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p><strong>Case: Karen Sims (estrogen-positive).</strong> Which order to verify? <strong>B. Oral contraceptives</strong> (estrogen could fuel the tumor).\nMale breast cancer meds: tamoxifen, aromatase inhibitors, fulvestrant only if receptor-positive; trastuzumab/pertuzumab for HER2+.</p>\n<h3>Breast cancer: treatment by stage</h3>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Stage</th>\n<th>Treatment plan</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>LCIS</strong></td>\n<td>No treatment; surveillance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>DCIS</strong></td>\n<td>Surgery (&gt;4 cm → mastectomy); antiestrogen ↓ recurrence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Early invasive</strong></td>\n<td>Excision → radiation or chemo; endocrine therapy (tamoxifen) if hormone +</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Intermediate/advanced (operable)</strong></td>\n<td>Neoadjuvant chemo → surgery → radiation; targeted; endocrine 5–10 yrs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Locally advanced/inoperable</strong></td>\n<td>Neoadjuvant chemo → modified radical mastectomy → post-op chemo + radiation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Recurrence/metastatic</strong></td>\n<td>Mastectomy, excision, radiation, chemo; weigh benefit vs toxicity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><ul>\n<li><strong>Skin changes</strong> include <strong>peau d&#39;orange</strong>. Classic cancerous lump: hard, irregular, painless, immovable (fixed).</li>\n<li><strong>Modified radical mastectomy</strong> = all breast tissue + axillary nodes; chest muscle stays. <strong>Lumpectomy</strong> = tumor + margin; always followed by radiation (chemo first if indicated).</li>\n<li><strong>Most common long-term complication = immobility of the shoulder (&quot;frozen shoulder&quot;)</strong> (purple note). Radiation effects: red, itchy, irritated skin; patchy desquamation; breast swelling; <strong>pneumonitis (dry cough) 6 weeks–6 months later.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Slide wording:</strong> <em>Genetic carriers have a 50–80% lifetime risk.</em> Breast cancer &quot;most commonly impacts soft tissue such as abdomen and lung&quot; (your notes).</li>\n<li><strong>Epidemiology:</strong> the single risk factor for breast cancer is a woman&#39;s age. 2.3 million new cases globally (2022); 321,910 invasive and 60,730 in-situ cases in the US; 2,670 in men.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Cervical screening guidelines (exam topic) ★</h3>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Age</th>\n<th>Screening recommendation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>&lt;21</strong></td>\n<td>No screening</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>21–29</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Pap every 3 years</strong> (HPV test only reflexively if atypical cells)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>25+ (if available)</strong></td>\n<td>Primary HPV screening every 5 years (ACS prefers starting at 25)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>30–65</strong></td>\n<td>Primary HPV, or Pap + HPV cotest every 5 years, or Pap alone every 3 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>&gt;65</strong></td>\n<td>May stop if adequate negative screening and no CIN 2 in 20 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hysterectomy (benign)</strong></td>\n<td>No more Paps if no history of CIN 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>High risk</strong></td>\n<td>HIV, immunosuppression, DES exposure, prior dysplasia → more often</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p><strong>Case:</strong> a 25-year-old, last Pap at 21, no primary HPV available. Today: <strong>breast exam, pelvic exam, Pap smear (no HPV cotest under 30).</strong>\nDiagnosis path: Pap → colposcopy + cervical biopsy (diagnostic) → MRI, PET, CT, CXR. Surgical: radical trachelectomy (early stage, wants fertility) · radical hysterectomy + node dissection (no fertility desire) · pelvic exenteration (recurrence). Medical: chemoradiation (brachytherapy = internal radiation into/next to tumor) · chemotherapy. <strong>HPV vaccine at 11–12, can start at 9.</strong></p>\n<h3>Four female cancers: key fact row</h3>\n<p><strong>Breast:</strong> 2nd cancer death in women · <strong>Ovarian:</strong> deadliest gyn · <strong>Uterine:</strong> most common gyn · <strong>Cervical:</strong> most preventable. Typical ages: breast ↑ with age · ovarian 70s · uterine 55–65 · cervical 35–44. Treatment summary: breast surgery ± chemo, radiation, endocrine · ovarian debulking → chemo · uterine hysterectomy ± radiation/chemo · cervical surgery or chemoradiation.</p>\n<h3>Ovarian and uterine extras</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ovarian:</strong> history of breastfeeding and OC use is protective; progesterone-only HRT. Risk with long-term estrogen/progesterone HRT, advanced age (mostly 70s), BRCA1/2, hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer. Protective = anything that stops ovulation. Surgical: debulking, removal of uterus, ovaries, and tubes.</li>\n<li><strong>Uterine:</strong> 2.6% lifetime risk in developed nations; 2–5% hereditary. Obesity: fat makes estrogen. <strong>Teach prevention:</strong> obesity and unopposed estrogen are the most common risk factors. Also early menarche/late menopause, PCOS, DM.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"female--chapter-65-powerpoint-word-for-word\"><h2>Chapter 65 PowerPoint, word for word<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#female--chapter-65-powerpoint-word-for-word\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><p>Your instructor&#39;s slides for this chapter, kept in slide order.</p>\n\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 1 · WHO gets breast cancer</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Learning Objectives: 1- Describe the epidemiology of reproductive disorders · 2- Correlating clinical manifestations to pathophysiological processes of: a. breast cancer b. ovarian cancer c. uterine cancer d. cervical cancer · 3. Describe the diagnostic results used to confirm the diagnosis of reproductive disorders · 4. Discuss the interprofessional management · 5. Develop a comprehensive plan of nursing care · 6. Design a teaching plan that includes pharmacological, dietary and lifestyle considerations for patients with reproductive disorders Epidemiology: Second most cause of cancer-related deaths in women · In 2022 – est – 2.3 million new cases diagnosed globally · In US statistics est 321,910 new invasive breast ca cases · Estimated 60,730 new in-situ cases · In men, there are 2,670 est new cases · The single risk factor for breast cancer is woman&#39;s age · Black population, younger than 50 has higher risk of developing breast cancer Risk Factors: Higher number of ovulatory cycles in a lifetime · Early menarche and late menopause – 30 to 50% · Lack of pregnancy and live childbirth – 2x the risk · Family Hx with known genetic mutations (BRCA1, BRCA2) Breast Cancer gene – 5 to 10% · Genetic carriers have a 50–80% lifetime risk · Other factors: excessive alcohol use, inactivity, obesity and the use of hormones</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 2 · How it grows and is staged</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Pathophysiology: Cells grow and divide abnormally and rapidly · Can invade nearby tissues and spread to other areas through the bloodstream and lymphatic system · Can be categorized or staged according to size, spread to lymph nodes and distant spread to other organs or tissues Breast Cancer Staging: Stage 0 – in situ; no lymph node; no metastasis · Stage IA – tumor size 2 cm; no lymph node; no metastasis · Stage IB – no tumor or tumor size &lt; 2 cm; +lymph node is &lt; 2 mm; no metastasis · Stage IIA – no tumor; or tumor is &lt; 2 cm; or tumor is 2–5 cm; +lymph node &gt; 2 mm; no metastasis · Stage IIB – tumor is 2–5 cm; +lymph node; no metastasis · Stage IIIA – tumor is &gt; 5 cm or tumor of any size; no lymph node for 5 cm but has +4–8 lymph nodes for tumor of any size; no metastasis · Stage IIIB – tumor is &gt; 5 cm or tumor of any size that spread to chest wall; +9 lymph nodes; no metastasis · Stage IIIC – tumor of any size and spread to the chest wall; +10 or more lymph nodes; no metastasis · Stage IV – tumor of any size and has lymph nodes and has metastasized</p>\n<p>[Image: Four Stages of Breast Cancer] Four breast drawings. Stage I: small tumor (≤ 2 cm), no node spread.</p>\n<p>Stage II: larger tumor, may reach the axillary nodes. Stage III: large tumor spreading to more nodes, chest wall, or skin. Stage IV: any size, spread to distant organs.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 3 · Signs and how it's diagnosed</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Clinical Manifestations: New mass or lump – cancerous masses are typically hard, irregular and painless, maybe round, soft and tender · Changes in breast shape or swelling · Skin changes – peeling, flaking, pitting, dimpling or redness · Nipple change – inversion, thickening of the tissue or drainage Medical and Surgical Management – Diagnosis: Mammography · Ultrasonography · Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) · Mammography with MRI – high risk · Biopsy – definitive results for Dx · Sentinel node biopsy – determine the stage · Labs – CBC, platelets, LFTs, CXR · Bone scan &amp; liver scan – skeletal symptoms · CT abdomen &amp; bone scan – for stage II or higher</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 4 · Surgery, therapies &amp; treatment by stage</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>[Image: Breast Cancer Surgery] (A) Lumpectomy: only the tumor and a margin of surrounding tissue are removed; the breast stays. (B) Modified radical mastectomy: all breast tissue is removed along with the axillary lymph nodes; the chest muscle stays.</p>\n<p>[Image: Mastectomy] Photo of a healed chest after bilateral mastectomy, with flat horizontal scars where the breasts were. Treatment – depends on the stage of the cancer: Surgery (lumpectomy or mastectomy) – Neoadjuvant – before · Chemotherapy – cytotoxic drugs · Radiation – high energy x-rays or particles · Targeted therapy – target genes, proteins or tissue · Immunotherapy – stimulates own body&#39;s immune system · Endocrine therapy – blocks the hormones that stimulate cancer growth · Adjuvant therapy Treatment: Lobular carcinoma in situ (LCIS) – no treatment · Ductal carcinoma in situ (DCIS) – requires surgery · Early stage invasive ca – surgical excision f/b radiation or chemotherapy; endocrine therapy – tamoxifen · Intermediate stage and advanced stage (operable) – first line of treatment – neoadjuvant chemo f/b radiation; targeted therapy; endocrine therapy – 5 to 10 years · Locally advanced and inoperable ca – neoadjuvant chemo then modified radical mastectomy – post-op chemo &amp; radiation · Local recurrence and systemic metastases – complete mastectomy for those who have undergone conservation therapy, surgical excision, radiation, chemotherapy, or therapeutic benefit has to be weighed</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 5 · After surgery &amp; treatment</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Complications – Immediate post op: Infection · Wound dehiscence · Pulmonary embolism · Pneumonia · Pain · Scarring · Lymphedema – removal of axillary node · Shoulder immobility – most common LT effect</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 6 · Drugs for female reproductive disorders</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Table 65.3 – Medications: Selective estrogen receptor modulator = endo therapy · Aromatase inhibitor · Chemotherapy · Immunotherapy · Targeted therapy · Combined oral contraceptive pills (OCPs) · Progesterone contraceptive injectable – Progestin · Levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine devices · Gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) · Progestins · Estrogens</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 7 · Assess, act, teach, evaluate</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Assessment and Analysis – Initial manifestations: Presence of a tumor – lump in the breast · Skin and nipple changes. Later manifestations: Chemotherapy side effects · Lymph node removal – lymphedema · Immunotherapy – skin rashes, flu-like symptoms, diarrhea · Endocrine therapy – hot flashes, vaginal dryness · Radiation – skin irritation, redness, itchiness, burning · Breast swelling and non-productive cough Nursing Diagnosis: Pain/discomfort · Risk for infection · Nutrition, altered: less than body requirements · Body image disturbance Assessments: Vital signs – elevated BP &amp; HR, T? · Pain · Daily weight – nutritional deficit? · Intake and output · Wound evaluation – early signs of infection? · Monitor laboratory data – CBC &amp; metabolic profile · Assess mood and nonverbal cues – great risk for psychological issues – depression Actions: Give chemotherapeutic drugs &amp; targeted therapy as ordered · Give pain meds · Give antiemetics · Cough and deep breathe post-op · Wound care · Encourage verbalization Teaching: Explain treatment course · Medications and treatment side effects · Wound care · Nutritional counseling · Support groups · Breast cancer screening with mammography · Self-breast examination is not recommended Evaluating care outcomes: Manage nausea and vomiting · Maintain nutrition and weight · Surgical site healing · Minimum scarring and lymphedema · Managed anxiety and depression</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 8 · The silent, deadliest gyn cancer</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Epidemiology: 5th cancer cause of death in women · 70%–75% not discovered until late stages III &amp; IV · Hx of breastfeeding, oral contraceptive use has protective effect · Use of progesterone alone as hormone replacement Risk Factors: Protective effect: breastfeeding, pregnancy, oral contraceptives · Hormonal replacement therapy – LT use of progesterone/estrogen increases the risk · Advanced age – mostly in 70&#39;s · Family Hx – BRCA1 or BRCA2 gene mutations · Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer Pathophysiology: Cells divide abnormally and uncontrollably</p>\n<p>[Image: Ovarian Cancer] Uterus with fallopian tubes: the right ovary is normal; the left ovary is enlarged and filled with cancerous tissue. Clinical Manifestations – Early stage: Bloating · Pelvic or abdominal pain · Early satiety or problems eating · Urinary urgency or frequency. Later stage: Ascites, excessive fluid in the abdominal cavity Medical Management – No recommended screening test for Ovarian Ca. If suspected: Diagnosis · Biochemical marker CA 125 – blood test – CA 125 levels are elevated (50% in early cases are normal) · Pelvic ultrasonography – visualizes ovaries (biopsy not recommended at this time) · CT scan · Surgical removal of mass and biopsy – definitive Dx Medications: Chemotherapy follows surgical debulking – standard of care Surgical Management: Surgical debulking · Removal of uterus, ovaries, and fallopian tubes</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 9 · Assess, act, teach, evaluate</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Assessment and analysis: Clinical signs and symptoms are nonspecific and ovarian cancer is usually not found until the disease is advanced Nursing diagnoses: Ineffective coping · Acute pain · Altered nutrition, less than body requirements Assessments: Signs/symptoms · Vital signs · Abdominal girth – later stage – ascites · Weight · Diagnostic tests · Chemotherapy side effects Actions: Give pain medications as ordered · Give chemotherapeutic agents as ordered · Give antiemetics as ordered Teaching: Signs/symptoms of ovarian cancer · Methods to ease chemotherapy side effects · Support groups or counselors Evaluating care outcomes: Nausea under control · Adequate nutrition to maintain weight · Body image addressed · End of life issues addressed</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 10 · The most common gyn cancer</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Epidemiology: 2.6% lifetime risk for women in developed nations · Uterine ca or endometrial ca is the most common gyne ca in the US · Occurs in postmenopausal women – ages 55–65 · 2–5% are hereditary gene Risk Factors: Unopposed estrogen exposure – result of estrogen therapy in the absence of progesterone · Obesity Pathophysiology: Cells of the body grow and divide abnormally and uncontrollably · Cells can invade nearby tissues and spread to other areas through blood and lymph</p>\n<p>[Image: Uterine Cancer] Uterus cross-section showing two types: endometrioid adenocarcinoma growing from the inner lining (endometrium) and uterine sarcoma growing in the muscular wall (myometrium).</p>\n<p>Clinical Manifestations: Vaginal bleeding – most common symptom Medical Management – Diagnosis: Biopsy · Pelvic ultrasound · Surgical dilation and curettage, scraping the lining of the uterus for tissue sample Medical Management – Treatment: Surgical removal of uterus, fallopian tubes, and ovaries · Radiation · Chemotherapy</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 11 · Assess, act, teach, evaluate</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Assessment and analysis: Identification of at-risk patient (age 55–65) and diagnosis imperative to further assessment · Pelvic ultrasound · Endometrial biopsy · Presentation – abnormal uterine bleeding Nursing diagnoses: Acute pain associated to surgical procedure · Nausea associated with chemotherapy · Ineffective coping associated with the cancer diagnosis Assessments: Vital signs · Daily weight · Intake and output · Pain · Abnormal uterine bleeding – most common symptom · Patient age – 55–65 years of age Actions: Administer pain medications as ordered · Administer chemotherapy as ordered · Provide comfort measures Teaching: Educate about disease prevention – obesity &amp; unopposed estrogen are the most common risk factors · Discuss chemotherapy comfort measures · Provide psychological support resources Evaluating care outcomes: Maintain adequate weight · Achieve most daily functions</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 12 · The preventable gyn cancer</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Epidemiology: Cervical cancer in women in US has declined since the introduction of the Pap smear in mid 20th century · Introduction of HPV screening in recent years · Common age diagnosed 35–44 years · Low to middle income countries account for new diagnoses and deaths – 90% · 4th most common death in females worldwide Risk Factors: Human papillomavirus (HPV) – cause · Herpes simplex virus type 2 · Smoking · Douching · Low socio-economic status · Poor nutrition · Immunosuppression · Early sexual activity · Multiple partners · Oral contraceptive use Pathophysiology: Slow-developing disease · Early cervical dysplasia or abnormal cell maturation – evolving into invasive cancer if untreated · 90% squamous cells · Cervical cancers are adenocarcinomas originating from glandular cells of endocervix Clinical Manifestations: Vaginal bleeding – most common symptom · Post coital bleeding Medical Management – Diagnosis: Preventable with routine screening (Pap smear) · 1st Pap smear age 21 · Women between 21–30 with normal Pap smears should have every 3 years · Radiological evaluation</p>\n<p>[Image: Colposcopy] Side view of the pelvis: a speculum holds the vagina open while a colposcope (a lighted magnifier outside the body) is aimed at the cervix; bladder and uterus labeled. Medical Management – Treatment: Chemoradiation (brachytherapy) – internal radiation placed into or next to the tumor · Chemotherapy Surgical Management: Radical trachelectomy – removal of uterine &amp; cervix only (early stage); for women who desire to maintain fertility · Radical hysterectomy – for women without desire for fertility, a radical hysterectomy with lymph node dissection is performed</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 13 · Assess, act, teach, evaluate</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Assessment and analysis: Focus is on prevention (Pap smear) as there are no symptoms until the disease is very advanced Nursing diagnoses: Ineffective health maintenance (lack of Pap screening) · Ineffective coping · Acute pain · Nausea Assessments: Vital signs · Pap smear results – cervical cell change · Intake and output · Daily weight · Pain scale Actions: Refer for colposcopy/biopsy · Give medications as ordered · Give antiemetics as ordered · Give support and offer resources Teaching: Educate on the need for routine screening · Eligible patients need to receive HPV vaccination · Provide information on support group and resources Evaluating care outcomes: Prevention of disease · Pain under control · Nutritional needs met · Psychological support</p>\n</div></details></section>"},{"id":"male","title":"Male Reproductive and Breast Disorders","part":"Part 8 · Ch 66","summary":"BPH, prostate cancer (≈5 exam questions), testicular cancer, male breast cancer, testicular trauma, JP drain and Hemovac.","sections":[{"id":"male--benign-prostatic-hyperplasia-bph-not-cancer","title":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) · NOT cancer"},{"id":"male--prostate-cancer-slow-growing-5-questions","title":"Prostate cancer · slow growing ★ (5 questions)"},{"id":"male--testicular-cancer-young-men-very-curable","title":"Testicular cancer · young men, very curable"},{"id":"male--male-breast-cancer-brca2","title":"Male breast cancer · BRCA2"},{"id":"male--testicular-trauma-ages-25-40","title":"Testicular trauma · ages 25–40"},{"id":"male--jp-drain-and-hemovac-what-to-know-class-only","title":"JP drain and Hemovac: what to know *(class only)*"},{"id":"male--more-from-your-first-study-guide","title":"More from your first study guide"},{"id":"male--chapter-66-powerpoint-word-for-word","title":"Chapter 66 PowerPoint, word for word"}],"html":"<section id=\"male--male-reproductive-and-breast-disorders\"><p>BPH · Prostate cancer · Testicular cancer · Male breast cancer · Testicular trauma.</p>\n\n<aside class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"chead\"><span class=\"tag\">Class note</span> Class emphasis</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p><strong>Prostate cancer is worth about 5 exam questions</strong> (per your notes). Class added finasteride safety, brachytherapy PPE, and drains.</p>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"male--benign-prostatic-hyperplasia-bph-not-cancer\"><h2>Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) · NOT cancer<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#male--benign-prostatic-hyperplasia-bph-not-cancer\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><aside class=\"callout matters\"><div class=\"chead\">Why it matters</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Affects <strong>50% of men aged 51–60 and 90% of men over 80</strong>, across all races. <strong>BPH is NOT cancer and is not a precursor to it</strong>, although a man can have BPH and prostate cancer at the same time.</p>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout patho\"><div class=\"chead\">Patho</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>The walnut-sized prostate surrounds the urethra and starts growing around age 25. Theories: an estrogen/testosterone imbalance, buildup of <strong>DHT (dihydrotestosterone)</strong>, or a diet high in calories, protein, calcium, and polyunsaturated fat. The enlarged gland pinches the urethra and <strong>OBSTRUCTS urine flow.</strong></p>\n</div></aside>\n<p><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-062-027.jpg\" alt=\"Normal vs enlarged prostate: the enlarged gland narrows the urethra, causing a weak stream and urine retention\"><figcaption>Normal vs enlarged prostate: the enlarged gland narrows the urethra, causing a weak stream and urine retention</figcaption></figure></p>\n\n<aside class=\"callout ss\"><div class=\"chead\">Clinical manifestations (LUTS) ★</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p><strong>Obstructive:</strong> difficulty <strong>STARTING</strong> the stream (even when straining), hesitancy · weak stream · multiple interruptions while urinating · dribbling at the end.\n<strong>Bladder changes:</strong> urgency, frequency, <strong>NOCTURIA</strong> (expected finding), the feeling of incomplete emptying, then incontinence and bed-wetting.\n<strong>Bladder outlet obstruction:</strong> acute urinary retention, UTI (stasis), bladder stones, bladder damage, hydronephrosis, postrenal AKI, pyelonephritis.</p>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout dx\"><div class=\"chead\">Diagnosis, and why</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>Based on symptoms, confirmed by <strong>DRE</strong> (an enlarged prostate can be felt).</li>\n<li><strong>Urinalysis</strong> <em>→ why: rule out infection as the cause.</em></li>\n<li><strong>PSA:</strong> ⚠ PSA can&#39;t tell BPH from cancer, because infection and enlargement also raise it.</li>\n<li><strong>Bladder scan for post-void residual (PVR)</strong> <em>→ why: shows whether he can empty his bladder.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout tx\"><div class=\"chead\">Treatment (stepwise)</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Watchful waiting / active surveillance</strong> for minimal symptoms and minimal enlargement: a yearly exam with DRE.<ul>\n<li>⚠ <strong>Avoid tranquilizers and DECONGESTANTS (OTC cold meds)</strong>, which worsen obstruction (they cause constriction and increase urinary retention).</li>\n<li>Limit fluids in the evening (less nocturia).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Medications</strong> help 35–40% of men. Combining the two classes delays progression by up to 67%. Anticholinergic (oxybutynin) if he has an overactive bladder.</li>\n<li><strong>Catheterization</strong> (intermittent or indwelling) if meds fail and he can&#39;t have surgery.</li>\n<li><strong>Herbal:</strong> saw palmetto, African plum, South African star grass, Cernilton (long-term safety unknown).</li>\n<li><strong>Surgery:</strong><ul>\n<li><strong>TURP is the most common.</strong> A resectoscope through the urethra removes the inner prostate. ⚠ Bleeding and infection. Catheter for 3–5 days. Light activity only.</li>\n<li><strong>TUIP:</strong> 2 small cuts, nothing removed (for a small to moderate gland).</li>\n<li><strong>Open prostatectomy</strong> for a very large gland. ⚠ Higher risk of incontinence, impotence, and retrograde ejaculation.</li>\n<li><strong>Laser:</strong> immediate relief and less bleeding, so it works for patients on anticoagulants.</li>\n<li><strong>TUMT</strong> (microwave), <strong>TUNA</strong> (radio waves, outpatient, good when bleeding is a concern), stents (UTIs and pain, not long-term), water thermotherapy, ethanol ablation.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<p><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-063-028.jpg\" alt=\"BPH treatment flowchart: mild symptoms that don&amp;#39;t interfere with daily life → watchful waiting. Moderate to severe → meds, surgery, or other treatment. Surgery is usually indicated for retention, kidney damage, overflow incontinence, bladder stones, recurrent UTIs, or severe hematuria\"><figcaption>BPH treatment flowchart: mild symptoms that don&amp;#39;t interfere with daily life → watchful waiting. Moderate to severe → meds, surgery, or other treatment. Surgery is usually indicated for retention, kidney damage, overflow incontinence, bladder stones, recurrent UTIs, or severe hematuria</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-063-029.jpg\" alt=\"TURP before and after: the inner prostate is removed and only the outer layer is left\"><figcaption>TURP before and after: the inner prostate is removed and only the outer layer is left</figcaption></figure></p>\n<h3>BPH medications ★ (know how they work and what to watch for)</h3>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Class</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n<th>Onset</th>\n<th>⚠ Adverse effects / teaching</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Alpha-adrenergic blockers</strong>: tamsulosin (<strong>Flomax</strong>), terazosin, doxazosin, alfuzosin, silodosin</td>\n<td><strong>RELAX the smooth muscle of the prostate and bladder neck</strong>, so the urethra is squeezed less and urine flows more easily. (It does NOT dilate the urethra or lower PSA.)</td>\n<td>2 weeks to 4 months</td>\n<td><strong>Orthostatic/postural hypotension</strong> (rise slowly, fall risk), dizziness, drowsiness, headache, nasal congestion, reflex tachycardia, retrograde or delayed ejaculation, sexual dysfunction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>5-alpha reductase inhibitors (5-ARIs)</strong>: finasteride (<strong>Proscar</strong>), dutasteride (Avodart)</td>\n<td><strong>Block testosterone → DHT, so the prostate SHRINKS.</strong> Antiandrogens but don&#39;t change circulating testosterone. Cut acute retention by 57–59% and surgery by 36–55%</td>\n<td>3–6 months</td>\n<td>↓libido, ED, ↓ejaculate volume, breast enlargement or tenderness (gynecomastia), rash, orthostatic hypotension, blurred vision. May reduce male-pattern baldness (&quot;hair pill&quot;). <strong>Finasteride is teratogenic: a pregnant person should not handle crushed or broken tablets</strong> <em>(class only)</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"chead\"><span class=\"tag\">Class note</span> Class vs book</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Your notes say Flomax &quot;dilates the urethra.&quot; The book says alpha blockers <strong>relax the smooth muscle of the prostate and bladder neck</strong> and do NOT dilate the urethra. Pick <strong>&quot;relaxes the prostate muscle&quot;</strong> if it is an option. Flomax helps relieve urinary retention and makes urine easier to expel.</p>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout nursing\"><div class=\"chead\">Nursing, and why</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Dx:</strong> disturbed sleep (nocturia) · risk for infection (urinary stasis) · increased fall risk</li>\n<li><strong>Urinary symptoms</strong> <em>→ why: severity guides treatment.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Temperature</strong> <em>→ why: fever points to a UTI from stasis.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Focused abdominal exam</strong> <em>→ why: a distended bladder means he can&#39;t empty.</em></li>\n<li>Bladder scan (PVR) and urinalysis.</li>\n<li><strong>Catheterize</strong> <em>→ why: checks PVR and relieves a distended bladder, protecting it from damage.</em></li>\n<li>Give 5-ARIs and/or alpha blockers.</li>\n<li><strong>Post-op prostatectomy:</strong> manage <strong>continuous bladder irrigation</strong> (removes clots) and give stool softeners.</li>\n<li><strong>Teaching:</strong> watchful waiting: recognize worsening symptoms and when to come back. Decrease fluids in the evening. <strong>No decongestants or tranquilizers.</strong> (&quot;I&#39;m glad I can still take my decongestants&quot; means more teaching is needed.) Med side effects. Follow-up. Surgical options. Post-op site care. <strong>Report pain, fever, N/V</strong> <em>→ why: urine stasis from incomplete emptying can cause a UTI.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Outcomes:</strong> less retention, a stronger stream, less nocturia.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"male--prostate-cancer-slow-growing-5-questions\"><h2>Prostate cancer · slow growing ★ (5 questions)<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#male--prostate-cancer-slow-growing-5-questions\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><aside class=\"callout matters\"><div class=\"chead\">Why it matters and risk</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>One of the most commonly diagnosed cancers in men worldwide and the <strong>most common in US men</strong>. 1 in 8 men (about 6 in 10 men ≥65). Average age at diagnosis is 66. Rare under 40.</li>\n<li><strong>African American men</strong> (and Caribbean men of African ancestry) have higher incidence and <strong>2× the mortality.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Risk:</strong> family history (BRCA1/2, Lynch), diet high in <strong>RED MEAT</strong> (heterocyclic amines), high <strong>CALCIUM</strong> intake, high BMI, smoking, chemicals, STIs, more than 7 multivitamins a week.</li>\n<li><strong>Protective:</strong> vegetables, vitamin E, lycopene, selenium (oysters, tuna, whole wheat, sunflower seeds), antioxidants.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout patho\"><div class=\"chead\">Patho ★</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Slow growing and asymptomatic until advanced.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>⚠ <strong>Tumors grow on the PERIPHERY of the gland</strong>, so they do NOT obstruct urine flow early and go unnoticed until they cause pain. That is how a patient can have <strong>late-stage disease without ever having trouble urinating.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Metastasizes to:</strong> lymph nodes, <strong>BONE</strong>, rectum, bladder (spreads cell-to-cell to the rectum, pelvic floor, lower spine, and hip). May be curable when localized and responds to treatment even when widespread.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<p><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-065-030.jpg\" alt=\"Normal prostate vs prostate cancer\"><figcaption>Normal prostate vs prostate cancer</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-065-031.jpg\" alt=\"Gleason scoring: grade 1 is well differentiated (best prognosis, greatest chance of cure). Grade 5 is poorly differentiated (poor prognosis). Two sites are graded and added together, for a maximum of 10\"><figcaption>Gleason scoring: grade 1 is well differentiated (best prognosis, greatest chance of cure). Grade 5 is poorly differentiated (poor prognosis). Two sites are graded and added together, for a maximum of 10</figcaption></figure></p>\n\n<aside class=\"callout ss\"><div class=\"chead\">Clinical manifestations ★</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Early: none.</strong> When it advances, it can look like BPH: trouble urinating, weak stream.</li>\n<li><strong>Local disease:</strong> asymptomatic, ↑PSA, weak stream, hesitancy, incomplete emptying, frequency, urgency, urge incontinence, UTI.</li>\n<li><strong>Locally invasive:</strong> hematuria, dysuria, perineal and suprapubic pain, ED, incontinence, loin pain or anuria (ureter obstruction), renal failure, <strong>hemospermia</strong> (blood in semen), rectal symptoms (tenesmus). Bone pain with mets.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout dx\"><div class=\"chead\">Diagnosis (controversial)</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>PSA</strong> has been the &quot;gold standard&quot; for early detection, but it is overdiagnosing and underdiagnosing, so some providers follow serial PSAs. <strong>PSA ≥4.0 ng/mL means further evaluation</strong> (starts at 4; watchful waiting below). 2.5–4.0 gets an individual risk assessment. &lt;2.5: retest every 2 years. ≥2.5: retest yearly. No PSA value guarantees no cancer.</li>\n<li><strong>DRE</strong> from age 50 (ACS), or 45 for African American men or a first-degree relative diagnosed before 65, or 40 with more than one early-onset relative. Requires informed, shared decision-making and life expectancy of ≥10 years.</li>\n<li><strong>Biopsy</strong> (needle, in the office, local anesthetic) after an abnormal DRE or PSA. Then <strong>Gleason score</strong> and TNM.</li>\n<li>Genomic tests (Oncotype DX Prostate, Polaris, ProMark, Decipher) predict growth and spread.</li>\n<li><strong>TNM stages:</strong> T1 can&#39;t be felt · T2 confined to the prostate · T3 locally extensive · T4 fixed to or invading neighboring organs (may spread).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<p><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-066-032.jpg\" alt=\"Stages of prostate cancer (TNM): T1 can&amp;#39;t be felt · T2 confined to the prostate · T3 locally extensive · T4 fixed to or invading neighboring organs\"><figcaption>Stages of prostate cancer (TNM): T1 can&amp;#39;t be felt · T2 confined to the prostate · T3 locally extensive · T4 fixed to or invading neighboring organs</figcaption></figure></p>\n\n<aside class=\"callout tx\"><div class=\"chead\">Treatment</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Radiation</strong> is the nonsurgical option. External beam runs 5×/week for 8 weeks. CyberKnife takes 4–5 sessions.</li>\n<li><strong>Brachytherapy:</strong> radioactive seeds placed permanently through the perineum. Outpatient, for cancer that hasn&#39;t spread outside the prostate.</li>\n<li><strong>Cryotherapy:</strong> liquid nitrogen freezes the prostate. Low success rate, often repeated.</li>\n<li><strong>Ablative hormone therapy</strong> suppresses testosterone (an androgen that feeds the tumor) when cancer has spread to lymph nodes or bone. It is called <strong>neoadjuvant</strong> when used to shrink the prostate before radiation.</li>\n<li><strong>Chemotherapy</strong> (docetaxel, cabazitaxel, mitoxantrone, estramustine) once the cancer is hormone-refractory.</li>\n<li><strong>Radical prostatectomy</strong> removes the prostate and seminal vesicles ± pelvic nodes (open retropubic, laparoscopic or robotic, or perineal). ⚠ <strong>Almost always causes impotence.</strong> The nerve-sparing approach tries to protect the erection nerves.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout watch\"><div class=\"chead\">Watch</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Radiation and brachytherapy:</strong> incontinence and/or impotence.</li>\n<li><strong>Brachytherapy safety:</strong> no sex for 2 weeks, then use a condom to protect the partner from radiation. <strong>If a client is receiving brachytherapy: minimize contact and wear proper (thicker) PPE</strong> <em>(class only).</em></li>\n<li><strong>Cryotherapy:</strong> impotence, incontinence, rectal complications.</li>\n<li><strong>Chemo:</strong> hair loss, mouth sores, N/V/D, ↓appetite, infection, bruising, and bleeding, fatigue.</li>\n<li><strong>Post-prostatectomy:</strong> infection (fever, increasing pain, swelling, bleeding, N/V), clots. Manage bladder irrigation and give stool softeners (no straining).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout nursing\"><div class=\"chead\">Nursing</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Dx:</strong> risk for infection · sexual dysfunction · emotional distress</li>\n<li><strong>Assess:</strong> risk factors · PSA ≥4 ng/mL · DRE: a large palpable prostate plus high PSA needs workup · urinary symptoms (pain comes from obstruction and bladder distention once advanced).</li>\n<li><strong>Actions:</strong> hormone therapy and chemo · aseptic wound care · bladder irrigation · stool softeners.</li>\n<li><strong>Teach:</strong> disease process, brachytherapy safety, chemo and radiation effects, signs of infection, prevention (limit calcium, red meat, and high-dose multivitamins; eat selenium-rich foods).</li>\n<li><strong>Outcomes:</strong> normal sexual function, normal urination, no incontinence.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<h3>BPH vs prostate cancer ★</h3>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>BPH</th>\n<th>Prostate cancer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>What it is</strong></td>\n<td>Benign enlargement, not cancer</td>\n<td>Malignant, slow-growing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Location</strong></td>\n<td>Squeezes the urethra (central)</td>\n<td>Grows on the periphery, so no early obstruction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Early symptoms</strong></td>\n<td>LUTS early: hesitancy, weak stream, dribbling, nocturia</td>\n<td>None early. Late: urinary symptoms plus pain, hematuria, hemospermia, ED, bone pain (mets)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>DRE</strong></td>\n<td>Enlarged prostate</td>\n<td>Large, palpable, hard prostate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>PSA</strong></td>\n<td>May be ↑ (can&#39;t distinguish)</td>\n<td>≥4 needs workup. Biopsy confirms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Treatment</strong></td>\n<td>Watchful waiting, alpha blockers, 5-ARIs, TURP or laser</td>\n<td>Radiation, brachytherapy, cryotherapy, hormone ablation, chemo, radical prostatectomy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></section>\n<section id=\"male--testicular-cancer-young-men-very-curable\"><h2>Testicular cancer · young men, very curable<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#male--testicular-cancer-young-men-very-curable\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><aside class=\"callout matters\"><div class=\"chead\">Why it matters and risk</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>Not common, but increasing over the last 20 years. About 9,910 cases a year (2022). <strong>Average age 33</strong>, a disease of young to middle-aged men. White men have 4–5× the risk.</li>\n<li><strong>CRYPTORCHIDISM (undescended testicle) = GREATEST RISK.</strong> One or both testes fail to descend from the abdomen into the scrotum.</li>\n<li>Others: Down syndrome, Klinefelter&#39;s, HIV, family history, cancer in the other testicle, smoking, diet, age.</li>\n<li><strong>Excellent prognosis: 90–100% cure if found early.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout patho\"><div class=\"chead\">Patho</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Seminoma:</strong> slow-growing, men in their 30s–40s, very sensitive to <strong>RADIATION</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Nonseminoma</strong> (embryonal, teratoma, and others): teens to 40s, grows and spreads faster, <strong>NOT responsive to radiation</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Spreads through</strong> (1) the testicular wall into the blood supply or sperm ducts, (2) <strong>lymph nodes to the back of the abdomen (most common)</strong>, or (3) the bloodstream to the brain, lung, and bone.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<p><figure><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/s-068-033.jpg\" alt=\"Testicular mass in the testis (vas deferens and urethra labeled)\"><figcaption>Testicular mass in the testis (vas deferens and urethra labeled)</figcaption></figure></p>\n\n<aside class=\"callout ss\"><div class=\"chead\">Clinical manifestations ★</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>PAINLESS mass</strong> (the classic sign).</li>\n<li>Some have a dull abdominal ache or a dull ache in the scrotum. A firm or <strong>HEAVY</strong> feeling in the scrotum. Swelling and redness.</li>\n<li><strong>Mets:</strong> GI symptoms, low back pain, gynecomastia, dyspnea, cough, hemoptysis, a neck mass.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout dx\"><div class=\"chead\">Diagnosis</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>Physical exam. <strong>Self-exam</strong> may find it first and is best done in a <strong>warm shower</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Ultrasound</strong> separates cancer from infection, fluid, torsion, or hernia.</li>\n<li><strong>Tumor markers: ALPHA-FETOPROTEIN (AFP) and hCG.</strong> They should fall after the tumor is removed. If they don&#39;t, look for more tumors.</li>\n<li><strong>Staging is TNM plus S (serum tumor markers)</strong>, using x-ray and CT.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout tx\"><div class=\"chead\">Treatment</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>ALL types start with REMOVING the affected testicle (orchiectomy)</strong> and its blood supply through an abdominal incision. This is the <strong>&quot;best treatment&quot;</strong> answer.</li>\n<li><strong>Seminoma:</strong> surgery, then radiation and/or chemo.</li>\n<li><strong>Nonseminoma:</strong> surgery plus lymph node removal, then chemo (not radiation).</li>\n<li><strong>Chemo:</strong> cisplatin, etoposide, bleomycin, ifosfamide, paclitaxel, vinblastine.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout watch\"><div class=\"chead\">Watch</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Chemo toxicity:</strong> hair loss, mouth sores, N/V/D, infection, bleeding, kidney damage (monitor creatinine, BUN, GFR), neuropathy, ototoxicity, lung damage (<strong>bleomycin</strong>), hemorrhagic cystitis (<strong>ifosfamide</strong>). Chemo can also cause ↓testosterone and ↑cardiovascular risk (angina, MI).</li>\n<li><strong>Retroperitoneal lymph node resection (RPLND)</strong> can damage the nerves that control ejaculation.</li>\n<li><strong>Post-op:</strong> hypotension and tachycardia mean volume loss. Fever means infection.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout nursing\"><div class=\"chead\">Nursing</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Dx:</strong> ineffective health maintenance · risk for infection · pain. Assess lumps, swelling, redness, and vital signs.</li>\n<li><strong>Actions:</strong> aseptic wound care, chemo.</li>\n<li><strong>Teach:</strong> signs of infection, chemo effects, nerve damage from RPLND, <strong>BANK SPERM BEFORE TREATMENT</strong> to preserve fertility, and <strong>follow up for at least 10 years</strong> (the usual window for recurrence).</li>\n<li><strong>Outcomes:</strong> early diagnosis, cure, successful family planning.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"male--male-breast-cancer-brca2\"><h2>Male breast cancer · BRCA2<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#male--male-breast-cancer-brca2\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><aside class=\"callout matters\"><div class=\"chead\">Why it matters and risk</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>100 times less common</strong> than female breast cancer (1 in 100,000). Ages 60–70. Non-Hispanic Black men have higher risk, and a first-degree relative doubles it.</li>\n<li><strong>BRCA2</strong> (more common) or BRCA1 mutation, family history, excess alcohol, testicular disorders, hormone imbalance (↑estrogen from liver disease or Klinefelter&#39;s), obesity, gynecomastia, heat or radiation, estrogen therapy (prostate cancer or gender transition).</li>\n<li><em>SLIDE vs BOOK:</em> slide: &quot;2,360 cases in 2014.&quot; Book: &quot;1 in 100,000 worldwide.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout patho\"><div class=\"chead\">Patho and S/S</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Uncontrolled growth in the small amount of breast tissue behind the nipple. Because there is so little tissue, it spreads easily to the nipple, skin, and muscle, but the mass is easier to feel. Mostly ductal (DCIS, infiltrating ductal). Lobular is rare.\n<strong>S/S:</strong> a lump or swelling, dimpling, nipple discharge, inversion, or bleeding, erythema, skin ulceration, axillary nodes. <strong>Inflammatory type:</strong> red, warm, swollen breast. <strong>Paget&#39;s:</strong> tumor grows into the nipple surface.</p>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout dx\"><div class=\"chead\">Diagnosis</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>Clinical breast exam. <strong>Ultrasound if &lt;25 years old. Mammogram if ≥25.</strong> MRI.</li>\n<li><strong>ER/PR receptor test</strong> tells whether hormone therapy will work. The <strong>HER2 test</strong> tells whether monoclonal antibody therapy will work.</li>\n<li>Sentinel node biopsy, chest x-ray, CT, bone scan, PET for mets.</li>\n<li><strong>Staging:</strong> 0 in situ · I &lt;2 cm, no nodes · II up to 5 cm · III &gt;5 cm ± nodes · IV mets.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout tx\"><div class=\"chead\">Treatment</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Mastectomy is usual</strong> (simple, modified radical, or radical). Lumpectomy is rarely used because there is so little tissue.</li>\n<li>Chemo (adjuvant or neoadjuvant = BEFORE surgery to help complete excision).</li>\n<li><strong>Hormone therapy only for receptor-POSITIVE tumors:</strong> tamoxifen, aromatase inhibitors, fulvestrant. ⚠ <strong>Question antiestrogen therapy in a receptor-NEGATIVE patient.</strong></li>\n<li>Targeted therapy (trastuzumab, pertuzumab) for HER2+: watch for hypertension, bleeding, poor wound healing, clots, kidney damage. Radiation is rarely used.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout nursing\"><div class=\"chead\">Nursing</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Dx:</strong> ineffective health maintenance · risk for infection · emotional distress.</li>\n<li><strong>Post-op:</strong> ↑temperature means infection, ↓BP means bleeding or hypovolemia, ↑HR means bleeding, pain, or anxiety. Check the incision (approximation, minimal serosanguineous drainage) and pain scale.</li>\n<li><strong>Actions:</strong> meds, change the dressing (aseptic), <strong>empty the drain and record color and amount</strong>, pain meds (helps with cough, deep breathing, and ambulation).</li>\n<li><strong>Teach:</strong> suture care, signs of infection, breast self-exam (to monitor for recurrence), follow-up, emotional support. Transgender men after top surgery may still need mammograms.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"male--testicular-trauma-ages-25-40\"><h2>Testicular trauma · ages 25–40<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#male--testicular-trauma-ages-25-40\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><aside class=\"callout tx\"><div class=\"chead\">Types</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Blunt (most common):</strong> <strong>sports injuries are the #1 cause</strong>, plus a kick to the groin, MVA, falls, straddle injuries. It can dislocate a testicle. (Class: groin injury in football = blunt trauma.)</li>\n<li><strong>Penetrating:</strong> stab or gunshot wounds, bites.</li>\n<li><strong>Degloving / avulsion (least common):</strong> scrotal skin is sheared off by heavy machinery or farm accidents.</li>\n<li>Rupture of the <strong>tunica albuginea</strong> causes bleeding into the tunica vaginalis (a <strong>hematocele</strong>) or a scrotal hematoma.</li>\n<li><em>SLIDE vs BOOK:</em> slide: 85% blunt / 15% penetrating. Book: 80% / 20%.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout tx\"><div class=\"chead\">Management</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>History (mechanism of injury) and <strong>scrotal ultrasound</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Blunt with no rupture: conservative.</strong> NSAIDs, scrotal support (sling or folded towels), ice packs (with a towel), bedrest 24–48 hours. Antibiotics for epididymitis or UTI.</li>\n<li><strong>Surgical exploration</strong> for internal damage: debridement, irrigation, microsurgery. Orchiectomy is rare.</li>\n<li><strong>Degloving:</strong> primary closure using the remaining scrotal skin. If there isn&#39;t enough skin, temporarily relocate the testis to the thigh (cooler, so sperm production continues) and reconstruct in 4–6 weeks. <strong>Reimplantation within 8 hours if amputated.</strong></li>\n<li>⚠ Bleeding and infection are the major risks. Antibiotics before and after surgery. Drains.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout nursing\"><div class=\"chead\">Nursing</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Assess:</strong> bleeding, swelling, skin avulsion, pain (usually intense), temperature, wound (redness, warmth, pus).</li>\n<li><strong>Actions:</strong> conservative care, aseptic wound care, antibiotics, <strong>monitor drain output (excess means bleeding; foul or discolored means infection)</strong>, compassion.</li>\n<li><strong>Teach:</strong> NSAID dosing, scrotal elevation, ice with a towel, rest, drain care. Call for fever, redness, heaviness, swelling, bleeding, N/V, or worsening pain. Long term: hormone problems, infertility, impotence.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"male--jp-drain-and-hemovac-what-to-know-class-only\"><h2>JP drain and Hemovac: what to know <em>(class only)</em><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#male--jp-drain-and-hemovac-what-to-know-class-only\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><ul>\n<li>Both are <strong>closed-suction surgical drains</strong> that pull fluid from the wound to prevent hematoma and infection.</li>\n<li><strong>JP (Jackson-Pratt)</strong> = small bulb. <strong>Hemovac</strong> = larger round, spring-loaded canister for more drainage.</li>\n<li><strong>Suction only works when the device is compressed:</strong> empty it, squeeze the bulb or press the canister flat, then close the plug.</li>\n<li>Empty when half to two-thirds full (or per order) and record the amount, color, and consistency on I&amp;O.</li>\n<li><strong>Expected:</strong> bloody → serosanguineous → serous. Report a sudden increase, bright red blood, or foul or cloudy drainage.</li>\n<li>Keep it below the wound, secured to the gown, with no kinks; aseptic technique; assess the insertion site.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"male--more-from-your-first-study-guide\"><h2>More from your first study guide<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#male--more-from-your-first-study-guide\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><ul>\n<li><strong>BPH drug classes:</strong> alpha blockers (&quot;-osin&quot;) and 5-ARIs (&quot;-steride&quot;). <strong>5-ARIs also ↓PSA</strong> and shrink the gland over 3–6 months. Anticholinergic: oxybutynin for overactive bladder.</li>\n<li><strong>Screening talk starts at:</strong> 50 (average risk; DRE age 50 per ACS) · 45 (African American or first-degree relative diagnosed &lt;65) · 40 (more than one first-degree relative at an early age). PSA ≥4 → further assessment (your notes: starts at 4 for watchful waiting); &lt;2.5 retest every 2 years.</li>\n<li><strong>Prostate post-op complications ★ (exam):</strong> impotence/ED (most expected after radical prostatectomy) · urinary incontinence · infection (fever, ↑pain, swelling, redness, drainage, N/V) · bleeding/hemorrhage and clots in urine (<strong>bladder irrigation clears clots</strong>) · retrograde ejaculation (TURP/open prostatectomy) · rectal complications (cryotherapy) · sexual dysfunction and emotional distress (nursing dx).</li>\n<li>Also standard after prostatectomy/TURP: continuous bladder irrigation keeps urine light pink, catheter care/traction, bladder spasms (antispasmodics), Kegel exercises for incontinence, early ambulation for DVT prevention.</li>\n<li><strong>Prostate cancer:</strong> good 5-year survival; PSA is the &quot;preferred standard&quot; test. Brachytherapy: minimize contact and wear thicker PPE; no sex 2 weeks, then condoms. Chemo when hormone-refractory (docetaxel).</li>\n<li><strong>Testicular:</strong> average age 33 (20–34 per Table 13.2). <strong>Cure rate 90–100% if early; 95% overall, 80% with mets.</strong> Chemo: cisplatin, etoposide, bleomycin (watch kidneys, lungs, hearing). Teach testicular self-exam in a warm shower.</li>\n<li><strong>Male breast cancer meds:</strong> tamoxifen in men: ED, headaches, hot flashes. Neoadjuvant = before (shrink); adjuvant = after (insurance vs recurrence).</li>\n<li><strong>Slide wording, Ch 66:</strong> testicular nursing diagnoses: ineffective health maintenance, risk for infection. Cryptorchidism = incomplete descent of one or both testes from the abdomen into the inguinal canal.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Case: Scott Andrews, 65 (BPH)</h3>\n<p>Difficulty urinating, <strong>pain with urination, occasional blood-tinged urine</strong>, urgency, <strong>nocturia 3–4 times a night</strong>, occasional incontinence. Plan: PSA, urinalysis, DRE. DRE result, <strong>PSA &lt;2 ng/mL</strong>, normal urinalysis → best intervention is <strong>watchful waiting.</strong> If symptoms worsen: 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors or alpha-adrenergic blockers. Nurse teaches watchful waiting and the importance of follow-up.\nAnswers: why BPH causes symptoms = obstruction of urine flow · why watchful waiting = minimal symptoms and enlargement · needs more teaching = &quot;Glad I can take my decongestants&quot; · Flomax = relaxes the prostate muscle · report pain, fever, N/V = urine stasis can cause a UTI.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"male--chapter-66-powerpoint-word-for-word\"><h2>Chapter 66 PowerPoint, word for word<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#male--chapter-66-powerpoint-word-for-word\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><p>Your instructor&#39;s slides for this chapter, kept in slide order.</p>\n\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 1 · What BPH is</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Epidemiology: 50% of men aged 51–60 · 90% of men over age 80 Pathophysiology: Enlargement of the prostate – size of a walnut</p>\n<p>[Image: Prostate] Side-by-side drawing: a normal prostate sits under the bladder with the urethra running freely through it; an enlarged prostate (BPH) bulges and squeezes the urethra, narrowing the channel urine flows through.</p>\n<p>Clinical Manifestations: Difficulty starting flow of urine · Weak urine stream · Multiple interruptions during urination · Dribbling once urination is complete · Urgency, frequency · Nocturia · Bladder outlet obstruction</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 2 · Finding it and the first-line approach</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Medical Management – Diagnosis: Digital rectal examination · Urinalysis · Prostate-specific antigen Medical Management – Treatment: Watchful waiting/active surveillance · Avoid tranquilizers and decongestants</p>\n<p>[Image: Treatment Options for Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia] Flowchart: patient with lower urinary tract symptoms → history, physical, DRE, urinalysis (± PSA) → AUA symptom score. Mild symptoms → watchful waiting. Moderate to severe → discuss treatment options (medications, minimally invasive therapy, or surgery). Obstructive complications (retention, recurrent UTI, stones, kidney damage) → surgery.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 3 · Drugs &amp; alternatives</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Medical Management – Medications: 5-alpha reductase inhibitors · Alpha-adrenergic blockers · Anticholinergic</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 4 · Procedures</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Surgical Management: TURP · Transurethral incision of prostate · Open prostatectomy · Laser surgery</p>\n<p>[Image: Transurethral Resection of the Prostate (TURP)] Before: the enlarged prostate narrows the urethra under the bladder and a resectoscope is passed up through the penis. After: the entire inner prostate is removed, leaving only the outer layer, so the channel is wide open and urine flows easily.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 5 · Assess, act, teach, evaluate</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Assessment and analysis: Clinical manifestations are caused by obstruction of urine flow due to an enlarged prostate Nursing diagnoses: Disturbed sleep pattern · Risk for infection Assessments: Urinary symptoms · Temperature · Focused abdominal examination · Bladder scan · Urinalysis Actions: Check for PVR · Catheterization · Administer medications as ordered – 5 alpha reductase inhibitors to limit the production of DHT, a substance that encourages the prostate to hyperplasia; Alpha adrenergic blockers to relax smooth muscles Teaching: Watchful waiting – recognition of worsening symptoms · Decreased liquid intake in the evening · Medication therapy education · Follow-up · Surgical options · Post surgical care Evaluating care outcomes: Less urinary retention · Strong urine stream · Decreased nocturia</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 6 · Epidemiology, risk, pathophysiology, signs</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Epidemiology: One of the most commonly diagnosed cancers in men, globally · 5 year survival rate · Diagnosed at age 65 Risk Factors: Family history · Diet high in red meat (heterocyclic amines) · Consumption of calcium · High BMI Pathophysiology: Slow growing and asymptomatic until advanced · Metastasis to lymph nodes, bone, rectum, and bladder</p>\n<p>[Image] Normal prostate next to a prostate with cancer: the tumor sits in the outer edge of the gland, away from the urethra, so urine flow isn&#39;t blocked early.</p>\n<p>Clinical Manifestations: Trouble urinating · Weak stream of urine</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 7 · PSA, biopsy, gleason, TNM</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Medical Management – Diagnosis: Digital rectal exam – age 50 (ACS) · Prostate-specific antigen – gold standard · Biopsy</p>\n<p>[Image: Gleason Scoring System] Five panels of prostate tissue. Grade 1: small, uniform, well-formed glands that look nearly normal. Grades 2–4: glands become more irregular, crowded, and fused. Grade 5: sheets of cells with almost no gland structure (poorly differentiated).</p>\n<p>[Image: Stages of Prostate Cancer] Four drawings of the prostate under the bladder. T1: tiny tumor you can&#39;t feel. T2: tumor still inside the prostate. T3: tumor breaks through the capsule into surrounding tissue. T4: tumor invades neighboring organs (bladder, rectum).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 8 · Radiation, freezing, hormones, chemo, surgery</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Medical Management – Treatment: Radiation – non surgical option · Brachytherapy – radioactive seeds · Cryotherapy – liquid nitrogen (freeze the prostate) · Ablative hormone therapy – used in men metastasized to lymph nodes – suppression of testosterone that promotes tumor · Chemotherapy – hormone refractory Surgical Management: Radical prostatectomy – prostate and seminal vesicles are removed – almost always results in impotence · (new) Nerve sparing prostatectomy – attempts to keep the nerves intact that control erection</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 9</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Assessment and analysis: Clinical manifestations evident once disease is advanced related to obstruction of flow of urine and mass causing pain Nursing diagnoses: Risk for infection · Sexual dysfunction Assessments: Risk factors · PSA levels – 4 ng/ml requires further assessment · DRE results – large palpable prostate · Urinary symptoms Actions: Administer medications as ordered · Postoperative surgical care Teaching: Disease process · Brachytherapy · Chemotherapy/radiation · Signs of infection · Prevention Evaluating care outcomes: Normal sexual functioning · Normal urination · No incontinence</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 10 · The young man's cancer</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Epidemiology: Increased incidence over last 20 years · Due to environmental · Genetics · Increased risk in males with Down&#39;s syndrome Risk Factors: Cryptorchidism – greatest risk · incomplete descent of one or both testes from the abdomen into the inguinal canal resulting in absence of scrotum Pathophysiology: Begin in testicles · Spread through blood supply, tubes responsible for sperm transport, lymph system</p>\n<p>[Image] Cross-section of a testis inside the scrotum with a distinct mass growing within the testicle.</p>\n<p>Clinical Manifestations: Painless mass · Some patients have abdominal dull pain, dull ache · Feeling firm or heavy in the scrotum · The mass may also present with swelling and redness Diagnosis: Physical assessment · Ultrasonography · Laboratory results – alpha-fetoprotein and human chorionic gonadotropin Treatment: Removal of affected testicle for seminomas · Radiation · Chemotherapy Assessment and analysis: Clinical manifestations due to mass or lump in testes · Abdominal pain · Firm or fullness in the scrotum Nursing diagnoses: Ineffective health maintenance · Risk for infection Assessments: Physical assessment – lumps, redness · Vital signs – hypotension, tachycardia, increased temperature Actions: Wound care · Administer chemotherapeutic agents as ordered Teaching: Signs of infection · Chemotherapy · Retroperitoneal lymph node resection – possible nerve damage · Sperm storage – preserve potential fertility · Follow-up Evaluating care outcomes: Early diagnosis · Cure of disease · Successful family planning</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 11 · Rare, spreads easily</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Epidemiology: 100 times less common than female breast cancer · 2,360 cases in 2014 Risk Factors: Advanced age between 60 and 70 · Mutation BRCA2 gene or BRCA1 gene · Family history · Excessive alcohol consumption · Testicular disorders · Environmental factors · Hormonal imbalances Pathophysiology: Uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells of breast tissue · Clinical manifestation – Mass in breast tissue Diagnosis: Clinical breast examination · Ultrasonography · MRI Treatment: Surgical resection · Chemotherapy · Radiation · Targeted therapy Assessment and analysis: Clinical manifestations due to mass or lump in breast tissue – lump, swelling, dimpling, nipple discharge, inverted nipple, red warm and swollen breast Nursing diagnoses: Ineffective health maintenance · Risk for infection Assessments: Lump in breast · Dimpling or discharge from nipple · Nipple inversion · Redness · Postoperative assessment Actions: Administer medications as ordered · Change dressing · Empty drain at surgical site · Administer pain medication per order Teaching: Care of suture site · Signs and symptoms of infection · Breast self-examination · Follow-up appointment · Provide emotional support and resources Evaluating care outcomes: Good surgical site healing · Emotional healing</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 12 · Blunt, penetrating &amp; degloving</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Epidemiology: 85% from blunt trauma – sports injuries, kick to the groin, MVA, straddling of an object, falls, injury (25–40 years old) · 15% from penetrating trauma Surgical Management: Primary closure using remaining scrotal skin</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 13 · Scott Andrews, 65, BPH</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Episode 1: Scott Andrews is a 65-year-old male patient who presents to his healthcare provider with a complaint of difficulty urinating. He complains of pain with urination and occasional blood-tinged urine. He states that he has an increased urgency to urinate and wakes up three to four times during the night to urinate. He also complains of occasional incontinence.</p>\n<p>Wrap-Up: After assessment of his symptoms and education, Scott agrees to a blood draw for PSA levels, a urinalysis, and a DRE. Based on the results of the DRE, a PSA of less than 2 ng/mL, and urinalysis within normal limits, Scott is told the best intervention at this time is &quot;watchful waiting.&quot; He is told that if his symptoms worsen, he may be prescribed medication therapy such as 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors or alpha-adrenergic blockers. The nurse provides patient education focusing on watchful waiting and the importance of future provider follow- up.</p>\n</div></details></section>"},{"id":"pain","title":"Pain Management","part":"Part 9 · Ch 11","summary":"Acute vs chronic, pain types, OPQRST-AAA, acetaminophen, NSAIDs, opioids, adjuvants, older adults.","sections":[{"id":"pain--acute-vs-chronic","title":"Acute vs chronic"},{"id":"pain--pain-by-origin","title":"Pain by origin"},{"id":"pain--assessment-opqrst-aaa","title":"Assessment: OPQRST-AAA ★"},{"id":"pain--nonopioid-analgesics","title":"Nonopioid analgesics"},{"id":"pain--opioids","title":"Opioids"},{"id":"pain--adjuvants-and-other-options","title":"Adjuvants and other options"},{"id":"pain--more-from-your-first-study-guide","title":"More from your first study guide"},{"id":"pain--chapter-11-powerpoint-word-for-word","title":"Chapter 11 PowerPoint, word for word"}],"html":"<section id=\"pain--pain-management\"><p>Pain is <strong>&quot;an unpleasant sensory and emotional experience associated with actual or potential tissue damage&quot;</strong> (IASP 2020), and it is <strong>whatever the patient says it is</strong> (McCaffery). More than two-thirds of cancer patients have moderate to severe pain.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"pain--acute-vs-chronic\"><h2>Acute vs chronic<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#pain--acute-vs-chronic\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Acute</th>\n<th>Chronic</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Time</strong></td>\n<td>Sudden, short (hours to weeks), linked to tissue injury</td>\n<td>Lasts more than 3–6 months, may have no clear injury</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Purpose</strong></td>\n<td>Protective</td>\n<td>Serves no biological purpose</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Feels like</strong></td>\n<td>Sharp, easy to pinpoint</td>\n<td>Achy, dull, or burning, hard to localize. Resists treatment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Examples</strong></td>\n<td>Post-op, trauma</td>\n<td>Migraine, low back pain, neuropathy, fibromyalgia, phantom limb</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><ul>\n<li><strong>Gate control theory:</strong> the spinal cord &quot;opens or closes the gate&quot; to pain signals, and the brain shapes how pain is perceived.</li>\n<li><strong>Peripheral and central sensitization</strong> amplify pain and can make it chronic (synaptic &quot;cellular memory&quot;).</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"pain--pain-by-origin\"><h2>Pain by origin<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#pain--pain-by-origin\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><ul>\n<li><strong>Nociceptive:</strong> cutaneous (skin) and somatic (bone, joint, muscle).</li>\n<li><strong>Visceral:</strong> hollow organs (heart, bladder, GI). Hard to localize, often referred. <strong>Autonomic signs:</strong> sweating, pallor, nausea, low HR and BP, syncope.</li>\n<li><strong>Neuropathic:</strong> from nerve damage. Shooting, stinging, &quot;pins and needles&quot; with numb areas. Examples: postherpetic neuralgia, phantom limb, diabetic and chemo-induced neuropathy. <strong>The hardest type to treat.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Older adults</strong> may show atypical pain: confusion, restlessness, poor appetite. Up to 30% have no pain with an MI.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"pain--assessment-opqrst-aaa\"><h2>Assessment: OPQRST-AAA ★<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#pain--assessment-opqrst-aaa\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>O</strong> Onset</td>\n<td>When did it start, sudden or gradual</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>P</strong> Provocation</td>\n<td>What makes it worse (movement, turning)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Q</strong> Quality</td>\n<td>Sharp, dull, burning, stabbing, crushing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>R</strong> Region / Radiation</td>\n<td>Point to it, does it spread</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>S</strong> Severity</td>\n<td>0–10 scale, effect on function</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>T</strong> Time / duration</td>\n<td>How long, has it happened before</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>AAA</strong></td>\n<td><strong>A</strong>ggravating and <strong>A</strong>lleviating factors, <strong>A</strong>ssociated symptoms</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p>(&quot;What makes the pain worse?&quot; assesses <strong>aggravating factors.</strong>)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The 0–10 scale measures <strong>intensity only</strong>, and scores of <strong>4 or higher</strong> usually prompt intervention. Also assess function. Physical exam: palpation, ROM, neuro checks.</li>\n<li><strong>Cognitively impaired patients:</strong> attempt self-report first, assume pain if a painful condition exists, establish a behavioral baseline (guarding, rubbing, rocking, grimacing), use <strong>PAINAD</strong> or the Checklist of Nonverbal Pain Indicators, give scheduled nonopioids, and avoid psychotropics (they blunt pain behaviors).</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"pain--nonopioid-analgesics\"><h2>Nonopioid analgesics<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#pain--nonopioid-analgesics\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><aside class=\"callout watch\"><div class=\"chead\">Acetaminophen</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>Analgesic with few anti-inflammatory effects. <strong>Max 1,000 mg per dose and 3,000 mg in 24 hours.</strong></li>\n<li>⚠ <strong>Liver damage.</strong> Watch combination products (Percocet, cold medicines) and check labels. Alcohol and barbiturates increase hepatotoxicity. Monitor AST, ALT, and bilirubin.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout watch\"><div class=\"chead\">NSAIDs and aspirin</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>Block COX and prostaglandins (<strong>anti-inflammatory</strong>).</li>\n<li>⚠ <strong>COX-1 inhibition causes GI ulcers and bleeding</strong>, even short term.</li>\n<li>⚠ <strong>Renal problems</strong> (caution in CKD and older adults). Avoid in the 3rd trimester.</li>\n<li>⚠ <strong>Aspirin inhibits platelets</strong>, causing bleeding and thrombocytopenia.</li>\n<li>⚠ <strong>OTC NSAIDs increase warfarin&#39;s effect</strong> (warfarin teaching).</li>\n<li><strong>COX-2 selective (celecoxib):</strong> ⚠ MI and stroke risk (Vioxx was withdrawn).</li>\n<li><strong>Older adults (Beers):</strong> avoid chronic NSAIDs (GI bleed risk, especially over 75 or with anticoagulants or steroids). Avoid NSAIDs in symptomatic heart failure.</li>\n<li><strong>Ketorolac:</strong> bleeding risk with anticoagulants, renal impairment with ACE inhibitors.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"pain--opioids\"><h2>Opioids<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#pain--opioids\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><aside class=\"callout watch\"><div class=\"chead\">Opioids</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li>For <strong>moderate to severe acute pain</strong> (post-op, trauma). Less effective for chronic pain and should not be started for it. For acute pain, prescribe short courses (less than 1 week). Work better combined with NSAIDs.</li>\n<li><strong>Morphine is the gold standard.</strong> Hydromorphone is <strong>5× morphine.</strong> <strong>Fentanyl is about 100× morphine</strong> (IV, or a transdermal patch for chronic cancer pain). Oxycodone (OxyContin) is highly addictive.</li>\n<li>⚠ <strong>RESPIRATORY DEPRESSION is the most critical adverse effect</strong> (from <strong>mu receptors</strong>). Monitor sedation and respirations, especially after IV doses and in the first 24 hours for opioid-naïve patients.</li>\n<li>⚠ <strong>Constipation</strong> (mu receptors, ↓GI motility). Stool softeners and laxatives are almost always needed. More common in older adults.</li>\n<li>⚠ Nausea and vomiting (aspiration risk post-op), urinary retention, pruritus, <strong>confusion and delirium in older adults</strong> (more common than respiratory depression), and paradoxical ↑pain in chronic use.</li>\n<li><strong>Tolerance</strong> = needing a higher dose for the same effect. <strong>Addiction</strong> = compulsive use despite harm. They are not the same. Fewer than 5% of cancer patients become addicted.</li>\n<li><strong>PCA:</strong> a low background dose plus patient-controlled boluses within preset limits.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"pain--adjuvants-and-other-options\"><h2>Adjuvants and other options<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#pain--adjuvants-and-other-options\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><ul>\n<li><strong>Corticosteroids:</strong> anti-inflammatory, used as adjuvants in cancer pain.</li>\n<li><strong>Local anesthetics:</strong> <strong>lidocaine</strong> (onset 10 minutes, lasts 2 hours) and <strong>bupivacaine</strong> (more potent, CNS and cardiac toxicity). ⚠ <strong>Lidocaine with epinephrine is NOT used on fingers, toes, or the penis</strong> (necrosis). <strong>EMLA</strong> is applied 45–60 minutes before a needle stick.</li>\n<li><strong>Neuropathic pain:</strong> gabapentin, pregabalin, TCAs (in older adults use nortriptyline or desipramine, and get an ECG), duloxetine (SNRI).</li>\n<li>Anticonvulsants (carbamazepine, phenytoin) can increase warfarin&#39;s effect.</li>\n<li><strong>Ketamine</strong> (NMDA blocker) for resistant pain. Clonidine intrathecal or epidural. Baclofen.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubefacients and capsaicin: not on radiated skin.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>WHO ladder (Ch 26):</strong> nonopioid → moderate opioid plus nonopioid → strong opioid plus nonopioid. <strong>Give around the clock for cancer pain.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nonpharmacologic:</strong> heat and cold, relaxation, massage, exercise, guided imagery, music, CBT, self-management education.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<aside class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"chead\"><span class=\"tag\">Class note</span> Class: slide 22</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Know why we use opioids vs nonopioids. <strong>Nonopioids</strong> (acetaminophen, NSAIDs) for mild pain and anti-inflammation; <strong>opioids</strong> for moderate to severe pain; in cancer, use the WHO ladder and dose around the clock. Know your pain medications.</p>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"pain--more-from-your-first-study-guide\"><h2>More from your first study guide<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#pain--more-from-your-first-study-guide\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><ul>\n<li><strong>Why pain matters:</strong> most universal symptom; 1 of 3 ED chief complaints; 60% of patients report pain at primary care visits; costs $100 billion per year.</li>\n<li><strong>Acute vs chronic (slide):</strong> acute alerts and prompts withdrawal, avoidance, predictable in course, intensity improves. Chronic: months–years, sustained or spontaneous, disrupts normal activities, unpredictable, inconsistently responsive to treatment.</li>\n<li><strong>Chronic malignant (cancer) pain:</strong> rapidly dividing cells invade bone/tissue; <strong>&lt;5% become addicted</strong> to opioid therapy. <strong>Breakthrough pain:</strong> bursts on top of controlled pain. Neuropathic: burning, shooting, tingling; <strong>worse at night</strong> (chemo neuropathy, phantom limb).</li>\n<li><strong>Why two people feel the same injury differently:</strong> prior experience, expectations, anxiety, sociocultural factors, gender, genetics; older adults: mixed evidence, decrease in acute pain.</li>\n<li><strong>Scales:</strong> numeric 0–10 · verbal best for older adults/limited vision · <strong>FACES for preverbal</strong> · <strong>PAINAD for cognitive impairment.</strong> Self-report = gold standard; <strong>score ≥4 requires a nursing response.</strong> Goal = improve comfort and function, not zero pain.</li>\n<li><strong>Referred pain:</strong> heart → left chest, left shoulder/arm, jaw · liver and gallbladder → right shoulder/scapula, right upper abdomen · lungs and diaphragm → neck and shoulder · stomach and pancreas → epigastric (pancreas also mid-back) · small intestine, appendix, colon → around umbilicus/lower abdomen · kidneys, ureters, bladder → flank, groin, lower abdomen, low back · ovary → lower abdomen/pelvis.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Medication table (slide 22: know why we use opioids vs nonopioids)</h3>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Drug</th>\n<th>Key facts</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Nonopioids</strong></td>\n<td>Mild–moderate pain (WHO step 1) and inflammatory pain. <strong>Kept at every step</strong> to potentiate opioids and lower opioid dose. No respiratory depression or addiction. Limits: GI bleed, kidney injury, bleeding (NSAIDs); liver (APAP)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Acetaminophen</strong></td>\n<td>Max 3,000 mg/24 h (1,000 mg/dose); liver toxicity; hidden APAP in Percocet, Vicodin, cold meds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Aspirin</strong></td>\n<td>Inhibits platelet aggregation → bleeding; ↑warfarin effect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>NSAIDs</strong> (ibuprofen, naproxen, ketorolac)</td>\n<td>COX-1 block → GI ulcers/bleeding; kidney injury; ↑bleeding with warfarin; can cause thrombocytopenia. <strong>Ketorolac max 5 days, 40 mg/24 h.</strong> Don&#39;t combine with steroids</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>COX-2 (celecoxib)</strong></td>\n<td>Spares stomach; slow onset (chronic OA); ↑MI/stroke risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Corticosteroids</strong></td>\n<td>Anti-inflammatory; bone pain, cancer pain adjuvant. Hyperglycemia; taper</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Morphine</strong></td>\n<td>Gold standard opioid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hydromorphone (Dilaudid)</strong></td>\n<td>5× morphine; good with kidney/liver impairment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Fentanyl</strong></td>\n<td>~100× morphine; patch for long-term cancer pain (24–72 h to work)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Oxycodone</strong></td>\n<td>Potent, highly addictive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Adjuvants</strong></td>\n<td>Neuropathic: gabapentin, pregabalin, duloxetine, amitriptyline (Elavil); ketamine (Ketalar); lidocaine; clonidine</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><ul>\n<li><strong>Mu receptor</strong> = respiratory depression, miosis, euphoria, ↓GI motility. Opioids → start a bowel regimen (laxative + stool softener). Older adults: <strong>start low, go slow; avoid meperidine.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Rubefacients:</strong> menthol ointments, cause local vasodilation, cool sensation. Local anesthetics topical or injected.</li>\n<li><strong>WHO principles:</strong> analgesics at regular intervals · according to pain intensity · goal: functional quality of life · individualized dosing · evaluate response. <strong>Ladder:</strong> Step 1 mild: nonopioid (APAP, NSAIDs) ± adjuvant · Step 2 moderate: weak opioid (codeine, hydrocodone, oxycodone) + nonopioid ± adjuvant · Step 3 severe: strong opioid (morphine, fentanyl) + nonopioid ± adjuvant. Give at regular intervals, not &quot;on demand&quot;; oral first; PCA = basal + bolus with lockout.</li>\n<li><strong>Heat/cold:</strong> cold for acute strains and back spasm (20–30 min); heat for back pain without spasm, cramps (5–15 min). Nonpharm: distraction (music, guided imagery, hypnosis, prayer), massage, positioning, TENS, relaxation, deep brain stimulation (also tx for PD tremors).</li>\n<li><strong>Older adults:</strong> underdiagnosed, undertreated → depression, anxiety, impaired mobility, social isolation, falls, sleep disturbance, malnutrition. Cognitive impairment: self-report, cognition tests, behavior observation.</li>\n<li><strong>Care planning:</strong> therapeutic partnership; assess pain and functional status; multidisciplinary team (PT, pharmacist, social worker); measure effectiveness (sleep length and quality).</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"pain--chapter-11-powerpoint-word-for-word\"><h2>Chapter 11 PowerPoint, word for word<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#pain--chapter-11-powerpoint-word-for-word\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><p>Your instructor&#39;s slides for this chapter, kept in slide order.</p>\n\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 1 · Why pain matters</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Most universal symptom · Significant public health problem 1 of 3 ED chief complaints is pain · 60% of patients report pain at primary care visits Costs $100 billion per year</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 2 · Gate control theory</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Two brain–spinal cord diagrams. Left: high-intensity pain goes up the ascending pathway, gate is open.</p>\n<p>Right: emotion, motivation, memory, and cognition send signals down the inhibitory descending pathway, gate is closed.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 3 · Classifying pain by duration</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Acute pain – short duration, hours to weeks, sudden onset, alerts and prompts withdrawal, avoidance, predictable in course; intensity improves Chronic pain – long term, months–years, sustained or spontaneous, disrupts normal activities, unpredictable in course intensity, inconsistently responsive to both common and adjuvant pain management strategies</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 4 · Classifying pain by origin</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Nociceptive pain: cutaneous, somatic, visceral · Neuropathic pain</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 5 · How pain signals travel and become chronic</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Injury in the hand → peripheral nociceptor → ascending pathway in the spinal cord → brain perceives pain → descending pathway back down.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 6 · Why two people feel the same injury differently</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Prior experience with pain · Expectations of pain · Anxiety Sociocultural determinants: values and expectations, access to healthcare resources, language barriers Gender: women have lower pain thresholds, rate noxious stimuli as more painful, have lower tolerance for intense pain; differences present after puberty; men and women respond differently to analgesia Genetics: pain threshold, pain tolerance, response to analgesics Pain and older adults: mixed evidence on prevalence and pattern of pain; decrease in acute pain</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 7 · The 0–10 scale and its cousins</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>0–10 scale · Measure of pain intensity Numeric rating scales · Verbal rating scales · Visual analog scales</p>\n<p>Figure: Simple Descriptive Scale, 0–10 Numeric Scale, Visual Analog Scale (VAS), and a pain thermometer</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 8 · OPQRST-AAA + physical exam</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>OPQRST-AAA: Onset · Quality · Region and radiation · Severity · Time and duration · Aggravating/alleviating factors and associated clinical manifestations Physical exam: range of motion, neurological assessment, motor function Multidisciplinary team: physical therapist, pharmacist, social worker</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 9 · Partnership, care planning &amp; nursing diagnoses</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Patient and nurse – the therapeutic partnership: assessment of pain, assessment of functional status, interviewing Care-planning process: data collection, evaluation of data, form diagnostic hypotheses Nursing diagnoses (full list below)</p>\n<p>Measuring effectiveness of care: review of laboratory values, structured questioning, length and quality of sleep</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 10 · NSAIDs, aspirin &amp; acetaminophen</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Pharmacological options – nonopioid analgesics: NSAIDs Monitoring risks and benefits: prostaglandin synthesis – chemicals released that stimulate inflammation; COX-1 &amp; COX-2 COX-1 – harmful to GI, disrupts protective lining · COX-2 – both anti-inflammatory &amp; antipyretic Inhibit platelet aggregation · Thrombocytopenia – reduced platelet count</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 11 · Anti-inflammatory steroids</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Pharmacological options – corticosteroids: anti-inflammatory</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 12 · Numb the area, stay awake</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Local anesthetics: topically applied; injected into nerves and tissue</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 13 · Counterirritants and capsaicin</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Topical rubefacients: topically applied menthol-containing ointments; cause local vasodilation; produce cool sensation</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 14 · Receptors, potency &amp; side effects</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Opioids: bind to opioid receptors in the brain; available via many routes</p>\n<p>Figure (continued_6): relative potency chart of opioids and NSAIDs compared with morphine, from most potent (top) to least potent (bottom)</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 15 · Adjuvants for tough pain</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Clonidine · Tricyclic antidepressants – Elavil · Glutamate receptor antagonist (principal excitatory neurotransmitter in CNS) – Ketalar · Ion-channel blockers – lidocaine</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 16 · Choosing the right drug and route</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Decision making for effective dose, route, and medication choice: best pain relief, fewest adverse effects</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 17 · WHO principles &amp; three-step ladder</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>WHO principles: analgesics given at regular intervals; administered according to pain intensity; goal is to improve functional quality of life; individualized dosing; attention to dose and response; evaluation of therapeutic response WHO three-step ladder (figure)</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 18 · Positioning, heat/cold, rom, massage</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Positioning · Heat and cold · Range of motion and exercise</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 19 · Refocusing attention</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Music therapy · Guided imagery · Hypnosis · Prayer</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 20 · TENS, DBS &amp; expectancy</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation · Deep brain stimulation – lead is placed subcutaneously; also tx for PD tremors</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slide 21 · Older adults &amp; cognitive impairment</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Older adults: underdiagnosed, undertreated, ineffective management → depression, anxiety, impaired mobility, social isolation, falls, sleep disturbance, reduced food and fluid intake, malnutrition Adults with cognitive or communication impairments: self-report, tests of cognition, observations of behavior, changes in routine behavior</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"callout slides\"><summary><span class=\"tag\">Slide</span> Slides · Image-only slides (written out)</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p>[Image: Common areas of referred pain] Heart → left chest, left shoulder/arm, jaw · Liver &amp; gallbladder → right shoulder/scapula, right upper abdomen · Lungs &amp; diaphragm → neck and shoulder · Stomach &amp; pancreas → epigastric (pancreas also mid-back) · Small intestine, appendix, colon → around the umbilicus/lower abdomen · Kidneys, ureters, bladder → flank, groin, lower abdomen, low back · Ovary → lower abdomen/pelvis.</p>\n</div></details></section>"},{"id":"cheat","title":"Tell-Them-Apart Cheat Sheets","part":"Part 10","summary":"Tumor markers, must-know numbers, precautions side by side, metastasis sites, electrolyte and CBC values from class.","sections":[{"id":"cheat--tumor-markers-and-signature-labs","title":"Tumor markers and \"signature\" labs"},{"id":"cheat--must-know-numbers","title":"Must-know numbers"},{"id":"cheat--precautions-side-by-side","title":"Precautions side by side"},{"id":"cheat--painless-vs-painful-masses","title":"Painless vs painful masses"},{"id":"cheat--which-cancers-spread-where","title":"Which cancers spread where"},{"id":"cheat--lab-values-from-class-nur-291","title":"Lab values from class (NUR 291)"}],"html":"<section id=\"cheat--tell-them-apart-cheat-sheets\"><p>High-yield comparisons gathered from all the chapters.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"cheat--tumor-markers-and-signature-labs\"><h2>Tumor markers and &quot;signature&quot; labs<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#cheat--tumor-markers-and-signature-labs\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Marker / test</th>\n<th>Cancer / disorder</th>\n<th>Remember</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>PSA</strong></td>\n<td>Prostate (and BPH, prostatitis)</td>\n<td>≥4 ng/mL needs workup. High sensitivity, low specificity. Can&#39;t tell BPH from cancer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CA-125</strong></td>\n<td>Ovarian</td>\n<td>Normal in 50% of early cases. Also rises with endometriosis, fibroids, cirrhosis, diverticulitis. Not a screening test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>AFP + hCG</strong></td>\n<td>Testicular</td>\n<td>Should fall after orchiectomy. If they don&#39;t, look for more tumor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Bence Jones protein</strong> (urine), <strong>M protein</strong></td>\n<td>Multiple myeloma</td>\n<td>Light chains are toxic to the kidney. CRAB criteria</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Reed–Sternberg cells</strong></td>\n<td>Hodgkin&#39;s lymphoma</td>\n<td>Diagnosed by lymph node biopsy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Philadelphia chromosome</strong></td>\n<td>CML (and some B-ALL)</td>\n<td>Chromosome 9;22 swap in &gt;90% of CML</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>JAK2 mutation</strong></td>\n<td>Polycythemia vera</td>\n<td>About 90%. ↑RBC, WBC, and platelets with a low EPO level</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>↓Ferritin, ↑TIBC, ↓MCV</strong></td>\n<td>Iron deficiency anemia</td>\n<td>Microcytic and hypochromic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>↑MMA</strong> (normal homocysteine)</td>\n<td>B₁₂ deficiency</td>\n<td>Macrocytic plus neuro signs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>↑Homocysteine</strong> (normal MMA)</td>\n<td>Folate deficiency</td>\n<td>Macrocytic, no lasting neuro deficits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>↑PT/PTT, ↓fibrinogen, ↑D-dimer</strong></td>\n<td>DIC</td>\n<td>Clots everywhere, then bleeds everywhere</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>↑Eosinophils</strong></td>\n<td>Allergy (type I)</td>\n<td>Normal 1–2%. Up to 12% with severe allergic rhinitis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Heinz bodies, ↑retics</strong></td>\n<td>G6PD deficiency</td>\n<td>Hemolysis</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></section>\n<section id=\"cheat--must-know-numbers\"><h2>Must-know numbers<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#cheat--must-know-numbers\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Number</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>ANC &lt;1,500</strong></td>\n<td>Neutropenia (infection risk)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ANC ≤500</strong></td>\n<td>Neutropenic precautions. Fever means IV antibiotics now</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Temp ≥100.4°F / 38°C</strong></td>\n<td>Call the provider (chemo, neutropenia)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Platelets &lt;150,000</strong></td>\n<td>Thrombocytopenia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>&lt;100,000 / &lt;50,000</strong></td>\n<td>Start bleeding precautions / spontaneous bleeding possible, no sex</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>&lt;20,000</strong></td>\n<td>Spontaneous bleeding. Platelets usually transfused</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>&lt;5,000</strong></td>\n<td>Fatal CNS or GI bleed possible</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SpO₂ &lt;90%</strong></td>\n<td>Significant oxygenation problem. Goal ≥90%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hct &lt;45%</strong></td>\n<td>PV phlebotomy goal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ca &gt;11 / &gt;11.5 mg/dL</strong></td>\n<td>Hypercalcemia (lymphoma / myeloma CRAB)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>INR 2–3 / 2.5–3.5</strong></td>\n<td>Warfarin goal / mechanical valve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Acetaminophen 3,000 mg</strong></td>\n<td>24-hour max (1,000 mg per dose)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Gleason 2–10</strong></td>\n<td>Two grades (1–5) added. Higher is worse</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>RBC life 120 days</strong></td>\n<td>Sickle cell RBC 15–20 days. Platelets 7–10 days</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></section>\n<section id=\"cheat--precautions-side-by-side\"><h2>Precautions side by side<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#cheat--precautions-side-by-side\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Neutropenic (↓WBC)</th>\n<th>Bleeding (↓platelets)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Hand hygiene, private room</td>\n<td>Soft toothbrush, no floss</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Avoid crowds, sick people, small kids, pets. Mask outside</td>\n<td>Electric razor, shoes on, clutter-free</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No fresh flowers or plants, no standing water</td>\n<td>Stool softener, don&#39;t blow your nose</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wash produce. No raspberries or blackberries</td>\n<td>No IM injections. Minimal BP cuff pressure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Daily temperature. Fever ≥100.4°F means call or go now</td>\n<td>Pressure or ice ≥5 minutes for cuts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>BOTH:</strong> no rectal temperatures, suppositories, or enemas</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></section>\n<section id=\"cheat--painless-vs-painful-masses\"><h2>Painless vs painful masses<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#cheat--painless-vs-painful-masses\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th></th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Painless</strong></td>\n<td>Breast cancer lump (typically), lymphoma nodes, testicular cancer, malignant lymph nodes (less tender than infected ones)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pain is the clue</strong></td>\n<td>Myeloma (back and bone), leukemia (bone), sickle cell crisis, prostate cancer (late), spinal cord compression (back pain first)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></section>\n<section id=\"cheat--which-cancers-spread-where\"><h2>Which cancers spread where<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#cheat--which-cancers-spread-where\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Cancer</th>\n<th>Common metastasis sites</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Breast</td>\n<td>Bone, lung, lymph nodes, liver, brain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lung</td>\n<td>Brain, bone, adrenal gland, other lung</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prostate</td>\n<td>Bone, lymph nodes, rectum, bladder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ovarian</td>\n<td>Peritoneum, omentum, pleura</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cervical</td>\n<td>Endometrium, vagina, pelvic wall</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Testicular</td>\n<td>Lymph nodes (back of the abdomen), lung, liver, bone, brain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Uterine</td>\n<td>Lymph nodes, vagina, abdominal cavity, omentum</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></section>\n<section id=\"cheat--lab-values-from-class-nur-291\"><h2>Lab values from class (NUR 291)<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#cheat--lab-values-from-class-nur-291\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><p><em>Need to know lab numbers for exams (electrolytes, CBC).</em></p>\n<h3>Electrolytes</h3>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Electrolyte</th>\n<th>Normal</th>\n<th>Too low / too high</th>\n<th>Memory trick</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Sodium (Na⁺)</strong></td>\n<td>135–145 mEq/L</td>\n<td>&lt;135 hyponatremia · &gt;145 hypernatremia</td>\n<td>Commit to memory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Potassium (K⁺)</strong></td>\n<td>3.5–5.0 mEq/L</td>\n<td>&lt;3.5 hypokalemia · &gt;5.0 hyperkalemia</td>\n<td>A bunch of bananas has 3–5, and you want them half ripe (.5): 3.5–5.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Phosphorus (P)</strong></td>\n<td>2.5–4.5 mg/dL</td>\n<td>&lt;2.5 hypophosphatemia · &gt;4.5 hyperphosphatemia</td>\n<td>&quot;PHOR&quot; = 4, &quot;us&quot; = 2 (me + you); don&#39;t forget the .5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Calcium (Ca²⁺)</strong></td>\n<td>9–11 mg/dL</td>\n<td>&lt;9 hypocalcemia · &gt;11 hypercalcemia</td>\n<td>Call 911</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Magnesium (Mg²⁺)</strong></td>\n<td>1.5–2.5 mg/dL</td>\n<td>&lt;1.5 hypomagnesemia · &gt;2.5 hypermagnesemia</td>\n<td>MAGnifying glass: 1.5–2.5, a little bigger than normal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Chloride (Cl⁻)</strong></td>\n<td>95–105 mEq/L</td>\n<td>&lt;95 hypochloremia · &gt;105 hyperchloremia</td>\n<td>A chlorinated pool on a 95–105°F day</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p><strong>Where these show up in this guide:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Calcium:</strong> hypercalcemia from bone mets, lymphoma, and myeloma (CRAB &gt;11.5), DiGeorge hypocalcemia (tetany), tumor lysis hypocalcemia.</li>\n<li><strong>Potassium and phosphorus:</strong> tumor lysis syndrome raises both (peaked T waves, heart block).</li>\n<li><strong>Sodium:</strong> SIADH from brain and lung cancer causes dilutional hyponatremia (confusion, seizures).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Complete blood count (class) ★</h3>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Lab</th>\n<th>Memory hook</th>\n<th>Normal (class)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>WBC</strong></td>\n<td>Wanna Buy a Car</td>\n<td>5,000–10,000/mcL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>RBC</strong></td>\n<td>Blood</td>\n<td>4.5–5.5 million/mcL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hemoglobin</strong></td>\n<td>Oxygen</td>\n<td>12–18 g/dL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hematocrit</strong></td>\n<td>Hemato-&quot;crisis&quot;</td>\n<td>37–50%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Platelets</strong></td>\n<td>China plates</td>\n<td>150K–400K/mcL</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"chead\"><span class=\"tag\">Class note</span> Class vs book</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p>Your class CBC sheet and the textbook (Table 33.7) use slightly different ranges.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>WBC:</strong> class 5,000–10,000 vs book 4,500–11,100</li>\n<li><strong>Platelets:</strong> class 150K–400K vs book 150K–450K</li>\n<li><strong>Hgb:</strong> class 12–18 vs book F 11.7–15.5, M 14–17.3</li>\n<li><strong>Hct:</strong> class 37–50% vs book F 36–48%, M 42–52%</li>\n<li><strong>RBC:</strong> class 4.5–5.5 million vs book F 4.01–5.51, M 4.51–6.01</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Exam questions usually use values clearly outside both ranges, but ask your instructor which set the exam uses.</p>\n</div></aside></section>"},{"id":"answers","title":"Practice Question Answer Key","part":"Part 11","summary":"Case study and Connection Check questions from your slides and chapters, plus your class PLQ answers. Click a question to reveal the answer.","sections":[{"id":"answers--chapter-13-cancer-overview","title":"Chapter 13 · Cancer overview"},{"id":"answers--chapter-26-lung-and-asthma","title":"Chapter 26 · Lung and asthma"},{"id":"answers--chapter-33-heme-assessment","title":"Chapter 33 · Heme assessment"},{"id":"answers--chapter-34-heme-disorders","title":"Chapter 34 · Heme disorders"},{"id":"answers--chapters-18-19-immune","title":"Chapters 18–19 · Immune"},{"id":"answers--chapter-64-repro-assessment","title":"Chapter 64 · Repro assessment"},{"id":"answers--chapter-65-female-cancers","title":"Chapter 65 · Female cancers"},{"id":"answers--chapter-66-male-disorders","title":"Chapter 66 · Male disorders"},{"id":"answers--chapter-11-pain","title":"Chapter 11 · Pain"},{"id":"answers--chapter-20-ms-logan-ra-case-study","title":"Chapter 20 · Ms. Logan (RA) case study"}],"html":"<section id=\"answers--practice-question-answer-key\"><p>The slides don&#39;t show answers, so these are worked out from your textbook content, each with the reason. Double-check any you&#39;re unsure of with your instructor. Click a question to reveal its answer.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"answers--chapter-13-cancer-overview\"><h2>Chapter 13 · Cancer overview<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#answers--chapter-13-cancer-overview\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"qa\"><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Cancer is the ___ most common cause of death in the US.</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>B. Second.</strong></div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Benign vs malignant cells?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C.</strong> Malignant cells lack contact inhibition.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Key lab for cancer with bone metastasis?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>B. Calcium</strong> (hypercalcemia).</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Most specific way to diagnose malignancy?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>D. Biopsy.</strong></div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Chemo before definitive surgery is?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C. Neoadjuvant.</strong></div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Which statement shows chemo teaching worked?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>B.</strong> &quot;It is important that I monitor my temperature.&quot;</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Mrs. Jones, stage III?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C.</strong> Large, locally advanced, possibly in local nodes. (Class notes said D, &quot;spread to lymph and blood systems.&quot; The book says C. Confirm with your instructor.)</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Oral hormonal therapy after mastectomy?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>B. Adjuvant.</strong></div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Call the provider immediately for?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C. Fever</strong> (myelosuppression).</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Before first paclitaxel (SATA)?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>B, D</strong> (and <strong>C</strong> per class): emergency equipment ready (hypersensitivity), check IV blood return (irritant/vesicant), and ECG within 24 hours (class adds this; paclitaxel can affect heart rhythm). Go with B, C, D.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Left chest wall radiation, which systems (SATA)?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>B, D, E</strong> (and <strong>C</strong> per class): integumentary (radiodermatitis), cardiac (late cardiac risk), respiratory (pneumonitis), plus musculoskeletal (shoulder stiffness and fibrosis). Go with B, C, D, E.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Class PLQ 1: Regulated cell death that cancer cells lose is called?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C. Apoptosis.</strong></div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Class PLQ 2: Stage III T3, N2, M1: which statement needs more teaching?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>A.</strong> &quot;I am relieved that the cancer is still local.&quot; M1 = metastasis present, so it is not local.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Class PLQ 3: Leukemia patient with chemo neutropenia: include in the plan?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C.</strong> Restricting visitors with communicable diseases.</div></details></div></section>\n<section id=\"answers--chapter-26-lung-and-asthma\"><h2>Chapter 26 · Lung and asthma<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#answers--chapter-26-lung-and-asthma\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"qa\"><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>When to start palliative care in lung cancer?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>A. Upon diagnosis.</strong></div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Lung cancer plan of care (SATA)?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>A, B, D:</strong> education, frequent pain assessment, frequent oxygenation checks.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Mr. Davis, most important part of management?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>B.</strong> Disease management through education.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>No wheezes and diminished breath sounds?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C.</strong> Partially obstructed airway (not moving air). <strong>Emergency.</strong></div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Priority with a suspected partial obstruction?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>A. Airway management.</strong></div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Major part of his treatment program?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>A. Smoking cessation.</strong></div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Which statement needs more teaching?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C.</strong> &quot;I can stop all my meds when my asthma is under control.&quot;</div></details></div></section>\n<section id=\"answers--chapter-33-heme-assessment\"><h2>Chapter 33 · Heme assessment<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#answers--chapter-33-heme-assessment\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"qa\"><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>IDA manifestations (SATA)?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>D, E:</strong> ↑HR, ↑RR.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Result that confirms IDA?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>A. Decreased transferrin saturation</strong> (&quot;always low&quot; in IDA). TIBC is ↑, ferritin ↓, MCV ↓ too.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Compensatory finding in IDA?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>A.</strong> Increased respiratory rate.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Mouth finding in IDA?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C.</strong> Red, swollen, tender tongue (glossitis).</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Serum iron instructions?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>B.</strong> Drawn in the morning (no iron 24 hours before).</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Appropriate H&amp;P action?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C.</strong> Inspect oral mucosa and tongue (never deeply palpate the spleen).</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>A differential tells you?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>B.</strong> The proportion of each WBC type.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Priority goal with thrombocytopenia?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>B. Prevention of injury.</strong></div></details></div></section>\n<section id=\"answers--chapter-34-heme-disorders\"><h2>Chapter 34 · Heme disorders<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#answers--chapter-34-heme-disorders\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"qa\"><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Greatest IDA risk?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>B.</strong> 15-year-old who is pregnant.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Higher risk of secondary polycythemia?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C. Smoking.</strong></div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Action for thrombocytopenia?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>A. Avoid IM injections.</strong></div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Result that raises infection risk in leukemia?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>B. ANC 400.</strong></div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Most essential for a lymphoma patient starting chemo?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>D.</strong> Neutropenic and bleeding precautions.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Mr. Owens, highest leukemia risk?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C.</strong> Oil refinery work (benzene).</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>CBC result that correlates with leukemia?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>D.</strong> Platelets 97,000 (crowded marrow, thrombocytopenia).</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>First priority after chemo (ANC 0.6)?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>D. Infection.</strong></div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Needs more bleeding-precaution teaching?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>B.</strong> &quot;I have an appointment to get my teeth cleaned.&quot;</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Go to the ED now (SATA)?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>A, B, C:</strong> temperature 102°F, chills, nosebleed &gt;10 minutes.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Class PLQ: What all leukemias have in common?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>A.</strong> All involve unregulated proliferation of white blood cells.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Class PLQ: IDA menu that shows teaching worked?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>A.</strong> Roast beef, steamed spinach, tomato soup, and orange juice (heme iron + vitamin C).</div></details></div></section>\n<section id=\"answers--chapters-18-19-immune\"><h2>Chapters 18–19 · Immune<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#answers--chapters-18-19-immune\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"qa\"><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Central lymphoid organs?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>D. Thymus and bone marrow.</strong></div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Needs more bone marrow biopsy teaching?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C.</strong> &quot;Odds are I will die from this procedure.&quot;</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Most likely XLA?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C.</strong> 7-month-old male with recurrent infections.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Monitor in XLA?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>B.</strong> Recurrent respiratory infections.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Prophylactic antibiotics in DiGeorge?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>B.</strong> Prevent life-threatening infections.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Low IgG correlates with?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C. HIGM syndrome</strong> (hyper-IgM).</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Severe anaphylaxis finding?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>A. Hypotension.</strong></div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>How epinephrine helps?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>A.</strong> Dilates bronchioles, constricts vessels, improves cardiac contraction.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Teaching priority after bee-sting anaphylaxis?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C. EpiPen use.</strong></div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Mr. Smith (PCN anaphylaxis): nursing diagnosis?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>A.</strong> Risk for ineffective gas exchange.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Mr. Smith: priority assessment?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C. Respiratory status.</strong></div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Mr. Smith: priority action?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C.</strong> Discontinue the IV penicillin.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Mr. Smith: allergic reaction finding?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>D.</strong> Hypotension and tachycardia.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Mr. Smith: what shows it is progressing?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>A. Circulatory failure.</strong></div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Class PLQ 1: Expected action of an immunoglobulin infusion?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>D.</strong> They enhance the recognition of antigens.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Class PLQ 2: Humoral vs cell-mediated immunity?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>D.</strong> Humoral = B cells, in charge of antibodies; T cells directly destroy infected or abnormal cells.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Class PLQ 3: Primary immune deficiency warning signs (SATA)?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>D, E, F:</strong> frequent, severe infections needing hospitalization · persistent lymphadenopathy · failure to thrive.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Class PLQ 4: CATCH (DiGeorge) components (SATA)?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>D, E, F, G, H:</strong> cardiac defects · abnormal facies · thymic hypoplasia · cleft palate · hypocalcemia.</div></details></div></section>\n<section id=\"answers--chapter-64-repro-assessment\"><h2>Chapter 64 · Repro assessment<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#answers--chapter-64-repro-assessment\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"qa\"><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Main site of sexual pleasure?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>A. Clitoris.</strong></div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Luteal phase hormones?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>B.</strong> ↑Progesterone and estrogen.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Negative feedback in spermatogenesis?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>D. Inhibin.</strong></div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Repro assessment includes (SATA)?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>A, D, E:</strong> history, physical, labs such as PSA.</div></details></div></section>\n<section id=\"answers--chapter-65-female-cancers\"><h2>Chapter 65 · Female cancers<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#answers--chapter-65-female-cancers\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"qa\"><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Patient with LCIS: schedule which?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C. Mammogram</strong> (no treatment, just surveillance).</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Priority follow-up call?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C.</strong> Full and bloated, hard to eat (ovarian).</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>60-year-old most concerning complaint?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>D. Vaginal bleeding</strong> (uterine).</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Age 25, last Pap at 21, no primary HPV available?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>A.</strong> Breast exam, pelvic exam, and Pap smear (no HPV cotest before 30).</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>After breast conservation surgery?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>A.</strong> Chemo, then radiation.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Order to verify?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>B. Oral contraceptives</strong> (ER+ tumor).</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Most concerning wound finding?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>A.</strong> Bloody drainage saturating the dressing.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Pain assessment includes (SATA)?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>A, B, D:</strong> pain scale, HR, BP.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Radiation side effect to monitor?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>D.</strong> Redness, itching, irritation of the radiated skin.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Neoadjuvant chemo?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>B.</strong> Before surgery to help complete excision.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Question which order after mastectomy (receptor-negative)?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>A. Antiestrogen therapy.</strong></div></details></div></section>\n<section id=\"answers--chapter-66-male-disorders\"><h2>Chapter 66 · Male disorders<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#answers--chapter-66-male-disorders\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"qa\"><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>True of BPH?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>B.</strong> It is not cancer.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Expected BPH finding?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C.</strong> Frequent nocturia.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Why a catheter in BPH (SATA)?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C, D:</strong> evaluate PVR, empty the bladder completely.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Late-stage prostate cancer with no urinary trouble?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>A.</strong> Tumors grow at the edges (periphery) of the prostate.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Best testicular cancer treatment?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>D.</strong> Remove the affected testicle.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Groin injury in football?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>A. Blunt trauma.</strong></div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Scott: why BPH causes symptoms?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>B.</strong> Obstruction of urine flow.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Scott: why watchful waiting?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>A.</strong> Minimal symptoms and enlargement.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Scott: needs more teaching?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C.</strong> &quot;Glad I can take my decongestants.&quot;</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Scott: Flomax?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>A. Relaxes the prostate muscle.</strong></div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Scott: report pain, fever, N/V?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>D.</strong> Urine stasis can cause a UTI.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Class PLQ 1: Cancer client with fever, malaise, and a high WBC: what principle guides care?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>B.</strong> Infection is most likely.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Class PLQ 2: Client with a stage IV tumor designation</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>D.</strong> (Question text wasn&#39;t in your notes; stage IV = metastasized.)</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Class PLQ 3: (question text not in your notes)</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C, E</strong></div></details></div></section>\n<section id=\"answers--chapter-11-pain\"><h2>Chapter 11 · Pain<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#answers--chapter-11-pain\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"qa\"><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>&quot;What makes the pain worse?&quot; assesses?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>D. Aggravating factors.</strong></div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Warfarin teaching?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>B.</strong> OTC NSAIDs increase the anticoagulant effect.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Respiratory depression and ↓GI motility come from which receptor?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C. Mu.</strong></div></details></div></section>\n<section id=\"answers--chapter-20-ms-logan-ra-case-study\"><h2>Chapter 20 · Ms. Logan (RA) case study<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#answers--chapter-20-ms-logan-ra-case-study\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"qa\"><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Priority to follow up with the provider about?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C. Oxycodone 5 mg q6h prn</strong> (opioids aren&#39;t recommended for chronic RA pain). <em>Blank in your notes; worked out.</em></div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Methotrexate statement showing teaching worked?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C.</strong> &quot;I will use two effective means of birth control.&quot; (teratogenic)</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Nursing diagnosis for her plan of care?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C.</strong> Risk of injury related to unstable gait.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Monitor for after starting methotrexate?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>B.</strong> Elevated liver enzymes.</div></details><details class=\"qaitem\"><summary>Lifestyle changes (SATA)?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><strong>C, E:</strong> ROM exercises for flexibility · aerobic exercise for weight control.</div></details></div></section>"},{"id":"other","title":"Other Exam Topics","part":"Part 12","summary":"Hospice vs palliative care, organ donation, dosage calculation (all on your exam but not in the chapter PDFs).","sections":[{"id":"other--hospice-vs-palliative-care-2-questions","title":"Hospice vs. palliative care · 2 questions"},{"id":"other--organ-donation-keep-organs-viable","title":"Organ donation: keep organs viable"},{"id":"other--dosage-calculation-2-questions","title":"Dosage calculation · 2 questions"}],"html":"<section id=\"other--other-exam-topics\"><p>Topics from your exam guide and purple notes that are not in your chapter PDFs. These are filled in from standard nursing content.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"other--hospice-vs-palliative-care-2-questions\"><h2>Hospice vs. palliative care · 2 questions<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#other--hospice-vs-palliative-care-2-questions\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Palliative care</th>\n<th>Hospice care</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>When</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Any stage</strong>, from diagnosis of a serious illness (Ch 26: starts on day 1)</td>\n<td><strong>End of life:</strong> prognosis of <strong>≤6 months</strong> if the disease runs its usual course</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Curative treatment?</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Yes</strong>, can continue alongside chemo, radiation, surgery</td>\n<td><strong>No:</strong> patient stops curative treatment and chooses comfort care</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Goal</strong></td>\n<td>Relieve symptoms (pain, dyspnea, N/V), improve quality of life for patient and family</td>\n<td>Comfort, dignity, peaceful death; family support including bereavement after death</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Who qualifies</strong></td>\n<td>Anyone with serious illness, any age</td>\n<td>Terminal illness certified by physicians (Medicare hospice benefit)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Where</strong></td>\n<td>Hospital, clinic, home</td>\n<td>Usually home; also hospice facility, nursing home</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout pearl\"><div class=\"chead\">Remember</div><div class=\"cbody\"><p><strong>All hospice is palliative, but not all palliative care is hospice.</strong> &quot;Palliative care is not the same as hospice&quot; (Ch 26). Patients can leave hospice to restart curative treatment.</p>\n</div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout nursing\"><div class=\"chead\">Signs death is near, and nursing care</div><div class=\"cbody\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Signs:</strong> ↓ intake and urine output · mottled, cool extremities · Cheyne-Stokes breathing / apnea periods · noisy secretions (&quot;death rattle&quot;) · ↓ LOC · <strong>hearing is often the last sense lost.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Nursing:</strong> manage pain (around the clock; opioids/morphine for dyspnea), mouth care, repositioning, emotional and spiritual support, honor advance directives/DNR.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></aside></section>\n<section id=\"other--organ-donation-keep-organs-viable\"><h2>Organ donation: keep organs viable<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#other--organ-donation-keep-organs-viable\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><ul>\n<li><strong>Ventilator:</strong> transplant needs viable organs, so after brain death the patient is kept on the ventilator (and hemodynamic support) to keep oxygen and blood flowing to the organs until recovery.</li>\n<li><strong>Brain death</strong> = irreversible loss of all brain function (including brainstem), declared by a physician. The heart can keep beating with ventilator support.</li>\n<li><strong>Maintain organ perfusion:</strong> BP/MAP support (fluids, vasopressors), oxygenation, normal temperature, urine output, electrolytes (<strong>diabetes insipidus</strong> is common).</li>\n<li>Hospitals must notify the <strong>organ procurement organization (OPO)</strong> of every death or imminent death; trained OPO staff usually approach the family.</li>\n<li><strong>Tissues</strong> (corneas, skin, bone, heart valves) can be donated after cardiac death; most solid organs need a perfused donor.</li>\n<li>Family pays no donation costs; open-casket funeral is still possible; most major religions support donation.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"other--dosage-calculation-2-questions\"><h2>Dosage calculation · 2 questions<a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#other--dosage-calculation-2-questions\" aria-label=\"Link to section\">#</a></h2><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Formula</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Basic (desired over have)</strong></td>\n<td>(D ÷ H) × Q</td>\n<td>Order 500 mg; have 250 mg/5 mL → (500 ÷ 250) × 5 = <strong>10 mL</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Weight-based</strong></td>\n<td>mg/kg × kg (lb ÷ 2.2 = kg)</td>\n<td>15 mg/kg for 44 lb → 20 kg × 15 = <strong>300 mg</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>IV pump rate</strong></td>\n<td>mL ÷ hr = mL/hr</td>\n<td>1,000 mL over 8 h = <strong>125 mL/hr</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Gravity drip</strong></td>\n<td>(mL × drop factor) ÷ minutes = gtt/min</td>\n<td>1,000 mL over 8 h, 15 gtt/mL → (1,000 × 15) ÷ 480 = <strong>31 gtt/min</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Infusion time</strong></td>\n<td>Total mL ÷ mL/hr = hours</td>\n<td>500 mL at 100 mL/hr = <strong>5 h</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Concentration (heparin, units)</strong></td>\n<td>Ordered units/hr ÷ units/mL = mL/hr</td>\n<td>1,000 units/hr; 25,000 units/250 mL (100 units/mL) → <strong>10 mL/hr</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div><p><strong>Conversions:</strong> 1 kg = 2.2 lb · 1 g = 1,000 mg · 1 mg = 1,000 mcg · 1 tsp = 5 mL · 1 tbsp = 15 mL · 1 oz = 30 mL. Round per your program&#39;s rules (usually tenths for mL, whole numbers for gtt). <strong>Check units before you calculate.</strong></p>\n\n<div class=\"calcnote\"><p>Practice: use the calculator on the Tools page to check your own dosage problems.</p>\n<a class=\"btn\" href=\"#tools\">Open the dosage calculator</a></div></section>"}],"search":[{"ch":"start","chTitle":"Start Here: Blueprint and Cram Sheet","id":"start--exam-1-blueprint","title":"Exam 1 blueprint","text":"Topics and question counts from your Exam 1 study guide and notes. Topic Qs Where to study it Leukemia: risk factors, medications 5 (notebook says 10) Heme Disorders Prostate cancer (post-op complications) 5 (per notes) Male Reproductive Dosage calculation 2 Other Exam Topics End of life: hospice vs. palliative 2 Other Exam Topics Organ donation not specified Other Exam Topics Breast cancer (male + female) medications not specified Female Cancers, Male Reproductive Labs: CBC, WBC, platelets (+ TIBC, diet, clotting) not specified Heme Assessment, Cheat Sheets Lung cancer not specified Lung Cancer Multiple myeloma: nursing interventions not specified Heme Disorders Cancer risk factors (Table 13.2) not specified Cancer Overview Hodgkin lymphoma: complications, symptoms not specified Heme Disorders Cancer staging: tumor size, TNM (Davis table) not specified Cancer Overview Cervical guidelines not specified Female Cancers Testicular cancer not specified Male Reproductive Biopsy procedures not specified Cancer Overview Uterine cancer not specified Female Cancers Class note Housekeeping from your notes The exam has questions on oncology staging . You need to know lab numbers (electrolytes, CBC). It takes questions from tables , so study every table. Class note Your notes also say to concentrate on Chapters 19 and 20 Immune disorders (Ch 19) and connective tissue disorders (Ch 20: OA, RA, lupus) are in the Immune part. HIV (Ch 22) and bioterrorism are there too."},{"ch":"start","chTitle":"Start Here: Blueprint and Cram Sheet","id":"start--what-the-tags-mean","title":"What the tags mean","text":"Colored boxes on each disease: Why it matters, Patho, Signs and symptoms ★, Diagnosis, Treatment, Watch (side effects and danger signs), Nursing (with → why reasons), Pearl. Purple \"Class note\" boxes hold things your instructor emphasized or that were only in your NUR 291 notes. \"Class vs book\" flags spots where your notes and the textbook disagree. Ask your instructor which one the exam uses. \"Slide vs book\" flags where the slides and the textbook give different numbers. ★ marks high-yield items (starred in your notebook or tested). Every chapter ends with your PowerPoint slides, word for word ."},{"ch":"start","chTitle":"Start Here: Blueprint and Cram Sheet","id":"start--one-page-cram-sheet","title":"One-page cram sheet ★","text":"If you only read one page. Oncology Cancer cells: no apoptosis, no contact inhibition. Carcinogens = #1 risk factor; BRCA1/2 → breast/ovarian. TNM: T size · N nodes · M mets. M1 = not local. Breast sizes: ≤2 · 2–5 · >5 cm. CAUTION >2 weeks → evaluate. Biopsy = only definitive diagnosis. Neo = before · Adjuvant = after. Chemo + fever ≥100.4°F = call now. Palliative = from diagnosis; hospice = ≤6 months, no curative tx. Lung: smoking 90%; NSCLC 85% good; SCLC 15% bad; never clamp chest tube. Breast: age #1 risk; frozen shoulder; lumpectomy → radiation; ER+ → tamoxifen, stop OCPs. Ovarian: bloating, early satiety, no screening, CA-125. Uterine: unopposed estrogen + obesity; vaginal bleeding. Cervical: HPV; Pap at 21, every 3 years; postcoital bleeding. Prostate: PSA ≥4; Gleason max 10; impotence + incontinence post-op; brachytherapy: thicker PPE, no sex 2 weeks. Testicular: age 33, cryptorchidism, painless mass, AFP/hCG, sperm bank. Male breast: BRCA2, mastectomy, hormone tx only if receptor +. Heme and immune Left shift = immature neutrophils (bands) = active infection. Neutrophils: bacteria/fungi, first to arrive, 6 hours. Intrinsic → PTT (heparin) · Extrinsic → PT/INR (warfarin). Ferritin = stores · transferrin = transport · TIBC ↑ in IDA = low O₂ sat. IDA diet: red meat, spinach, beans + vitamin C, no dairy; iron on empty stomach. Anemia: BP ↓, HR ↑, RR ↑; low albumin = nutrition. PV: thick blood → hydrate, phlebotomy; stroke/DVT risk. Platelets <50k: no IM, no rectal, soft brush; Bactrim #1 drug cause. Leukemia: immature WBCs → infection, anemia, bleeding; benzene, Down syndrome. Neutropenic: no plants, no humidifiers, reverse isolation (you wear PPE). Lymphoma: painless nodes + B symptoms; Reed–Sternberg; SVC syndrome. Myeloma: CRAB, hypercalcemia (tingling), push fluids, bisphosphonates, ambulate. IgG crosses placenta · IgM first · IgE allergy · IgA secretions. Humoral = B cells/antibodies · Cellular = T cells. XLA: boys, 6 months, BTK, IVIG every 3–4 weeks · DiGeorge: CATCH, ↓Ca, FISH. Anaphylaxis: stop drug → IM epi → O₂; don't drop BP. Only NS with blood · O donor · AB recipient. Latex ↔ banana, avocado, kiwi, chestnut."},{"ch":"cancer","chTitle":"Overview of Cancer Care","id":"cancer--overview-of-cancer-care","title":"Overview of Cancer Care","text":"Cancer is a group of disorders with uncontrolled abnormal cell growth. It is the 2nd most common cause of death in the US (2nd leading cause worldwide, after heart disease). US death rates dropped 32% (1991–2019) because of less smoking, earlier diagnosis, and newer targeted drugs."},{"ch":"cancer","chTitle":"Overview of Cancer Care","id":"cancer--key-terms-and-pathophysiology","title":"Key terms and pathophysiology","text":"Term Meaning Malignant Cancerous, unregulated, invasive cell growth Non-malignant Not cancerous Benign Not cancerous, does not invade or metastasize, but can become cancerous Carcinogen An exposure that damages DNA (tobacco, ionizing radiation, benzene, HPV, sun). The most common risk factor. Promotes carcinogenesis Carcinogenesis Initiation and promotion of cancer after carcinogen exposure plus genetic mutations that the immune system misses Apoptosis Programmed cell death. Cancer cells skip it Contact inhibition Normal cells stop growing when they touch other cells. Cancer cells lack it Dysplasia Deranged growth (cells vary in size, shape, organization). A precursor to cancer but not the same as cancer. No apoptosis results in dysplasia Neoplasia Uncontrolled proliferation Anaplasia Cell has lost its expected structure and function (no longer looks or acts like the parent cell) Metastasis Abnormal cells invade surrounding tissue and gain access to lymph and blood vessels Carcinoma / Squamous cell / Sarcoma Carcinoma = epithelial cells. Squamous cell = squamous cells. Sarcoma = connective tissue Remission / Palliation Disease-free state / relieving symptoms when cure is not possible Vesicant A drug that causes extensive tissue damage if it leaks out of the vein Two hallmarks 1. Uncontrolled cell growth Lack contact inhibition, so they don't stop growing when they touch other cells. No apoptosis (no programmed cell death). No anchorage dependence: they grow in atypical places, make their own blood vessels, and survive anoxia and acidosis. 2. Altered differentiation : the cell no longer looks or acts like the parent cell. Well differentiated cells look like the parent cell. Poorly differentiated cells are harder to treat and more aggressive. This is the basis of grading . Mild dysplasia slide: the yellow cells are abnormal, sitting above the basement membrane Stages of metastasis: (1) cancer cells at the primary site → (2) invade surrounding tissue and vessels → (3) travel through the circulatory or lymphatic system → (4) reinvade and grow at a new site How cancer spreads Cell-to-cell (direct invasion). Example: prostate spreads to the rectum, pelvic floor, lower spine, and hip. Lymphatic. Travels lymph channels to nodes, where it can be destroyed, grow, lie dormant, or spread further. Hematogenous (blood). Example: colorectal goes through the portal circulation to the liver. A metastatic tumor keeps the identity of its origin . Prostate cancer in the rectum is still prostate cancer (a secondary tumor). Types and naming Solid tumors arise from a body organ and grow into masses (e.g., lung). Hematologic (\"liquid\") malignancies come from hematopoietic stem cells (myeloid and lymphoid lines, lymph nodes and spleen): leukemia (blood cells), lymphoma (lymph system), multiple myeloma (plasma cells and immunoglobulins). Who gets cancer Most common cancers worldwide: lung, breast, prostate, colorectal, skin, stomach. US: men, prostate > lung > colorectal. Women, breast > lung > colorectal. Children, leukemia > brain. 78% of US cancers are diagnosed at age ≥55, partly because immune surveillance weakens with aging. Incidence, prevalence, and mortality vary by gender and socioeconomic indicators."},{"ch":"cancer","chTitle":"Overview of Cancer Care","id":"cancer--risk-factors-table-13-2","title":"Risk factors (Table 13.2)","text":"Carcinogens · environment · hormones · genetics · lifestyle · infectious disease · medications (e.g., beta blockers as an example from class) · immune status · nutrition. Risks are cumulative. Non-modifiable: age and genetics. 5–10% of cancers come from known genetic syndromes such as BRCA1/2, p53 (Li-Fraumeni), FAP, and retinoblastoma. Modifiable: sedentary lifestyle, poor diet, smoking. Red flags for hereditary cancer: multiple primaries, bilateral tumors in paired organs, younger-than-usual age, a tumor in the sex not usually affected (e.g., male breast cancer), ≥2 first-degree relatives with the same cancer. BRCA1 raises the risk of breast and ovarian cancer. BRCA2 also raises the risk of pancreatic, prostate, and melanoma."},{"ch":"cancer","chTitle":"Overview of Cancer Care","id":"cancer--clinical-presentation-caution","title":"Clinical presentation: CAUTION ★","text":"C Change in bowel or bladder habits A A sore that does not heal U Unusual bleeding or discharge T Thickening or lump (breast or anywhere) I Indigestion or difficulty swallowing O Obvious change in a wart or mole N Nagging cough or hoarseness If a sign lasts more than 2 weeks , the patient needs evaluation. Constitutional signs: vague symptoms, fatigue, fever of unknown cause, unexplained weight loss, night sweats. In lymphoma these are called \"B symptoms\" . Malignancy-specific signs: usually come from the tumor mass compressing or eroding nearby structures."},{"ch":"cancer","chTitle":"Overview of Cancer Care","id":"cancer--staging-and-grading","title":"Staging and grading","text":"TNM staging (exam topic, tables are tested) T = tumor size · N = number of lymph nodes involved · M = metastasis (yes or no) Stage I: small, no obvious spread outside the organ Stage II: invades deeper tissues or local lymph nodes Stage III: large or locally advanced, possibly in local nodes Stage IV: metastasized All hematologic malignancies are stage IV at diagnosis because they involve the whole body from the start. Grading describes how differentiated the cells are. Poorly differentiated = more aggressive. Class note TNM decode (from your class PLQ) T = size of tumor · N = lymph nodes affected · M = metastasis? yes or no. A patient with T3, N2, M1 who says \"I am relieved that the cancer is still local\" needs more teaching: M1 means it has spread. Staging S0–S4: the tumor goes deeper through mucosa, submucosa, and muscle, then reaches lymph nodes. In S4 it has spread to other organs."},{"ch":"cancer","chTitle":"Overview of Cancer Care","id":"cancer--diagnosis-goals-and-tools","title":"Diagnosis: goals and tools","text":"Goals: find whether a tumor is present and how far it extends, look for metastasis, check how involved and uninvolved organs are working, and get tissue for stage and grade. Labs such as PSA, CBC, and tumor markers support the diagnosis and are tracked during treatment. PSA has high sensitivity but low specificity (false positives, e.g., prostatitis). Imaging: CT shows mass location and vessel or organ involvement. PET lights up metabolically active \"hot spots\" (glucose must be controlled before PET). MRI is best for brain, joint, and breast. Biopsy is the MOST SPECIFIC test and is required for a definitive (cytopathologic) diagnosis. Excisional biopsy (whole mass plus margin) is preferred over incisional (core) biopsy, which can track cells through normal tissue. FNA is less traumatic but yields fewer cells. A liquid biopsy is a blood test that looks for tumor DNA. Results take 3–10 days. Endoscopic procedures (e.g., bronchoscopy) let the provider see inside hollow organs and take tissue. Fine-needle aspiration (FNA) of a breast mass Bronchoscopy lets the provider see the airways and biopsy the tumor"},{"ch":"cancer","chTitle":"Overview of Cancer Care","id":"cancer--oncologic-emergencies","title":"Oncologic emergencies","text":"Acute complications that need urgent treatment, grouped as structural, metabolic, or hematologic . Slide examples: neutropenic fever, arrhythmia, DVT, hypercalcemia, spinal cord compression. Treat aggressively if the patient can recover. If the emergency signals progressive disease, the team may choose a palliative approach. Table 13.3 Oncologic emergencies ★ Emergency / who gets it What's happening Look for Treatment Nursing Hypercalcemia (bone mets from breast, lung, renal; lung and pancreatic tumors that make PTH-like hormone) Bone breakdown releases calcium into the blood Delirium, somnolence, muscle weakness, polyuria , bradycardia, nausea, constipation NS hydration (dilutes calcium, increases excretion), bisphosphonates (pamidronate, zoledronate), denosumab Monitor Ca, phosphorus, and renal function. Give fluids. Why: bone mets mean calcium is a key lab. Spinal cord compression (tumors near the spine) A mass compresses the epidural space or its blood supply Back pain and sensory changes (numbness, tingling) come before motor weakness. Unsteady gait, can't tell hot from cold. Bowel and bladder dysfunction is late Radiation (most common, rapid relief), rapid-onset corticosteroids, laminectomy, vertebroplasty or kyphoplasty Assess back pain, weakness, gait, constipation or incontinence Superior vena cava syndrome (lung cancer, lymphoma, chest tumors) Tumor or nodes squeeze the soft-walled SVC, so blood can't return from the upper body Dyspnea (most common). Edema of neck, face, eyes, worst in the morning . Prominent upper-body veins. Headache, visual changes, confusion (cerebral edema) Immediate chemo or radiation, corticosteroids, thrombolytics if a clot is present, stent Early: dyspnea and facial edema. Late: confusion, cyanosis, hypotension, tachycardia Tumor lysis syndrome (leukemia, lymphoma, fast-responding tumors) Rapid cell lysis dumps contents faster than the kidneys can clear them Hyperkalemia (peaked T, heart block), hypocalcemia (perioral and fingertip tingling, laryngospasm), hyperphosphatemia , hyperuricemia , renal failure (↑BUN and creatinine, ↓urine output) Large-volume IV fluids plus diuretics. Allopurinol to prevent hyperuricemia. Rasburicase if uric acid already high. Phosphate binders (Amphojel). Dialysis or CRRT ECG changes, signs of low calcium, renal labs SIADH (brain and lung cancer) Excess ADH causes water retention Dilutional hyponatremia leading to confusion, seizures, coma. Fluid overload and hypertension Fluid restriction first. Hypertonic saline (correct Na no faster than 0.5 mEq/hr). Vaptans Restrict fluids. Watch for overload and neuro changes Leukostasis (AML with very high WBC) Immature WBCs sludge the capillaries, causing clots and ruptured vessels Kidney, lung, and brain signs: blurred vision, headache, TIA or stroke, dyspnea, oliguria IV fluids, emergency chemo, leukapheresis Monitor WBC. Assess for occluded microcirculation and bleeding Pericardial effusion / tamponade (lung and breast cancer, lymphoma) Excess fluid around the heart blocks venous return, so cardiac output drops Dyspnea, poor perfusion, right heart failure signs Pericardiocentesis or pericardial window. Large IV fluid volumes until then Vital signs, perfusion. Prepare for pericardiocentesis Pleural effusion (breast, lung, leukemia, lymphoma, ovarian) Fluid in the pleural space compresses the alveoli Dyspnea, accessory muscle use, unequal chest excursion, diminished breath sounds, hypoxemia (confusion, anxiety) Thoracentesis, chest tube or pleural catheter, pleurodesis Breath sounds, chest excursion, how often symptoms recur Tracheobronchial obstruction (lymphoma, lung cancer) Tumor blocks the major airways Dyspnea with stridor , wheezing. Absent breath sounds late. More hypoxic than hypercarbic Bronchodilators and steroids, then stent, laser, or brachytherapy Respiratory distress, stridor Bowel obstruction (colon, pancreatic, ovarian, prostate) A mass blocks the bowel. Greatest danger is rupture or perforation Vomiting, distention. Bowel sounds ↑ before the obstruction and ↓ or absent after it. Thin, ribbon-like stools Surgery. NPO or TPN. No peristaltic stimulants if the obstruction is complete Detailed GI assessment Other cancer complications that are not emergencies: anorexia and cachexia, hypercoagulation (DVT prophylaxis is common), effusions, electrolyte and glucose problems, depression. Constipation in a patient on opioids could also mean bowel obstruction, hypercalcemia, or spinal cord compression."},{"ch":"cancer","chTitle":"Overview of Cancer Care","id":"cancer--prevention-and-screening","title":"Prevention and screening","text":"Primary: prevent it Secondary: find it early Tertiary: reduce harm Risk factor modification, immunization, chemoprevention, avoiding carcinogens. Quitting tobacco for 10 years lowers lung cancer risk 30–50%. PPE for asbestos, daily sunscreen, healthy weight, exercise, plant-based diet, limit alcohol (≤1 drink/day women, ≤2 men). Immunization: HPV (cervical, head and neck), hepatitis. Chemoprevention: hormone drugs for high-risk patients. Nutraceuticals are not proven Screening for early detection: general check-up, breast, cervical, colon, prostate. A good screening test is sensitive (few false negatives) and specific (few false positives) Reduce morbidity and mortality after diagnosis: treat the cancer and manage side effects Table 13.4 Screening guidelines (ACS) ★ Cancer Guideline General Age ≥20: discuss risk and screening at the annual physical. Share family history Breast Ages 40–44 may choose an annual mammogram. Ages 45–54: annual. Age ≥55: may switch to every 2 years, and continue while healthy with ≥10 years of life expectancy. Annual clinical breast exam. Ages 20–39: clinical breast exam every 3 years Cervical Ages 25–65: Pap plus HPV every 5 years, OR Pap every 3 years plus HPV every 5 years. May stop at 65 if prior screens were normal. (See Chapter 65 for the ACOG start-at-21 schedule) Endometrial At menopause, counsel all patients about the risk and to report any unexpected vaginal bleeding or spotting Colon Age ≥45: stool test, OR flex sigmoidoscopy every 5 years, OR colonoscopy every 10 years, OR barium enema or CT colonography every 5 years. Stop over age 85 Prostate Age ≥50 (≥45 with risk factors): talk to the provider about PSA ± DRE. African American men have the highest incidence and 2× the mortality Lung No general guideline. Yearly low-dose helical CT for high-risk patients: current smokers or those who quit within 15 years, with a 20 pack-year history Melanoma; NHL, pancreatic, myeloma Melanoma: monitor your own skin. The others have no guideline (for myeloma, consider a urine Bence Jones protein test if family history is strong)"},{"ch":"cancer","chTitle":"Overview of Cancer Care","id":"cancer--treatment-goals-and-the-4-modalities","title":"Treatment: goals and the 4 modalities","text":"Goals of care Cure: often defined as 5 years disease-free. Possible with early resectable breast, cervix, colon, testicular, and uterine cancers. Remission: disease-free, but unclear whether it's gone for good. The usual goal in heme malignancies. Control: extend life. Palliation: relieve symptoms when cure isn't possible. Timing terms ★ (testable) Primary therapy: the definitive treatment (often surgery). Neoadjuvant = given BEFORE definitive treatment to shrink the tumor (e.g., chemo before mastectomy). Adjuvant = given AFTER , when the patient is disease-free but at risk from leftover microscopic cells (e.g., radiation or oral hormonal therapy after surgery). 1 · Surgery The preferred modality and primary treatment for solid tumors. Goal is total excision plus a margin. Other types: debulking (partial removal when the whole tumor can't come out), redirection of vital functions (e.g., an ostomy), and reconstruction . Cancer patients may come to surgery with chronic pain meds, weight loss and malnutrition, poor skin integrity, organ dysfunction (slower anesthetic clearance), and infection risk. Watch for phantom limb after limb surgery. 2 · Radiation Localized ionizing radiation destroys the DNA of cancer cells. External beam is given in daily \"fractions\" (5 days on, 2 off). CyberKnife is stereotactic: one precise high-dose treatment. Internal = brachytherapy: radioactive \"seeds\" placed in a body cavity (cervix, prostate, breast, others). Low-dose seeds stay in for days or are permanent. High-dose sources dwell for a set time and are then removed. Systemic: radioactive substance given IV (e.g., radioactive iodine for thyroid cancer). Brachytherapy: radioactive seeds placed in the prostate near the tumor Radiation side effects Effects match the body area treated. Local injury peaks at weeks 2–3 . Inflammation comes first, then fibrosis (a late effect: adhesions, skin changes, strictures, so a vaginal dilator may be needed). Radiodermatitis: erythema, pruritus, dry or moist desquamation. Use mild pH-neutral soap. Calendula or hyaluronic acid cream can prevent it. No rubefacients (Icy Hot, Bengay) on radiated skin. Fatigue is very common, builds throughout treatment, peaks at 3–6 weeks. Pace activities and get mild to moderate exercise. Reassure the patient that fatigue does not mean the cancer is spreading. Chest wall radiation: watch skin, lungs (pneumonitis: dry cough), and heart (late cardiac effects). Nurse safety: wear PPE. Brachytherapy patients may eliminate radioactive waste in body fluids, so teach the family too. 3 · Antineoplastic medications (Table 13.5) Chemotherapy (alkylating agents, antimetabolites, antitumor antibiotics) destroys DNA. It can be cell-cycle specific or nonspecific. It kills all rapidly dividing cells , including hair, skin, GI lining, and bone marrow, which explains its side effects. Many are hazardous drugs. Class example: methotrexate . Immunotherapy (pembrolizumab/Keytruda) boosts immune recognition of the tumor. Assess for allergic reactions. Checkpoint inhibitors can cause immune-mediated adverse effects for up to a year after treatment. Targeted agents (HER-2 blockers like trastuzumab; Bcr-Abl inhibitors like imatinib; EGFR agents) block a tumor's unique pathway. Many are oral, with drug–food interactions. Check insurance coverage. Hormonal agents (tamoxifen, aromatase inhibitors, androgen suppression) change the hormone environment that feeds the tumor. Regimens combine classes and are given in cycles . Delays or dose reductions can compromise the response, so patients should not skip a treatment. Immune-mediated adverse effects (checkpoint inhibitors) Pro-inflammatory effects can hit any organ: myocarditis, diabetes, hypothyroidism, hypopituitarism, colitis and diarrhea (perforation), hepatitis, pneumonitis, nephritis, rashes, fatigue. They can be mistaken for infection, and missing treatment with corticosteroids or infliximab could be fatal. EHRs often flag patients who got these drugs in the past year. Table 13.6 Chemo adverse effects and what the nurse does ★ Adverse effect Nursing implications Myelosuppression ⚠ CBCs checked frequently. Teach the patient to call the provider IMMEDIATELY for a fever . Myelosuppression plus fever is treated aggressively to prevent sepsis and shock. Neutropenia is ANC <1,500; fever is ≥38°C (100.4°F) Alopecia Starts soon after therapy begins and grows back weeks after it ends, often different (curly, thicker, a new color). Address body image, suggest wigs, cover the head in the sun. Scalp cooling (cap 20–30 min before to 2 hr after) works for solid tumors. Contraindicated in heme malignancies and cold agglutinin disease Nausea / vomiting Prophylactic antiemetics. 5-HT3 antagonists are the most effective ; others are NK1 antagonists, steroids, phenothiazines, H1 blockers. Lorazepam is for anticipatory N/V. Assess fluid and nutrition status Oral mucositis Rinse every 2–4 hours and brush with a soft toothbrush. 2% viscous lidocaine rinse for pain. Cryotherapy (ice chips) with 5-FU or melphalan. Palifermin Hemorrhagic cystitis IV hydration before and after. Mesna protects the bladder Hypersensitivity Premedicate as ordered. Emergency equipment readily available (e.g., paclitaxel) Cardiotoxicity Echo or MUGA to monitor ejection fraction. Many drugs are held if EF drops. Troponin with doxorubicin. Keep fluids and electrolytes balanced (dysrhythmias) Pulmonary fibrosis Periodic PFTs (e.g., bleomycin) Hepatic / renal toxicity Check LFTs regularly. Review renal function before each dose and ask whether the dose needs changing Clotting problems Some drugs are never given within 6–8 weeks of major surgery (bleeding). Prophylactic anticoagulation for clots. Bleeding precautions: avoid falls, sharps, razors; use stool softeners Peripheral neuropathy Fall precautions: remove scatter rugs and cords, use nonslip bath mats Diarrhea · skin (hand-foot) Antimotility drugs, fluids. Emollients. Report severe skin toxicity because therapy may need a break Infertility · menopause sx Offer sperm or egg banking before therapy. Teach about hot flashes, insomnia, fatigue Capillary permeability Fluid replacement for hypovolemia. Daily weights Vesicants and extravasation Vesicant = a drug that destroys tissue on contact if it leaks out of the vein. Give by IV push or a short infusion. A central line is ideal . A peripheral IV should be <24 hours old, in a superficial vein you can watch. Check blood return before, every 2 mL or 2 minutes, and after . No blood return, pain, or swelling: STOP immediately. Put no more fluid into that vein. Call the pharmacist. An antidote goes through the catheter and into the tissue if one exists; otherwise use hot or cold compresses per policy. Mark the area with indelible marker, document, and follow up. A plastic surgery consult may be needed. Hazardous drug safety Most antineoplastics are Group 1 hazardous drugs: carcinogenic and teratogenic. PPE: impermeable gown plus TWO pairs of nitrile gloves (cuffs over the sleeves), a mask, and a face shield if there is a splash risk. Use closed-system transfer devices. Dispose of waste in hazardous-drug containers. Patient excreta are hazardous too , so teach the family. 4 · Cellular therapy: stem cell and bone marrow transplant Two steps: (1) high-dose chemo ± radiation, which would normally destroy the marrow permanently, then (2) infusion of stem cells or marrow that \"rescues\" the patient. Autologous = the patient's own cells (collected and frozen beforehand). Allogeneic = from a related or matched donor, with a possible graft-versus-tumor benefit but a risk of GVHD . Used mostly for heme malignancies and nonmalignant blood or immune disorders. Not routine for solid tumors. Other options: CAR T-cell therapy, donor lymphocyte infusion. Autologous transplant: (1) collect stem cells → (2) process → (3) cryopreserve → (4) high-dose chemo or radiation → (5) reinfuse the thawed cells"},{"ch":"cancer","chTitle":"Overview of Cancer Care","id":"cancer--nursing-management-cancer-general","title":"Nursing management (cancer, general)","text":"Cancer care spans every med-surg specialty, across acute and chronic settings. Pain control is central. Bleeding risk (platelets <50,000): monitor the CBC. Keep platelets at least 10,000–20,000 to prevent spontaneous bleeding, and transfuse below that threshold. Infection risk (ANC <1,500): hand hygiene, no sick visitors, no gardening or soil, no cat litter, flu vaccine, prophylactic antimicrobials. Anorexia: corticosteroids or megestrol. Fatigue: rule out anemia and depression, encourage exercise, conserve energy. Older adults: frailty, polypharmacy, renal dosing. Infection may show up as acute confusion without a fever. Fall risk. Cancer patients should not skip a treatment. Case study: Mrs. Jones She has a 2 cm immovable mass in the upper outer breast near the axilla. Her mother had ovarian cancer. The plan is chemo first ( neoadjuvant ) to shrink the tumor, then mastectomy, then adjuvant radiation and oral hormonal therapy. For paclitaxel (a hypersensitivity risk and irritant/vesicant): have emergency equipment ready and check blood return."},{"ch":"cancer","chTitle":"Overview of Cancer Care","id":"cancer--class-notes-add-on-nur-291","title":"Class notes add-on (NUR 291)","text":"Class note Exam tips from class The exam has questions on oncology staging (TNM and stages I–IV). Know lab numbers (electrolytes, CBC). Both tables are in the Cheat Sheets part. The exam takes questions from tables. Study every table (13.3 emergencies, 13.4 screening, 13.6 chemo effects, 33.7 labs). Class note Your instructor emphasized Apoptosis: no cell death with cancer cells. Stages of cancer cell metastasis (slide 7): primary site → invade tissue and vessels → travel → reinvade. Tumor staging and grading. CAUTION symptoms. Hematologic (\"liquid\") malignancies arise from hematopoietic cells: lymphoid and myeloid lines. No apoptosis results in dysplasia, which can be a precursor to cancer. Health promotion: Primary = risk factor modification, immunization, chemoprevention, avoiding carcinogens. Secondary = screening for early detection (general check-up, breast, cervical, colon, prostate). Tertiary = reducing morbidity and mortality once disease is diagnosed."},{"ch":"cancer","chTitle":"Overview of Cancer Care","id":"cancer--more-from-your-first-study-guide","title":"More from your first study guide","text":"Table 13.2 · risk factors → signs → where it spreads ★ (tables are tested) Cancer Risk factors Signs and symptoms Common mets Breast Early menarche, late menopause, nulliparity, late 1st child; BRCA1/2, p53; obesity, high-fat diet Mass, axillary nodes, asymmetry, nipple discharge Bone, lung, nodes, liver, brain Lung Tobacco, asbestos, coal/soot/tar, pollution, wood dust Chronic cough, hemoptysis, dyspnea, chest discomfort, weight loss, paraneoplastic syndromes Brain, bone, adrenal, other lung Colon Alcohol, polyps, obesity/high-fat diet, Crohn's/UC Bowel change, ribbon stools, blood in stool, anemia, weight loss Liver, lung, muscle Prostate Age, family history, pesticides Pelvic/rectal pain, dysuria, nocturia, ED Bone, nodes, rectum, bladder Cervical HPV, multiple partners, early sexual contact, tobacco, long-term OCPs Postcoital bleeding, malodorous discharge, dyspareunia Endometrium, vagina, pelvic wall, vulva Ovarian BRCA1/2, nulliparity, no breastfeeding, long-term OCPs* Abdominal fullness, bloating, fatigue Peritoneum, omentum, pleura Uterine Early menarche/late menopause, estrogen therapy, PCOS, tamoxifen, DM, obesity Bleeding between cycles, lower abdominal cramping Nodes, vagina, peritoneum Bladder Tobacco Hematuria, abdominal discomfort, low-back pain Nodes, bone, lung, skin, liver Kidney Pesticides, solvents, tobacco Flank pain, hematuria, weight loss Nodes, lung, liver, bone, adrenal, brain Pancreatic BRCA2, tobacco, DM, pancreatitis, obesity N/V, early satiety, vague abdominal pain, jaundice Liver, peritoneum, lungs, bones Gastric H. pylori, alcohol, ulcers, asbestos, smoking Indigestion, postprandial fullness, N/V Nodes, esophagus, liver Esophageal Alcohol, smoking Dysphagia, reflux, chest discomfort Liver, lung, bones Hepatic Alcohol, hepatitis A/B/C, Budd-Chiari Jaundice, easy bruising, N/V Omentum, peritoneum, pleura Head and neck Alcohol, tobacco, wood dust, HPV Oral lesions/bleeding, neck masses, jaw pain Nodes, lung Brain Excessive alcohol Headaches, personality change, memory/visual deficits Spinal cord Leukemia Alkylating agents, radiation, genetic mutations Fatigue, anemia, infections, bruising, petechiae, gum bleeding Diffuse at diagnosis Lymphoma Chronic immunosuppression, radiation, EBV, HIV Lymphadenopathy, subcutaneous masses Diffuse at diagnosis Multiple myeloma Older age, Black race Fatigue, bone pain, dark infrequent urine Bone and kidney infiltration Melanoma/skin Sun/UV, ionizing radiation, family Irregular, new or growing lesions; palms/soles Nodes, lung, brain, liver, bone Testicular Undescended testes, HIV, age 20–34 Painless scrotal lump, heavy sensation Nodes, lung, liver, bone, brain Thyroid Ionizing radiation Enlarged irregular thyroid, dysphagia Nodes, lung, bone Sarcoma Arsenic, dioxins, herbicides, radiation Soft-tissue mass or organ dysfunction Lung, abdomen, local recurrence *Table 13.2 lists long-term OCPs for ovarian; Ch 65 says OCPs are protective for ovarian cancer. If asked about ovarian protection, OCPs protect. Also: the 9 risk factors are carcinogens, environment, hormones, genetics, lifestyle, infectious disease, medications, immune status, nutrition. Carcinogen exposure = #1, but exposure alone rarely causes cancer. Risks are cumulative. You can't prevent it, so screen often (secondary prevention) . Disease-linked risks (IBD → colon, hep C → liver) are modifiable only as far as the disease is managed. Hereditary red flags also include rare histology, and ≥3 relatives in 2 generations. BRCA2 also raises male breast cancer risk. The BRCA1 and BRCA2 test detects breast cancer risk. Metastasis: routes and examples Route How Classic example Cell-to-cell Direct invasion of adjacent cells Prostate → rectum, pelvic floor, spine, hip Lymphatic Through lymph channels of the organ Breast → axillary nodes Hematogenous Into veins draining the organ Colon → liver (portal circulation) Four steps: 1 primary site → 2 invasion → 3 transport → 4 new growth. Solid tumors arise from organs and grow into masses that invade and erode tissue. Liquid (hematologic) tumors are all stage IV at diagnosis. Stage definitions by cancer (all the size tables in your chapters) Cancer Stage → size / spread TNM stages 0 in situ (mucosa only; colon figure S0 stays in mucosa) · I small, no obvious spread outside the organ · II deeper tissues or local nodes · III large or locally invasive · IV metastasized Breast (female, Ch 65) 0 = in situ (DCIS) · IA ≤2 cm, no nodes · IB none or <2 cm + node micromets <2 mm · IIA none/<2 cm with node >2 mm, or 2–5 cm no nodes · IIB 2–5 cm + nodes, or >5 cm no nodes · IIIA >5 cm or any size + 4–8 nodes · IIIB any size to chest wall/skin (inflammatory), up to 9 nodes · IIIC any size to chest wall, 10+ nodes · IV any size + distant mets Male breast (Table 66.6) 0 = DCIS, Paget's of nipple, LCIS · I <2 cm, no nodes · II up to 5 cm ± nodes · III >5 cm, nearby/supraclavicular nodes · IV mets to bone, brain, liver, lungs Prostate (Table 66.4) T1 impalpable, not on US · T2 confined to prostate · T3 locally extensive · T4 invades neighboring organs · N0 none → N1 single node <2 cm → N2 2–5 cm → N3 >5 cm · M1a nonregional nodes / M1b bone / M1c other Lymphoma (Table 34.4) I single node · II ≥2 nodes same side of diaphragm · III nodes on both sides · IV organs + nodes (\"E\" extranodal, \"S\" spleen) Ovarian (FIGO) I ovaries · II pelvis · III peritoneal mets or nodes · IV distant Uterine (FIGO) I uterine body · II + cervix · III outside uterus in pelvis · IV bladder/bowel or distant Prostate grade Gleason: 2 sites each graded 1–5, added; max 10 (higher = worse) Breast size breakpoints to memorize: ≤2 cm (stage I), 2–5 cm (stage II), >5 cm (stage IIB/III). Nodes: 4–8 (IIIA), up to 9 (IIIB), 10+ (IIIC). Staging = how big, how far (anatomy). Grading = how differentiated the cells look under the microscope (poorly differentiated = higher grade = more aggressive). Biopsy procedures ★ (on your exam list) Biopsy type Key facts Excisional Removes whole mass + margin. Preferred Incisional (core) Cuts into the mass; risk of tracking cancer cells through normal tissue Fine-needle aspiration (FNA) Needle (by feel or imaging) suctions cells near the skin (breast). Less traumatic but fewer cells, limited molecular testing Core needle Wider needle with a cutter; larger sample Vacuum-assisted Imaging-guided probe through small incision; vacuum + rotating cutter; local anesthesia; no sutures Surgical excision (breast) Mass <2.5 cm removed entirely; nonpalpable mass marked with a hooked wire first; sutures Sentinel node biopsy First node the cancer drains to; found with radioactive tracer/blue dye. Determines stage. Negative → no more nodes removed. Positive → axillary dissection (20–40 nodes) Bone marrow aspiration/biopsy For hematologic cancers Lymph node biopsy Confirms lymphoma Liquid biopsy Blood test for cancer cells/tumor DNA (NSCLC, breast, colorectal, GI, ovarian) Prostate needle biopsy Office, local anesthetic, after abnormal DRE/PSA; tissue from 2 sites → Gleason score. Can hurt, bleed, get infected Colposcopy + cervical biopsy Cervix swabbed with acetic acid, viewed with lighted magnifier; abnormal areas biopsied. Cervical biopsy is diagnostic Endometrial biopsy / D&C Uterine cancer: endometrial biopsy (99% sensitive for high grade); D&C scrapes the lining if needed Ovarian Biopsy NOT recommended (spreads cells). Definitive = surgical removal of mass + pathology Bronchoscopy / mediastinoscopy Lung: scope through mouth/nose to view and biopsy airways; mediastinoscopy through chest incision checks nodes Imaging and labs: CT = mass location/organ involvement · PET = metabolically active \"hot spots\" (needs glucose control) · MRI = brain, joint, breast · contrast shows vessel involvement. Labs are supportive, not definitive. Tumor markers and blood counts track response. Nurse's role: explain purpose, prep, post-care, what to expect and when results come. Treatment extras Neo = NEW = first. Neoadjuvant before, adjuvant after (kills microscopic cells). External beam: machine (CyberKnife = one-time stereotactic). Patient is NOT radioactive. Internal (brachytherapy): body fluids may be radioactive: PPE, limit exposure. Systemic: IV radioactive substance (radioactive iodine). Radiation skin care: mild pH-neutral soap; don't wash off markings ; no menthol rubs on radiated skin. Fatigue = most common, peaks weeks 3–6. Targeted: imatinib (Bcr-Abl, CML), trastuzumab (HER2), ibrutinib, sorafenib. Often oral: teach food/drug interactions. Hormonal: breast and prostate cancer. Stem cell transplant: autologous = self · allogeneic = donor (graft-vs-tumor bonus, risk of GVHD) · matched unrelated · haploidentical (half match). Constitutional signs: in lymphoma, fever + night sweats + weight loss = B symptoms (fatigue is NOT a B symptom). Malignancy-specific example: brain tumor → nerve pain and ↑ICP. Patients who get chemo do not drink a lot. Encourage lots of fluids beforehand or they can vomit. Tumor lysis memory: cells burst and dump K⁺, phosphate, uric acid (all UP). Calcium binds phosphate, so Ca goes DOWN. Table 13.7 · chemo priority nursing problems Problem Cue Management Risk for infection Neutropenia: WBC <1,500; temp ≥38°C Monitor CBC, hand hygiene, no sick visitors, no gardening/soil, no cat litter, flu vaccine, prophylactic antimicrobials Risk for bleeding Thrombocytopenia: platelets <50,000 Transfuse below 10–20k; bleeding and fall precautions N/V Nausea, emesis 5-HT3 (ondansetron) + NK1 + steroids on a schedule; lorazepam for anticipatory Mucositis Oral pain, sores Soft toothbrush, rinse q2–4h, viscous lidocaine, ice chips with 5-FU Anorexia/cachexia ↓BMI, ↓albumin/prealbumin Steroids, megestrol, dietary counseling Alopecia Starts soon after therapy Regrows (may differ); wigs; protect scalp from sun Hemorrhagic cystitis Hematuria IV hydration; mesna Cardiotoxicity ↓EF (doxorubicin) Echo/MUGA scans Peripheral neuropathy Numbness, tingling Fall precautions Infertility Offer sperm/egg banking before therapy Geriatric: infection may show as confusion, not fever; renal dosing; fall risk; polypharmacy. Fever ≥100.4°F (≥38°C) in a chemo patient = call the provider NOW. Hair loss, dry skin, nausea are expected. Screening extras (Table 13.4) Breast: 40–44 may start yearly mammogram · 45–54 yearly · ≥55 every 2 years (or yearly) · self-breast exam NOT recommended. Endometrial: at menopause report any unexpected bleeding/spotting; yearly biopsy if high risk. Melanoma: annual skin self-exam. Ovarian, NHL, pancreatic, myeloma: no general screening guideline."},{"ch":"cancer","chTitle":"Overview of Cancer Care","id":"cancer--chapter-13-powerpoint-word-for-word","title":"Chapter 13 PowerPoint, word for word","text":"Your instructor's slides for this chapter, kept in slide order. Slide Slide 1 · Key definitions Cancer – group of disorders characterized by uncontrolled abnormal cell growth Malignant cells – are characteristic of cancers Non-malignant – non cancerous Benign – not cancerous, but can become cancerous Carcinogens – promotes carcinogenesis Metastasis – abnormal cells that invade the surrounding tissue & gain access to lymph and blood vessels Slide Slide 2 · How a normal cell becomes cancer Carcinogenesis – multiple carcinogen exposures & genetic mutations Uncontrolled cell growth – no apoptosis, results in dysplasia – can be precursor to cancer Altered cell differentiation – do not look like the parent cell Metastasis – spread of tumors Slide Slide 3 · WHO gets cancer Second leading cause of death worldwide · Expected to increase over time Most common types globally: lung, breast, prostate, colorectal, skin, stomach Incidence, prevalence & mortality vary on gender, socioeconomic indicators; carcinogen exposure – most common risk factor Slide Slide 4 · What raises cancer risk Carcinogens · Environment · Hormones · Genetics · Lifestyle · Infectious disease · Medications · Immune status · Nutrition · Table 13.2 – common cancers with risk factors Slide Slide 5 · Solid vs. hematological Solid tumor malignancies – arise from specific body organs, grow into masses Hematological malignancies – arise from hematopoietic cells; give rise to myeloid & lymphoid cells (lymph nodes, spleen) Slide Slide 6 · TNM and stages I–IV Staging determines the size of the tumor, the lymph node involvement & metastasis Tumor, nodes, metastasis (TNM): T – tumor size · N – number of lymph nodes involved · M – presence of metastasis Stages 1 to 4 Slide Slide 7 · CAUTION, constitutional & specific signs CAUTION symptoms · Constitutional signs – vague symptoms, fatigue, fever of unknown etiology, unexplained weight loss, night sweats · Malignancy-specific signs & symptoms – often related to tumor- mass compression or erosion Slide Slide 8 · The ones that can kill fast Acute clinical complications – require urgent treatment secondary to tumor growth Categorized as structural, metabolic, or hematological Organ dysfunction may be present Common ex: neutropenic fever, arrhythmia, DVT, hypercalcemia, spinal cord compression Slide Slide 9 · Primary, secondary, tertiary Primary: risk factor modification, immunization, chemoprevention, avoiding carcinogens Secondary: screening for early detection – general check-up, breast, cervical, colon, prostate Tertiary: reducing morbidity and mortality once disease is diagnosed Slide Slide 10 · Goals and diagnostic tests Determine presence, extent of tumor · Identify possible metastasis · Evaluate functions of involved and uninvolved body systems · Obtain tissue and cells for analysis, including tumor stage & grade Laboratory tests · Imaging · Biopsy · Endoscopic procedures Slide Slide 11 · Surgery Surgical resection of a tumor – solid tumor malignancies; goal: total excision · Debulking of tumor mass · Redirection of vital functions · Reconstruction Slide Slide 12 · External, internal, systemic Radiation therapy – localized delivery of ionizing radiation, inducing cell death · External – via external beam; CyberKnife · Internal – placement in a cavity (brachytherapy) · Systemic – vascular administration of a radioactive substance Slide Slide 13 · Chemo, immunotherapy, targeted, hormonal Antineoplastic medications · Chemotherapy · Immunological therapy · Targeted agents · Hormonal agents Slide Slide 14 · Stem cell & bone marrow transplant Stem cell and bone marrow transplants · Administration of high-dose antineoplastic therapy · Infusion of stem cells or bone marrow Slide Slide 15 · Caring for the oncology patient All subspecialties of med-surg – oncology; geriatric nursing · Variety of care settings – acute or chronic · Pain control Slide Slides · Image-only slides (written out) [Image: Mild Dysplasia] Normal pink cells sit in orderly layers on the basement membrane; the yellow abnormal cells are irregular in size and shape and disorganized. [Image: Staging (colon wall)] S0 stays in mucosa → S1 into submucosa → S2 into muscle → S3 through the wall and to lymph nodes → S4 spread to other organs. [Image: Fine-needle aspiration] Needle drawing cells from a breast mass. [Image: Bronchoscopy] Scope used in the lung cancer workup. [Image: Brachytherapy] Radioactive seeds in the prostate. [Image: Autologous transplant] 1. Collection (stem cells taken from patient's marrow or blood) → 2. Processing (lab purifies and concentrates) → 3. Cryopreservation (frozen) → 4. High-dose chemo and/or radiation → 5. Reinfusion (thawed cells infused back)."},{"ch":"lung","chTitle":"Lung Cancer","id":"lung--lung-cancer","title":"Lung Cancer","text":"Lung cancer is the #1 cancer killer in the US for both men and women. More than 90% is caused by smoking. It is often silent until a late stage and is most common after age 65."},{"ch":"lung","chTitle":"Lung Cancer","id":"lung--why-it-matters-and-risk-factors","title":"Why it matters and risk factors","text":"ACS 2022: lung cancer is the 2nd most common cancer in both men and women and the leading cause of cancer death (~130,180 deaths). Survival depends on stage at diagnosis. Stage IA has 49% survival; stage IV has 1%. Black men have higher incidence than White men. White women have higher incidence than Black women. SLIDE vs BOOK: one slide says \"leading cause of cancer,\" but the book (and another slide) says 2nd most common cancer and #1 cause of cancer death. Risk factors: Smoking (90%): risk rises with the number of cigarettes and the years smoked. The most preventable cause. Second-hand smoke Radon Occupational pollutants, asbestos (also coal, soot, tar, wood dust, pollution per Ch 13) Screening: yearly low-dose CT for high-risk patients (≥20 pack-years, current smoker or quit within 15 years). Pathophysiology Carcinogens such as cigarette smoke cause dysplasia of the lung epithelium. Repeated exposure leads to genetic mutations: oncogenes switch on, tumor suppressors switch off, cells evade apoptosis, and DNA repair fails. The result is uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells (carcinogenesis)."},{"ch":"lung","chTitle":"Lung Cancer","id":"lung--sclc-vs-nsclc-know-this-difference","title":"SCLC vs NSCLC ★ (know this difference)","text":"Small cell (\"oat cell\") Non–small cell % of lung cancers 15% 85% Cell types Small (oat) cells 3 types: adenocarcinoma, squamous cell carcinoma, large cell carcinoma Behavior Grows quickly and spreads to other organs Slower Prognosis Poor at any stage. Only 5–10% survive 5 years. Class: life expectancy 6 months–1 year Good 5-year survival if found at an early stage Surgery? Not the main treatment Surgery is used primarily for NSCLC when there is no metastasis Class note Class vs book Your notes say small cell life expectancy is 6 months–1 year . The book says only 5–10% survive 5 years. These fit together, but if a question asks for a number, the class number is 6 months–1 year. Clinical manifestations (\"recognizing cues\") ★ Usually not evident early; show up as the disease advances. A new cough that won't go away , or a change in a chronic cough Hemoptysis (small vessels rupturing) Shortness of breath, wheezing, hoarseness Chest pain Headache and bone pain , which suggest metastasis (brain, bone) Weight loss Frequent pneumonia or bronchitis Common metastasis sites (Ch 13): brain, bone, adrenal gland, the other lung . Lung tumors can also cause paraneoplastic syndromes such as SIADH and hypercalcemia . Diagnosis, and why each test is done Chest x-ray / CT of the chest find the pulmonary lesion. Sputum cytology may identify the specific tumor cells. Bronchoscopy (scope through the mouth or nose) shows the trachea and bronchi and allows biopsy. Mediastinoscopy is a surgical incision in the chest to view the mediastinum and biopsy. PET scan looks at metabolism, not structure . It detects areas of high activity (rapidly dividing cancer cells). Bone and abdominal scans look for metastasis. Nonsurgical treatment Depends on the type, size, location, and stage of the tumor. Chemotherapy: primary treatment for advanced cancer or a patient too ill for surgery. Can be neoadjuvant (shrink first) or adjuvant (kill leftover cells). Radiation: used when surgery isn't an option, before or after surgery, or for palliation of symptoms and pain. Pain management follows the WHO ladder. Step 1: nonopioid (acetaminophen, NSAIDs). Step 2: moderate opioid (codeine, hydrocodone, oxycodone) plus a nonopioid. Step 3: strong opioid (morphine, fentanyl) plus a nonopioid. Give around the clock, not on demand , to avoid pain peaks. Palliative care should begin AT DIAGNOSIS. It manages pain, dyspnea, and nausea and improves quality of life. Surgery (preferred if no metastasis) Lobectomy: removes an entire lobe, when the cancer is localized to one lobe. Pneumonectomy: removes the entire lung, when cancer is spread diffusely through it. Wedge resection: removes a small section of a lobe, for cancer in one small area. Types of lung surgery: lobectomy, pneumonectomy, wedge resection"},{"ch":"lung","chTitle":"Lung Cancer","id":"lung--nursing-care","title":"Nursing care","text":"Assessments, and why Actions, and why SpO₂: low because of poor gas exchange. <90% = significant problem Give O₂ to keep SpO₂ ≥90% Temperature: a fever means infection Semi-Fowler's position → why: allows full lung expansion and better oxygenation Breath sounds: wheezes suggest airway obstruction. Rhonchi mean secretions in the upper airways Small, frequent meals plus supplements → why: a large meal pushes on the diaphragm; supplements add calories for the work of breathing Cough: irritation of the tracheobronchial tree. Hemoptysis means small vessels are rupturing Give meds: pain and anti-anxiety → why: relaxation lets the lungs expand better ; bronchodilators → why: open the airway and decrease the work of breathing Pain: increases respiratory rate and lowers quality of life Monitor temperature, cough, pain, and breath sounds Appetite and weight: chemo side effects and the work of breathing both cause weight loss Nursing diagnoses: ineffective gas exchange · anxiety (can't breathe) · activity intolerance (fatigue, dyspnea) · pain Post-op: lobectomy / pneumonectomy Vital signs: hypotension or tachycardia may mean excessive blood or fluid loss. Breath sounds: diminished or absent sounds may mean atelectasis. Suture line: red, warm, with thick yellow drainage means infection. Chest tube: excess bloody drainage suggests a bleed. Cloudy drainage suggests infection. Water-seal chamber: persistent bubbling = an air leak in the system. Keep the system CLOSED to prevent air leaks. NEVER CLAMP THE CHEST TUBE! → why: air or fluid builds up in the pleural space, worsening the pneumothorax and possibly causing a TENSION pneumothorax. Teaching, and why Breathing techniques / pursed-lip breathing → why: clear sputum, reduce breathlessness, remove trapped air Pace activities → why: conserves energy and lowers the work of breathing Smoking cessation → why: can change the course of the disease Small frequent meals · medication regimen (proper inhaler use) Take pain meds around the clock → why: keeps a steady level of control instead of peaks of pain Outcomes: pain controlled, enough appetite and weight, adequate oxygenation. Refer for pulmonary rehab (respiratory therapists) and palliative care."},{"ch":"lung","chTitle":"Lung Cancer","id":"lung--from-your-slides-asthma-case-clifford-davis","title":"From your slides: asthma case (Clifford Davis)","text":"A 39-year-old with asthma, a 15-year one-pack-a-day smoker. He is in the tripod position using pursed-lip breathing and can't finish a conversation. HR 150, RR 40, bilateral wheezes, SpO₂ 88% . Treatment: O₂ (up to 96%), prednisone (he missed his daily anti-inflammatory), albuterol nebulizers ×3, peak flow below baseline, then IM prednisone. Discharged on a prednisone taper with an updated asthma action plan. Most important part of management: disease management through education , using an asthma action plan built on peak flow readings . ⚠ Wheezing goes away AND breath sounds are diminished means he is not moving air (worsening obstruction or exhaustion). Emergency: respiratory failure, possible need for ventilation. A rising PaCO₂ signals impending failure. Priority when a partially obstructed airway is suspected: airway management. A major part of his treatment program: smoking cessation. Rescue med = short-acting β₂ agonist (albuterol). Controller = inhaled corticosteroid (rinse the mouth to prevent oral candidiasis). Never stop prednisone suddenly (taper). Asthma meds are not stopped when asthma is controlled. Peak flow: blow 3 times and record the highest reading."},{"ch":"lung","chTitle":"Lung Cancer","id":"lung--class-notes-add-on-nur-291","title":"Class notes add-on (NUR 291)","text":"From class Small cell (bad) Non–small cell (good!!) % of lung cancer 15% 85% Outlook Life expectancy 6 months–1 year 5-year survival rate for those diagnosed early Types Small (oat) cells 3 types: adenocarcinoma · squamous cell carcinoma · large cell 90% of lung cancer is from smoking. No apoptosis in these cells. 2 major types: small cell and non–small cell. Signs and symptoms (recognizing cues): new or changed cough, hemoptysis, SOB, wheezing, hoarseness, chest pain, headache and bone pain (mets), weight loss, frequent pneumonia. Diagnosis and medical management (nonsurgical vs surgical). Connection check When should palliative care start? Upon diagnosis. It manages pain, dyspnea, and nausea and improves quality of life, so it starts at diagnosis, not at the end of life."},{"ch":"lung","chTitle":"Lung Cancer","id":"lung--more-from-your-first-study-guide","title":"More from your first study guide","text":"Memory: small cell = S peedy, S preads, S ad survival. NSCLC types = \"All Smokers Lose\" ( A deno, S quamous, L arge). Headache = brain mets, the #1 site. Bone pain (bone mets) → check calcium. Lung cancer can also cause SIADH, hypercalcemia, and SVC syndrome. \"New cough that doesn't go away\" is the N in CAUTION. Lung cancer risk factors also include age >65, air pollution, coal/soot/tar, wood dust. Slide wording: the nursing diagnoses are ineffective gas exchange related to disruption of pulmonary cells , anxiety related to inability to breathe effectively , and activity intolerance related to fatigue and dyspnea . Day 1: palliative care starts upon diagnosis (Connection Check 26.4). It's not the same as hospice. Asthma case reminder: a quiet chest (no wheezes + diminished breath sounds) = obstruction/emergency → airway first. Controller meds daily even when well; don't stop prednisone suddenly. Only biopsy confirms lung cancer."},{"ch":"lung","chTitle":"Lung Cancer","id":"lung--chapter-26-powerpoint-word-for-word","title":"Chapter 26 PowerPoint, word for word","text":"Your instructor's slides for this chapter, kept in slide order. Slide Slide 1 · WHO gets lung cancer Leading cause of cancer in US for both men and women 2nd most common cancer in both men and women as per ACS statistics, 2022 > 90% caused by smoking · Often asymptomatic until late stage · Most prevalent in persons over age 65 Slide Slide 2 · How lung cancer develops Uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells in the lungs; repeated exposure leads to genetic mutations, promoting carcinogenesis 2 major types: Non-small cell – 85% · Small cell or oat-cell cancer – 15% Slide Slide 3 · Small cell vs. non-small cell Small cell or oat cell: represents 15% of LC; grows quickly & spreads to other organs; survival rate only 5–10% in 5 years Non-small cell: represents 85% of LC; 3 types of cell: adenocarcinoma, squamous cell carcinoma & large cell; 5-yr survival rate for those diagnosed in early stage is good Slide Slide 4 · What causes lung cancer Smoking (90% of cases) · Second-hand smoke · Radon · Occupational pollutants · Asbestos Slide Slide 5 · Signs and symptoms New cough that does not go away · Changes to chronic cough · Hemoptysis · Shortness of breath · Wheezing · Hoarseness · Chest pain · Headache · Bone pain · Weight loss Slide Slide 6 · How lung cancer is diagnosed Chest x-ray · CT scan of the chest · PET scans · Sputum for cytology · Bronchoscopy and mediastinoscopy · Bone scan and abdominal scans – to look for metastasis Slide Slide 7 · Chemo, radiation, pain & palliative care Nonsurgical: depends on size, location, and stage of tumor · Radiation · Chemotherapy · Pain management Slide Slide 8 · Lobectomy, pneumonectomy, wedge Surgical intervention: lobectomy · pneumonectomy · wedge resection Figure: three lungs showing a lobectomy, a pneumonectomy, and a wedge resection Slide Slide 9 · What to assess and nursing diagnoses Assessment and analysis: persistent cough · dyspnea · wheezing · hemoptysis · chest pain · frequent pneumonia or bronchitis Problem list Nursing diagnoses: ineffective gas exchange related to disruption of pulmonary cells · anxiety related to inability to breathe effectively · activity intolerance related to fatigue and dyspnea Slide Slide 10 · What the nurse does Provide oxygen · Monitor temperature · Assess breath sounds · Monitor for cough, pain · Position – semi-Fowler · Monitor appetite/weight – provide small, frequent meals with supplements Provide O₂ – increase SpO₂ to ≥ 90% · Position semi-Fowler · Small frequent meals with dietary supplements · Administer meds as ordered: pain medications, antianxiety, bronchodilators Slide Slide 11 · After lung surgery & chest tubes Post-op care, monitor: vital signs · breath sounds · suture line · chest tube – maintain a closed tube system · monitor water-seal chamber · NEVER CLAMP THE CHEST TUBE! Slide Slide 12 · Patient education Teaching: breathing techniques · pace activities · smoking cessation · nutritional needs · medication regimen · pain control Evaluating care outcomes: pain control · sufficient appetite and weight"},{"ch":"heme-assess","chTitle":"Assessment of Hematologic Function","id":"heme-assess--assessment-of-hematologic-function","title":"Assessment of Hematologic Function","text":"Blood makes up 7–10% of body weight , and an adult has 5–6 L . Its jobs: deliver oxygen, form clots, and fight infection through inflammation and immunity."},{"ch":"heme-assess","chTitle":"Assessment of Hematologic Function","id":"heme-assess--anatomy-and-physiology","title":"Anatomy and physiology","text":"Bone marrow is the main site of blood formation (hematopoiesis), about 4–5% of body weight. Its stem cells become either the myeloid line (RBCs, WBCs, platelets) or the lymphoid line (T and B lymphocytes). RBCs (erythrocytes) are the most abundant cells: small biconcave discs with no nucleus. They carry O₂ from lungs to tissues and CO₂ back. They live about 120 days . Hemoglobin is the O₂-carrying part of the RBC. Its pigment (heme) contains iron . WBCs (leukocytes) defend against infection. Normal is 4,500–11,000 . Granulocytes protect during acute inflammation. Neutrophils fight bacteria and fungi, are phagocytic, and are the first cells to arrive at a site. Eosinophils destroy foreign cells and respond in allergy. Platelets (thrombocytes) activate clotting factors and form a stable fibrin clot. Plasma is about 90% water, 6.5–8% protein, and 2% other substances. Spleen (LUQ) filters blood and removes abnormal WBCs, platelets, and old RBCs. Left shift: more immature neutrophils (bands) than normal are in the blood, meaning the marrow is pumping out cells to fight infection or inflammation Blood composition: plasma 55%, buffy coat (WBCs and platelets) <1%, RBCs 45% RBC life cycle: the marrow makes RBCs (needs iron, B₁₂, folic acid). They live 120 days. The spleen breaks down old cells: globin becomes amino acids, heme becomes iron (recycled) plus bilirubin (excreted in bile) Clotting cascade: the intrinsic pathway is monitored by PTT (heparin). The extrinsic pathway is monitored by PT/INR (warfarin). Both join the common pathway: prothrombin → thrombin, fibrinogen → fibrin clot Too many RBCs is also bad Excess RBCs increase blood viscosity, which decreases O₂ transport. Polycythemia vera works this way. Class note Clotting cascade from class Pathway Drug Healing Lab Intrinsic Heparin Healing the mucosa lining Check PT and PTT Extrinsic Warfarin Healed with good coagulation factor Check PT/INR Class vs book: your notes list PT and PTT for the intrinsic pathway. The book and slide picture pair heparin with PTT and warfarin with PT/INR . If a question asks which lab monitors heparin, PTT is the safe answer."},{"ch":"heme-assess","chTitle":"Assessment of Hematologic Function","id":"heme-assess--hematologic-assessment","title":"Hematologic assessment","text":"History Any change in energy level? Trouble keeping up daily activities? Pain, burning, or tingling? Skin color or temperature changes? Edema? Bleeding or easy bruising, dizziness, fatigue, swollen or tender lymph nodes? PMH: hepatitis, cirrhosis, cancer, clotting factor problems. Pregnancy can cause gestational thrombocytopenia. Meds: anticoagulants, aspirin and salicylates, herbs, supplements (goldenseal and green tea can reduce anticoagulant effect). Long-term antibiotics can cause coagulopathy and neutropenia. Aspirin irreversibly blocks platelets for their whole life span (4–7 days). Drugs that predispose to bleeding: aspirin, NSAIDs, heparin and LMWH, warfarin, DOACs, clopidogrel, antineoplastics, penicillins and cephalosporins, sulfonamides, thiazides, and antibiotics (they lower vitamin K). Ginger and garlic promote bleeding (ask about herbs in the med history). Physical exam Skin and mucosa: color, temperature, cap refill, edema. Look for petechiae, purpura, bruising. Tongue: red, swollen, smooth, shiny, tender ( glossitis ) points to IDA or pernicious anemia. Respiratory: are the lungs working harder to deliver O₂? (RR <12 or >20 is abnormal.) Cardiovascular: vital signs, murmurs, gallops. Systolic BP is lower in anemia and higher with too many RBCs. Renal: check the meatus for bleeding and look for hematuria. Musculoskeletal: bone pain. In leukemia, packed WBCs raise pressure inside the bone. In sickle cell, sickled RBCs cause infarction and swelling. Abdomen: palpate the LUQ LIGHTLY. → why: an enlarged spleen is tender and can RUPTURE with deep palpation. Stand on the opposite (right) side of the patient for the spleen assessment. Normal lymph nodes should not be palpable nor painful. Light palpation of the LUQ for splenomegaly. Never palpate deeply because of the rupture risk."},{"ch":"heme-assess","chTitle":"Assessment of Hematologic Function","id":"heme-assess--table-33-7-lab-profile-normal-values-and-what-abnormal-means","title":"Table 33.7 Lab profile, normal values, and what abnormal means ★","text":"Test Normal (adult) Significance RBC F 4.01–5.51 · M 4.51–6.01 million/mm³ ↑ high altitude, chronic anoxia, polycythemia · ↓ anemia, blood loss, destruction, missing nutrients, marrow suppression Hgb F 11.7–15.5 · M 14–17.3 g/dL Measures the severity of anemia or polycythemia and the response to therapy Hct F 36–48% · M 42–52% ↓ anemia, hemodilution, massive blood loss · ↑ polycythemia, dehydration MCV (cell size) 78–100/102 μm³ ↑ macrocytic : pernicious anemia, folic acid deficiency · ↓ microcytic : IDA, lead poisoning MCHC (color) 31–37% ↓ hypochromic: IDA or a hemoglobinopathy WBC 4.5–11.1 ×10³/mm³ ↑ infection, inflammation, leukemia · ↓ prolonged infection, marrow depression. Get a differential Reticulocytes 0.5–2.5% of RBCs Immature RBCs. ↑ = marrow responding to hemolysis or blood loss (or to therapy). ↓ = hypoproliferative marrow (aplastic, pernicious anemia) Platelets 150,000–450,000/mm³ ↓ thrombocytopenia. <50,000: spontaneous bleeding possible. <5,000: fatal CNS or GI bleed possible. ↑ PV, malignancy, after splenectomy Serum iron 30–160 mcg/dL ↓ IDA, hemorrhage. Draw in the MORNING (lower in the evening). No iron supplements for 24 hours before. An overnight fast is recommended Ferritin (iron stores) 20–400 ng/mL ↓ iron deficiency, chronic GI bleed, heavy menses. Infection and inflammation can falsely raise it TIBC 250–410 mcg/dL ↑ in IDA (many empty binding spots). ↓ in hemochromatosis or chronic disease Transferrin sat. 20–50% ↓ in IDA (always low) Hgb electrophoresis Hgb A 95–100% Separates normal from abnormal Hgb. Diagnoses thalassemias and sickle cell (Hgb S 70–98%). Ask about transfusions in the past 4 months Coombs Negative Positive means antibodies on RBCs: hemolytic disease of the newborn, transfusion reaction, autoimmune hemolytic anemia LAP — Enzyme from mature neutrophils. ↑ with infection, stress, inflammation, pregnancy, steroids, leukemia. High neutrophils WITHOUT high LAP suggests CML PT 10–13 sec Extrinsic pathway. Monitors warfarin. Prolonged with low factors or vitamin K deficiency INR 0.9–1.1 (no anticoag) Warfarin goal 2–3. Mechanical valve 2.5–3.5. ↑ with DIC, cirrhosis, vitamin K deficiency PTT / aPTT PTT 60–70 · aPTT 30–40 sec Intrinsic pathway. Monitors heparin (and argatroban) Bleeding time 3–8 min Platelet function. Not done if platelets <75,000. Stop the test if bleeding lasts more than 15 minutes Platelet aggregation — Checks abnormal platelet function. Impaired in von Willebrand disease and with aspirin or NSAIDs Tube colors: CBC uses a lavender (EDTA) tube. PT, INR, and PTT use sodium citrate . Remove the tourniquet as soon as blood flows (to prevent hemolysis), invert tubes gently, and hold pressure until bleeding stops (longer if on an anticoagulant). Class note Iron studies emphasized in class (Diagnostic Studies slide 31) Avoid iron supplements for at least 24 h before the test. Serum ferritin = ability to store iron. Transferrin = iron-transporting capacity. TIBC measures the amount of iron in the plasma. High TIBC = low O₂ saturation. Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase: elevated, \"thing about the bones\" (as written in your notes). With hematological disorders, the bone marrow needs to be examined for diagnosis. Know reticulocyte count, thalassemia, Coombs, clotting studies, bone marrow aspiration vs biopsy."},{"ch":"heme-assess","chTitle":"Assessment of Hematologic Function","id":"heme-assess--bone-marrow-aspiration-and-biopsy","title":"Bone marrow aspiration and biopsy","text":"Why: to diagnose thrombocytopenia, leukemias, anemias, granulomas, and primary or metastatic tumors, to stage Hodgkin's, to evaluate chemo, and to monitor myelosuppression. Aspiration removes fluid. Needle biopsy removes a core of marrow. Usually done together. Most common site: posterior iliac crest , patient prone or side-lying. Sternum or anterior iliac crest: supine. Before: informed consent, allergies, bleeding risk (platelets, coags, anticoagulants). Explain they will feel pressure on insertion and a brief pulling pain on aspiration, and may hear crunching. Takes 5–10 minutes. A mild sedative is OK. After: direct pressure 5–10 minutes and a sterile dressing. Rest several hours. Leave the dressing on ≥24 hours. Report bleeding that soaks the dressing or lasts more than 24 hours. Watch for infection and give analgesia. Bone marrow aspiration from the posterior iliac crest"},{"ch":"heme-assess","chTitle":"Assessment of Hematologic Function","id":"heme-assess--class-only-nursing-tips","title":"Class-only nursing tips","text":"Multiple myeloma: tingling and burning sensation due to hypercalcemia. Patients who get chemo do not drink a lot. Encourage a lot of fluids beforehand or they can vomit. Most common site of oral cancer is the bottom of the tongue . Xerostomia = dry mouth."},{"ch":"heme-assess","chTitle":"Assessment of Hematologic Function","id":"heme-assess--case-marge-starr-25","title":"Case: Marge Starr, 25","text":"Fatigue, SOB, needs afternoon naps. HR 118, BP 90/72, RR 26. Pale skin and nailbeds, red swollen tongue. Labs: RBC 3 million, Hct 31%, Hgb 9, ↓ ferritin. This is iron deficiency anemia . The ↑HR and ↑RR are compensation , as the heart and lungs work harder to deliver O₂."},{"ch":"heme-assess","chTitle":"Assessment of Hematologic Function","id":"heme-assess--more-from-your-first-study-guide","title":"More from your first study guide","text":"White blood cells: the differential ★ (\"know neutrophils\") Cell % Job ↑ / ↓ Neutrophils 50–70 Bacteria, fungi; first to arrive ↑ bacterial infection · ↓ chemo, radiation Eosinophils 1–3 Destroy invading foreign cells; allergies, parasites ↑ allergies, parasites · ↓ steroids Basophils 0.4–1 Release histamine and heparin ↑ allergy, inflammation Lymphocytes 20–35 B cells → antibodies; T cells kill foreign/tumor cells ↑ viral infections · ↓ HIV, cancer, radiation Monocytes 3–8 Largest WBC → macrophages ↑ viral, chronic inflammation Neutrophils remain about 6 hours in circulation; mature = \"segs,\" immature = \"bands.\" ↑ in bacterial infection; ↓ with chemo/radiation → infection risk. Left shift = immature neutrophils (bands) = active infection. The marrow rushes out immature cells (normal bands 3–5%). Bands aren't very effective at fighting infection. WBCs are the least abundant formed element (~1% of blood) but required for life. Normal 4,500–11,000/mm³. WBC differential = the percentage (proportion) of each type of WBC. Plasma proteins and other facts Albumin (60%): made in the liver; keeps fluid in vessels (osmotic pressure). Low albumin = problems with nutrition (also liver disease, burns, hemorrhage) ★ notebook. Globulins transport and help clotting; gamma globulins = antibodies . Fibrinogen → fibrin (clot framework). Plasma = water 90%, proteins 8% (albumin, globulin, fibrinogen), other solutes 2%. Bone marrow = primary site of hematopoiesis; 4–5% of body weight; red marrow in flat bones (sternum, ribs, pelvis, vertebrae). Myeloid → RBCs, WBCs (granulocytes, monocytes), platelets. Lymphoid → T and B lymphocytes. RBCs: made when the kidneys release erythropoietin (EPO) ; need iron, B₁₂, folic acid, B6, protein. Each Hgb carries 4 O₂. Platelets: from megakaryocytes (large marrow cells); 25–40% stored in spleen; live 7–10 days; normal 150,000–450,000 (lecture: 150–400k). Spleen: LUQ \"coffee-bean.\" Filters blood; removes old RBCs and abnormal WBCs and platelets; recycles iron; stores platelets. Splenomegaly causes: anemia, PV, leukemia, cirrhosis, mono. Spleen is normally not palpable. Assessment table: pallor = anemia · petechiae, purpura, bruising = bleeding · glossitis = iron deficiency or pernicious anemia · anemia → ↑HR, ↓ systolic BP, SOB · too many RBCs → ↑BP · hematuria · bone pain. Anemia vital signs (notebook ★): BP↓ HR↑ (and ↑RR). Gestational thrombocytopenia 5–11%. Hematology functions: oxygen delivery · clotting cascade · inflammation and immunity (eliminate cause of injury, remove damaged tissue, generate new tissue). Clotting: 5 steps, pathways, and factors 1 Vasospasm (injured vessel constricts, ↓ flow) → 2 Platelet plug (platelets stick; need von Willebrand factor) → 3 Clot formation (cascade → fibrin mesh) → 4 Clot retraction (platelets contract, pull edges together) → 5 Dissolution (fibrinolysis removes clot after repair). Pathway Trigger Factors Lab Drug Intrinsic (slower) Vessel injury, factors inside the blood XII → XI → IX + VIII → X PTT / aPTT Heparin Extrinsic (faster) Tissue trauma; tissue factor (III) III + VII → X PT / INR Warfarin Common Both meet at factor X X + V → prothrombin (II) → thrombin → fibrinogen (I) → fibrin; XIII cross-links Memory: PITT = PTT-Intrinsic (heparin). PET = PT-Extrinsic-Tissue (warfarin, \"War\"). Vitamin K makes factors II, VII, IX, X. Calcium (factor IV) is needed in all pathways. Factors: I fibrinogen · II prothrombin · III tissue factor (starts extrinsic) · VIII/IX missing in hemophilia A/B (Christmas factor) · XII Hageman (starts intrinsic). Notebook ★: Intrinsic → check PTT . Extrinsic → check PT . Lab extras A tourniquet >1 minute falsely ↑ RBC/Hgb/Hct. Lavender (EDTA) tube for CBC; light blue (citrate) tube for coag studies, filled completely. ANC ≤500 = severe neutropenia → neutropenic precautions. Platelets: <100k precautions · <50k spontaneous bleed risk · <20k transfuse · <5k fatal CNS/GI bleed. MCHC ↑: spherocytosis. Ferritin ↑: liver disease, inflammation. Serum iron ↑: hemochromatosis. INR ↑ = bleeding risk. aPTT ↑: heparin, hemophilia. Coombs also diagnoses transfusion reaction and autoimmune hemolytic anemia. LAP: high neutrophils without high LAP → CML. Ferritin ≤100 indicates IDA (slide wording). Transferrin: low–normal in IDA. TIBC ↑ and transferrin saturation ↓ in IDA. Bus analogy: transferrin is the bus, iron is the passengers. In IDA the bus has lots of empty seats (high TIBC) and few passengers (low saturation). Ferritin is the bus garage, and it's empty. Patient prep for iron studies: avoid iron supplements ≥24 h before · draw serum iron in the morning (levels drop in the evening) · overnight fast recommended. Class note Class vs class Your notes' PLQ answer for \"which result confirms IDA\" is C, increased TIBC (first guide). The newer guide's worked answer was A, decreased transferrin saturation . Both are true of IDA (TIBC ↑, transferrin saturation ↓). If only one is offered, pick it; if both are offered, ask your instructor. Standard chemistry values (ranges vary slightly by lab) Sodium 135–145 mEq/L · Potassium 3.5–5.0 mEq/L · Chloride 98–106 mEq/L · Calcium 9.0–10.5 mg/dL (≈8.6–10.2) · Magnesium 1.3–2.1 mEq/L · Phosphorus 3.0–4.5 mg/dL · BUN 10–20 mg/dL · Creatinine 0.6–1.2 mg/dL · Glucose (fasting) 70–100 mg/dL · Albumin 3.5–5.0 g/dL · Uric acid ≈2.5–7.0 mg/dL. Myeloma hypercalcemia criterion: Ca >11.5 mg/dL; lymphoma hypercalcemia emergency: Ca >11. (Your class electrolyte table is in the Cheat Sheets part.) Connection checks Blood is formed mostly in flat bones · EPO stimulates RBC production · low Hgb → pallor and fatigue · clotting factor deficiency → easy bruising, gum bleeding · inspect mouth/tongue (never deep-palpate the spleen) · differential = proportion of each WBC · thrombocytopenia priority = prevent injury ."},{"ch":"heme-assess","chTitle":"Assessment of Hematologic Function","id":"heme-assess--chapter-33-powerpoint-word-for-word","title":"Chapter 33 PowerPoint, word for word","text":"Your instructor's slides for this chapter, kept in slide order. Slide Slide 1 · What the hematological system is Structure: hematological system – blood, blood cells, lymph & organs · Plasma (clear yellow, protein- rich) · Solutes (proteins, electrolytes, organic elements) Function: blood accounts for 7–10% of body weight; average adult has 5–6 L in the circulatory system Slide Slide 2 · Where blood is made Primary site for blood formation and maturation (hematopoiesis) Involved in immune response & makes up 4–5% of total body weight Produces stem cells that can become any type of blood cell: Myeloid – RBC, WBC, platelets · Lymphoid – T or B lymphocytes Slide Slide 3 · Erythrocytes & hemoglobin Erythrocytes – most abundant, small biconcave disk with no nucleus; pick up O₂ from lungs & transport; pick up CO₂ from tissues & deliver to lungs Hemoglobin – oxygen-carrying component of an RBC; contains thousands of Hgb molecules with a pigment (heme) that contains iron Slide Slide 4 · Leukocytes & the left shift Leukocytes – defense against infection; least abundant (4,500–11,000 WBC) – 1% Granulocytes – protect against harmful microorganisms during acute inflammation/infection Neutrophils – fight bacteria & fungi, cell debris, foreign substances; phagocytic; first cell to arrive at a site; remain 6 hours in circulation Eosinophils – destroy invading foreign cells Diagram of left shift: more immature cells are present in the blood than normal Slide Slide 5 · Thrombocytes Thrombocytes – from large cells in the bone marrow · Activate coagulation factors in the blood plasma · Initiate stable clot formation – fibrin Slide Slide 6 · The liquid part of blood Plasma (clear yellow): complex mixture of water, proteins, nutrients, electrolytes, nitrogenous wastes, hormones, and gases · Extracellular fluid · 90% water; 6.5–8% proteins; 2% other molecular substances Figure: centrifuged blood tube = plasma (55%), buffy coat of WBCs and platelets (< 1%), RBCs (45%). Plasma = water 90%, proteins 8% (albumin, globulin, fibrinogen), other solutes 2% (nutrients, electrolytes, waste products, hormones, gases) Slide Slide 7 · The blood filter & RBC life cycle Spleen: filter · removal of abnormal WBC and platelets Figure (continued_12): RBC life cycle. Red marrow makes erythrocytes → RBCs circulate 120 days → old/damaged RBCs broken down in spleen → hemoglobin splits into heme and globin → globin becomes amino acids; heme becomes iron (recycled to marrow) and bilirubin (to liver → bile) → nutrients, iron, B12, and folic acid from the intestine return to the marrow Slide Slide 8 · Carrying oxygen Hemoglobin transports oxygen (respiration) · Sufficient & balanced RBCs · Excessive increase in RBCs can raise blood viscosity, decreasing O₂ transport Slide Slide 9 · Hemostasis & coagulation pathways Clotting cascade · Coagulation process: intrinsic pathway, extrinsic pathway Figure (continued_2): intrinsic pathway (blood vessel injury; XII → XI → IX + VIII → monitored by PTT) and extrinsic pathway (trauma; tissue factor III + VII → monitored by PT) join at factor X → common pathway: prothrombin (II) → thrombin → fibrinogen (I) → fibrin → XIII cross-links → stable fibrin clot Slide Slide 10 · Blood's defense role Inflammation and immunity: eliminate cause of injury · remove damaged tissue · generate new tissue Slide Slide 11 · Nutrition, PMH & medications History – Nutrition: Any recent changes in energy level? Difficulty maintaining daily activities, recreation, sports? Pain, burning, or tingling? Skin color or temperature changes? Swelling (edema)? Bleeding or easy bruising, dizziness, fatigue, or lymph node changes (swelling, pain/tenderness, warmth)? Past medical history: chronic disease – hepatitis, cirrhosis, cancer, clotting factor · Pregnancy – gestational thrombocytopenia Medication history: anticoagulants, aspirin, salicylates, herbs, nutritional supplements, nutraceuticals Slide Slide 12 · Head-to-toe hematology exam Skin, head and neck: color of skin and mucous membranes; skin temperature, capillary refill, edema; palpate skin, lymph nodes; inspect oral mucous membranes and skin; inspect tongue Respiratory: rate, rhythm, depth, sound; do the lungs work harder to increase O₂ delivery? Cardiovascular: vital signs – BP, HR, R, SaO₂ abnormal? Auscultation – murmurs, gallops, irregular rhythms Renal and urinary: urinary meatus for bleeding; urine specimen – hematuria? Musculoskeletal: abdominal pain – hyper/hypoproliferative disorders (sickle cell, anemia, leukemia); bone pain – leukemia (WBC) increases pressure in bone; sickle cell (RBC) – pain, tissue infarctions & swelling; ROM – joint pain, swelling Abdominal: inspection – visible masses, abdominal distention, change in bowel & bladder pattern; palpation – light palpation LUQ Figure (continued_9): nurse lightly palpating the left upper abdomen for the spleen Slide Slide 13 · The CBC Complete blood count: hemoglobin and hematocrit · red blood cells · white blood cells · platelets Slide Slide 14 · Reticulocytes, electrophoresis, LAP, Coombs Reticulocyte count (retic cells are larger than mature RBCs) – useful in evaluating anemia · Increase – response to hemolysis or blood loss for more RBCs · Decrease – hypoproliferative bone marrow (anemias) Hemoglobin electrophoresis: separates and measures normal and abnormal Hgb · helps diagnose thalassemias – absent or reduced production of Hgb alpha or beta Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase – elevated from infection, stress, inflammation, pregnancy, steroid use & leukemia · enzyme made by normal mature neutrophils · venipuncture Coombs test: presence of immunoglobulins on RBCs – antibodies · used to diagnose hemolytic disease of the newborn Slide Slide 15 · Iron studies Iron (instruct patient to avoid taking iron supplements at least 24 h) · Serum ferritin – ability to store iron · Transferrin – iron-transporting capacity · Total iron-binding capacity – measures the amount of iron in the plasma – high TIBC = low O₂ saturation Slide Slide 16 · PT, INR, PTT, bleeding time, aggregation Prothrombin time – fibrin to clot · Bleeding time · International Normalized Ratio – oral anticoagulants · Partial thromboplastin time · Platelet aggregation – tests abnormal platelet function Slide Slide 17 · Bone marrow aspiration & biopsy Examination of bone marrow (to diagnose thrombocytopenia, leukemias, granulomas, anemias, primary and metastatic tumors) · Bone marrow aspiration – removal of fluid · Bone marrow biopsy – specimen; soft tissue · Needle biopsy – removal of a core of marrow cells Figure (continued_8): patient lying on side; needle and syringe inserted through skin and cortical bone into spongy bone and marrow at the posterior iliac crest of the hip bone"},{"ch":"heme-disorders","chTitle":"Hematologic Disorders","id":"heme-disorders--hematologic-disorders","title":"Hematologic Disorders","text":"The four components are RBCs, WBCs, platelets, and plasma. Most disorders come from having too many or too few of one cell type. Anemia means reduced O₂-carrying capacity (fewer RBCs or less Hgb)."},{"ch":"heme-disorders","chTitle":"Hematologic Disorders","id":"heme-disorders--iron-deficiency-anemia-ida-rbc-disorder","title":"Iron deficiency anemia (IDA) · RBC disorder","text":"Why it matters / risk factors The most prevalent nutritional deficiency in the world . Seen most in infants, children, pregnant people, and lower socioeconomic groups. More common in females. About 10% more anemia after age 65. Inadequate dietary iron is the #1 cause. Needs rise during pregnancy and growth. Hemorrhage and chronic blood loss: heavy menses, GI cancers (esophagus, colon, stomach), peptic ulcer disease. Poor absorption: celiac, Crohn's, gastric bypass or gastrectomy, long-term PPIs, H2 blockers, antacids, aspirin, NSAIDs. Socioeconomic: lead paint. Pica (craving ice, dirt, clay, paint) usually stops with iron replacement. IDA is often one of the earliest signs of another disease (such as GI cancer), so the cause must be worked up. Normal menses should not cause IDA. Patho Iron is needed to make hemoglobin. Without it, Hgb can't form, so not enough O₂ is carried. Iron is stored in the liver as ferritin and transported by transferrin . IDA means the iron stores are exhausted. It develops slowly in stages: negative iron balance → depletion → deficient RBC production → IDA. Anemia is a LATE sign of iron deficiency (except with hemorrhage). Clinical manifestations ★ Hypoxia from low Hgb causes fatigue, pallor, SOB . Tachycardia and tachypnea → why: the heart and lungs try to compensate for hypoxemia. Glossitis: painful, swollen, smooth, shiny, bright red tongue from flattened papillae. Also cracks at the corners of the mouth. Koilonychia: spoon-shaped nails after severe, prolonged deficiency. Changes in LOC, cognitive impairment (in children it can be permanent), pica. Distinguishing features: glossitis plus spoon nails, microcytic and hypochromic cells, ↓ferritin with ↑TIBC. No neuro symptoms (which separates it from B12 deficiency). Glossitis (red, swollen, shiny tongue) in IDA Koilonychia (spoon-shaped, thin nails) in IDA Diagnosis CBC: ↓Hgb and Hct, ↓MCV (microcytic), ↓MCHC (hypochromic). Often the first sign. Iron panel (needed to confirm): ↓iron, ↓ferritin (stores), transferrin low or normal, transferrin saturation ALWAYS low, TIBC HIGH (empty binding spots). No single lab makes the diagnosis; it takes the whole picture. Iron and ferritin can fluctuate with inflammation. SLIDE vs BOOK: slide says ferritin \"<100 ug/dL indicates IDA.\" Book says \"serum ferritin <10 mcg/L indicates IDA.\" Ask which number your instructor wants. Treatment Diet first. Heme iron: red meat, eggs. Non-heme iron: dark green leafy vegetables (spinach, broccoli, peas), beets, dried beans and legumes, nuts, fortified cereals and breads, Cream of Wheat. Vitamin C (oranges, grapefruit, citrus juice) increases absorption. Oral iron is first line. Take with citrus juice and NOT with milk or dairy → why: dairy binds iron and blocks absorption. Parenteral iron is for severe GI distress, malabsorption, or when levels must rise fast. IM iron uses the Z-track method → why: prevents leakage and skin staining. Old iron dextran was cardiotoxic. Newer IV forms are safer, but still monitor vital signs during the infusion. Side effects and complications Oral iron: nausea, abdominal discomfort, constipation and/or diarrhea. The patient should report these. Complications: heart failure, renal failure, impaired thermoregulation and immunity, psychomotor and cognitive impairment in children. In pregnancy: low birth weight, preterm labor, maternal or infant death. Iron overload (from pica or many transfusions, not usually oral pills): fatigue, palpitations, joint pain, GI upset, heart and artery damage. Nursing Dx: inadequate tissue perfusion · fatigue · activity intolerance Assess: vital signs (tachycardia and tachypnea are compensation), Hgb and ferritin, fatigue, pallor, SOB, LOC, blood loss, and the cause (diet, IBD, malabsorption). Actions: ↑dietary iron and vitamin C, give iron supplements, minimize blood loss (control any hemorrhage; hormones for heavy menses). Teach: iron food sources, take daily supplements as prescribed, report GI side effects, dangers of lead, prenatal iron. Outcomes: normal Hgb and ferritin, better perfusion, HR and RR back to normal."},{"ch":"heme-disorders","chTitle":"Hematologic Disorders","id":"heme-disorders--other-rbc-disorders-tell-them-apart","title":"Other RBC disorders: tell them apart ★","text":"Disorder Patho / cause Signature manifestations (beyond fatigue, pallor, ↑HR, ↑RR) Key labs Treatment and watch-outs Iron deficiency Iron stores exhausted, so Hgb can't be made Glossitis, spoon nails, pica Microcytic, ↓ferritin, ↑TIBC Iron plus vitamin C. Avoid dairy with doses. Z-track for IM Vitamin B₁₂ (pernicious) B₁₂ needs intrinsic factor (from stomach parietal cells) to be absorbed in the ileum . Pernicious anemia = autoimmune loss of intrinsic factor. Risks: older adults (gastric atrophy), GI resection, PPIs and H2 blockers, alcohol, vegans and vegetarians (B₁₂ only in animal foods) NEURO: symmetric paresthesias of hands and feet (first neuro sign), Lhermitte's sign (electric shock with neck flexion), confusion, depression, dementia, poor balance (especially in the dark), yellow-blue color blindness, tinnitus, glossitis. Pernicious anemia: vitiligo, early gray hair Macrocytic (↑MCV). Serum B₁₂ (200–1,000 pg/mL). ↑MMA (more sensitive) with normal homocysteine. Schilling test (rare) Diet (meat, seafood, eggs, dairy) or oral supplement. Severe deficiency needs weekly B₁₂ injections . ⚠ Neuro damage can become irreversible. ⚠ Folic acid can mask B₁₂ deficiency (fixes the anemia while the nerves keep degenerating). ⚠ Nitrous oxide can cause profound, possibly fatal postop anemia Folic acid Absorbed in the jejunum . Alcohol, malnutrition, gastric bypass or Whipple, meds (OCPs, metformin, antineoplastics) interfere Mood changes, bleeding risk (pancytopenia). NO lasting neuro deficits ; confusion is reversible Macrocytic. ↑Homocysteine with NORMAL MMA (the reverse of B₁₂). Fasting serum folate. RBC folate reflects the last 3 months Supplement. ≥400 mcg daily in pregnancy prevents neural tube defects. ⚠ Folic acid speeds phenytoin metabolism and can cause seizures, so correct the deficiency before starting phenytoin Sickle cell disease Genetic Hgb S . RBCs sickle with low O₂, becoming stiff and fragile, lodging in capillaries and forming thrombi. RBC life is 15–20 days. Triggers: hypoxemia, dehydration, cold, infection, high altitude Pain (joints, bones, chest, abdomen), swelling, jaundice, splenomegaly, fever, poor wound healing, hand-foot syndrome, priapism , infections (pneumococcus, H. influenzae). Symptoms start after 6 months of age (fetal Hgb protects until then) Hgb electrophoresis (Hgb S). ↑bilirubin from hemolysis. ↓retics in aplastic crisis Hydroxyurea (makes fetal Hgb; ⚠ myelosuppression, teratogenic, so counsel on contraception). Transfusions (⚠ iron overload, so give chelation such as desferrioxamine). Aggressive hydration , analgesia (often opioids at higher doses), O₂ only if critically low. Stem cell transplant is the only cure. Vaccines: pneumococcal and flu. ⚠ Crises: vaso-occlusive (most common), aplastic, sequestration (shock), hemolytic. ⚠ Acute chest syndrome, stroke Aplastic anemia Marrow fails (infection, chemicals, myelotoxins), so ALL 3 cell lines drop (pancytopenia) Anemia signs plus frequent infections (↓WBC) plus bruising, nosebleeds, petechiae, gum bleeding (↓platelets). Headache CBC shows pancytopenia with ↓retics. Bone marrow is mostly fat cells. ↑Hgb F Transfusions, growth factors (filgrastim, epoetin), immunosuppressants. Stem cell transplant for severe disease (keep transfusions to a minimum to avoid sensitization). ⚠ Mortality >70% from hemorrhage and infection G6PD deficiency Missing enzyme causes RBCs to hemolyze early Jaundice, dark urine (bilirubin), splenomegaly. Neonatal jaundice and kernicterus ↑retics, Heinz bodies on the smear, DNA testing Avoid triggering foods and drugs. Transfuse for severe hemolysis. Folic acid. ⚠ Kidney damage Polycythemia See next section Pruritus (after a warm bath), facial flushing, headache, splenomegaly ↑RBC, Hgb, Hct Phlebotomy Size clue Microcytic = iron deficiency (and lead). Macrocytic = B₁₂ or folate. To tell B₁₂ from folate, check MMA: ↑MMA = B₁₂ , while ↑homocysteine with normal MMA = folate . Neuro symptoms mean B₁₂."},{"ch":"heme-disorders","chTitle":"Hematologic Disorders","id":"heme-disorders--polycythemia-vera-pv-too-many-rbcs","title":"Polycythemia vera (PV) · too many RBCs","text":"Why it matters Rare: 5 per 1 million per year. More common in males, usually after age 60. JAK2 gene mutation in 90% (acquired after birth). Radiation exposure also raises risk. Primary (PV): a bone marrow disorder that overproduces RBCs AND WBCs AND platelets . Secondary: a response to hypoxia (high altitude, smoking, COPD, heart failure) through ↑erythropoietin, or an \"inappropriate\" response from kidney disease or EPO-secreting tumors. WBCs and platelets stay NORMAL. Treat the cause (e.g., stop smoking). Patho Too many RBCs make the blood thick ( hyperviscosity ). Circulation slows and the heart labors, so even with more RBCs, tissue perfusion and O₂ delivery FALL (hypoxemia). The slow flow causes clots , and the abnormal platelets mean bleeding too. Lab definition: Hct >48% (women) or >52% (men). Hgb >16.5 (women) or >18.5 (men) g/dL. RBC >6 million. WHO diagnosis: Hgb >16.5 (men) or >16.0 (women). Clinical manifestations ★ Slowly progressive, often found on routine labs. Caused by hyperviscosity, hypervolemia, and capillary engorgement. SOB, difficulty breathing lying flat Headache, dizziness, weakness, fatigue Blurred vision or blind spots Splenomegaly (about 75%), with fullness in the LUQ, early satiety, and weight loss Generalized pruritus, especially after a WARM bath (aquagenic) Flushing of the face (ruddy \"plethora\") Nosebleeds and bleeding gums. Angina and claudication. Diagnosis CBC: RBC >6 million, Hct can exceed 60%. ↑WBCs and platelets in PV (normal in secondary). Bone marrow biopsy: panmyelosis (overproduction of all 3 lines). Genetic testing: JAK2. Erythropoietin level: ↑ in secondary, low in PV . Uric acid: ↑ from rapid cell turnover, which can cause gout and kidney stones. Treatment: reduce viscosity, prevent hemorrhage Therapeutic phlebotomy: remove 350–500 mL weekly until Hct <45%, then as needed. Patients feel better right away as organ congestion drops. Hydroxyurea or interferon (or radiation) suppress the marrow when WBCs and platelets are high. Phlebotomy does not lower those. Low-dose aspirin or anagrelide reduce thrombosis. Medications for itching. Watch THROMBOSIS (the main killer): MI, stroke, PE, DVT. Sudden chest pain, headache, or SOB needs immediate attention. Hemorrhage (GI) from abnormal platelets. Phlebotomy: going too fast causes hypovolemia (↓BP, ↑HR, nausea), so check vital signs. Repeated phlebotomy causes IDA, so monitor ferritin. Late: myelofibrosis, transformation to AML, heart failure. Chemo and radiation carry a small risk of secondary cancer. Nursing actions, and why Dx: ineffective tissue perfusion · activity intolerance · risk for ineffective coping Routine CBCs: keep Hct <45% (men), <42% (women) during phlebotomy, and monitor myelosuppression if on chemo. Increase fluid intake → why: dilutes the thick blood. Adequate rest → why: the heart is working hard. Elevate legs when sitting → why: helps venous return and prevents clots from stasis. Modify CV risk factors: stop smoking, no crossed legs or restrictive clothing, DVT prophylaxis. Teach: signs of MI, stroke, and PE and to seek help immediately; why aggressive daily hydration matters; small frequent meals (abdominal fullness); avoid tight clothing and extreme temperatures; tepid baths ≤30 minutes, mild soap, pat dry, lotion, colloidal oatmeal; bleeding precautions; regular moderate exercise; regular labs; genetic testing."},{"ch":"heme-disorders","chTitle":"Hematologic Disorders","id":"heme-disorders--thrombocytopenia-and-bleeding-disorders-platelets-150-000","title":"Thrombocytopenia and bleeding disorders · platelets <150,000","text":"Why it matters A complication or syndrome, not a disease by itself. It comes from ↓platelet production, ↑platelet use, or ↑destruction. Low platelets are the most rapidly developing life-threatening cytopenia because of the bleeding risk. Risk factors: malignancy, infection, meds (sulfa), autoimmune disease (SLE), DIC, African descent, obesity. ITP female:male is 2:1, peaking at ages 20–30. Gestational thrombocytopenia occurs in 6–15% of pregnancies (mild, resolves postpartum). Acute ITP (children 2–6): rapid onset, resolves in weeks to 6 months. Chronic ITP (women 15–40): insidious and rarely remits. Clinical manifestations (all types) ★ Easy bruising (ecchymosis) and PETECHIAE are the FIRST signs. Petechiae do not blanch. Nosebleeds, bleeding gums, GI bleeding (black tarry stools), hematuria. Wet purpura (bruising of the buccal mucosa) is an early sign of worsening and usually needs treatment. Severe: bleeding into vital organs such as the brain can be fatal. Thresholds: start bleeding precautions <100,000, especially <50,000. Spontaneous bleeding <20,000, and platelets are usually transfused below 20,000 (or before procedures). The 4 causes: tell them apart ★ Patho Diagnosis Treatment and ⚠ ITP (immune / idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura) Antiplatelet antibodies (IgG) cause the spleen to destroy platelets in hours instead of their normal 7–10 days. In children it follows a viral infection or immunization (70% have a virus 1–3 weeks before). In adults it can be drug-induced or autoimmune CBC (<150,000). Usually a clinical diagnosis after ruling out other causes. Bone marrow biopsy if needed. Platelets >100,000: just observe Corticosteroids (↓antibodies, ↓phagocytosis). IVIG (near-immediate rise). Rituximab (↓B cells). Splenectomy in extreme cases (the spleen is where platelets are destroyed) Hemophilia X-linked , so it shows up in males. A female carrier has a 50% chance of passing it to each son. A = factor VIII, B = factor IX (Christmas disease). Minor injuries bleed a lot. Hemarthrosis (bleeding into joints and muscles) causes deformity Factor VIII and IX levels Replace the missing factor IV (recombinant, no human blood) as soon as possible after injury: up to 3 daily doses for minor injuries, 7–10 days for major trauma or surgery DIC Secondary to severe trauma (crush, burns, sepsis, shock), OB complications (abruption, retained products), extensive surgery, cancers (leukemia, widespread mets), chemo. Platelets and clotting factors are used up forming clots everywhere, then the patient bleeds from every orifice. Microclots cause organ necrosis ↑PT, PTT, and thrombin time, ↓fibrinogen, ↑fibrin split products and D-dimer Treat the cause (e.g., antibiotics for sepsis). Give blood, platelets, FFP, and cryoprecipitate (rich in factor VIII). ICU care. ⚠ High mortality HIT (heparin-induced thrombocytopenia) Immune system makes antibodies against heparin, usually 5–14 days after it is started. Platelets are consumed making NEW CLOTS, so the patient usually clots rather than bleeds . Heparin flushes of central lines may contribute Falling platelets while on heparin, with a new or enlarging thrombus STOP heparin first. Give a different anticoagulant ( argatroban , lepirudin). ⚠ No warfarin (causes skin gangrene). ⚠ No platelet transfusion (feeds more clots) Nursing actions, and why Dx: risk of injury (bleeding) · fear · ineffective regimen management Assess platelet count, ecchymosis, petechiae and purpura, frank bleeding. In pregnancy, check LFTs and platelets and watch for postpartum hemorrhage. Minimize blood loss: direct pressure and/or ice for ≥5 minutes → why: vasoconstriction stops bleeding. Avoid IM injections → why: bleeding into the muscle is hard to control. No rectal temps, enemas, suppositories, or douches → why: they can tear thin mucosa. Safe, clutter-free room → why: prevents bruising. Minimal BP cuff inflation , only until the pulse disappears, and avoid automatic cuffs → why: overinflation bruises. Minimize blood draws (consider a venous access device). Box 34.1 Bleeding precautions ★ Soft-bristle toothbrush or gauze. No flossing. Electric razor only. Shoes or slippers when out of bed (no bare feet). Clutter-free environment. Daily stool softener (no straining). No rectal thermometers, suppositories, enemas, or douches. No sexual intercourse when platelets are very low (slide: <50,000). Do not blow your nose. Use a sharp knife (a dull one needs more force). For a cut: direct pressure and/or ice ≥5 minutes. Keep IM injections, arterial sticks, and blood draws to a minimum. Avoid dental cleanings while platelets are low (case study). Know the emergency signs to report."},{"ch":"heme-disorders","chTitle":"Hematologic Disorders","id":"heme-disorders--leukemia-wbc-malignancy","title":"Leukemia · WBC malignancy","text":"Why it matters A malignant bone marrow disease in which leukocytes multiply uncontrollably. Fatal if untreated. Named by stem cell line and speed of onset: AML and ALL have an acute, sudden onset. ALL is the most common childhood leukemia. CML and CLL can be present for years but eventually turn acute. Most common in White males over 60. Risk factors: genetic anomalies ( Philadelphia chromosome , a 9;22 swap, in >90% of CML), Down syndrome (10–30× risk), radiation or benzene (the oil refinery in the case study), prior chemo or radiation, smoking. Patho An unknown stimulus mutates a myeloid or lymphoid stem cell. It clones itself into immature blasts that never mature and don't undergo apoptosis, producing leukocytosis . Marrow, spleen, and lymph tissue become congested with blasts: lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, mucosal infiltration. The crowded marrow can't make enough RBCs and platelets, causing ANEMIA and THROMBOCYTOPENIA . Blasts can't fight infection, causing neutropenia and infections. Only cells that escape into the blood show up on a CBC, so early diagnosis is hard. Clinical manifestations ★ Swollen lymph glands (neck, axillae, groin) and a swollen, painful LUQ (spleen) Increased infections and frequent low-grade fevers (from cytokines) Anemia: fatigue, pallor, weakness, SOB (worse on exertion or lying flat), dizziness Bleeding: bruising, petechiae, nosebleeds, bleeding gums Bone pain (marrow swelling in vertebrae, ribs, pelvis, long bones), gait changes Unintentional weight loss (high metabolic demand) It often presents as anemia or thrombocytopenia and may be found on a routine CBC in a patient with no symptoms. It can look like the flu. Diagnosis CBC: leukocytosis plus anemia plus thrombocytopenia. History and physical. Flow cytometry identifies the WBC markers and type. Can be run on blood if blasts are high; otherwise done on marrow. Bone marrow aspirate and biopsy confirm the type and extent. Genetic testing finds chromosome abnormalities that guide treatment. Treatment Chemotherapy (IV, intrathecal, oral, in combinations) in phases: induction (get remission) → consolidation (treat hidden disease) → maintenance (prevent relapse). Remission ≠ cure. CNS involvement is treated with radiation to the brain and spine → why: chemo can't cross the blood–brain barrier. Supportive transfusions of RBCs and platelets. Bone marrow / stem cell transplant for refractory or relapsed disease. Donor cells come from a pelvic marrow harvest or peripheral pheresis after growth factor. ⚠ GVHD. Immunotherapy: monoclonal antibodies, and CAR-T (the patient's T cells are engineered to kill leukemia cells; the effect is permanent, and patients may live without B cells). G-CSF growth factors (filgrastim) are generally not indicated in leukemia → why: they may fuel the disease, since it comes from hematopoietic stem cells (although the slide case did give filgrastim). Complications (mostly from treatment) MYELOSUPPRESSION from chemo or radiation leads to neutropenia, which brings a high risk of life-threatening infection. Neutropenic precautions when ANC ≤500. Fever in a patient on chemo means broad-spectrum antibiotics IMMEDIATELY. Fever is often the first and only sign of infection, and infection is the most common cause of death besides the disease itself. Hemorrhage from thrombocytopenia. Report bruising, petechiae, nosebleeds, conjunctival hemorrhage, bleeding gums, hemoptysis, hematuria, or GI bleeding right away. Anemia needs frequent RBC transfusions. Long-term organ damage: renal failure, cardiomyopathy, pulmonary fibrosis, neurocognitive and liver damage. Sterility. Nursing Dx: risk for infection · risk for impaired tissue perfusion · risk for bleeding · risk for compromised family coping Assess: vital signs (fever), anemia signs, bruising and petechiae (non-blanching), CBC and WBC. Actions: give chemo as prescribed. Neutropenic precautions. Prophylactic antibiotics, antivirals, and antifungals. IV antibiotics immediately if ANC <500 with infection signs. Symptom management (antiemetics, antidiarrheals, mouth sores). Blood products. Teach: neutropenic and bleeding precautions, signs of anemia and thrombocytopenia, the disease course, chemo and radiation reactions, sterility risk (bank sperm or eggs before therapy) . Outcomes: remission, fewer complications, less anemia, infection, and bleeding. Box 34.2 Neutropenic precautions ★ Frequent hand washing or alcohol sanitizer, for everyone. Avoid crowds. Wear a mask if going out. Avoid sick people, small children, and pets. Wash raw fruits and vegetables well. Avoid raspberries and blackberries (bumpy and hard to wash). Check temperature daily and call for >100.4°F (38°C) . For fever, rigors, or illness, seek care immediately (IV antibiotics prevent sepsis). No live plants or cut flowers (bacteria, mold), at home or in the hospital. No standing water (humidifiers). Take prophylactic antimicrobials daily and finish the whole course. Private room if hospitalized. No rectal temps, suppositories, or enemas → why: rectal bacteria can enter the blood."},{"ch":"heme-disorders","chTitle":"Hematologic Disorders","id":"heme-disorders--malignant-lymphomas-hodgkin-s-vs-non-hodgkin-s","title":"Malignant lymphomas · Hodgkin's vs non-Hodgkin's","text":"Why it matters Hodgkin's lymphoma is about 14% of lymphomas. Defined by Reed–Sternberg cells (giant lymphocytes) and has a better survival rate . Peaks in young adulthood and again after 55. Risks: Epstein–Barr virus, a first-degree relative with it. Non-Hodgkin's lymphoma has dozens of B- and T-cell subtypes and causes about 4% of cancers and 4% of cancer deaths (1 in 50 lifetime). More common in males. Risks: pesticides (farming), autoimmune disease, organ transplant (immunosuppressants), HIV, EBV, HHV-8, H. pylori, prior chemo or radiation. Patho and staging A single progenitor cell in a lymph node becomes a solid tumor that evades apoptosis. It collects where lymphocytes gather (nodes, mediastinum), spreads through lymph tissue and the spleen, and then reaches other organs. Spread beyond nodes is called extranodal . Staging (WHO): I = one node · II = ≥2 nodes on the same side of the diaphragm · III = both sides · IV = organs plus nodes. \"E\" = extranodal, \"S\" = spleen, plus bulky disease. Clinical manifestations ★ PAINLESS swelling of lymph nodes (cervical, axillary, inguinal, breast) \"B symptoms\": low-grade fevers with no clear cause, DRENCHING NIGHT SWEATS , unintentional weight loss. These signal a heavier disease burden. Fatigue, anemia, neutropenia, thrombocytopenia Generalized pruritus with excoriation, common in Hodgkin's Dry, persistent cough that is worse lying flat from a mediastinal mass, which can compress the trachea (airway risk) SOB and fullness when the spleen or liver is involved, or the vena cava or spine is compressed Diagnosis History and physical. CBC (marrow involvement). Uric acid and LDH are ↑ from high cell turnover. Viral testing rules out infection. Imaging: chest x-ray, CT, PET for extent and staging. Lymph node biopsy is definitive. Bone marrow biopsy if marrow involvement is suspected. Lumbar puncture if CNS involvement is suspected. Treatment Chemotherapy is the primary treatment , plus radiation and/or immunotherapy. Lymphoma is sensitive and responds quickly. Surgery to remove the tumor is not typically done. Splenectomy is now rare. Indolent (slow) lymphoma may get a wait-and-see approach. Stem cell transplant for relapse or poor response. Autologous if the marrow is not involved (\"rescue\" after high-dose therapy). Complications Superior vena cava syndrome ⚠ EMERGENCY: edema of the face and arms, SOB, chest pain, dysphagia. Spinal cord compression ⚠ EMERGENCY: neuro deficits, paralysis. Hypercalcemia (>11 mg/dL) ⚠ EMERGENCY: from bone mets, causing dehydration, polyuria, confusion, heart block. Myelodysplasia, hepatic or renal failure, hyperviscosity, venous thrombosis (leg pain or swelling). Nursing Dx: risk for infection · activity intolerance · risk for ineffective coping Assess: vital signs and B symptoms · pain, breathing, face and neck (SVC) · neuro status (cord compression) · nodes · skin (excoriation from itching) · CBC · lower extremities (clot) · serum calcium · activity tolerance. Actions: blood products, chemo, IV fluids → why: replace losses from fevers and sweats and dilute calcium , ease itching and night sweats, routine CBCs, emotional support. Teach: disease, infection risk, keep the treatment schedule, rest often, nutrition, report SOB, facial or arm swelling, chest pain, fever, or neuro changes, infertility and fertility preservation. Outcomes: quality of life, no bleeding, no infection."},{"ch":"heme-disorders","chTitle":"Hematologic Disorders","id":"heme-disorders--multiple-myeloma-plasma-cell-cancer-incurable","title":"Multiple myeloma · plasma cell cancer, incurable","text":"Why it matters 13% of hematologic cancers, about 5–7 per 100,000 per year. Incurable B-cell malignancy. Average age 62. 2× more common in people of African descent, and more common in males. Mean survival about 3 years (46.6% alive at 5 years). Risk factors: herbicides, insecticides, petroleum, heavy metals, plastics, radiation. Precursors: MGUS (monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance) and solitary plasmacytoma. Patho One clone of plasma cells (mature B cells that make antibodies) overgrows the marrow and pumps out monoclonal immunoglobulin (IgG or IgA), called the \"M protein\" (a monoclonal gammopathy). An osteoclast and osteoblast imbalance causes lytic bone lesions , which release calcium (hypercalcemia). Excess light chains ( Bence Jones protein in urine) are directly toxic to the kidney. Clinical manifestations ★ BONE PAIN, especially the BACK (and hips) , from lytic destruction Fatigue, weakness, pallor (normocytic anemia) Recurrent infections Weight loss Paresthesia, numbness and tingling (nerve root compression) Hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, renal stones 6 cardinal features: lytic bone disease · M protein · anemia · renal insufficiency · hypercalcemia · infection tendency Diagnosis: CRAB ★ C hyperCalcemia (>11.5 mg/dL) R Renal insufficiency (creatinine >2 mg/dL) A Anemia (Hgb <10 g/dL) B Bone disease (lytic lesions, osteopenia, pathologic fractures) Plasma cells ≥10% in marrow, M protein in serum or urine. Serum and urine protein electrophoresis, free light chains, Bence Jones protein in urine, β2-microglobulin (tumor burden), CBC, Ca, creatinine, uric acid. Bone marrow aspiration, skeletal survey, bone density. MRI if there is back pain (rule out cord compression). Treatment Bisphosphonates → why: reduce bone breakdown, so fewer fractures and lower calcium. Radiation DVT prophylaxis (thrombus prophylaxis) Dexamethasone (corticosteroid) High-dose chemo: thalidomide, lenalidomide, bortezomib. ⚠ Thalidomide and lenalidomide are teratogenic . Autologous stem cell transplant Complications Pathologic fractures. Sudden severe pain, or pain in a new spot, especially the back, may mean a fracture or vertebral collapse. Spinal cord compression causing numbness, tingling, and motor changes. Hypercalcemia. Renal failure (Bence Jones protein, stones). Anemia, marrow failure, thrombocytopenia and bleeding, infection. Chemo side effects. Bisphosphonates and denosumab can cause osteonecrosis of the jaw with long-term use (Ch 13). Nursing Dx: acute or chronic pain · impaired mobility · risk for infection · risk for injury (fractures) · impaired urinary output · fear Assess: vital signs, fatigue, pain, paresthesias, I&O, serum and urine calcium, BUN and creatinine → why: kidney damage from light chains and calcium. Actions: chemo, bisphosphonates, pain meds. Encourage fluids → why: dilute calcium and protect the kidneys. Position for comfort (eases nerve compression). Encourage ambulation → why: weight-bearing builds bone and prevents DVT and deconditioning. Teach: report fatigue, SOB, dizziness, or confusion · sudden severe pain · changes in sensation or motor function · nonpharmacologic pain management. Outcomes: pain controlled, optimal renal function, no infection."},{"ch":"heme-disorders","chTitle":"Hematologic Disorders","id":"heme-disorders--leukemia-vs-lymphoma-vs-myeloma","title":"Leukemia vs lymphoma vs myeloma ★","text":"Leukemia Lymphoma Multiple myeloma Cell / site WBC stem cell in the bone marrow (blasts) Lymphocyte in a lymph node (solid tumor) Plasma cell (mature B) in the marrow, making M protein Hallmark sign Anemia, bleeding, and infection from a crowded marrow. Bone pain Painless nodes plus B symptoms (fever, drenching night sweats, weight loss), itching (Hodgkin's) Back and bone pain, CRAB, Bence Jones protein Key risk Benzene and radiation, Down syndrome, prior chemo, smoking EBV (Hodgkin's), pesticides, HIV, transplant (NHL) Herbicides, petroleum, heavy metals, MGUS, African descent Definitive dx Flow cytometry, bone marrow biopsy Lymph node biopsy Marrow plasma cells ≥10%, M protein, CRAB Main treatment Chemo (induction → consolidation → maintenance), transplant, CAR-T Chemo ± radiation and immunotherapy (surgery not typical) Bisphosphonates, dexamethasone, chemo, autologous transplant, DVT prophylaxis Big danger Infection (neutropenia), hemorrhage SVC syndrome, cord compression, hypercalcemia Fractures, renal failure, hypercalcemia, cord compression"},{"ch":"heme-disorders","chTitle":"Hematologic Disorders","id":"heme-disorders--case-mr-owens-62-aml","title":"Case: Mr. Owens, 62, AML","text":"He has worked in an oil refinery since his teens ( benzene, his highest risk factor ) and smoked for 40+ years. Fatigue, SOB on stairs, bleeding gums. CBC: RBC 3.2, Hgb 9.4, Hct 28.1, WBC 6.2, platelets 97,000. A bone marrow biopsy confirms AML, treated with daunorubicin + cytarabine (Ara-C) . Two weeks later: Hgb 7.9, ANC 0.6 (severe neutropenia), platelets 19,000. He gets PRBCs, platelets, filgrastim, and prophylactic antibiotics, antivirals, and antifungals. Remission follows. Priority after chemo: INFECTION (ANC 0.6). Go to the ED for: temperature 102°F, chills, a nosebleed lasting more than 10 minutes. Teaching gap: \"I have an appointment to get my teeth cleaned.\" SLIDE vs BOOK: platelets were 19,000 on the slide and 12,000 in the book."},{"ch":"heme-disorders","chTitle":"Hematologic Disorders","id":"heme-disorders--class-notes-add-on-nur-291","title":"Class notes add-on (NUR 291)","text":"Class note Iron deficiency anemia Most prevalent nutritional deficiency in the world; risks include poor diet, hemorrhage, chronic blood loss, GI problems, lead paint. Ferritin less than 100 indicates IDA (slide). Know the diet: red meat, green leafy vegetables, vitamin C, oranges, grapefruit. Iron is taken with vitamin C on an empty stomach for best absorption. (class only) Class note Polycythemia vera (all purple in your notes) Hyperviscosity: the blood is thick, increased production of RBCs. Risk for blood clot and stroke. Presents with SOB, orthopnea (difficulty breathing lying flat), swollen spleen / splenomegaly, headache, dizziness, blurred vision. Diagnosis: labs, bone marrow testing, genetic testing. Treatment from class: reduce hyperviscosity, prevent hemorrhage, aggressive IV fluid hydration . (class only: IV) Class note Thrombocytopenia Number 1 side effect is bleeding. Most common medication cause: Bactrim (class only; the guide says \"sulfa,\" and Bactrim is a sulfa drug). Higher risk for women. Class note Leukemia and lymphoma Leukemia: teach neutropenic precautions (Box 34.2). Lymphoma manifestations: painless swelling of lymph nodes, low-grade fever. Lymphoma complication: superior vena cava syndrome. Class note Multiple myeloma and pain Think calcium (hypercalcemia, CRAB). Medical management: bisphosphonates, e.g. Boniva (ibandronate) (class only: Boniva by name). Nursing: encourage fluids , because the extra calcium can cause stones. Pain: know your pain medications. Know slide 22: why we use opioids vs nonopioids. Nonopioids (acetaminophen, NSAIDs) for mild pain and anti-inflammation; opioids for moderate to severe pain; in cancer, use the WHO ladder and dose around the clock."},{"ch":"heme-disorders","chTitle":"Hematologic Disorders","id":"heme-disorders--more-from-your-first-study-guide","title":"More from your first study guide","text":"Iron deficiency anemia Iron-rich foods: heme iron: red meat, liver, eggs, clams, oysters . Nonheme: dark green leafy vegetables (spinach, broccoli, peas), beets, dried beans/legumes, lima beans, nuts, seeds, fortified cereals/breads, Cream of Wheat. Boost absorption: vitamin C: oranges, grapefruits, citrus juice, peppers, strawberries, tomatoes . Avoid with iron: dairy (blocks absorption), antacids. Iron is taken with vitamin C on an empty stomach for best absorption (your notes). Not with milk. Oral iron side effects: nausea, abdominal pain, constipation, diarrhea, dark stools . IM iron = Z-track (stains skin). IV iron: monitor vital signs. Diet question on the test: the menu that shows teaching worked is roast beef, steamed spinach, tomato soup, and orange juice (heme iron + nonheme iron + vitamin C). Greatest IDA risk: a 15-year-old pregnant female (growth + pregnancy). Pregnancy complications: low birth weight, preterm labor. Polycythemia vera: the chain Too many cells (blood hyperviscous) → slow flow (heart labors; less O₂ to tissues) → clots (stasis → thrombosis: stroke, MI, DVT, PE) → bleeding (platelets used up/abnormal → nose, gum, GI bleeds). Lecture interventions: aggressive IV hydration (oral fluids if tolerated) · phlebotomy and monitor Hct/Hgb · oxygen for SOB · reduce stress, provide rest, elevate head · avoid tight clothing, extreme temps · small frequent meals · modify CV risk (stroke prevention) · regular blood tests and genetic testing. Prioritize with ABCs: airway/breathing first, then hydration and oxygen based on assessment. Assess: sudden severe headache = stroke. Watch vitals during phlebotomy. Notebook: high risk for stroke and DVT. Lecture: clots, aneurysm rupture, hemorrhage; engorged capillaries. Hydrate! Rapid / aggressive IV hydration to dilute the blood (starred). Secondary polycythemia = from hypoxia (smoking, COPD, altitude) → ↑EPO. Connection check: smoking raises the risk. Slide epidemiology: 5 out of 1 million persons diagnosed annually. Thrombocytopenia: extras Affects 3.8 million patients in the US. #1 side effect = bleeding. Most common medication cause = Bactrim (sulfa). Higher risk for women (F:M 2:1). Risk factors: malignancy (chemo), infection, medications (sulfa/Bactrim), autoimmune (SLE), DIC, African American descent, obese. Diagnosis: CBC with manual differential (low platelets), coagulation studies, bone marrow biopsy. Treatment: treat underlying cause · replace clotting factors · discontinue heparin (HIT) · transfuse platelets. ITP: antibodies destroy platelets; kids after virus/immunization (acute); women 15–40 (chronic). Observe if >100k; steroids, IVIG, splenectomy. Platelet thresholds: <100,000 start bleeding precautions · <50,000 precautions especially important; avoid intercourse; minimize invasive procedures (lecture) · <20,000 spontaneous bleeding; platelets usually transfused. Bleeding precautions, split: The nurse does: avoid IM injections · no rectal temps, suppositories, enemas · minimal BP cuff inflation (no automatic cuffs) · minimize blood draws/sticks; pressure after every stick · safe, clutter-free environment · transfuse platelets/factors as ordered. Teach the patient: soft toothbrush, no flossing · electric razor · shoes when up · daily stool softener, no straining · don't blow nose · no intercourse if platelets <50k · direct pressure ≥5 min for cuts · report bleeding signs · frequent labs needed. Gentle oral care and bowel care to avoid mucosal trauma. Leukemia: types and medications ★ (most-tested topic: 5 Qs; notebook says 10) Type Onset Key facts AML Acute Myeloid. Linked to radiation, benzene (oil refinery) , prior chemo, smoking ALL Acute Lymphoid. Most common childhood leukemia CML Chronic → acute Philadelphia chromosome (BCR/ABL) in >90% CLL Chronic → acute Lymphoid; can be present for years Process: 1 Mutation (myeloid or lymphoid stem cell) → 2 Clones (immature \"blasts\" never mature) → 3 No apoptosis (blasts don't die → leukocytosis) → 4 Crowding (marrow packed → ↓RBCs, ↓platelets, ↓working WBCs). The WBC count may be high, but the cells are immature and useless → infections + anemia + bleeding. Early on WBC may look normal. All types are fatal if untreated. Chronic can degenerate into an acute phase. Treatment Key points Chemotherapy (primary) Phases: induction (get remission) → consolidation (kill hidden disease) → maintenance (prevent relapse). IV, intrathecal, oral. AML example: daunorubicin + cytarabine (Ara-C) Targeted therapy Imatinib (Bcr-Abl → CML); ibrutinib (CLL) Immunotherapy Monoclonal antibodies; CAR-T (patient's T cells engineered to kill leukemia) Prophylactic antimicrobials Antibiotics (levofloxacin/moxifloxacin), antivirals (acyclovir), antifungals (fluconazole/Diflucan) Growth factor Filgrastim (G-CSF) ↑ neutrophils; bone pain side effect. Textbook: not generally indicated in leukemia (could fuel it), but the case patient got it Blood products PRBCs, platelets (supportive care) Tumor lysis prevention IV fluids, allopurinol (prevent), rasburicase Bone marrow / stem cell transplant Refractory or relapsed disease. Risk: graft-versus-host disease Remission = no detectable leukemia cells in marrow (not a cure). Lecture: remission possible, but recurrence risk when immune suppression occurs. Clinical manifestations explained: swollen lymph glands/LUQ pain = blasts congest nodes and spleen · increased infections, low-grade fever = few mature neutrophils · anemia = RBCs crowded out · bleeding = thrombocytopenia · weight loss, bone pain = high metabolism and swollen marrow ↑ pressure in bone. Diagnosis includes CBC, peripheral smear , H&P, bone marrow biopsy, genetic testing, flow cytometry. Complications: infection (top cause of death outside disease progression), hemorrhage, myelosuppression. Neutropenic precautions (notebook ★): no humidifiers · no plants · reverse isolation (YOU wear PPE) to protect the patient. Staff/visitors wear PPE when required; restrict visitors with communicable diseases. Connection check: which result raises infection risk? B. ANC 400/mm³. Lymphoma: extras Hodgkin Non-Hodgkin Hallmark Reed–Sternberg cells Dozens of B- and T-cell subtypes Frequency 14% of lymphomas; better survival 4% of all cancers and cancer deaths Ages Childhood–young adult, then after 55 Preadolescence, late adulthood Risk Epstein–Barr virus; first-degree relative Pesticides, autoimmune, transplant immunosuppression, HIV, EBV, H. pylori Lymphoma complications: SVC syndrome (edema of face and arms, SOB, chest pain, dysphagia; lecture: tachycardia, stress) · spinal cord compression (neuro deficits, paralysis → immediate radiation/chemo) · hypercalcemia (Ca >11) · myelodysplasia (anemia, neutropenia, thrombocytopenia) · hepatic/renal dysfunction, hyperviscosity, DVT. Exam focus: Hodgkin complications and symptoms. Multiple myeloma: extras ★ (exam: nursing interventions) Hypercalcemia signs: tingling and burning sensation (your notes) · confusion, lethargy · muscle weakness · polyuria, dehydration · constipation, nausea · bradycardia/heart block · kidney stones. Risk: oil refinery (petroleum), average age 62, men, 2× in people of African descent. Also dark, infrequent urine. Bisphosphonates slow bone breakdown. Notes: \"Boniva\" = ibandronate ; also pamidronate, zoledronate. Teach: report fatigue, SOB, dizziness, confusion · sudden severe pain (fracture) · new numbness/tingling (cord compression) · use nonpharm pain management ( guided imagery ) · energy conservation. Lecture: more common in men; nursing = pain management (pharm + nonpharm), high fluid intake to prevent stones and clear calcium, positioning, safe ambulation and weight bearing, monitor Ca/BUN/creatinine. Heme disorders at a glance (lecture summary) Disorder Key tests / diagnosis Primary nursing actions Iron-deficiency anemia CBC (↓Hgb, ↓MCV), ↓ferritin, ↑TIBC Iron + vitamin C diet, supplements, find bleeding Polycythemia vera CBC (↑RBC), bone marrow biopsy, genetic testing Hydration, phlebotomy, oxygen, elevate head, avoid tight clothing Thrombocytopenia CBC (↓platelets), coag studies, bone marrow biopsy Bleeding precautions, no IM injections, minimize draws, platelet transfusion Leukemia CBC, bone marrow biopsy, genetic testing Chemo, neutropenic precautions, infection monitoring Lymphoma Imaging, lymph node biopsy, bone marrow biopsy Symptom management, chemo/radiation, watch cord compression/SVC, emotional support Multiple myeloma CBC, serum/urine proteins, bone marrow biopsy, skeletal survey Pain control, bisphosphonates, fluids, monitor Ca/BUN/creatinine Lecture key terms: hyperviscosity = thick blood from ↑ cells · splenomegaly = enlarged spleen · myelosuppression = marrow suppression → cytopenias · neutropenic precautions = protect immunocompromised from infection · bleeding precautions = minimize bleeding with thrombocytopenia · hypercalcemia = high serum Ca from bone breakdown."},{"ch":"heme-disorders","chTitle":"Hematologic Disorders","id":"heme-disorders--chapter-34-powerpoint-word-for-word","title":"Chapter 34 PowerPoint, word for word","text":"Your instructor's slides for this chapter, kept in slide order. Slide Slide 1 · The 4 components & normal values Hematology – 4 components: red blood cells · white blood cells · platelets · plasma Slide Slide 2 · Epidemiology to complications Epidemiology: most prevalent nutritional deficiency in the world Risk factors: inadequate iron in the diet · hemorrhage · chronic blood loss · GI problems · socioeconomic – lead paint Pathophysiology: iron deficiency · insufficient hemoglobin to carry adequate oxygen Clinical manifestations: hypoxia · fatigue · pallor · tachycardia · tachypnea Figure (continued_4): glossitis (red, swollen, smooth tongue) and koilonychia (spoon-shaped nails) Diagnosis: CBC – Hgb & Hct · serum iron tests – ferritin (body's iron stores) < 100 indicates IDA; TIBC; MCV Treatment: increased iron intake – red meat, green leafy vegetables, vitamin C, oranges & grapefruits · iron supplements Complications: heart failure · renal failure · impaired thermoregulation · impaired immune function · psychomotor and cognitive impairment Slide Slide 3 · Assess, act, teach, evaluate Assessment and analysis: signs/symptoms related to decreased oxygenation Nursing diagnoses: inadequate tissue perfusion · fatigue · activity intolerance Assessments: vital signs · serum hemoglobin · fatigue · level of consciousness · blood loss Actions: increase dietary iron · increase intake of vitamin C · minimize blood loss Teaching: dietary sources of iron · signs/symptoms of bleeding · daily iron supplements · dangers of lead exposure · prenatal teaching Evaluating outcomes: adequate iron levels · adequate production of hemoglobin · improved tissue perfusion Slide Slide 4 · Too many cells, thick blood Epidemiology: 5 out of 1 million persons diagnosed annually Pathophysiology: increased production of RBCs · hyperviscosity – thick blood Clinical manifestations: shortness of breath · difficulty breathing when lying flat · headache · dizziness · weakness · splenomegaly · blurred vision Diagnosis: laboratory tests · bone marrow biopsy · genetic testing Treatment: reducing hyperviscosity · preventing hemorrhage Complications: thrombosis · hemorrhage Slide Slide 5 · Assess, act, teach, evaluate Assessment: signs and symptoms related to hyperviscosity, hypervolemia, engorgement of capillaries Nursing diagnoses: ineffective tissue perfusion · activity intolerance · risk for ineffective coping Assessments: shortness of breath · headache · dizziness · generalized pruritus · flushing of the face · fullness in left upper abdomen · fatigue · vision problems · weight loss · lab tests · bone marrow biopsy · vital signs Actions: obtain routine CBCs · increase fluid intake · ensure adequate rest · elevate lower extremities when sitting · modify cardiovascular risk factors Teaching: manifestations of severe thromboembolic events · rationale for aggressive hydration · small, frequent meals · avoid tight clothing · avoid extreme temperatures · bleeding precautions · routine moderate exercise · regular lab tests · genetic testing Outcomes: prevented or delayed complications · prevention of major thromboembolic events · prevention of hemorrhage Slide Slide 6 · ITP, hemophilia, DIC & HIT Epidemiology: 3.8 million patients in the US · Acute – presents rapidly and resolves within a few weeks to 6 months · Chronic – more insidious onset and rarely results in remission Risk factors: malignancy · infection · medications – sulfa · autoimmune conditions – SLE · DIC · African American descent, obese · female-to-male ratio 2:1 Pathophysiology: idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura – following viral infection or immunization in children · hemophilia – deficiency of factor VIII or IX, genetic · heparin-induced thrombocytopenia – immune response to heparin Clinical manifestations: decreased platelets – easy bruising, petechiae, bleeding Diagnosis: CBC with manual differential · coagulation studies · bone marrow biopsy Treatment: resolution of underlying condition · replacement of clotting factors · discontinue heparin Complications: hemorrhage Slide Slide 7 · Bleeding precautions Assessment: signs of low platelets – ecchymosis, petechiae, nosebleeds, black tarry stools, bleeding gums, hematuria Nursing diagnoses: risk of injury · risk for ineffective therapeutic regimen · fear Assessments: platelet count · ecchymosis · petechiae/purpura · frank bleeding Actions: implement bleeding precautions · minimize blood loss · avoid IM injections · avoid rectal temperatures · provide safe environment · minimal BP cuff inflation · minimize blood draws Teaching: bleeding precautions · avoid sexual intercourse when platelets < 50,000 · necessity of frequent lab tests Outcomes: prevention of hemorrhage · minimize risk of bleeding Slide Slide 8 · Uncontrolled immature white cells Epidemiology: acute or chronic · chronic may degenerate into acute phase Risk factors: genetic anomalies · Down syndrome Pathophysiology: unknown stimulus mutates myeloid or lymphoid stem cell · uncontrolled proliferation of leukemic cells (leukocytosis) · apoptosis doesn't occur · bone marrow, spleen, lymph congested with blasts Clinical manifestations: swollen lymph glands · increased infections · anemia · bleeding Diagnosis: lab tests · history and physical · bone marrow biopsy · genetic testing Treatment: chemotherapy · bone marrow transplant Complications: infection · hemorrhage · myelosuppression – result of chemo or radiation · bleeding Slide Slide 9 · Neutropenic precautions Assessment: disease may present as anemia or thrombocytopenia Nursing diagnoses: risk for infection · risk for impaired tissue perfusion · risk for bleeding · risk for compromised family coping Assessments: vital signs – low-grade fever response to infection · fatigue, pallor, dizziness, SOB · excessive bruising, petechiae · labs – CBC, WBC Actions: administer chemo as prescribed · institute neutropenic precautions · prophylactic antibiotics, antivirals, antifungals · symptom management Teaching: neutropenic precautions · manifestations of anemia · manifestations of thrombocytopenia · bleeding precautions · diagnosis of leukemia · adverse reactions of chemo/radiation · possibility of sterility Outcomes: remission · minimize/prevent complications · decreased manifestations of anemia, infection, bleeding Slide Slide 10 · Hodgkin's & non-Hodgkin's Epidemiology: Hodgkin's – 14% of lymphomas · Non-Hodgkin's – 4% of total cancer incidence, 4% of cancer- related deaths Pathophysiology: single progenitor in lymph node develops into solid tumor · spreads to other lymph tissue and spleen · involves other major organs Clinical manifestations: painless swelling of lymph nodes · low-grade fevers · drenching night sweats · unexplained weight loss Diagnosis: H&P · lab testing · radiological testing · lymph node biopsy · bone marrow biopsy · lumbar puncture Treatment: chemotherapy · radiation · surgical removal of tumor · stem cell transplant Surgical management: surgical removal of solid tumor · splenectomy Complications: SVC syndrome · spinal cord compression · hypercalcemia · myelodysplasia · hepatic or renal dysfunction · hyperviscosity · venous thrombotic events Slide Slide 11 · Assess, act, teach, evaluate Assessment: manifestations of systemic B lymphocyte involvement · painless swelling of cervical, axillary, inguinal nodes · low-grade fevers of unknown origin · drenching night sweats · fatigue, anemia, neutropenia, thrombocytopenia Nursing diagnoses: risk for infection · activity intolerance · risk for ineffective coping Assessments: vital signs · pain · neurological status · enlarged lymph nodes · skin · lab tests · lower extremities · activity tolerance · spinal cord & nerve root compression Actions: administer blood products · medications · IV fluids · symptom management · obtain labs · emotional support · primary treatment – chemotherapy Teaching: disease process · risk of infection · maintaining treatment schedules · frequent rest periods · dietary intake · manifestations of complications · potential for sterility Outcomes: optimized quality of life · minimized adverse reactions · no bleeding · no infections Slide Slide 12 · Plasma cell cancer Epidemiology: 13% of hematologic cancers · 5–7 cases per 100,000 per year Risk factors: exposure to herbicides, insecticides, petroleum, heavy metals, plastics, radiation · comorbid plasma cell disease – MGUS, solitary plasmacytoma Pathophysiology: asymptomatic premalignant proliferation of monoclonal plasma cells – less than 10% of bone marrow is made up of abnormal plasma cells Clinical manifestations: fatigue · weakness · bone pain · recurrent infections · weight loss · paresthesia Diagnosis: lab tests · bone marrow aspiration · skeletal survey · bone-density measurement Treatment: bisphosphonates · radiation therapy · DVT prophylaxis · dexamethasone · autologous stem cell transplant · chemotherapy Complications: chemo side effects · anemia · bone marrow failure · bleeding · infection · pathologic fractures · spinal cord compression · hypercalcemia Slide Slide 13 · Assess, act, teach, evaluate Assessment: multisystem effects · bone pain – back · numbness and tingling · anemia · weakness, pallor, fatigue · increased susceptibility to infection Nursing diagnoses: acute/chronic pain · impaired physical mobility · risk for infection · risk for injury · impaired urinary output · fear Assessments: vital signs · fatigue · pain · paresthesias · intake and output · serum and urine calcium · serum BUN Actions: chemo as ordered · bisphosphonate therapy · pain meds · encourage fluid intake as tolerated · position for comfort · encourage ambulation as tolerated Teaching: report fatigue, SOB, dizziness, confusion · report sudden severe pain · report changes in sensation · nonpharmacological pain management Outcomes: pain is controlled · optimal renal function"},{"ch":"immune","chTitle":"Immune Function and Disorders","id":"immune--immune-function-and-disorders","title":"Immune Function and Disorders","text":"Immune disorders come from either too little immunity (deficiency, which leads to infections) or too much (hypersensitivity, anaphylaxis, autoimmunity)."},{"ch":"immune","chTitle":"Immune Function and Disorders","id":"immune--ch-18-immune-function","title":"Ch 18 · Immune function","text":"3 lines of defense 1st, innate: physical, mechanical, and chemical barriers (skin, mucosa, coughing, tears, saliva). Nonspecific. 2nd, innate: inflammation. Histamine causes vasodilation and ↑capillary permeability, so WBCs move in (chemotaxis) and phagocytize. Immediate and nonspecific, with no memory. 3rd, adaptive: delayed but specific , and it has memory . Humoral (B cells): B cells become plasma cells that make antibodies against extracellular bacteria and viruses. Memory cells remember. Cell-mediated (T cells): cell-to-cell contact against intracellular organisms (viruses, fungi), tumors, and transplants. Helper ( CD4 ), cytotoxic ( CD8 ), and regulatory T cells. Active immunity comes from infection or a vaccine. Passive immunity is preformed antibodies (placenta, IVIG). Class: Agglutination traps antigens and makes them like a paste. Opsonization coats antigens (marks them for phagocytes). Organs and immunoglobulins Central lymphoid organs = thymus and bone marrow. B cells mature in the marrow. T cells mature in the thymus, which shrinks with age. Peripheral organs: spleen, lymph nodes, tonsils, adenoids, Peyer's patches. After splenectomy, high-risk patients may need lifelong antibiotics. Ig Remember it as IgG Most abundant. Crosses the placenta IgM Largest. First antibody made in a primary response IgA In secretions (breast milk, saliva, tears, mucus) IgE Least concentrated. Drives allergy and fights parasites IgD On developing B cells (activation) Assessment (Ch 18) History: infections (how often, how severe, response to treatment), immunizations, TB test, STIs, surgeries, meds (antibiotics, steroids, immunosuppressants, antineoplastics), nutrition (protein deficiency weakens immunity). Risk factors: liver, renal, and pulmonary disease, malnutrition, radiation, splenectomy, surgery, trauma. Lymph nodes: normally not easily felt. Enlarged, tender, or fixed nodes are a concern. Cancerous nodes are usually LESS tender than infected ones. Palpate for hepatosplenomegaly. Check joints for swelling and warmth. Labs: CBC with differential, CRP and ESR, HIV test, cultures, skin tests, bone marrow biopsy, chest x-ray. Aging (immunosenescence) ↓T-cell function and antibody response, plus ↑autoantibodies, lead to more infection (pneumonia and flu are top-10 causes of death after 65; shingles), more cancer, and more autoimmune disease. Infection may present as confusion. Malnutrition makes it worse."},{"ch":"immune","chTitle":"Immune Function and Disorders","id":"immune--ch-19-immune-deficiencies","title":"Ch 19 · Immune deficiencies","text":"Primary = born with it (genetic or congenital), usually found in childhood. Secondary = acquired from an outside cause: HIV, chemo (leukopenia), corticosteroids, radiation (pancytopenia), malnutrition, burns, removal of the spleen or thymus. Secondary is the most common type in adults. Immune deficiency is the opposite of autoimmune disorder. Warning signs of primary immune deficiency ≥8 new infections a year · ≥2 serious sinus infections a year · ≥2 pneumonias a year · ≥2 months on antibiotics with little effect · an infant not gaining weight · recurrent deep abscesses · persistent thrush after age 1 · needing IV antibiotics · ≥2 deep-seated infections · family history. Disorder Patho Manifestations Treatment / ⚠ X-linked agammaglobulinemia (XLA) · B cell X-linked recessive, so it shows in males and females are carriers. BTK gene mutation means B cells can't mature, so no antibodies are made. About 1 in 250,000 male births Starts at 6–12 months (when maternal antibodies wear off). Recurrent BACTERIAL infections: ears (most common before diagnosis), lungs, sinuses, skin, conjunctiva, meningitis, sepsis (S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae). Handles short viral infections, but chronic viruses are dangerous IVIG every 3–4 weeks (or weekly subcut). Prophylactic antibiotics, courses twice as long as usual. ⚠ NO LIVE VACCINES (5–10% got vaccine-associated polio), so give inactivated polio to the patient and siblings. Older adults on IVIG risk renal failure, fluid overload, clots, hemolysis. Safe food handling and handwashing CVID · B cell B cells normal in number but don't become plasma cells, so antibodies are low Like XLA but milder. Diagnosed in adulthood (class: age 15–35 ), both sexes. Autoimmune disease, GI symptoms, granulomas, cancers Same as XLA Selective IgA deficiency Most common Ig deficiency. Low IgA with other Igs normal Often asymptomatic (class: age 14). Recurrent sinus and lung infections. 20–30% have autoimmune disease Treat infections DiGeorge syndrome · T cell Chromosome 22 deletion (\"CATCH-22\"), autosomal dominant. Pharyngeal pouches don't develop in weeks 6–10, so the thymus is small or absent (no T cells) and the parathyroid is affected CATCH: C ardiac (tetralogy of Fallot), A bnormal face, T hymic aplasia, C left palate, H ypocalcemia (hypoparathyroidism, which causes tetany and twitching). Opportunistic infections: Candida, viral, fungal. Feeding problems, developmental delays Calcium supplements (prevent tetany, seizures, bronchospasm). IVIG (lasts 3–4 weeks), prophylactic antibiotics, aggressive infection treatment. Thymus transplant (ideally by 3–6 months). Bone marrow transplant. ⚠ GVHD from transfused T cells. Monitor calcium and WBC. Class: mother needs genetic testing while pregnant; FISH testing detects the chromosome 22 deletion; puree food (feeding problems, cleft palate) Hyper-IgM · T cell X-linked. T cells can't signal B cells to switch, so only IgM is made (low IgG and IgA) Infections in the first 2 years. Pneumocystis jiroveci Autoimmune blood disorders SCID · both T and B No T- or B-cell function at all (the \"boy in the bubble\") Potentially fatal infections Stem cell transplant Wiskott–Aldrich · both X-linked recessive Infections, eczema, thrombocytopenia (small platelets) Stem cell transplant Nursing: any immunodeficiency Assess: fever, behavior change (irritable, lethargic), appetite, RR and work of breathing, SpO₂, lung sounds, ears, eyes, skin and wounds. Findings may be subtle and nonspecific. Actions: protective isolation, limit visitors, assign a nurse who is not caring for infectious patients, gamma globulin, prophylactic antibiotics, treat infection promptly. Secondary (therapy-induced): consider changing or stopping the immunosuppressant if the infection risk outweighs the benefit. Signs of infection: fever, hypotension and tachycardia, cloudy or foul urine, CVA tenderness, rash. Teaching: protect from infection Hand washing. Avoid crowds and anyone who is ill. Low-bacteria diet: no salads, raw fruit or vegetables, undercooked meat, fish, or eggs, or sushi. Safe food handling. Avoid untested well water. No changing cat litter (toxoplasmosis). No turtles or reptiles (Salmonella). Report temperature >100°F (37.8°C) (Ch 19 secondary) or ≥100.5°F (38°C) (XLA), cough or URI symptoms, cloudy urine, or a wound that is slow to heal or warm. Finish the full course of antibiotics or antivirals."},{"ch":"immune","chTitle":"Immune Function and Disorders","id":"immune--hypersensitivity-reactions-overactive-immune-response","title":"Hypersensitivity reactions (overactive immune response) ★","text":"Type Mechanism Antibody / cell Examples I · Immediate (allergic) IgE on mast cells binds the allergen and the mast cell degranulates, releasing histamine, leukotrienes, and prostaglandins IgE, mast cells Allergic rhinitis (most common) , asthma, anaphylaxis (most severe) II · Cytotoxic IgG or IgM attack antigens on the cell surface, causing complement lysis IgG, IgM Transfusion reaction , Rh disease of the newborn (erythroblastosis fetalis), Goodpasture's , autoimmune ITP, myasthenia gravis III · Immune complex Antigen–antibody complexes lodge in vessel walls, joints, and kidneys, triggering complement and inflammation IgG, IgM, neutrophils SLE, RA , serum sickness (7–12 days after exposure), Arthus reaction (local, at an injection site) IV · Delayed (cell-mediated) Sensitized T cells release cytokines. Takes 24–72 hours. No antibody T cells, macrophages Poison ivy contact dermatitis, positive TB skin test (Mantoux) , latex allergy (can also be type I) V · Stimulated Autoantibody keeps a receptor switched ON Autoantibodies Graves' disease (TSH receptor leads to hyperthyroidism) Slide 49: type I hypersensitivity. First exposure: B cell → plasma cell releases IgE → IgE sits on mast cells. Second exposure: allergen binds → mast cell releases histamine → allergic reaction Type I patho (slide 49, learn it): first exposure: B cell → plasma cell releases IgE → IgE sits on mast cells. Second exposure: allergen binds → mast cell releases histamine → allergic reaction. IgE fights allergies."},{"ch":"immune","chTitle":"Immune Function and Disorders","id":"immune--anaphylaxis-type-i-emergency","title":"Anaphylaxis (Type I) · EMERGENCY","text":"Why it matters Lifetime prevalence 1–2%, and up to 1,500 people die of it each year. Penicillin causes about 400 deaths a year. Other triggers: foods (peanuts, tree nuts, seafood), insect stings, contrast media, latex, blood. 20% of people are atopic. If both parents have allergies, a child has an 80% chance of allergies. Patho: the first exposure makes IgE, which attaches to mast cells. The second exposure causes degranulation. Histamine causes vasodilation, ↑permeability, bronchoconstriction, and edema. Leukotrienes cause bronchoconstriction (wheezing). Clinical manifestations ★ Skin first: urticaria (hives), itching, rash, flushing or heat. Angioedema of the lips, mouth, and eyes. Skin signs occur in 90%. Dyspnea, wheezing, hoarseness, STRIDOR (airway narrowing), crackles N/V/D, abdominal cramps Anxiety, a sense of impending doom , dizziness Anaphylactic shock: HYPOTENSION + TACHYCARDIA from vasodilation and capillary leak (distributive shock). Circulatory failure means it is progressing. Local (allergic rhinitis): sneezing, nasal discharge, itchy watery eyes, ↑eosinophils. Diagnosis by skin scratch test: hold steroids, antihistamines (and NSAIDs per class) for 5 days beforehand. Results appear in 15–20 minutes. Priority actions, in order STOP the offending agent. If it's an IV drug: stop the infusion, change the IV tubing , and hang normal saline. IM EPINEPHRINE STAT , the first-line drug. It dilates the bronchioles, constricts blood vessels (↑BP), and strengthens cardiac contraction. Repeat every 5 minutes, and go to IV if IM isn't working. Glucagon if the patient is on beta blockers and not responding to epinephrine. 100% O₂ by non-rebreather. Raise the head of the bed, unless BP is low. Second line, only AFTER epinephrine: diphenhydramine (H1), famotidine (H2), corticosteroids, albuterol, vasopressors, IV fluid bolus. Have intubation or tracheostomy equipment ready. Monitor continuously and stay with the patient. Watch for a rebound reaction as the drugs wear off. Diphenhydramine is sedating (first generation). Second-generation antihistamines (cetirizine, loratadine, fexofenadine) are for daily use. Steroids: watch for infection risk, ↑glucose, ↑appetite, irritability, impaired skin integrity. Priority assessment: respiratory status. Nursing diagnosis: risk for ineffective gas exchange. DO NOT COMPROMISE BP (class): semi-Fowler's if having difficulty breathing; legs up if no breathing issues; do not move people with a hip fracture. Teaching Avoid triggers. Know the early signs (rash, itching). EpiPen use is the teaching priority after a bee-sting anaphylaxis. Medic Alert bracelet. Keep the allergy list updated. Transfusion reaction (Type II) ★ Type Has Can't receive A A antigen, anti-B B, AB B B antigen, anti-A A, AB AB Both antigens, no antibodies Universal recipient O No antigens, anti-A and anti-B A, B, AB ( universal donor ) Incompatible blood causes chills, fever, pain and heat at the site, rash. Agglutination leads to hemoglobinuria, acute renal failure, and then DIC-like bleeding. Treatment: stop the transfusion. Plasmapheresis for type II reactions. Saline is the only compatible solution with blood (class). Latex allergy (Type IV, can be Type I) About 2% of people. High risk: healthcare workers, multiple surgeries (spina bifida), atopic or hand dermatitis, females. Ranges from contact dermatitis on the hands, rhinitis, and conjunctivitis to pharyngeal edema and anaphylaxis (worse with repeat exposure). ⚠ Cross-reacting foods: avocado, banana, kiwi, chestnut, mango, papaya, passion fruit, tomato, raw potato, peach. Prevention is the best treatment: vinyl or neoprene gloves, cloth under the BP cuff, keep stethoscope tubing off the skin, don't inject through latex ports. Latex items: tourniquets, airways, ET tubes, electrode pads, rubber bands, rubber balls, baby bottle nipples. Medic Alert bracelet. Carry an EpiPen. Other type II and III disorders Goodpasture's (type II): antibodies attack the glomerular and alveolar basement membranes, causing glomerulonephritis plus lung hemorrhage (attacks lungs and kidneys). Treated with steroids, immunosuppressants, and plasmapheresis (± IVIG, dialysis). Serum sickness (type III): fever, arthralgia, lymphadenopathy, urticaria, nephritis 7–12 days after exposure (1–4 days on re-exposure). Treated by removing the agent, aspirin, antihistamines, ± steroids or epinephrine."},{"ch":"immune","chTitle":"Immune Function and Disorders","id":"immune--ch-20-connective-tissue-disorders","title":"Ch 20 · Connective tissue disorders","text":"Connective tissue binds the body together (bones, cartilage, tendons, ligaments, blood, lymph). Your notes said to concentrate on Chapters 19 and 20 . Osteoarthritis (OA) · degenerative, \"wear and tear\" Most common form of arthritis (DJD); leading cause of chronic disability. Hits the weight-bearing joints: knees, hips, feet, cervical and lumbar spine, DIP and PIP joints of the hands. Knees bear most of the body's weight. Patho: articular cartilage and synovial fluid break down → bone is unprotected → osteophytes (bone spurs) → narrowed joint space, pain, ↓ROM. Risk: female, over 55, obesity, prior injury, repetitive-motion jobs and sports. Manifestations: pain that worsens with activity and improves with rest . Morning stiffness lasting less than 30 minutes. ↓ROM, bony swelling, deformity, crepitus. Heberden's vs Bouchard's nodes: \"B (more proximal) before H.\" Bouchard's = PIP (middle knuckle); Heberden's = DIP (closest to the fingertip). Diagnosis: history (45+, pain, stiffness under 30 min); x-ray shows joint-space narrowing and spurs. Meds: acetaminophen (mild to moderate), NSAIDs, intra-articular steroid injections, opioids with caution. 3 g of Tylenol max a day → check LFTs and kidney function (NSAIDs stress the kidneys). Heat 15 min on and off; cold 20 min on and off. DO NOT put directly on the skin. Weight loss, exercise, PT/OT, assistive devices. Surgery: debridement, synovectomy, fusion, total joint replacement. 90° hip angle after surgery (don't flex the new hip past 90°). OA of the hand: Bouchard's node at the PIP, Heberden's node at the DIP Rheumatoid arthritis (RA) · autoimmune, systemic Chronic, systemic, autoimmune inflammatory disease. 1% of people; women > men; ages 30–50s. Risks: genetics, smoking. Joint pain is symmetrical; hands are mainly affected. Synovitis, swelling, erythema, fatigue, morning stiffness over 30 minutes , irreversible deformity (swan-neck, boutonnière, ulnar deviation). Dx: RF and anti-CCP2 tests; CRP and ESR show inflammation; renal, hepatic, CBC. Check PPD (TB test before immunosuppressants). Proper nutrition is controversial; class: avoid night(shade) plants d/t water content. METHOTREXATE and DMARDs: methotrexate is the first-line DMARD (others: leflunomide, hydroxychloroquine, sulfasalazine, Xeljanz). Also NSAIDs and glucocorticoids. Immunocompromised d/t steroids → report infection. Stay up to date with vaccines. Rest periods, ROM, aerobic exercise, PT/OT. Surgery: joint replacement, fusion. Methotrexate teaching (case study): weekly dose, no alcohol, two forms of birth control (teratogenic) , watch liver enzymes. RA deformities: swan-neck, boutonnière, ulnar deviation Lupus (SLE) · autoimmune, flares and remission Autoimmune, chronic inflammatory disease: immune complexes deposit in tissues (type III) . Most common in African American women of childbearing age . Triggers: pregnancy, sunlight, illness, surgery, silica dust, drug allergies. No set pattern; flares and remissions. Fatigue, joint pain, rash, photosensitivity, oral and nasal ulcers, alopecia. Dx: ANA (anti-dsDNA, anti-Sm). Treatment: hydroxychloroquine (antimalarial), NSAIDs, glucocorticoids, belimumab. Sunscreen daily (SPF 50+) , rest, energy conservation. No live vaccines ; keep other immunizations current. Avoid oral contraceptives (clot risk). Complications: renal failure, heart and lung disease, clots, stroke, infection. Quick reference (Ch 20 slides, not in your notes) Disorder Key patho Signature signs Treatment / teaching Scleroderma Excess collagen → hardened, fibrotic skin and organs Thick skin, Raynaud's, renal crisis (sudden HTN), pulmonary HTN; lung disease is the leading cause of death Steroids, immunosuppressants, vasodilators, skin care, ROM Gout Uric acid (urate) crystals in joints Sudden red, hot, swollen single joint (great toe); tophi in chronic gout; kidney stones Colchicine, indomethacin, steroids; allopurinol for chronic; avoid alcohol (beer), high-purine foods, aspirin Fibromyalgia CNS amplifies normal pain signals Widespread pain, 11+ of 18 tender points, non-restorative sleep, fatigue, \"fibro fog\" Symptom management; labs only rule out other causes"},{"ch":"immune","chTitle":"Immune Function and Disorders","id":"immune--ch-22-hiv-aids","title":"Ch 22 · HIV / AIDS","text":"A retrovirus that carries genetic RNA and targets CD4 T cells . PLWHA = person living with HIV/AIDS. Transmission: through fluids: blood and blood products, semen, vaginal secretions, mother to child (amniotic fluid, breast milk). Not casual contact. Risk factors: unprotected sex, IV drug use. Prevention: testing, PrEP, condoms every time, male circumcision, harm reduction (don't share needles). Healthcare worker exposure: standard precautions; post-exposure prophylaxis within 72 hours (2–3 drugs for 28 days). Stages of HIV ★ Acute infection (1–2 weeks) → Seroconversion (antibodies appear) → Asymptomatic (3–10 years) → Symptomatic (AIDS) → Death (about 2 years). CDC staging by CD4: Stage 1 = 500+ · Stage 2 = 200–499 · Stage 3 = less than 200 is AIDS. Labs: what you want Targets CD4: want CD4 HIGH, viral load DOWN. CD4 less than 200 = AIDS. Prophylaxis for opportunistic infections starts at a CD4 of 200. Diagnostics: HIV antibodies, CD4+ count, viral load. Slide 21: opportunistic infections by CD4 count Opportunistic infections by CD4 count (slide 21) Herpes zoster and TB as CD4 starts to fall · oral candidiasis near 300 · PCP at 200 · toxoplasmosis, cryptococcosis, MAC, CMV near 100 · cryptosporidiosis, PML below 50. Treatment and nursing Antiretroviral therapy (ART) , Table 22.3 classes. \" -vir \" = antiviral. Give ART on time; adherence is everything. Tie doses to a daily routine. Immunizations. With adherence, people with HIV can live a normal life span. Dx: risk for infection · risk for fluid volume deficit · anxiety · knowledge deficit. Assess: vital signs and SpO₂, weight trends, CD4, viral load, ART adherence, TB status. Actions: universal/standard precautions, ART on time, nutrient-dense small frequent meals, emotional support. Teach: avoid high-risk behaviors, condoms with every encounter, infection control at home, adherence, what to report. Complications: PCP, Kaposi sarcoma, candida esophagitis, toxoplasmosis, CMV, lymphoma, herpes zoster, IRIS, encephalopathy. Report right away (slides) New cough, increased fatigue, night sweats · fever below 97°F (36°C) or above 102°F (39°C) · new headache, new blurred vision, mental status change · new skin lesions, new diarrhea, weight loss over 10%."},{"ch":"immune","chTitle":"Immune Function and Disorders","id":"immune--bioterrorism","title":"Bioterrorism","text":"The intentional release of bacteria, viruses, or toxins. An attack is often first noticed as a pattern of unusual illness. Category What it means Agents A · highest priority Easily spread person to person, high death rate, can cause panic, needs special preparedness Anthrax · botulism · plague · smallpox (variola) · tularemia · viral hemorrhagic fevers (Ebola, Marburg, Lassa) B · second priority Moderately easy to spread, moderate illness, low death rate Staph (enterotoxin B) · viral encephalitis · E. coli (food safety threats with Salmonella, Shigella) · glanders (from horses) · psittacosis (from birds) · brucellosis · Q fever · ricin · typhus · melioidosis · water threats (cholera) C · emerging Could be engineered for mass spread in the future Viral encephalitis · novel influenza · Nipah virus · typhus · water threats. Viral encephalitis is on both the B and C lists Anthrax (Category A) Bacillus anthracis spores; contact with animal products, or mailed spores (2001). Cutaneous: painless papule → vesicle → painless black eschar on the hands, arms, face, neck; 7–14 day incubation. Inhalational: 1–7 days, then rapid respiratory failure, hemorrhagic mediastinitis, shock. GI: from undercooked infected meat. Dx: culture (blood, sputum, CSF, skin). Treatment: Cipro and doxy (ciprofloxacin, doxycycline). Smallpox (Category A) Also called variola . Eradicated in 1977, but contagious by aerosol, so it's a top threat. Lesions on the face, skin, and torso: macule → papule → vesicle → pustule. Diagnosis is clinical (PCR only at the CDC). Treatment is supportive (cidofovir shows some activity). Other agents Key points Plague Flea bites; painful swollen lymph node ( bubo ); pneumonic form → ARDS. Tx: streptomycin Tularemia Ticks, flies, animals; no person-to-person spread. Tx: gentamicin Q fever Cattle, sheep, goats; fever over 104°F. Tx: doxycycline Botulism Clostridium botulinum toxin, Category A Glanders From horses ( Burkholderia mallei ), Category B. Skin infection; droplets cause pneumonia and sepsis. Tx: imipenem Psittacosis From birds (parrots, cockatiels), Category B. \"Atypical pneumonia\": cough, fever, headache. Tx: macrolide, doxycycline, or cipro Public health response: detect early (surveillance), confirm the agent in the lab, alert ERs and labs, start prophylaxis, consider quarantine, and keep the public informed."},{"ch":"immune","chTitle":"Immune Function and Disorders","id":"immune--more-from-your-first-study-guide","title":"More from your first study guide","text":"Ch 18 · Immune function Innate (natural) Adaptive (acquired) When Present at birth Acquired after exposure Speed Immediate Delayed Specific? Nonspecific: same response to any invader Specific to one antigen Memory No Yes Lines of defense 1st (barriers) + 2nd (inflammation) 3rd (T cells = cellular, B cells = humoral) Physical barriers and mechanisms: intact skin · mucous membranes · protective lining of organs · sloughing, coughing, sneezing, vomiting, urination · chemical: acidic secretions, enzymes in tears and saliva. Acquired immunity: active (your body makes it; lasts years): natural infection or vaccine. Passive (borrowed, short): maternal IgG across placenta, IgA in breast milk, immune globulin. Phagocytosis: neutrophils, monocytes/macrophages engulf and destroy foreign bodies and toxins. Functions of the immune system: remove foreign antigens · phagocytosis · inflammatory response. Organ Key fact Thymus (central) In the chest; where T cells mature; shrinks with age Bone marrow (central) T and B cells form; B cells mature here Spleen (peripheral) LUQ; filters old/damaged RBCs; splenectomy → immunocompromised Lymph nodes, tonsils, adenoids, Peyer's patches Peripheral; filter lymph, trap inhaled germs, intestinal immune sensors Review bone anatomy and physiology (class note). Thymic hypoplasia = underdeveloped thymus (DiGeorge). Cell Job Neutrophils (granulocyte) Phagocytes of early inflammation; destroy bacteria; most numerous Basophils / mast cells Release histamine and heparin; allergic reactions Eosinophils Destroy allergens; fight parasites Monocytes → macrophages Digest microbes; present antigens B lymphocytes Become plasma cells → antibodies (humoral) T lymphocytes CD4 helper (activate B cells and others) · CD8 cytotoxic (kill infected/cancer cells) · suppressor · NK cells kill cancer cells Memory: granulocytes = \" NEB \" (Neutrophils, Eosinophils, Basophils). Other slide items: dendritic cells (macrophage-type, aid T and B cell recognition) · mast cells (skin, respiratory and GI linings; release heparin and histamine early) · cytokines (interleukins stimulate leukocyte production and maturation; interferon protects against viral and tumor growth; TNF-alpha ) · complement system (proteins that help antibodies destroy microbes; antibody binds pathogen → cascade of enzymes kills it). Immunoglobulin memory hooks ★ Ig Function Remember IgA In secretions: breast milk, sweat, saliva, mucus, tears A = All secretions IgD B-cell activation D = Develop B cells IgE Allergic responses; parasites E = Exaggerated allergy IgG Most abundant; crosses the placenta; bacteria, viruses, toxins G = Gestation IgM First antibody in a primary response; largest M = Made first Inflammation, humoral vs cellular Local (cardinal) signs: redness · heat · swelling (edema) · pain · drainage (serous or pus) ± loss of function. Systemic: fever · chills · malaise · leukocytosis (↑WBC). Acute inflammatory response: tissue damage → pathogen invasion → vasodilation (↑blood flow, chemotaxis of leukocytes) + ↑capillary permeability → invaders engulfed and phagocytized + fibrinogen clot border → cardinal signs → systemic response. Humoral Cellular Cell B cells → antibodies T cells How Circulating antibodies Direct cell-to-cell destruction Fights Extracellular bacteria, viruses Intracellular germs, tumors, transplants Agglutination = antibodies clump antigens together, \"traps and makes like a paste\" → easier phagocytosis. Opsonization = antigen–antibody complex \"coated with paste\" → easier phagocytosis. Variables affecting immune function: age and gender · nutrition and lifestyle · history of infection or immunization · allergies · conditions (autoimmune, cancer, chronic illness, surgery) · medications and transfusions · psychoneuroimmunologic factors. Labs: WBC 4.5–11.1; left shift = bands = infection; low ANC → reverse isolation. Aging (immunosenescence) → ↑infection, cancer, autoimmunity; Shingrix at 50+. Diagnostic studies: WBC and differential · skin tests; hypersensitivity test · bone marrow aspiration and biopsy · radiological imaging · specific antibody-antigen tests · HIV tests. Case: Grace Hall, 60 (shingles). 4 days of low-grade fever, malaise, headache, and itching/painful sensation on the right chest; erythematous maculopapular rash with vesicles. Herpes zoster = reactivation of the varicella-zoster virus (chickenpox) that can result in post-herpetic neuralgia. Increase fluids, avoid immunosuppressed people, report increasing fever or pain, follow up every 1–2 weeks. Ch 19 · Immune deficiency extras XLA: the most common type of primary immune deficiency per the slide ( 50% of PID cases ); 1 in 250,000 male newborns. BTK (Bruton tyrosine kinase) on the X chromosome. Hallmark: no circulating B cells or plasma cells; IgG <200 mg/dL. Each son of a carrier mother has a 50% chance of being affected. First indicator: recurrent bacterial infections of the respiratory tract in childhood. Diagnosis: detailed family history · B-lymphocyte surface marker assays · Western blot (BTK protein expressed?) · genetic testing · chest/sinus x-ray. IVIG = passive immunity (IgG, gammaglobulin) every 3–4 weeks or weekly subQ + low-dose prophylactic antibiotics. Complications: pneumonia, empyema, meningitis, sepsis, cellulitis, septic arthritis (60% in children); chronic enterovirus, enteroviral encephalitis, neurologic deterioration. Nursing diagnosis: risk for infection related to compromised host defenses secondary to B-cell immunodeficiency. Assess behavior (fever, change in eating, irritability, decreased activity, inconsolable crying in children). Start antibody replacement early → good prognosis. Teach: warning signs to parents, safe food handling and storage, handwashing, avoid contagious people, seek care for temp ≥100.5°F , genetic information and support groups. IVIG = immune globulin (mostly IgG) from human plasma; short-term passive immunity, lasts 3–4 weeks. CVID: low circulating B cells, like XLA but appears in adolescents/young adults ( 15–35 ). Selective IgA deficiency: 50% overcome it by age 14; can develop anaphylactic reactions when IgA is administered. IgG subclass deficiency: sinusitis, otitis media, pneumonia; prophylactic antibiotics, IVIG. Wiskott–Aldrich: B and T cells affected; X-linked; always the triad: infection, thrombocytopenia, eczema. SCID: 13 different genetic defects; \"boy in the bubble\"; cure = stem cell transplant. CMC (chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis): thymus and endocrine glands; recurrent Candida of skin, nails, mucous membranes; adrenal cortex hypofunction is the major cause of death. T-cell deficiency: CD4 helper T cells activate B cells, cytotoxic T cells, NK cells, and macrophages. Deficiency → fungal, viral, protozoan infections (Candida). DiGeorge: gestational diabetes implicated; pharyngeal pouches, 6–10 weeks. Recurrent infections: yeast, fungal, protozoa, viral (measles, chickenpox, rubella), Candida. CATCH: Cardiac (tetralogy of Fallot, murmur) · Abnormal face (low-set ears, wide-set eyes) · Thymic aplasia → infections · Cleft palate · Hypocalcemia. Hypocalcemia: twitching, tingling of lips/around mouth → tetany, seizures, laryngospasm → give calcium, monitor Ca. Nursing diagnoses: risk for opportunistic infections · risk for infection · high risk for ineffective airway clearance (laryngospasm related to hypocalcemia). Complications: delayed brain development, speech delays, learning disability, ADHD, autism spectrum, psychiatric and autoimmune disorders, GVHD. Coordinate cardiology, speech, infectious disease. DiGeorge finding to report immediately: rhinorrhea (early infection sign in T-cell deficiency). Therapy-induced (secondary): cytotoxic meds destroy WBCs. Corticosteroids keep T cells in marrow → lymphopenia, ↓IgG, suppress cell-mediated immunity. Assess temp >100°F, hypotension, tachycardia, breath sounds, cloudy/foul urine, skin rashes. Autoimmunity = body launches an immune response against self (SLE, RA, MS, type 1 DM, Graves'). Hypersensitivity = overactive immune response. Memory: ACID-S = Allergic · Cytotoxic · Immune complex · Delayed · Stimulated. Type II epidemiology: erythroblastosis fetalis (Rh incompatibility; 1 in 1,000 births to Rh-negative women) · Goodpasture's (1 case per million per year) · myasthenia gravis (autoantibodies block acetylcholine receptors; 2 per 100,000). Treatment: remove offending drug/blood product, plasmapheresis, corticosteroids, immunosuppressants, IVIG, dialysis, renal transplant. Type III: SLE (53 per 100,000; women 9× more than men), RA (women 2.5×), serum sickness (allergic reaction to meds with protein), Arthus reaction (local vasculitis after intradermal/subQ injection; raised red lesion after 4–10 hours, center ulcer). Treat: remove agent, symptom management, epinephrine or corticosteroids. Type IV: antibody independent; 24–72 h; sensitized T lymphocytes release cytokines; macrophages cause destruction; range from local dermatitis to anaphylactic shock. Prevention, antihistamines, steroidal creams. Latex nursing diagnoses: risk for localized tissue damage · risk for impaired oxygenation. Ask about history of repeated surgeries. Type V: Graves' disease: antibody binds to the TSH receptor on the thyroid. Most common food allergies: milk, eggs, peanuts, tree nuts, fish, shellfish, wheat, soy, sesame. The latex link is through fruits (latex-fruit syndrome). Type I treatment: antihistamines/decongestants (diphenhydramine), steroids, beta-agonist bronchodilators. Diagnosis: WBC differential shows ↑eosinophils; scratch test with glucocorticoids, antihistamines, NSAIDs stopped 5 days prior. Teaching: identify causes and avoid; signs of initial reaction; EpiPen use; Medic Alert bracelet. Outcomes: free of symptoms, normal oxygen, no rash or itching. Case: Samuel Smith, 50 (anaphylaxis to penicillin) Presents with pneumonia and gets IV penicillin . Then: short of breath, anxious, itching, dizzy; rapid pulse, blue-tinged lips, rash on chest and arms, wheezing, sense of impending doom. BP 85/50, HR 130, SpO₂ 89% . Hoarseness, hives, angioedema of lips and mouth. What the nurse did: immediately discontinued the IV penicillin , changed the IV tubing, started normal saline; epinephrine 0.5 mg IM stat ; 100% O₂ by non-rebreather ; cardiac monitor (HR 130 with PVCs); diphenhydramine 50 mg IV push ; IV fluids; albuterol nebulizer for bronchospasm; intubation/tracheotomy equipment ready. Improved: SpO₂ 95%, BP 110/60, HR 90. Ch 20/22 · standard additions OA / hip replacement: don't flex the hip past 90°, no leg crossing, abduction pillow, raised toilet seat. RA: methotrexate monitoring: LFTs, CBC, folic acid, no alcohol, teratogenic. Lupus (standard): butterfly rash, photosensitivity (sunscreen), joint pain, lupus nephritis (watch kidneys), hydroxychloroquine (yearly eye exams), steroids. Women 9:1. HIV (standard): retrovirus (RNA) uses reverse transcriptase to insert into CD4 T-helper cells and destroys them. Normal CD4 ≈500–1,500 cells/mm³. AIDS = CD4 <200 or an AIDS-defining opportunistic infection. ART is lifelong with strict adherence to prevent resistance; goal = undetectable viral load. Not spread by saliva, sweat, tears. Opportunistic infection (standard) Usual CD4 Note Pneumocystis pneumonia (PCP) <200 Dry cough, dyspnea; prophylaxis TMP-SMX (Bactrim) Oral/esophageal candidiasis <200–250 White plaques; dysphagia Toxoplasmosis <100 Brain lesions; avoid cat litter, undercooked meat MAC, CMV retinitis <50 CMV → vision loss Kaposi sarcoma, TB Any Purple skin lesions; TB at any CD4 Bioterrorism extras Anthrax: cutaneous = painless sore → black eschar; inhalation is deadliest. Not spread person-to-person (standard precautions). Ciprofloxacin or doxycycline. Smallpox: airborne + contact precautions; lesions start on face/extremities and are all the same stage (chickenpox = mixed stages, mostly trunk). Botulism: toxin → descending flaccid paralysis starting with cranial nerves (blurred/double vision, dysphagia, slurred speech) → respiratory failure. Antitoxin, ventilator support. Not contagious. CDC categories: A = highest risk. B = staph enterotoxin B, viral encephalitis, food safety threats (E. coli O157:H7, Salmonella, Shigella), glanders (horses), psittacosis (birds), brucellosis, Q fever, ricin, typhus, cholera. C = emerging (Nipah virus, hantavirus; pandemic flu often listed). Know category B: staph, viral encephalitis, E. coli. Cat C: viral encephalitis, flu."},{"ch":"immune","chTitle":"Immune Function and Disorders","id":"immune--bioterrorism-powerpoint-your-uploaded-slides","title":"Bioterrorism PowerPoint (your uploaded slides)","text":"Definition: terrorism involving the intentional release or dissemination of biological agents (bacteria, viruses, insects, fungi, or toxins), natural or human-modified. An attack may not be obvious: usually local health care workers report a pattern of unusual illness or a wave of sick people seeking emergency care. The first evidence may be symptoms of the disease. Officials may not immediately know what the illness is, how to treat it, or who is in danger. CDC categories (A, B, C) Slide Category A · highest priority Pathogens that pose a risk to national security because they (1) can be easily disseminated or transmitted person to person, (2) cause high mortality and major public health impact, (3) might cause public panic and social disruption, and (4) require special action for public health preparedness. Agents: Anthrax ( Bacillus anthracis ) · Botulism ( Clostridium botulinum toxin) · Plague ( Yersinia pestis ) · Smallpox (variola major) · Tularemia ( Francisella tularensis ) · Viral hemorrhagic fevers, including filoviruses (Ebola, Marburg) and arenaviruses (Lassa, Machupo). Slide Category B · second highest priority Moderately easy to disseminate; moderate morbidity and low mortality; require specific enhancements of CDC's diagnostic capacity and enhanced disease surveillance. Agents: Brucellosis · Epsilon toxin of Clostridium perfringens · Food safety threats ( Salmonella , E. coli O157:H7, Shigella ) · Glanders ( Burkholderia mallei ) · Melioidosis ( Burkholderia pseudomallei ) · Psittacosis ( Chlamydia psittaci ) · Q fever ( Coxiella burnetii ) · Ricin toxin (castor beans) · Staphylococcal enterotoxin B · Typhus fever ( Rickettsia prowazekii ) · Viral encephalitis (alphaviruses: eastern, Venezuelan, western equine encephalitis) · Water safety threats ( Vibrio cholerae , Cryptosporidium parvum ). Slide Category C · third priority Emerging pathogens that could be engineered for mass dissemination in the future because of availability, ease of production and dissemination, and potential for high morbidity and mortality. Agents: Influenza (novel strain) · Nipah virus · Typhus disease ( Rickettsia prowazekii ) · Viral encephalitis (alphaviruses) · Water safety threats ( Vibrio cholerae , Cryptosporidium parvum ). Anthrax (Category A) Sporulating gram-positive rod. Human disease mostly through contact with animal products (skins). In 2001, 22 cases occurred in the US through the postal system. Spores enter subcutaneously or by inhalation, become the vegetative form, and replicate. A thick capsule avoids phagocytosis; edema factor and lethal factor toxins drive edema, hemorrhage, and necrosis. Inhalational: incubation 1–7 days; within 24 hours respiratory failure, hemorrhagic mediastinitis , necrotizing pneumonia, shock, multiorgan failure, death. Shock plus hemorrhagic mediastinitis is the clinical hallmark. Cutaneous: incubation 7–14 days. Painless, possibly pruritic papule → central vesicle → painless black eschar. Edema, regional lymphadenopathy, fever, malaise. Hands, arms, face, neck. GI: from undercooked meat of infected animals; bowel edema, mesenteric lymphadenitis and necrosis, rapid shock and death. Diagnosis: culture of blood, sputum, pleural fluid, CSF, or skin. Treatment: ciprofloxacin, doxycycline. Smallpox (variola, Category A) Poxviridae. Eradicated 1977 , now a bioterrorism concern: unvaccinated population, contagious, spreads by aerosol. Only two stockpiles remain (CDC and Russian State Research Center). Five clinical categories: ordinary (macule → papule → vesicle → pustule) · modified (more rapid but less severe) · flat · hemorrhagic (more common in pregnant females) · variola sine eruptione. Diagnosis primarily clinical; RT-PCR only at the CDC. Treatment supportive; some evidence cidofovir has activity. Plague (Category A) Yersinia pestis . Transmitted by rodent-infected flea bites, animal scratches/bites, exposure to infected humans, bioterrorism. Bubonic: sudden fever, chills, headache, then painful swollen regional lymph node ( bubo , intensely tender, erythema, edema; fluctuation test negative). Septicemic: sepsis without a bubo. Pneumonic: short incubation, rapid ARDS. Diagnosis: culture of sputum or blood; ELISA/PCR mainly field testing. Treatment: streptomycin (drug of choice); gentamicin or doxycycline. Tularemia (Category A) Francisella tularensis , gram-negative, zoonotic. Spread by ticks and flies, infected animals, improperly prepared meat, animal bites, contaminated water, aerosol. No human-to-human transmission. Patterns: ulceroglandular, pneumonic, glandular, oropharyngeal, oculoglandular. Hard to culture; diagnosis by ELISA or histology. X-ray: patchy bilateral opacities, hilar adenopathy. Treatment: gentamicin (drug of choice); doxycycline and ciprofloxacin second choice. Category B agents Glanders ( Burkholderia mallei ): disease of horses ; humans infected by contact or droplet. Acute or chronic skin infection; droplets cause pneumonia and sepsis rapidly. Often misclassified on culture. Imipenem (3rd-gen cephalosporin, ciprofloxacin alternatives). Melioidosis ( B. pseudomallei ): soil and water reservoir; contact or inhalation; aerosol → pneumonia; sepsis and MODS rapidly. Imipenem or 3rd-gen cephalosporin, then 20 days of doxycycline or cotrimoxazole. Psittacosis ( Chlamydophila psittaci ): birds (parrots, cockatiels, canaries). Aerosol → \"atypical pneumonia\" (cough, fever, headache). ELISA. Macrolide, doxycycline, or ciprofloxacin. Q fever ( Coxiella burnetii ): cattle, sheep, goats (bacteria in milk, urine, feces, amniotic fluid, placenta); droplet spread. Fever >104°F , severe headache, myalgia, sore throat, nonproductive cough, N/V/D, chest pain; lasts 1–2 weeks; 30–50% develop pneumonia; endocarditis and chronic Q fever (>6 months) rare. Doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 15–21 days. Role of the public health system Being prepared: educate medical community and public · train special response teams · exercises for different scenarios · communication systems · interagency protocols. Initial response: early detection through surveillance/rapid assessment · mobilize laboratory · rapid confirmation of agent, site, at-risk population, prophylaxis/treatment · alert medical community, ERs, labs · implement disease-specific plans (e.g., smallpox) · determine resource needs and possible quarantine · coordinate with partner agencies. Continued response: monitor communication network · provide accurate, timely information to the public · continue epidemiologic investigation · assess environmental contamination · provide testing/prophylaxis/treatment to at-risk population · access biological stockpiles as necessary."},{"ch":"immune","chTitle":"Immune Function and Disorders","id":"immune--chapter-18-powerpoint-word-for-word","title":"Chapter 18 PowerPoint, word for word","text":"Your instructor's slides for this chapter, kept in slide order. Slide Slide 1 · Innate vs. adaptive immunity Immunity – refers to the body's ability to resist infection and disease Innate – natural immunity – first line of defense – present at birth, protects the host without remembering prior contact; response without memory Adaptive – is acquired – it is a natural exposure; examples are infection, transfer of maternal antibodies, vaccination, infusion of immune globulin. Both cellular & humoral mediated Components of the immune system: Bone marrow · Lymphoid tissue. Bone marrow: T cells & B cells. T lymphocytes mature in the thymus. B lymphocytes mature in bone marrow. Lymphoid tissue: Spleen & lymph nodes Slide Slide 2 · What the immune system does Function of the Immune system: Removes foreign antigens such as viruses and bacteria to maintain homeostasis · Phagocytosis – monocytes are responsible for engulfing and destroying foreign bodies and toxins · Inflammatory response – to injury or invading organisms; minimize blood loss; activate phagocytosis, formation of scar tissue and regeneration of injured tissue Immunity – Natural immunity: nonspecific response to any foreign invader. WBC action: release cell mediators such as histamine, bradykinin and prostaglandins – engulf foreign substance. Inflammatory response. Physical barriers: intact skin, chemical barriers, acidic secretions, enzymes – tears & saliva Physical, Mechanical, and Biochemical Barriers: Skin · Mucous membranes · Protective lining of organs · Mechanisms – sloughing, sneezing, vomiting, urination Acquired Immunity: is specific against a foreign antigen – result of prior exposure to an antigen – active or passive. Active immunity – develops by person's own body; lasts many years; maybe lifetime Slide Slide 3 · The immune response map [Image: The Immune Response] Flowchart: Immune responses split into innate (natural) immunity (nonspecific) and acquired (adaptive) immunity (specific). Innate → first line of defense (physical, biochemical, mechanical barriers) and second line of defense (inflammatory response). Acquired → third line of defense → naturally acquired (active: infection; passive: maternal antibodies) and artificially acquired (active: vaccination; passive: immune serum). Slide Slide 4 · Where immunity lives [Image: Lymph Nodes and the Lymphatic System] Left: a lymph node cut open, with lymph flowing in through incoming lymphatic vessels, filtered past clusters of lymphocytes and macrophages, and flowing out. Right: whole body with lymph nodes (cervical, axillary, inguinal), the thymus, spleen, bone marrow, thoracic duct, and right lymphatic duct emptying into the subclavian veins. A small inset shows the right upper body drains to the right lymphatic duct and the rest drains to the thoracic duct. Lymph nodes and the Lymphatic system: Thymus – located in the chest cavity; stimulates T cells & where they develop · Bone Marrow – B & T lymphocytes · Spleen – part of the lymphatic system; serves as a blood filter for old and damaged RBC Additional Lymphoid tissues: Tonsils – traps bacteria & viruses inhaled · Adenoids – located in nasopharynx, also traps inhaled bacteria & viruses · Peyer's Patches – lymphoid follicles located in the small intestines, known as the intestinal immune sensors against pathogens [Image: Central and Peripheral Organs of the Immune System] Body figure labeling central organs (thymus, bone marrow) and peripheral organs (adenoids, tonsils, lymph nodes, lymphatic vessels, spleen, Peyer's patches in the small intestine, lymph nodes in the jejunum). Slide Slide 5 · Leukocytes (WBCs) Leukocytes – are the mobile units, travel through the bloodstream to fight infection · Neutrophils – phagocytes – destroy bacteria at early inflammation · Basophils – release heparin as anticoag & histamine during early inflammation · Eosinophils – destroy allergens & combat parasitic infections · Macrophages – digest & destroy microorganisms Slide Slide 6 · B cells, t cells & immunoglobulins Lymphocytes: B Lymphocytes – found in lymph nodes, spleen & thymus – Immunoglobulins (Igs) produce antibodies (IgA, IgD, IgE, IgG and IgM) · T Lymphocytes – Natural killer cells – target and kill cancer cells Table 18.2 – Immunoglobulins that B cells produce: IgA – found in secretory gland secretions – breast milk, sweat, saliva, mucus and tears · IgD – plays a role in B cell activation · IgE – acts as a defender against parasitic infections; mediator of allergic responses · IgG – transported across placenta & effective against bacteria, viruses & toxins · IgM – first antibody produced during primary response to an antigen [Image: Process of Differentiation of T-Cell and B-Cell Lymphocytes] Flowchart from bone marrow. Left (humoral immunity): B cells → bone marrow maturation → B lymphocytes → memory cells and plasma cells → antibodies. Right (cellular, cell-mediated immunity): T cells → thymus → regulatory T cells (→ suppressor T cells) and effector T cells (→ helper T cells and cytotoxic T cells). Slide Slide 7 · Messengers & helpers Cont with Cells: Dendritic cells – macrophage cell, aids in T cell & B cell recognition · Mast cells – found in the skin and lining of the respiratory and GI tracts; similar to basophils they release heparin and histamine during early inflammatory response · Cytokines – are small proteins that regulate immune response – Interleukins – suppresses inflammation; stimulates leukocyte production & maturation · Interferon – proteins that protect against viral and tumor growth · Tumor necrosis factor alpha – produce macrophages, involved in production of immune cells Slide Slide 8 · The antibody's backup crew Anatomy of the Immune System: Complement system – is a system of proteins that complement or help the actions of antibodies and other cells in destroying bacteria or microbes · Whenever a pathogen enters the body, it gets recognized by antibodies present in our blood, then the binding of the antibody & pathogen occurs activating the complement system, by different cascades of enzymes killing the microbe Slide Slide 9 · The acute inflammatory response [Image: Acute Inflammatory Response] Flowchart: tissue damage → pathogen invasion → inflammatory response → splits into vasodilation (↑ blood flow, facilitating leukocyte movement [chemotaxis] to the inflamed area) and increased capillary permeability (leukocytes move into the tissue) → invading organisms engulfed and phagocytized + fibrinogen clot border forms → cardinal signs of inflammation: localized redness (erythema), warmth, edema, drainage (serous exudate or pus), pain → systemic response: fever, malaise, chills, elevated WBC count (leukocytosis). Slide Slide 10 · Cellular vs. humoral immunity Adaptive Immune Response: Cellular – mediated by T lymphocytes · Humoral – mediated by the B lymphocytes Cellular Mediated Response: T cells · Direct destruction of foreign or abnormal cells Humoral-Mediated Response: Agglutination – when 2 antigens react – clump – facilitates phagocytosis and enables the body to clear itself of the invading organism · Opsonization – process in which the antigen-antibody binding is coated with a pasty substance Slide Slide 11 · Variables, advances & the nurse's role Variables affecting Immune Function: Age & gender · Nutrition & lifestyle · Hx of infection or immunization · Allergies · Presence of conditions or disorders: autoimmune; cancer; chronic illness; surgery · Medications & transfusions · Psychoneuroimmunologic factors Advances in Immunology: Genetic engineering: DNA technology · Stem cells – research shows that stem cells can restore an immune system that has been destroyed · Clinical trials using stem cells – RA, SLE, scleroderma and multiple sclerosis Nurse's Role in Evaluation of the Immune System: Offer support · Reduce anxiety · Provide patient education and counseling Slide Slide 12 · Assessment of the immune system History – genetics? chem induced? · Age · Current medications – abx, anti-inflammatory · Family history; thyroid suppressive tx · Social history · Nutritional status – adequate vits/min · Infection history · Immunizations – childhood & adult · Chronic illnesses – allergies · Autoimmune disorders – immunosuppressants · Cancer – antineoplastic agents Physical examination: Vital signs, LOC · Inspection – skin (rashes, dermatitis-type lesions, hematoma, petechiae, purpura), mucous membranes, edema, gait, vision, hearing, cardio, respiratory, GI, GU · Auscultation – listen for adventitious sounds, heart sounds, bowel sounds · Palpation and percussion – lymph nodes, hepatosplenomegaly, masses, fluid in abd Slide Slide 13 · Testing immune function Diagnostic studies: Blood tests – WBC & differential · Skin tests; hypersensitivity test · Bone marrow aspiration and biopsy · Radiological imaging · Specific antibody-antigen tests · HIV infection tests Slide Slide 14 · Immunosenescence [Image: Age-Related Changes in the Immune System] Flowchart: The Aging Immune System → three branches. (1) Decreased immunologic function; less efficient immune responses → increased infections, especially secondary infections. (2) Increased production of autoantibodies → increased autoimmune disorders. (3) Decline in immune response; malignant cells not destroyed as quickly → increased cancer incidence. Slide Slide 15 · Grace Hall, 60: herpes zoster (shingles) Episode 1: Grace Hall is a 60-year-old female who presents to an urgent care clinic with a 4-day history of low- grade fever, malaise, headache, and an itching and painful sensation on the right side of her chest. She states that she has stopped exercising due to the discomfort and finds tight clothing to be irritating. Her maximum oral temperature has been 100.4°F (38°C). Ms. Hall denies weakness, dizziness, confusion, difficulty swallowing or breathing, cough, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and weight loss. She also denies exposure to ill individuals or recent travel. Episode 2: The nurse practitioner at the clinic obtains a comprehensive health history and performs a physical examination on Ms. Hall. Her vital signs show her to have a low-grade fever, with a temperature of 100.2°F (37.8°C), a heart rate of 88 bpm, and a blood pressure reading of 128/82 mm Hg. Her examination is significant for an erythematous maculopapular rash with several vesicles on the right upper chest. Wrap-up: Based on Ms. Hall's history of presenting symptoms and physical examination, she was diagnosed with herpes zoster (shingles). Herpes zoster is a reactivation of the chickenpox or varicella- zoster virus (VZV) that can result in post-herpetic neuralgia. The nurse practitioner instructs Ms. Hall to increase her fluid intake and avoid exposure to individuals who may be immunosuppressed. She is instructed to immediately report if she develops increasing fever or pain. She will follow up every 1 to 2 weeks until resolution of her symptoms."},{"ch":"immune","chTitle":"Immune Function and Disorders","id":"immune--chapter-19-powerpoint-word-for-word","title":"Chapter 19 PowerPoint, word for word","text":"Your instructor's slides for this chapter, kept in slide order. Slide Slide 1 · Primary vs. secondary immunodeficiency Learning outcomes in Chapter 19: There are 8 learning outcomes to assist you in understanding the pathophysiological process of the immune dysfunction and correlation of clinical manifestations in order to develop a comprehensive plan of care and teaching plan for patients with immune dysfunction. Introduction: Immune deficiency disorder is the lack or absence of adequate immune response · Opposite of autoimmune disorder · Affected people are more susceptible to infections and have no fully functioning immune system. There are 2 immune deficiency disorders: Primary · Secondary Primary & Secondary deficiency: Primary immunodeficiency – occurs from genetic or developmental abnormality. Defects from T & B cells, natural killer cells, phagocytic cells or the complement system · Secondary immunodeficiencies occur after birth secondary to disease process (malnutrition, cancer, drugs/radiation therapy, loss of spleen, exposure to chemicals, infection or stress) Table 19.1 – Warning signs of PID: Eight or more new infections within 1 year · Two or more serious sinus infections within 1 year · Two or more months on antibiotics · Two or more pneumonias within 1 year · Failure of an infant to gain weight · Recurrent deep skin or organ abscess · Persistent thrush in the mouth or elsewhere on skin after age 1 · Need for IV antibiotics to clear infections · A family history of immune deficiency Slide Slide 2 · XLA: the disease Epidemiology: XLA – an inherited or congenital immune disorder · The most common type of Primary Immune Deficiency · Accounts for 50% of PID cases · 1 in 250,000 male newborns · XLA manifests approx 6 months of age Pathophysiology: Deficiency of BTK (Bruton Tyrosine Kinase) – gene essential in development of B lymphocytes – present in the X chromosome · BTK protein alerts B cells to mature – unable to mature, no antibody response · Hallmark: No circulating B cells or plasma cells · Lab studies show markedly reduced immunoglobulins (IgG < 200 mg/dL) · Prone to severe bacterial infections [Image: X-Linked Recessive Genetic Inheritance of Disease] \"X-Linked Recessive, Carrier Mother.\" An unaffected father (XY) and a carrier mother (one altered X) have four possible children: unaffected son, unaffected daughter, carrier daughter (one altered X, no disease), and affected son (his only X is altered). Each son has a 50% chance of being affected. Clinical Manifestations: Infections of the ears, lungs, skin, conjunctiva, and central nervous system · Bacterial infections such as pharyngitis, sinusitis, otitis media, bronchitis and pneumonia · Presence of recurrent bacterial infections of the respiratory tract in childhood is the first indicator Medical Management – Diagnosis: Detailed family history – to detect abnormal immune response · B- lymphocyte surface marker assays – identifies specific cells involved in immune response · Western blot test – determines if the BTK protein is being expressed · Genetic testing – women with XLA · Chest or sinus X-ray Medical Management – Treatment: IV immune globulin (IVIG) – provides passive immunity – 1980's – Immunoglobulin G (IgG) – Gammaglobulin – protects against infection from various diseases; given every 3–4 weeks or weekly thru subQ injections (serum IgG) · Prophylactic antibiotics – low dose Complications: Severe, life-threatening bacterial infection – pneumonia, empyema, meningitis, sepsis, cellulitis, septic arthritis – 60% in children · Chronic viral enterovirus · Enteroviral encephalitis · Neurological deterioration Slide Slide 3 · Assess, act, teach, evaluate Assessment and analysis: Related to infection of the respiratory tract, skin, conjunctiva, or ears · Common findings – fever, change in eating patterns, irritability, decreased activity, changes in behaviour, in children – inconsolable crying Nursing diagnosis: Risk for infection related to compromised host defenses secondary to B-cell immunodeficiency Assessments: Vital signs – T? RR? · Assess lung sounds – adventitious? · Inspect eyes and ears, skin and nails, rashes and lesions – assess the color of the skin & texture · Take note of current meds · Evaluate the infant or child's behavior and eating patterns Actions: Administer IV gamma globulin as ordered – assess any problems post infusion · Administer prophylactic antibiotic therapy as ordered · Anticipate prompt treatment with antibiotics when infection is present Teaching: Teach parents of at-risk children about the warning signs · Institute precautions to prevent infection · Protect the patient from direct contact with anyone with a contagious illness · Safe food handling and storage practices · Signs and symptoms of infection and when to seek medical attention · Good hand washing · Information regarding genetic disorders and community support groups Evaluating care outcomes: Infection prevention · When to contact healthcare provider · Start antibody replacement early – have good prognosis Slide Slide 4 · CVID, IgA & IgG subclass deficiency Other B-Cell Deficiencies: Common Variable Immune Deficiency (CVID) – low levels of circulating B cells, similar to XLA except that it appears in adolescents or young adults (15–35 y/o) · Selective Immunoglobulin A – 50% affected overcome by age 14; develop anaphylactic reactions when IgA is administered · Immunoglobulin G Subclass Deficiency – risk for sinusitis, otitis media, PNA – Tx prophylactic abx; IVIG Type of Antibodies – 5 types of antibodies & function: IgG – triggers autoimmune disease · IgA – found in mucosal tissues · IgM – helps B cell remember pathogen · IgE – responsible for allergic response · IgD – signals the release of front-line IgM to fight disease and infection. Kick starts the immune response Slide Slide 5 · DiGeorge: the disease Primary Immune Dysfunction: T-cell Deficiencies: CD4 helper cells · CD8 cytotoxic T cells · Cell- mediated immune response – protects against fungal, protozoan, viral, and intracellular bacterial infections · Control malignant cell proliferation Epidemiology: Autosomal-dominant genetic condition – presence of one altered gene · Arises from 22nd chromosome · Spontaneously present at birth · DiGeorge syndrome is a congenital thymic hypoplasia, present at birth; gestational diabetes is implicated [Image: Autosomal-Dominant Genetic Inheritance of DiGeorge's Syndrome] An affected father and an unaffected mother have four possible children: affected son, unaffected daughter, unaffected son, affected daughter. Each child has a 50% chance; boys and girls are equally affected. Pathophysiology: Disturbance of normal development of pharyngeal pouches – occurs during 6–10 weeks gestation · Effects depend on which pharyngeal pouch is affected – dysfunction of thymus and parathyroid – facial deformities (cleft palate) – heart anomalies Clinical Manifestations: Recurrent infections – yeast, fungal, protozoa; viral – measles, chicken pox, rubella, candida · Cardiac abnormality – heart murmur · Abnormal faces – low set ears, wide set eyes · Thymic aplasia – atrophy of the thymus · Cleft palate · Hypocalcemia – hypoparathyroidism – twitching, tingling in the lips, around the mouth Diagnosis: Genetic testing – finding of submicroscopic deletion of chromosome 22, detected by fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH) – molecular cytogenetic technique that allows the localization of specific DNA Treatment: Pharmacologic therapy – calcium supplements – prevents tetany & seizures · Treatment of infections · IVIG – provides ST passive immunity (3–4 weeks) · Prophylactic antibiotics · Bone marrow transplant in severe cases Surgical Management: Thymus tissue transplant (3–6 months after birth) · Cardiac surgery Complications: Delayed brain development · Speech delays · Learning disability · Attention deficit- hyperactivity disorder · Autism spectrum disorder · Psychiatric disorders · Autoimmune disorders · Graft-versus-host disease – T cells attack Slide Slide 6 · Assess, act, teach, evaluate Assessment and analysis: Disturbance of normal embryonic development – thymus – parathyroid gland – facial structures – cardiac structures Nursing diagnoses: Risk for complications – opportunistic infections · Risk for infection – compromised host defense · High risk for ineffective airway clearance – laryngospasm related to hypocalcemia Assessments: Vital signs – T · Lung sounds · Calcium levels – presence of hypoparathyroidism · Tetany · Monitor WBCs Actions: Administer calcium supplements as ordered · Infection control precautions and standard precautions · Strategies for addressing feeding difficulties · Coordinate care for cardiac, infectious disease specialists, and speech pathologists Teaching: Overview of the disease process · Growth and development milestones – early assessment for learning disabilities · Emotional support and networking Evaluating care outcomes: Limited infections · No seizures · Growth and development within normal limits · Support from interprofessional team Slide Slide 7 · CMC, Hyper-IgM, SCID, Wiskott–Aldrich Other T-Cell Immune Deficiencies: Chronic Mucocutaneous Candidiasis – involves thymus and other endocrine glands. Recurrent Candida infections of the skin, nails, mucous membranes. High morbidity due to endocrine dysfunction. Hypofunction of the adrenal cortex is the major cause of death · Hyper- IgM Syndrome – X-linked immunodeficiency with low IgG and IgA serum levels Primary Immune Deficiency of Both T Cells and B Cells: Severe combined immune deficiency (SCID) – there are 13 different genetic defects · Wiskott–Aldrich syndrome – combined B & T cell defect, characteristics include recurrent infection, eczema, and thrombocytopenia Slide Slide 8 · Therapy-induced immunodeficiency Epidemiology: Caused by medication-induced immunosuppression, radiation, or surgery Pathophysiology: Cytotoxic medications destroy WBCs – remaining lymphocytes cannot release antibodies and lymphokines · Corticosteroids interfere with cell-mediated immunity or production of antibodies – decreased T cells (keep T cells in bone marrow) – suppression of cell-mediated immunity and lymphopenia – decreased IgG production – decreased antibody-antigen binding Medical Management: Prevention · Hand washing · Avoiding contact with known infectious persons · Regular provider follow up · Prompt action when signs of infection develop Assessment and analysis: Protect patient from infection · Assess for signs/symptoms of infection Nursing diagnoses: Risk for complications · Risk for infection Assessments: Vital signs – T, hypotension, tachycardia · Breath sounds – may be decreased or adventitious · Urine – cloudy, foul smell – UTI · Skin rashes or lesions Actions: Practice good hand washing · Treat infections as ordered · Anticipate changes in immunosuppressive medications Teaching: Avoid crowds or large gatherings · Do not change cat litter boxes – avoid toxoplasmosis exposure · Avoid turtles or reptiles as pets – salmonella exposure · Eat a low bacteria diet · Report any signs/symptoms of infection Evaluating care outcomes: Takes precautions to prevent infection · Practices good hand washing · Knows when to contact provider · Treats infections promptly Slide Slide 9 · When the immune system overreacts Excessive Immune Response: Autoimmunity – body initiates an immune response against self; body fails to recognize normal cells and causes immune reaction against the perceived antigen · Hypersensitivity reaction – immune response is overactive. An antigen stimulates an immune response in a susceptible individual Slide Slide 10 · Immediate reactions & anaphylaxis Epidemiology: Genetic predisposition – 80% chance if both parents have allergies · Allergic rhinitis most common type I hypersensitivity reaction · Incidence of anaphylaxis not known Pathophysiology [image]: Allergic reaction cycle. (1) First exposure: initial contact with the allergen → (2) B cell becomes a plasma cell and releases IgE antibodies → (3) IgE attaches to receptors on mast cells → (4) Second exposure: the allergen binds the IgE on the mast cell → (5) mast cell degranulates, releasing histamine and other chemicals → allergic reaction. Clinical Manifestations: Nasal discharge · Sneezing · Pruritus of upper airways · Headache · Sinus pressure · Itchy, watery eyes · Anaphylaxis: systemic response – dyspnea, wheezes/crackles, skin reaction, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, anxiety, flush of heat, angioedema, bronchospasm, hoarseness, and stridor Diagnosis: WBC with differential – reveals increase in eosinophils · Skin testing – scratch test · Glucocorticoids, antihistamines and NSAIDs are discontinued 5 days prior to testing Treatment: Identification, treatment, prevention of allergic responses · Antihistamines and decongestants – diphenhydramine hydrochloride · Steroids · Beta-agonist bronchodilators Slide Slide 11 · Assess, act, teach, evaluate Assessment and analysis: Bronchoconstriction · Increased capillary permeability · Runny nose, itchy, red eyes, rash · Dyspnea · Wheezes Nursing diagnosis: Risk for complications of allergic reaction Assessments: Monitor respiratory rate and depth, lung sounds, pulse ox, and ABG · Monitor blood pressure and pulse · General systems assessment Actions: Discontinue offending agent · Administer oxygen as ordered · Elevate head of bed for ventilation; do not compromise BP · Administer medications as ordered Teaching: Identification of potential causes and avoidance · Signs and symptoms of initial reaction · EpiPen use · Medic Alert bracelet Evaluating care outcomes: Free of symptoms · Normal oxygen level · No evidence of rash, itching, or difficulty breathing Slide Slide 12 · Cytotoxic reactions Type II Hypersensitivity Reaction: Cytotoxic: Complement and antibody-mediated cell destruction · Complement and antibody receptor-mediated inflammation · Antibody-mediated cellular dysfunction Epidemiology: Erythroblastosis fetalis – Rh desensitization; 1 in 1,000 births to Rh-neg women – due to incompatibility · Goodpasture's syndrome – produced antibodies attack the lungs & kidneys – 1 case per one million people per year · Myasthenia gravis – 2 out of 100,000 people – cell dysfunction in the transmission of nerve impulse Subtype 1: Complement is not working · Susceptible to autoimmune diseases · Examples: blood transfusion reactions · hemolytic disease due to blood type or Rh incompatibility · certain medication reactions [Image: Compatibility of Blood Types] Diagram: type O (top) has no antigens and both A and B antibodies; type AB (bottom) has A and B antigens and no antibodies. Green arrows (compatible) run from O to every type and from A and B to AB. Red crossed arrows (incompatible) run between A and B. Subtype 2: Goodpasture's syndrome: immune system attacks Goodpasture's antigen in the glomerular basement membrane · Deposits of IgG form along basement membrane of lungs or kidneys · Glomerulonephritis and hemorrhage of lung Subtype 3: Antibodies bind to cell-surface receptors · Myasthenia gravis – the autoantibodies either block the action of acetylcholine or destroy the receptors Medical Management – Treatment: Removal of medication or blood product causing reaction · Plasmapheresis · Corticosteroids · Immunosuppressive medications · IVIG · Dialysis · Renal transplant Complications: Renal failure · Hemolytic reaction · Lung damage · Anemia, leukopenia, or thrombocytopenia · Hemorrhage Slide Slide 13 · Immune complex reactions Immune complex-mediated reactions: Categories: systemic or local immune complex reactions · Examples: Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) · Rheumatoid arthritis · Serum sickness – allergic reaction to meds with protein · Arthus reaction – local immune complex – acute inflammatory response after vaccination Epidemiology: SLE – genetic, environmental, or unknown factors – 53 per 100,000 people – women 9× more than men · Rheumatoid arthritis – women 2.5× more than men · Arthus reaction – rare, no specific prevalence Local Immune Complex Reactions: Deposit of immune complexes in dermal vessels · Arthus reaction is a localized vasculitis due to deposit of immune complexes in dermal vessels after intradermal or subcut injections – raised red lesion develops after 4–10 hours, an ulcer forms in the center Medical Management: Removal of offending agent · Symptom management · Epinephrine or systemic corticosteroids Slide Slide 14 · Delayed reactions & latex allergy Epidemiology: Delayed hypersensitivity – poison ivy – Mantoux test for tuberculosis – latex allergy Pathophysiology and Clinical Manifestations: Antibody independent · Occurs 24–72 hours after exposure · Sensitized T lymphocytes attack antigens and release cytokines · Macrophages responsible for tissue destruction · Range from local dermatitis to anaphylactic shock Medical management: Prevention · Antihistamines · Steroidal skin creams Assessment and analysis: Tissue damage related to inflammatory response – local skin reactions – conjunctivitis – rhinitis – respiratory distress Nursing diagnoses: Risk for localized tissue damage · Risk for impaired oxygenation Assessments: Vital signs – respiratory distress, hypotension, tachycardia · Skin · Previous history of latex allergy · History of repeated surgical procedures · Allergies – avocado, chestnut, mango, papaya, peach, banana, kiwi, raw potato, tomato, passion fruit Actions: Eliminate exposure Teaching: Avoid exposure to items commonly made of latex · Medic Alert bracelet · EpiPen use Evaluating Care Outcomes: Heightened awareness of products that contain latex · EpiPen · Medic Alert bracelet Slide Slide 15 · Stimulatory reactions Type V Hypersensitivity: Stimulated: Antibody-mediated cellular dysfunction · Excess stimulation of normal cell- surface receptor by antibody (stimulatory reaction) · Example: Graves' disease – antibody binds to the TSH receptor sites on the thyroid gland Slide Slide 16 · Samuel Smith, 50: anaphylaxis to penicillin Episode 1: Samuel Smith is a 50-year-old male patient who presents in the emergency department with a temperature of 101°F (38.3°C), a productive cough, fatigue, and difficulty breathing. He has a history of seasonal allergic rhinitis and mild asthma but no other significant health history. He does not report any known medication allergies. Initial orders include: chest radiography · complete blood count · complete metabolic profile · pulse oximetry · sputum for culture and sensitivity. Episode 2: Mr. Smith's chest radiograph reveals pneumonia. He is placed on IV penicillin. The nurse returns to check on the patient and finds he is short of breath and extremely anxious about his symptoms and states that his skin is \"itching\" and he feels dizzy. Upon initial assessment, his pulse is rapid, his lips are blue tinged, a rash is apparent on his chest and arms, and wheezing is heard on auscultation of his lungs. The patient expresses a sense of impending doom. Vital signs: BP 85/50 mmHg · HR 130 bpm · pulse oximetry 89%. Wrap-up: On auscultation, audible wheezing with decreased breath sounds in the lower lobes is noted. Mr. Smith complains of severe dyspnea and shortness of breath. Hoarseness is evident. Itchy skin wheals or hives have spread over his body, forming large, red blotches. Angioedema of his lips and mouth is visible. The nurse immediately discontinues the IV penicillin. The IV tubing is changed, and normal saline is initiated. Epinephrine 0.5 mg is administered IM stat. Oxygen therapy is provided via a 100% high-flow nonrebreather face mask. Oxygen saturation is monitored via pulse oximetry. Mr. Smith is placed on a cardiac monitor, which reveals a heart rate of 130 bpm with occasional premature ventricular contractions (PVCs). His BP is 85/50 mm Hg. His oxygen saturation improves to 90% after the initiation of oxygen. Diphenhydramine 50 mg is administered via IV push, and IV fluids are initiated. Albuterol, a beta agonist, is administered via nebulizer to relieve the bronchospasm. Emergency equipment for endotracheal intubation and possible tracheotomy is readied. Mr. Smith's condition begins to improve. His shortness of breath lessens, and pulse oximetry indicates improved oxygenation at 95%. Vital signs: BP 110/60 · HR 90."},{"ch":"repro-assess","chTitle":"Assessment of Reproductive Function","id":"repro-assess--assessment-of-reproductive-function","title":"Assessment of Reproductive Function","text":"History, the physical exam, and the diagnostic tests you'll see used for the reproductive cancers."},{"ch":"repro-assess","chTitle":"Assessment of Reproductive Function","id":"repro-assess--key-assessment-points","title":"Key assessment points","text":"Gravida = total number of pregnancies. Parity (TPAL) = Term, Preterm, Abortions, Living. Example: one baby at 40 weeks plus one miscarriage = G2 P1011 . Normal cycle: 21–35 days, lasting 2–8 days, blood loss 20–80 mL. Ask about the LMP, flow, painful periods, and bleeding between periods or after sex. Family history of breast, ovarian, uterine, cervical, or prostate cancer. Speculum exam: warm and lubricate the speculum, announce each step, and the patient may stop at any time. Cervical motion tenderness suggests PID. Change gloves between the vaginal and rectal exam. Breast exam: inspect sitting with arms in different positions. Palpate the clavicles and axillae for nodes, then palpate supine in light, medium, and firm circles down to the chest wall, including the nipple and areola. Note any discharge. Male exam: done standing. Transilluminate scrotal masses in a dark room (benign masses let light through). Bear down to check for a hernia. DRE for all men >50 and any man with trouble starting urination or frequency."},{"ch":"repro-assess","chTitle":"Assessment of Reproductive Function","id":"repro-assess--labs","title":"Labs","text":"Pap test samples the squamocolumnar junction , where cervical cancer develops. Wet prep: Trichomonas, Candida, bacterial vaginosis. Hormones: FSH, LH, estrogen, progesterone, testosterone. hCG confirms pregnancy. PSA is a protein made by the prostate, used as a tumor marker. ↑PSA raises prostate cancer risk 25–60%, but there are false positives AND false negatives, so testing is a shared decision with the provider. Menopause: estrogen and progesterone ↓, FSH ↑ . Hot flashes, vaginal atrophy, dyspareunia, ↑CVD risk, osteoporosis. With aging, men have more ED, BPH, and prostate cancer, and all ED and urinary complaints should be investigated."},{"ch":"repro-assess","chTitle":"Assessment of Reproductive Function","id":"repro-assess--table-64-3-diagnostic-studies-prep-and-nursing-implications","title":"Table 64.3 Diagnostic studies: prep and nursing implications ★","text":"Study Why / abnormal findings Prep and nursing Mammography Finds tumors <5 mm that can't be felt on exam No cream, deodorant, or lotion on the day. Results take a few days Ultrasound First line for the pelvis (inexpensive). Shows tumors, abscesses, and a thickened endometrial stripe. Guides breast biopsy. Shows scrotal masses Full bladder for a pelvic scan. Explain the transvaginal probe if used MRI Soft tissue: tumors, abscess, bleeding Remove jewelry. Ask about implanted metal, pregnancy, and claustrophobia. Check allergies before contrast Colposcopy After an abnormal Pap. Magnifies the cervix. Acetic acid and Lugol's iodine are applied, and abnormal tissue does not stain brown. Biopsy is taken Empty the bladder. Nothing in the vagina for 24 hours. Reschedule if menstruating heavily. Pregnancy test, consent. ⚠ Lugol's is contraindicated with iodine or shellfish allergy Laparoscopy Ovarian cysts, fibroids, endometriosis (CO₂ distends the abdomen) NPO the night before, consent, pregnancy test. Rest 2 days. Bloating and shoulder or abdominal pain from CO₂ Hysteroscopy Looks inside the uterus and can remove polyps and fibroids NPO, consent. Rest 2 days. Nothing in the vagina for 4 weeks D&C Endometrial sampling: molar pregnancy, endometrial cancer NPO, consent. Normal postop bleeding (count pads). Cramping relieved by NSAIDs Endometrial biopsy Endometrial cancer, endometritis. Done for abnormal uterine bleeding Pregnancy test, consent. Cramping and spotting afterward (NSAIDs) Hysterosalpingogram Blocked fallopian tubes, uterine abnormalities (x-ray with contrast) Pregnancy test. Iodine or contrast allergy. Cramping afterward"},{"ch":"female","chTitle":"Female Reproductive and Breast Cancers","id":"female--female-reproductive-and-breast-cancers","title":"Female Reproductive and Breast Cancers","text":"Breast · Ovarian · Uterine (endometrial) · Cervical. The \"tell them apart\" table is at the end."},{"ch":"female","chTitle":"Female Reproductive and Breast Cancers","id":"female--breast-cancer-2nd-leading-cause-of-cancer-death-in-women","title":"Breast cancer · 2nd leading cause of cancer death in women","text":"Why it matters and risk factors The 2nd most common cause of cancer death in females. About 2.3 million new cases worldwide (2022). In the US, about 321,910 invasive and 60,730 in situ cases. About 2,670 cases in men. THE single greatest risk factor = AGE. People aged 80–85 have 15× the risk of those aged 30–35. Black women <50 have a higher risk. Black women have higher breast cancer mortality. More lifetime ovulatory cycles: early menarche and late menopause raise risk 30–50%. No pregnancy or live birth before 30 nearly doubles the risk. Only a live birth lowers it. BRCA1 and BRCA2 cause 5–10% of breast cancers. Carriers have a 50–85% lifetime breast risk and a 45% ovarian risk by age 70. 70–80% of breast cancers occur by chance. Proliferative benign disease (hyperplasia) raises risk up to 70%. Other risks: excessive alcohol, inactivity, obesity, exogenous hormones. BRCA testing is only for high-risk patients: breast cancer before 50, triple-negative cancer, ovarian cancer, bilateral disease, a relative diagnosed before 45. Patho and in-situ lesions Cells grow and divide abnormally and rapidly, invade nearby tissue, and spread through blood and lymph. Staged by size, nodes, and metastasis. The 2018 AJCC system also uses grade, hormone receptor (ER/PR) status, HER2 status, and Oncotype DX. DCIS (ductal carcinoma in situ) is stage 0 , confined to the lining of the milk ducts. It requires SURGERY . LCIS (lobular carcinoma in situ) is in the milk-producing lobules. It is no longer considered cancer and has NO treatment . The focus is risk reduction and surveillance (mammogram). Both DCIS and LCIS increase the risk of later invasive cancer. Breast cancer staging Stage Tumor / nodes / mets 0 In situ (DCIS). No nodes, no mets IA Tumor up to 2 cm. No nodes IB No tumor or <2 cm, with node involvement <2 mm IIA No tumor with nodes >2 mm, OR <2 cm with nodes, OR 2–5 cm with no nodes IIB 2–5 cm with nodes, OR >5 cm with no nodes IIIA Any size with 4–8 nodes, or >5 cm with nodes IIIB Spread to the chest wall, 9 nodes, or inflammatory breast cancer IIIC Chest wall with ≥10 nodes IV Any size, nodes, AND metastasis Four stages of breast cancer. I: ≤2 cm, no node spread · II: larger, may reach nearby nodes · III: may involve nodes, chest wall, or skin · IV: any size with distant metastasis Clinical manifestations ★ New mass or lump: cancerous masses are typically HARD, IRREGULAR, PAINLESS and often immovable, but they may be round, soft, and tender. Change in breast shape or swelling Skin changes: peeling, flaking, pitting, DIMPLING , redness Nipple changes: inversion, thickening, drainage Axillary node enlargement. Mets go to bone, lung, nodes, liver, and brain. (Class: \"soft tissue such as abdomen and lung are most impacted.\") Diagnosis, and why Mammography (screening). Mammogram plus MRI for high-risk patients. Ultrasound further defines an area seen on mammogram. BIOPSY = DEFINITIVE: FNA, core needle, vacuum-assisted, or surgical excision (a wire marks a nonpalpable mass). Sentinel node biopsy (blue dye or radioactive tracer) finds the first node cancer would reach, which determines stage. If it's negative, the full axillary dissection (20–40 nodes) can be skipped. Labs: CBC, platelets, LFTs, chest x-ray. Bone and liver scans if skeletal symptoms or abnormal LFTs. CT of the abdomen plus bone scan for stage II or higher. Treatment depends on stage Surgery: lumpectomy (breast conservation, removes only the involved tissue plus a margin) or mastectomy (modified radical removes the whole breast plus axillary nodes). For early disease the two are equivalent, so the patient chooses. Lumpectomy is ALWAYS followed by radiation. Order: surgery → chemo → THEN radiation (case study). Chemotherapy: for tumors ≥1 cm or positive nodes, and offered to all premenopausal patients. Neoadjuvant chemo is first line for intermediate and advanced operable cancer and makes breast conservation more likely. Radiation: after lumpectomy, after mastectomy if ≥4 nodes are positive, for recurrence, for palliation. Targeted therapy: HER2+ → trastuzumab (Herceptin). Also used for hormone-receptor+, BRCA, and triple-negative tumors. Immunotherapy: pembrolizumab with chemo before surgery for high-risk triple-negative cancer. Endocrine (hormone) therapy for ER/PR+ tumors, given 5–10 years after chemo: tamoxifen or aromatase inhibitors. Locally advanced or inoperable: neoadjuvant chemo, then modified radical mastectomy, then chemo plus radiation. No immediate reconstruction. Metastatic: focus on quality and quantity of life; weigh benefit against toxicity. Lumpectomy: only the involved tissue is removed Modified radical mastectomy: all breast tissue plus axillary nodes are removed Chest after mastectomy Complications and treatment side effects Immediately post-op: infection, wound dehiscence, PE, pneumonia (immobility). Bloody drainage SATURATING the dressing is the most concerning wound finding. Serosanguineous drainage is expected. Long term: pain, scarring, LYMPHEDEMA (arm swelling after axillary nodes are removed), and shoulder immobility (the most common long-term effect; \"frozen shoulder\") . Chemo: hair loss, N/V, mouth pain, diarrhea or constipation, peripheral neuropathy, rash, nail changes, ↓WBC (fever and infection), premature ovarian failure (bone loss, infertility), later MDS or leukemia. Immunotherapy: skin rashes, flu-like symptoms, thyroid problems, diarrhea, autoimmune organ damage. Endocrine therapy: hot flashes, vaginal dryness, ↑osteoporosis, rarely endometrial cancer. Radiation: redness, patchy desquamation, itching, irritation of the skin (the key thing to monitor). Breast swelling for 6–18 months. Pneumonitis (nonproductive cough with an infiltrate, 6 weeks to 6 months later). Late cardiac risk. Hormone drugs (Table 65.3) ★ Tamoxifen (SERM) blocks estrogen at its receptor. Taken for 5–10 years. Common: hot flashes, night sweats, irregular bleeding or spotting, ↓libido, vaginal discharge or dryness. Rare but serious: DVT and PE, uterine/endometrial cancer, stroke, cataracts, bone loss (premenopausal). ⚠ Avoid SSRIs (they reduce tamoxifen metabolism). Monitor for endometrial hyperplasia. Aromatase inhibitors (anastrozole, letrozole, exemestane) block the conversion of androgens to estrogen. Taken for 5–10 years. ⚠ Osteoporosis and fractures. ⚠ Question an order for ORAL CONTRACEPTIVES in a patient with ER+ breast cancer (case study). Trastuzumab: fatigue, joint and muscle pain, diarrhea, infusion reactions, rash, fever. Pembrolizumab: fatigue, muscle pain, itching, rash, diarrhea. Nursing, and why Dx: pain · risk for infection · altered nutrition · body image disturbance Vital signs: ↑BP and HR suggest pain or anxiety. ↑Temperature and HR suggest infection. Pain scale. Daily weight (nutrition deficit). I&O (tolerance, vomiting). Wound: redness, warmth, purulent drainage. Radiated skin checks. CBC and metabolic panel: chemo lowers WBC, Hgb, and Hct and raises LFTs. Mood and nonverbal cues: high risk of depression. Give chemo, targeted therapy, pain meds, and antiemetics. Cough and deep breathe post-op → why: prevents atelectasis and pneumonia. Keep the wound clean and dry. Encourage verbalization. Teaching: treatment course · medication and radiation side effects · wound care (report infection or dehiscence) · nutrition · support groups. Mammography screening: 40–44 optional annual · 45–54 annual · ≥55 annual or every 2 years while healthy. Breast self-exam is NOT recommended (per Ch 65 and the slides). Lifelong follow-up mammograms. Outcomes: N/V controlled, weight maintained, wound healed, minimal scarring and lymphedema, anxiety and depression managed."},{"ch":"female","chTitle":"Female Reproductive and Breast Cancers","id":"female--ovarian-cancer-most-deadly-gyn-cancer-silent","title":"Ovarian cancer · most deadly gyn cancer, \"silent\"","text":"Why it matters 5th most common cancer in females (5th cause of cancer death per the slides). 70–75% are not found until stage III or IV , which makes it the most deadly gynecologic cancer. Stage I has >73% 5-year survival; stage III is 21% and stage IV is <5%. Uncommon before 40, and most cases occur in the 70s. Advanced age is the most common risk. Risk vs protection ★ PROTECTIVE: breastfeeding, pregnancy, oral contraceptives, progesterone-only HRT. RISK: long-term estrogen or estrogen/progesterone HRT, advanced age, family history and BRCA1/2, HNPCC (Lynch), obesity and PCOS (androgens), possibly infertility and ovulation-stimulating drugs. Ovarian cancer: cancer tissue on the left ovary Clinical manifestations (vague) ★ Early (often dismissed as nothing): bloating · pelvic or abdominal pain · early satiety or trouble eating · urinary urgency or frequency. Nonspecific, not noticed until the disease has progressed. Late: ASCITES (fluid in the abdomen), so measure abdominal girth. Priority call-back: \"I have trouble eating because I feel full and bloated.\" Diagnosis NO recommended screening test for the general population. CA-125 (blood marker) rises in ovarian cancer, BUT is normal in 50% of early cases and also rises with diverticulitis, endometriosis, cirrhosis, and fibroids. Pelvic ultrasound or CT to see the ovaries. CA-125 plus ultrasound is used to screen only high-risk patients (BRCA, HNPCC). ⚠ Biopsy of the mass is NOT recommended → why: it could spread the cancer. Definitive diagnosis = surgical removal of the mass plus pathology. FIGO staging is based on surgical findings: I = ovaries · II = pelvis · III = peritoneum and/or nodes · IV = distant. Treatment Surgical staging and DEBULKING (remove as much tumor as possible) plus peritoneal washings, biopsies, omentectomy, lymphadenectomy, and usually hysterectomy with both ovaries and tubes. Fertility-sparing surgery only in rare early cases. Chemotherapy AFTER debulking is the standard of care. Given IV, oral, or intraperitoneal. Neoadjuvant chemo is used in select patients. Nursing Dx: ineffective coping · acute pain (surgery) · altered nutrition Assess: symptoms, vital signs, abdominal girth (ascites) , weight (loss = nutrition; gain = fluid), CA-125 and imaging, chemo side effects (N/V/D, constipation, hair loss, ↓WBC and RBC), anxiety and depression. Actions: pain meds, chemo, antiemetics (for nutrition). Teach ALL female patients the symptoms. Ease chemo side effects. Support groups and counselors. Outcomes: nausea controlled, weight maintained, body image addressed, end-of-life issues addressed."},{"ch":"female","chTitle":"Female Reproductive and Breast Cancers","id":"female--uterine-endometrial-cancer-most-common-gyn-cancer-in-the-us","title":"Uterine (endometrial) cancer · most common gyn cancer in the US","text":"Why it matters and risk The most common gynecologic cancer in the US , with a 2.6% lifetime risk. Postmenopausal, ages 55–65 (median 60). Rare under 45. 2–5% are hereditary (HNPCC). More common and more fatal in Black women. #1 risk factors: UNOPPOSED ESTROGEN and OBESITY. Unopposed estrogen means estrogen therapy without progesterone, tamoxifen, obesity, cirrhosis, estrogen-producing tumors, or anovulation. (Ch 13 adds early menarche, late menopause, PCOS, infertility, and diabetes.) Patho Usually arises in the endometrium. ~80% are endometrioid adenocarcinoma (type I): estrogen-driven, usually early, 88% survival. Type II (serous or clear cell) is aggressive and occurs in older patients with a poor prognosis. Sarcoma (muscle) is 3–5%. Metastatic or recurrent disease is incurable (about 1-year median survival). Uterine cancer: endometrioid adenocarcinoma (lining) vs uterine sarcoma (muscle) Clinical manifestations ★ ABNORMAL UTERINE / VAGINAL BLEEDING is the MOST COMMON symptom. Any postmenopausal bleeding needs evaluation. Premenopausal irregular or heavy bleeding with risk factors, or that doesn't respond to treatment. Ch 13 also lists lower abdominal pain and cramping. Most concerning call from a 60-year-old: \"I have vaginal bleeding.\" Diagnosis Endometrial biopsy: 99% sensitive for high-grade cancer. Pelvic (transvaginal) ultrasound: shows a thickened endometrium. D&C (scrape the lining) if the biopsy is negative but bleeding continues. Treatment Surgery: remove the uterus, fallopian tubes, and ovaries, plus washings and lymphadenectomy. Early stage I is often cured by surgery alone. Progestin therapy for stage I if surgery isn't possible. Radiation is the usual adjuvant. Chemo for high-risk or higher-stage disease, with only a modest response. Nursing Dx: acute pain (surgery) · nausea (chemo) · ineffective coping Assess: vital signs, daily weight, I&O, pain, abnormal bleeding, age 55–65. Actions: pain meds, chemo, comfort (antiemetics, salt-water rinses for stomatitis). Teach prevention: healthy weight and exercise. Anyone on estrogen who has a uterus needs progesterone too. Tamoxifen users need monitoring for endometrial hyperplasia. For stomatitis, avoid hot or spicy food."},{"ch":"female","chTitle":"Female Reproductive and Breast Cancers","id":"female--cervical-cancer-almost-completely-preventable","title":"Cervical cancer · almost completely preventable","text":"Why it matters and risk Rates have dropped since the Pap smear (mid-20th century) and HPV screening. Most often diagnosed at ages 35–44 (average 50). Rare under 20. More common in rural areas. 90% of new cases and deaths are in low- and middle-income countries. It is the 4th most common cause of cancer death in females worldwide. HPV (high-risk subtypes) is THE CAUSE. Nearly 100% of cervical cancers test positive. HPV risks: early sexual activity, multiple partners, low socioeconomic status, immunosuppression. Contributors: HSV-2, smoking, douching, poor nutrition, long-term oral contraceptive use. HPV vaccine at age 11–12 (can start at 9). It does not treat an existing infection. Patho A slow-developing disease: cervical dysplasia (low grade, then high grade) becomes invasive only if untreated. Catching dysplasia early prevents cancer. 90% are squamous cell carcinomas , arising in the transformation zone where the endocervix meets the ectocervix. Most of the rest are adenocarcinomas from the glandular cells of the endocervix. Clinical manifestations ★ Dysplasia has NO symptoms and usually can't be seen. Vaginal bleeding is the most common symptom of invasive cancer. Postcoital bleeding (especially). Ch 13 adds vaginal discomfort, malodorous discharge, and pain with intercourse. Women who are not sexually active may have no symptoms until very advanced. Screening and diagnosis ★ (ACOG / ASCCP / SGO) No screening before age 21. First Pap is at age 21. Ages 21–29: Pap every 3 years. HPV testing is not a screen in this age group, only a reflex test if atypical cells are found. Primary HPV screening may start at 25 and repeat every 5 years. (ACS prefers starting at 25 with primary HPV.) Ages 30–65: primary HPV or Pap plus HPV cotest every 5 years, OR Pap alone every 3 years. HIV, immunosuppression, DES exposure, or prior dysplasia may need more frequent screening. Hysterectomy for a benign cause with no CIN 2 history: stop. Over 65 with normal results: may stop. Abnormal result plus high-risk HPV leads to colposcopy with biopsy (acetic acid or Lugol's). Biopsy is diagnostic. Before treatment: MRI (best for tumor volume), PET (distant mets), CT, chest x-ray, IV pyelogram. FIGO staging I–IV. Colposcopy: the scope looks through a speculum at the cervix, which has been swabbed with acetic acid, and abnormal areas are biopsied Treatment depends on stage Early: cone biopsy, or radical trachelectomy (removes the cervix only) for a patient who wants to keep her fertility. If fertility isn't desired: simple or radical hysterectomy with lymph node dissection. Chemoradiation for higher stages: chemo makes the cells more sensitive to radiation. Radiation is usually brachytherapy (internal, placed in or next to the tumor) ± external pelvic beam. Recurrence confined to the pelvis: pelvic exenteration (all pelvic organs removed) can be curative. Mortality about 5% and morbidity about 70%, leaving a colostomy and urinary diversion. Already radiated? Then chemo is the only option. 5-year survival: localized 92%, regional 58%, distant 17%. Nursing Dx: ineffective health maintenance (no Pap screening) · ineffective coping · acute pain · nausea (chemoradiation) Focus = PREVENTION (screening plus HPV vaccine) because there are no symptoms until it's advanced. Assess: vital signs, Pap and HPV results, I&O, daily weight, pain. Actions: refer for colposcopy and biopsy, give meds and antiemetics, support and resources. Teach: routine screening, HPV vaccination for eligible patients, support groups. Transgender men still need breast, Pap, and pelvic screening."},{"ch":"female","chTitle":"Female Reproductive and Breast Cancers","id":"female--female-cancers-tell-them-apart","title":"Female cancers: tell them apart ★","text":"Breast Ovarian Uterine / endometrial Cervical Typical age Risk rises with age (Black women <50 at higher risk) 70s (rare <40) 55–65, postmenopausal 35–44 Biggest risk Age. More ovulatory cycles, BRCA, no live birth before 30 BRCA, family history, HNPCC, HRT, age Unopposed estrogen and OBESITY (tamoxifen too) HPV Protective Live birth Pregnancy, breastfeeding, OCPs Progesterone with estrogen, healthy weight HPV vaccine, screening Key symptom Hard, irregular, painless, fixed lump, dimpling, nipple inversion or discharge Vague: bloating, early satiety, pelvic pain, urinary frequency, late ascites Abnormal or postmenopausal bleeding Often none. Vaginal and postcoital bleeding Screening Mammogram (BSE not recommended) NONE (CA-125 plus ultrasound only if high risk) None. Counsel at menopause to report bleeding Pap ± HPV from 21 Definitive dx Biopsy. Sentinel node for staging Surgical removal plus pathology (no biopsy) Endometrial biopsy, ultrasound, D&C Colposcopy plus biopsy Main treatment Lumpectomy plus radiation, or mastectomy. Chemo, targeted therapy, hormones Debulking, then chemo Remove the uterus, tubes, and ovaries, then radiation ± chemo Trachelectomy or hysterectomy, chemoradiation (brachytherapy) Signature watch Lymphedema, shoulder immobility, radiation skin Ascites (girth), chemo toxicity, end of life Bleeding, nutrition Prevention, nausea"},{"ch":"female","chTitle":"Female Reproductive and Breast Cancers","id":"female--more-from-your-first-study-guide","title":"More from your first study guide","text":"Table 65.3 · medications for breast cancer and female reproductive disorders ★ (exam: medications) Class Examples Key nursing points SERM (endocrine) Tamoxifen (Nolvadex) Blocks estrogen receptor; 5–10 yrs. Common: hot flashes, night sweats, irregular bleeding, vaginal dryness, ↓libido. Rare: DVT/PE, stroke, uterine cancer, cataracts. Avoid SSRIs (reduce tamoxifen metabolism) Aromatase inhibitor Anastrozole, exemestane, letrozole Block androgen → estrogen conversion; 5–10 yrs. Osteoporosis, fractures Chemotherapy Doxorubicin, paclitaxel, docetaxel, 5-FU, cyclophosphamide, carboplatin Hair loss, N/V, mouth sores, ↓WBC (infection), neuropathy, premature ovarian failure Immunotherapy Pembrolizumab (Keytruda) Given with chemo before surgery. Fatigue, rash, diarrhea, thyroid problems Targeted Trastuzumab (Herceptin) For HER2+. Fatigue, joint pain, diarrhea, fever; cardiotoxicity Combined OCPs Sprintec, Yaz, patch, NuvaRing ↑DVT risk. Avoid with estrogen-positive breast cancer Progestin injection Depo-Provera IM every 3 months; weight gain Levonorgestrel IUD Mirena, Kyleena Bleeding changes; rare perforation GnRH agonist Leuprolide (Lupron) Hot flashes, ↓bone density Progestins / estrogens Provera / Premarin Estrogen without progestin (intact uterus) ↑ endometrial cancer Case: Karen Sims (estrogen-positive). Which order to verify? B. Oral contraceptives (estrogen could fuel the tumor). Male breast cancer meds: tamoxifen, aromatase inhibitors, fulvestrant only if receptor-positive; trastuzumab/pertuzumab for HER2+. Breast cancer: treatment by stage Stage Treatment plan LCIS No treatment; surveillance DCIS Surgery (>4 cm → mastectomy); antiestrogen ↓ recurrence Early invasive Excision → radiation or chemo; endocrine therapy (tamoxifen) if hormone + Intermediate/advanced (operable) Neoadjuvant chemo → surgery → radiation; targeted; endocrine 5–10 yrs Locally advanced/inoperable Neoadjuvant chemo → modified radical mastectomy → post-op chemo + radiation Recurrence/metastatic Mastectomy, excision, radiation, chemo; weigh benefit vs toxicity Skin changes include peau d'orange . Classic cancerous lump: hard, irregular, painless, immovable (fixed). Modified radical mastectomy = all breast tissue + axillary nodes; chest muscle stays. Lumpectomy = tumor + margin; always followed by radiation (chemo first if indicated). Most common long-term complication = immobility of the shoulder (\"frozen shoulder\") (purple note). Radiation effects: red, itchy, irritated skin; patchy desquamation; breast swelling; pneumonitis (dry cough) 6 weeks–6 months later. Slide wording: Genetic carriers have a 50–80% lifetime risk. Breast cancer \"most commonly impacts soft tissue such as abdomen and lung\" (your notes). Epidemiology: the single risk factor for breast cancer is a woman's age. 2.3 million new cases globally (2022); 321,910 invasive and 60,730 in-situ cases in the US; 2,670 in men. Cervical screening guidelines (exam topic) ★ Age Screening recommendation <21 No screening 21–29 Pap every 3 years (HPV test only reflexively if atypical cells) 25+ (if available) Primary HPV screening every 5 years (ACS prefers starting at 25) 30–65 Primary HPV, or Pap + HPV cotest every 5 years, or Pap alone every 3 years >65 May stop if adequate negative screening and no CIN 2 in 20 years Hysterectomy (benign) No more Paps if no history of CIN 2 High risk HIV, immunosuppression, DES exposure, prior dysplasia → more often Case: a 25-year-old, last Pap at 21, no primary HPV available. Today: breast exam, pelvic exam, Pap smear (no HPV cotest under 30). Diagnosis path: Pap → colposcopy + cervical biopsy (diagnostic) → MRI, PET, CT, CXR. Surgical: radical trachelectomy (early stage, wants fertility) · radical hysterectomy + node dissection (no fertility desire) · pelvic exenteration (recurrence). Medical: chemoradiation (brachytherapy = internal radiation into/next to tumor) · chemotherapy. HPV vaccine at 11–12, can start at 9. Four female cancers: key fact row Breast: 2nd cancer death in women · Ovarian: deadliest gyn · Uterine: most common gyn · Cervical: most preventable. Typical ages: breast ↑ with age · ovarian 70s · uterine 55–65 · cervical 35–44. Treatment summary: breast surgery ± chemo, radiation, endocrine · ovarian debulking → chemo · uterine hysterectomy ± radiation/chemo · cervical surgery or chemoradiation. Ovarian and uterine extras Ovarian: history of breastfeeding and OC use is protective; progesterone-only HRT. Risk with long-term estrogen/progesterone HRT, advanced age (mostly 70s), BRCA1/2, hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer. Protective = anything that stops ovulation. Surgical: debulking, removal of uterus, ovaries, and tubes. Uterine: 2.6% lifetime risk in developed nations; 2–5% hereditary. Obesity: fat makes estrogen. Teach prevention: obesity and unopposed estrogen are the most common risk factors. Also early menarche/late menopause, PCOS, DM."},{"ch":"female","chTitle":"Female Reproductive and Breast Cancers","id":"female--chapter-65-powerpoint-word-for-word","title":"Chapter 65 PowerPoint, word for word","text":"Your instructor's slides for this chapter, kept in slide order. Slide Slide 1 · WHO gets breast cancer Learning Objectives: 1- Describe the epidemiology of reproductive disorders · 2- Correlating clinical manifestations to pathophysiological processes of: a. breast cancer b. ovarian cancer c. uterine cancer d. cervical cancer · 3. Describe the diagnostic results used to confirm the diagnosis of reproductive disorders · 4. Discuss the interprofessional management · 5. Develop a comprehensive plan of nursing care · 6. Design a teaching plan that includes pharmacological, dietary and lifestyle considerations for patients with reproductive disorders Epidemiology: Second most cause of cancer-related deaths in women · In 2022 – est – 2.3 million new cases diagnosed globally · In US statistics est 321,910 new invasive breast ca cases · Estimated 60,730 new in-situ cases · In men, there are 2,670 est new cases · The single risk factor for breast cancer is woman's age · Black population, younger than 50 has higher risk of developing breast cancer Risk Factors: Higher number of ovulatory cycles in a lifetime · Early menarche and late menopause – 30 to 50% · Lack of pregnancy and live childbirth – 2x the risk · Family Hx with known genetic mutations (BRCA1, BRCA2) Breast Cancer gene – 5 to 10% · Genetic carriers have a 50–80% lifetime risk · Other factors: excessive alcohol use, inactivity, obesity and the use of hormones Slide Slide 2 · How it grows and is staged Pathophysiology: Cells grow and divide abnormally and rapidly · Can invade nearby tissues and spread to other areas through the bloodstream and lymphatic system · Can be categorized or staged according to size, spread to lymph nodes and distant spread to other organs or tissues Breast Cancer Staging: Stage 0 – in situ; no lymph node; no metastasis · Stage IA – tumor size 2 cm; no lymph node; no metastasis · Stage IB – no tumor or tumor size < 2 cm; +lymph node is < 2 mm; no metastasis · Stage IIA – no tumor; or tumor is < 2 cm; or tumor is 2–5 cm; +lymph node > 2 mm; no metastasis · Stage IIB – tumor is 2–5 cm; +lymph node; no metastasis · Stage IIIA – tumor is > 5 cm or tumor of any size; no lymph node for 5 cm but has +4–8 lymph nodes for tumor of any size; no metastasis · Stage IIIB – tumor is > 5 cm or tumor of any size that spread to chest wall; +9 lymph nodes; no metastasis · Stage IIIC – tumor of any size and spread to the chest wall; +10 or more lymph nodes; no metastasis · Stage IV – tumor of any size and has lymph nodes and has metastasized [Image: Four Stages of Breast Cancer] Four breast drawings. Stage I: small tumor (≤ 2 cm), no node spread. Stage II: larger tumor, may reach the axillary nodes. Stage III: large tumor spreading to more nodes, chest wall, or skin. Stage IV: any size, spread to distant organs. Slide Slide 3 · Signs and how it's diagnosed Clinical Manifestations: New mass or lump – cancerous masses are typically hard, irregular and painless, maybe round, soft and tender · Changes in breast shape or swelling · Skin changes – peeling, flaking, pitting, dimpling or redness · Nipple change – inversion, thickening of the tissue or drainage Medical and Surgical Management – Diagnosis: Mammography · Ultrasonography · Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) · Mammography with MRI – high risk · Biopsy – definitive results for Dx · Sentinel node biopsy – determine the stage · Labs – CBC, platelets, LFTs, CXR · Bone scan & liver scan – skeletal symptoms · CT abdomen & bone scan – for stage II or higher Slide Slide 4 · Surgery, therapies & treatment by stage [Image: Breast Cancer Surgery] (A) Lumpectomy: only the tumor and a margin of surrounding tissue are removed; the breast stays. (B) Modified radical mastectomy: all breast tissue is removed along with the axillary lymph nodes; the chest muscle stays. [Image: Mastectomy] Photo of a healed chest after bilateral mastectomy, with flat horizontal scars where the breasts were. Treatment – depends on the stage of the cancer: Surgery (lumpectomy or mastectomy) – Neoadjuvant – before · Chemotherapy – cytotoxic drugs · Radiation – high energy x-rays or particles · Targeted therapy – target genes, proteins or tissue · Immunotherapy – stimulates own body's immune system · Endocrine therapy – blocks the hormones that stimulate cancer growth · Adjuvant therapy Treatment: Lobular carcinoma in situ (LCIS) – no treatment · Ductal carcinoma in situ (DCIS) – requires surgery · Early stage invasive ca – surgical excision f/b radiation or chemotherapy; endocrine therapy – tamoxifen · Intermediate stage and advanced stage (operable) – first line of treatment – neoadjuvant chemo f/b radiation; targeted therapy; endocrine therapy – 5 to 10 years · Locally advanced and inoperable ca – neoadjuvant chemo then modified radical mastectomy – post-op chemo & radiation · Local recurrence and systemic metastases – complete mastectomy for those who have undergone conservation therapy, surgical excision, radiation, chemotherapy, or therapeutic benefit has to be weighed Slide Slide 5 · After surgery & treatment Complications – Immediate post op: Infection · Wound dehiscence · Pulmonary embolism · Pneumonia · Pain · Scarring · Lymphedema – removal of axillary node · Shoulder immobility – most common LT effect Slide Slide 6 · Drugs for female reproductive disorders Table 65.3 – Medications: Selective estrogen receptor modulator = endo therapy · Aromatase inhibitor · Chemotherapy · Immunotherapy · Targeted therapy · Combined oral contraceptive pills (OCPs) · Progesterone contraceptive injectable – Progestin · Levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine devices · Gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) · Progestins · Estrogens Slide Slide 7 · Assess, act, teach, evaluate Assessment and Analysis – Initial manifestations: Presence of a tumor – lump in the breast · Skin and nipple changes. Later manifestations: Chemotherapy side effects · Lymph node removal – lymphedema · Immunotherapy – skin rashes, flu-like symptoms, diarrhea · Endocrine therapy – hot flashes, vaginal dryness · Radiation – skin irritation, redness, itchiness, burning · Breast swelling and non-productive cough Nursing Diagnosis: Pain/discomfort · Risk for infection · Nutrition, altered: less than body requirements · Body image disturbance Assessments: Vital signs – elevated BP & HR, T? · Pain · Daily weight – nutritional deficit? · Intake and output · Wound evaluation – early signs of infection? · Monitor laboratory data – CBC & metabolic profile · Assess mood and nonverbal cues – great risk for psychological issues – depression Actions: Give chemotherapeutic drugs & targeted therapy as ordered · Give pain meds · Give antiemetics · Cough and deep breathe post-op · Wound care · Encourage verbalization Teaching: Explain treatment course · Medications and treatment side effects · Wound care · Nutritional counseling · Support groups · Breast cancer screening with mammography · Self-breast examination is not recommended Evaluating care outcomes: Manage nausea and vomiting · Maintain nutrition and weight · Surgical site healing · Minimum scarring and lymphedema · Managed anxiety and depression Slide Slide 8 · The silent, deadliest gyn cancer Epidemiology: 5th cancer cause of death in women · 70%–75% not discovered until late stages III & IV · Hx of breastfeeding, oral contraceptive use has protective effect · Use of progesterone alone as hormone replacement Risk Factors: Protective effect: breastfeeding, pregnancy, oral contraceptives · Hormonal replacement therapy – LT use of progesterone/estrogen increases the risk · Advanced age – mostly in 70's · Family Hx – BRCA1 or BRCA2 gene mutations · Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer Pathophysiology: Cells divide abnormally and uncontrollably [Image: Ovarian Cancer] Uterus with fallopian tubes: the right ovary is normal; the left ovary is enlarged and filled with cancerous tissue. Clinical Manifestations – Early stage: Bloating · Pelvic or abdominal pain · Early satiety or problems eating · Urinary urgency or frequency. Later stage: Ascites, excessive fluid in the abdominal cavity Medical Management – No recommended screening test for Ovarian Ca. If suspected: Diagnosis · Biochemical marker CA 125 – blood test – CA 125 levels are elevated (50% in early cases are normal) · Pelvic ultrasonography – visualizes ovaries (biopsy not recommended at this time) · CT scan · Surgical removal of mass and biopsy – definitive Dx Medications: Chemotherapy follows surgical debulking – standard of care Surgical Management: Surgical debulking · Removal of uterus, ovaries, and fallopian tubes Slide Slide 9 · Assess, act, teach, evaluate Assessment and analysis: Clinical signs and symptoms are nonspecific and ovarian cancer is usually not found until the disease is advanced Nursing diagnoses: Ineffective coping · Acute pain · Altered nutrition, less than body requirements Assessments: Signs/symptoms · Vital signs · Abdominal girth – later stage – ascites · Weight · Diagnostic tests · Chemotherapy side effects Actions: Give pain medications as ordered · Give chemotherapeutic agents as ordered · Give antiemetics as ordered Teaching: Signs/symptoms of ovarian cancer · Methods to ease chemotherapy side effects · Support groups or counselors Evaluating care outcomes: Nausea under control · Adequate nutrition to maintain weight · Body image addressed · End of life issues addressed Slide Slide 10 · The most common gyn cancer Epidemiology: 2.6% lifetime risk for women in developed nations · Uterine ca or endometrial ca is the most common gyne ca in the US · Occurs in postmenopausal women – ages 55–65 · 2–5% are hereditary gene Risk Factors: Unopposed estrogen exposure – result of estrogen therapy in the absence of progesterone · Obesity Pathophysiology: Cells of the body grow and divide abnormally and uncontrollably · Cells can invade nearby tissues and spread to other areas through blood and lymph [Image: Uterine Cancer] Uterus cross-section showing two types: endometrioid adenocarcinoma growing from the inner lining (endometrium) and uterine sarcoma growing in the muscular wall (myometrium). Clinical Manifestations: Vaginal bleeding – most common symptom Medical Management – Diagnosis: Biopsy · Pelvic ultrasound · Surgical dilation and curettage, scraping the lining of the uterus for tissue sample Medical Management – Treatment: Surgical removal of uterus, fallopian tubes, and ovaries · Radiation · Chemotherapy Slide Slide 11 · Assess, act, teach, evaluate Assessment and analysis: Identification of at-risk patient (age 55–65) and diagnosis imperative to further assessment · Pelvic ultrasound · Endometrial biopsy · Presentation – abnormal uterine bleeding Nursing diagnoses: Acute pain associated to surgical procedure · Nausea associated with chemotherapy · Ineffective coping associated with the cancer diagnosis Assessments: Vital signs · Daily weight · Intake and output · Pain · Abnormal uterine bleeding – most common symptom · Patient age – 55–65 years of age Actions: Administer pain medications as ordered · Administer chemotherapy as ordered · Provide comfort measures Teaching: Educate about disease prevention – obesity & unopposed estrogen are the most common risk factors · Discuss chemotherapy comfort measures · Provide psychological support resources Evaluating care outcomes: Maintain adequate weight · Achieve most daily functions Slide Slide 12 · The preventable gyn cancer Epidemiology: Cervical cancer in women in US has declined since the introduction of the Pap smear in mid 20th century · Introduction of HPV screening in recent years · Common age diagnosed 35–44 years · Low to middle income countries account for new diagnoses and deaths – 90% · 4th most common death in females worldwide Risk Factors: Human papillomavirus (HPV) – cause · Herpes simplex virus type 2 · Smoking · Douching · Low socio-economic status · Poor nutrition · Immunosuppression · Early sexual activity · Multiple partners · Oral contraceptive use Pathophysiology: Slow-developing disease · Early cervical dysplasia or abnormal cell maturation – evolving into invasive cancer if untreated · 90% squamous cells · Cervical cancers are adenocarcinomas originating from glandular cells of endocervix Clinical Manifestations: Vaginal bleeding – most common symptom · Post coital bleeding Medical Management – Diagnosis: Preventable with routine screening (Pap smear) · 1st Pap smear age 21 · Women between 21–30 with normal Pap smears should have every 3 years · Radiological evaluation [Image: Colposcopy] Side view of the pelvis: a speculum holds the vagina open while a colposcope (a lighted magnifier outside the body) is aimed at the cervix; bladder and uterus labeled. Medical Management – Treatment: Chemoradiation (brachytherapy) – internal radiation placed into or next to the tumor · Chemotherapy Surgical Management: Radical trachelectomy – removal of uterine & cervix only (early stage); for women who desire to maintain fertility · Radical hysterectomy – for women without desire for fertility, a radical hysterectomy with lymph node dissection is performed Slide Slide 13 · Assess, act, teach, evaluate Assessment and analysis: Focus is on prevention (Pap smear) as there are no symptoms until the disease is very advanced Nursing diagnoses: Ineffective health maintenance (lack of Pap screening) · Ineffective coping · Acute pain · Nausea Assessments: Vital signs · Pap smear results – cervical cell change · Intake and output · Daily weight · Pain scale Actions: Refer for colposcopy/biopsy · Give medications as ordered · Give antiemetics as ordered · Give support and offer resources Teaching: Educate on the need for routine screening · Eligible patients need to receive HPV vaccination · Provide information on support group and resources Evaluating care outcomes: Prevention of disease · Pain under control · Nutritional needs met · Psychological support"},{"ch":"male","chTitle":"Male Reproductive and Breast Disorders","id":"male--male-reproductive-and-breast-disorders","title":"Male Reproductive and Breast Disorders","text":"BPH · Prostate cancer · Testicular cancer · Male breast cancer · Testicular trauma. Class note Class emphasis Prostate cancer is worth about 5 exam questions (per your notes). Class added finasteride safety, brachytherapy PPE, and drains."},{"ch":"male","chTitle":"Male Reproductive and Breast Disorders","id":"male--benign-prostatic-hyperplasia-bph-not-cancer","title":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) · NOT cancer","text":"Why it matters Affects 50% of men aged 51–60 and 90% of men over 80 , across all races. BPH is NOT cancer and is not a precursor to it , although a man can have BPH and prostate cancer at the same time. Patho The walnut-sized prostate surrounds the urethra and starts growing around age 25. Theories: an estrogen/testosterone imbalance, buildup of DHT (dihydrotestosterone) , or a diet high in calories, protein, calcium, and polyunsaturated fat. The enlarged gland pinches the urethra and OBSTRUCTS urine flow. Normal vs enlarged prostate: the enlarged gland narrows the urethra, causing a weak stream and urine retention Clinical manifestations (LUTS) ★ Obstructive: difficulty STARTING the stream (even when straining), hesitancy · weak stream · multiple interruptions while urinating · dribbling at the end. Bladder changes: urgency, frequency, NOCTURIA (expected finding), the feeling of incomplete emptying, then incontinence and bed-wetting. Bladder outlet obstruction: acute urinary retention, UTI (stasis), bladder stones, bladder damage, hydronephrosis, postrenal AKI, pyelonephritis. Diagnosis, and why Based on symptoms, confirmed by DRE (an enlarged prostate can be felt). Urinalysis → why: rule out infection as the cause. PSA: ⚠ PSA can't tell BPH from cancer, because infection and enlargement also raise it. Bladder scan for post-void residual (PVR) → why: shows whether he can empty his bladder. Treatment (stepwise) Watchful waiting / active surveillance for minimal symptoms and minimal enlargement: a yearly exam with DRE. ⚠ Avoid tranquilizers and DECONGESTANTS (OTC cold meds) , which worsen obstruction (they cause constriction and increase urinary retention). Limit fluids in the evening (less nocturia). Medications help 35–40% of men. Combining the two classes delays progression by up to 67%. Anticholinergic (oxybutynin) if he has an overactive bladder. Catheterization (intermittent or indwelling) if meds fail and he can't have surgery. Herbal: saw palmetto, African plum, South African star grass, Cernilton (long-term safety unknown). Surgery: TURP is the most common. A resectoscope through the urethra removes the inner prostate. ⚠ Bleeding and infection. Catheter for 3–5 days. Light activity only. TUIP: 2 small cuts, nothing removed (for a small to moderate gland). Open prostatectomy for a very large gland. ⚠ Higher risk of incontinence, impotence, and retrograde ejaculation. Laser: immediate relief and less bleeding, so it works for patients on anticoagulants. TUMT (microwave), TUNA (radio waves, outpatient, good when bleeding is a concern), stents (UTIs and pain, not long-term), water thermotherapy, ethanol ablation. BPH treatment flowchart: mild symptoms that don't interfere with daily life → watchful waiting. Moderate to severe → meds, surgery, or other treatment. Surgery is usually indicated for retention, kidney damage, overflow incontinence, bladder stones, recurrent UTIs, or severe hematuria TURP before and after: the inner prostate is removed and only the outer layer is left BPH medications ★ (know how they work and what to watch for) Class How it works Onset ⚠ Adverse effects / teaching Alpha-adrenergic blockers : tamsulosin ( Flomax ), terazosin, doxazosin, alfuzosin, silodosin RELAX the smooth muscle of the prostate and bladder neck , so the urethra is squeezed less and urine flows more easily. (It does NOT dilate the urethra or lower PSA.) 2 weeks to 4 months Orthostatic/postural hypotension (rise slowly, fall risk), dizziness, drowsiness, headache, nasal congestion, reflex tachycardia, retrograde or delayed ejaculation, sexual dysfunction 5-alpha reductase inhibitors (5-ARIs) : finasteride ( Proscar ), dutasteride (Avodart) Block testosterone → DHT, so the prostate SHRINKS. Antiandrogens but don't change circulating testosterone. Cut acute retention by 57–59% and surgery by 36–55% 3–6 months ↓libido, ED, ↓ejaculate volume, breast enlargement or tenderness (gynecomastia), rash, orthostatic hypotension, blurred vision. May reduce male-pattern baldness (\"hair pill\"). Finasteride is teratogenic: a pregnant person should not handle crushed or broken tablets (class only) Class note Class vs book Your notes say Flomax \"dilates the urethra.\" The book says alpha blockers relax the smooth muscle of the prostate and bladder neck and do NOT dilate the urethra. Pick \"relaxes the prostate muscle\" if it is an option. Flomax helps relieve urinary retention and makes urine easier to expel. Nursing, and why Dx: disturbed sleep (nocturia) · risk for infection (urinary stasis) · increased fall risk Urinary symptoms → why: severity guides treatment. Temperature → why: fever points to a UTI from stasis. Focused abdominal exam → why: a distended bladder means he can't empty. Bladder scan (PVR) and urinalysis. Catheterize → why: checks PVR and relieves a distended bladder, protecting it from damage. Give 5-ARIs and/or alpha blockers. Post-op prostatectomy: manage continuous bladder irrigation (removes clots) and give stool softeners. Teaching: watchful waiting: recognize worsening symptoms and when to come back. Decrease fluids in the evening. No decongestants or tranquilizers. (\"I'm glad I can still take my decongestants\" means more teaching is needed.) Med side effects. Follow-up. Surgical options. Post-op site care. Report pain, fever, N/V → why: urine stasis from incomplete emptying can cause a UTI. Outcomes: less retention, a stronger stream, less nocturia."},{"ch":"male","chTitle":"Male Reproductive and Breast Disorders","id":"male--prostate-cancer-slow-growing-5-questions","title":"Prostate cancer · slow growing ★ (5 questions)","text":"Why it matters and risk One of the most commonly diagnosed cancers in men worldwide and the most common in US men . 1 in 8 men (about 6 in 10 men ≥65). Average age at diagnosis is 66. Rare under 40. African American men (and Caribbean men of African ancestry) have higher incidence and 2× the mortality. Risk: family history (BRCA1/2, Lynch), diet high in RED MEAT (heterocyclic amines), high CALCIUM intake, high BMI, smoking, chemicals, STIs, more than 7 multivitamins a week. Protective: vegetables, vitamin E, lycopene, selenium (oysters, tuna, whole wheat, sunflower seeds), antioxidants. Patho ★ Slow growing and asymptomatic until advanced. ⚠ Tumors grow on the PERIPHERY of the gland , so they do NOT obstruct urine flow early and go unnoticed until they cause pain. That is how a patient can have late-stage disease without ever having trouble urinating. Metastasizes to: lymph nodes, BONE , rectum, bladder (spreads cell-to-cell to the rectum, pelvic floor, lower spine, and hip). May be curable when localized and responds to treatment even when widespread. Normal prostate vs prostate cancer Gleason scoring: grade 1 is well differentiated (best prognosis, greatest chance of cure). Grade 5 is poorly differentiated (poor prognosis). Two sites are graded and added together, for a maximum of 10 Clinical manifestations ★ Early: none. When it advances, it can look like BPH: trouble urinating, weak stream. Local disease: asymptomatic, ↑PSA, weak stream, hesitancy, incomplete emptying, frequency, urgency, urge incontinence, UTI. Locally invasive: hematuria, dysuria, perineal and suprapubic pain, ED, incontinence, loin pain or anuria (ureter obstruction), renal failure, hemospermia (blood in semen), rectal symptoms (tenesmus). Bone pain with mets. Diagnosis (controversial) PSA has been the \"gold standard\" for early detection, but it is overdiagnosing and underdiagnosing, so some providers follow serial PSAs. PSA ≥4.0 ng/mL means further evaluation (starts at 4; watchful waiting below). 2.5–4.0 gets an individual risk assessment. <2.5: retest every 2 years. ≥2.5: retest yearly. No PSA value guarantees no cancer. DRE from age 50 (ACS), or 45 for African American men or a first-degree relative diagnosed before 65, or 40 with more than one early-onset relative. Requires informed, shared decision-making and life expectancy of ≥10 years. Biopsy (needle, in the office, local anesthetic) after an abnormal DRE or PSA. Then Gleason score and TNM. Genomic tests (Oncotype DX Prostate, Polaris, ProMark, Decipher) predict growth and spread. TNM stages: T1 can't be felt · T2 confined to the prostate · T3 locally extensive · T4 fixed to or invading neighboring organs (may spread). Stages of prostate cancer (TNM): T1 can't be felt · T2 confined to the prostate · T3 locally extensive · T4 fixed to or invading neighboring organs Treatment Radiation is the nonsurgical option. External beam runs 5×/week for 8 weeks. CyberKnife takes 4–5 sessions. Brachytherapy: radioactive seeds placed permanently through the perineum. Outpatient, for cancer that hasn't spread outside the prostate. Cryotherapy: liquid nitrogen freezes the prostate. Low success rate, often repeated. Ablative hormone therapy suppresses testosterone (an androgen that feeds the tumor) when cancer has spread to lymph nodes or bone. It is called neoadjuvant when used to shrink the prostate before radiation. Chemotherapy (docetaxel, cabazitaxel, mitoxantrone, estramustine) once the cancer is hormone-refractory. Radical prostatectomy removes the prostate and seminal vesicles ± pelvic nodes (open retropubic, laparoscopic or robotic, or perineal). ⚠ Almost always causes impotence. The nerve-sparing approach tries to protect the erection nerves. Watch Radiation and brachytherapy: incontinence and/or impotence. Brachytherapy safety: no sex for 2 weeks, then use a condom to protect the partner from radiation. If a client is receiving brachytherapy: minimize contact and wear proper (thicker) PPE (class only). Cryotherapy: impotence, incontinence, rectal complications. Chemo: hair loss, mouth sores, N/V/D, ↓appetite, infection, bruising, and bleeding, fatigue. Post-prostatectomy: infection (fever, increasing pain, swelling, bleeding, N/V), clots. Manage bladder irrigation and give stool softeners (no straining). Nursing Dx: risk for infection · sexual dysfunction · emotional distress Assess: risk factors · PSA ≥4 ng/mL · DRE: a large palpable prostate plus high PSA needs workup · urinary symptoms (pain comes from obstruction and bladder distention once advanced). Actions: hormone therapy and chemo · aseptic wound care · bladder irrigation · stool softeners. Teach: disease process, brachytherapy safety, chemo and radiation effects, signs of infection, prevention (limit calcium, red meat, and high-dose multivitamins; eat selenium-rich foods). Outcomes: normal sexual function, normal urination, no incontinence. BPH vs prostate cancer ★ BPH Prostate cancer What it is Benign enlargement, not cancer Malignant, slow-growing Location Squeezes the urethra (central) Grows on the periphery, so no early obstruction Early symptoms LUTS early: hesitancy, weak stream, dribbling, nocturia None early. Late: urinary symptoms plus pain, hematuria, hemospermia, ED, bone pain (mets) DRE Enlarged prostate Large, palpable, hard prostate PSA May be ↑ (can't distinguish) ≥4 needs workup. Biopsy confirms Treatment Watchful waiting, alpha blockers, 5-ARIs, TURP or laser Radiation, brachytherapy, cryotherapy, hormone ablation, chemo, radical prostatectomy"},{"ch":"male","chTitle":"Male Reproductive and Breast Disorders","id":"male--testicular-cancer-young-men-very-curable","title":"Testicular cancer · young men, very curable","text":"Why it matters and risk Not common, but increasing over the last 20 years. About 9,910 cases a year (2022). Average age 33 , a disease of young to middle-aged men. White men have 4–5× the risk. CRYPTORCHIDISM (undescended testicle) = GREATEST RISK. One or both testes fail to descend from the abdomen into the scrotum. Others: Down syndrome, Klinefelter's, HIV, family history, cancer in the other testicle, smoking, diet, age. Excellent prognosis: 90–100% cure if found early. Patho Seminoma: slow-growing, men in their 30s–40s, very sensitive to RADIATION . Nonseminoma (embryonal, teratoma, and others): teens to 40s, grows and spreads faster, NOT responsive to radiation . Spreads through (1) the testicular wall into the blood supply or sperm ducts, (2) lymph nodes to the back of the abdomen (most common) , or (3) the bloodstream to the brain, lung, and bone. Testicular mass in the testis (vas deferens and urethra labeled) Clinical manifestations ★ PAINLESS mass (the classic sign). Some have a dull abdominal ache or a dull ache in the scrotum. A firm or HEAVY feeling in the scrotum. Swelling and redness. Mets: GI symptoms, low back pain, gynecomastia, dyspnea, cough, hemoptysis, a neck mass. Diagnosis Physical exam. Self-exam may find it first and is best done in a warm shower . Ultrasound separates cancer from infection, fluid, torsion, or hernia. Tumor markers: ALPHA-FETOPROTEIN (AFP) and hCG. They should fall after the tumor is removed. If they don't, look for more tumors. Staging is TNM plus S (serum tumor markers) , using x-ray and CT. Treatment ALL types start with REMOVING the affected testicle (orchiectomy) and its blood supply through an abdominal incision. This is the \"best treatment\" answer. Seminoma: surgery, then radiation and/or chemo. Nonseminoma: surgery plus lymph node removal, then chemo (not radiation). Chemo: cisplatin, etoposide, bleomycin, ifosfamide, paclitaxel, vinblastine. Watch Chemo toxicity: hair loss, mouth sores, N/V/D, infection, bleeding, kidney damage (monitor creatinine, BUN, GFR), neuropathy, ototoxicity, lung damage ( bleomycin ), hemorrhagic cystitis ( ifosfamide ). Chemo can also cause ↓testosterone and ↑cardiovascular risk (angina, MI). Retroperitoneal lymph node resection (RPLND) can damage the nerves that control ejaculation. Post-op: hypotension and tachycardia mean volume loss. Fever means infection. Nursing Dx: ineffective health maintenance · risk for infection · pain. Assess lumps, swelling, redness, and vital signs. Actions: aseptic wound care, chemo. Teach: signs of infection, chemo effects, nerve damage from RPLND, BANK SPERM BEFORE TREATMENT to preserve fertility, and follow up for at least 10 years (the usual window for recurrence). Outcomes: early diagnosis, cure, successful family planning."},{"ch":"male","chTitle":"Male Reproductive and Breast Disorders","id":"male--male-breast-cancer-brca2","title":"Male breast cancer · BRCA2","text":"Why it matters and risk 100 times less common than female breast cancer (1 in 100,000). Ages 60–70. Non-Hispanic Black men have higher risk, and a first-degree relative doubles it. BRCA2 (more common) or BRCA1 mutation, family history, excess alcohol, testicular disorders, hormone imbalance (↑estrogen from liver disease or Klinefelter's), obesity, gynecomastia, heat or radiation, estrogen therapy (prostate cancer or gender transition). SLIDE vs BOOK: slide: \"2,360 cases in 2014.\" Book: \"1 in 100,000 worldwide.\" Patho and S/S Uncontrolled growth in the small amount of breast tissue behind the nipple. Because there is so little tissue, it spreads easily to the nipple, skin, and muscle, but the mass is easier to feel. Mostly ductal (DCIS, infiltrating ductal). Lobular is rare. S/S: a lump or swelling, dimpling, nipple discharge, inversion, or bleeding, erythema, skin ulceration, axillary nodes. Inflammatory type: red, warm, swollen breast. Paget's: tumor grows into the nipple surface. Diagnosis Clinical breast exam. Ultrasound if <25 years old. Mammogram if ≥25. MRI. ER/PR receptor test tells whether hormone therapy will work. The HER2 test tells whether monoclonal antibody therapy will work. Sentinel node biopsy, chest x-ray, CT, bone scan, PET for mets. Staging: 0 in situ · I <2 cm, no nodes · II up to 5 cm · III >5 cm ± nodes · IV mets. Treatment Mastectomy is usual (simple, modified radical, or radical). Lumpectomy is rarely used because there is so little tissue. Chemo (adjuvant or neoadjuvant = BEFORE surgery to help complete excision). Hormone therapy only for receptor-POSITIVE tumors: tamoxifen, aromatase inhibitors, fulvestrant. ⚠ Question antiestrogen therapy in a receptor-NEGATIVE patient. Targeted therapy (trastuzumab, pertuzumab) for HER2+: watch for hypertension, bleeding, poor wound healing, clots, kidney damage. Radiation is rarely used. Nursing Dx: ineffective health maintenance · risk for infection · emotional distress. Post-op: ↑temperature means infection, ↓BP means bleeding or hypovolemia, ↑HR means bleeding, pain, or anxiety. Check the incision (approximation, minimal serosanguineous drainage) and pain scale. Actions: meds, change the dressing (aseptic), empty the drain and record color and amount , pain meds (helps with cough, deep breathing, and ambulation). Teach: suture care, signs of infection, breast self-exam (to monitor for recurrence), follow-up, emotional support. Transgender men after top surgery may still need mammograms."},{"ch":"male","chTitle":"Male Reproductive and Breast Disorders","id":"male--testicular-trauma-ages-25-40","title":"Testicular trauma · ages 25–40","text":"Types Blunt (most common): sports injuries are the #1 cause , plus a kick to the groin, MVA, falls, straddle injuries. It can dislocate a testicle. (Class: groin injury in football = blunt trauma.) Penetrating: stab or gunshot wounds, bites. Degloving / avulsion (least common): scrotal skin is sheared off by heavy machinery or farm accidents. Rupture of the tunica albuginea causes bleeding into the tunica vaginalis (a hematocele ) or a scrotal hematoma. SLIDE vs BOOK: slide: 85% blunt / 15% penetrating. Book: 80% / 20%. Management History (mechanism of injury) and scrotal ultrasound . Blunt with no rupture: conservative. NSAIDs, scrotal support (sling or folded towels), ice packs (with a towel), bedrest 24–48 hours. Antibiotics for epididymitis or UTI. Surgical exploration for internal damage: debridement, irrigation, microsurgery. Orchiectomy is rare. Degloving: primary closure using the remaining scrotal skin. If there isn't enough skin, temporarily relocate the testis to the thigh (cooler, so sperm production continues) and reconstruct in 4–6 weeks. Reimplantation within 8 hours if amputated. ⚠ Bleeding and infection are the major risks. Antibiotics before and after surgery. Drains. Nursing Assess: bleeding, swelling, skin avulsion, pain (usually intense), temperature, wound (redness, warmth, pus). Actions: conservative care, aseptic wound care, antibiotics, monitor drain output (excess means bleeding; foul or discolored means infection) , compassion. Teach: NSAID dosing, scrotal elevation, ice with a towel, rest, drain care. Call for fever, redness, heaviness, swelling, bleeding, N/V, or worsening pain. Long term: hormone problems, infertility, impotence."},{"ch":"male","chTitle":"Male Reproductive and Breast Disorders","id":"male--jp-drain-and-hemovac-what-to-know-class-only","title":"JP drain and Hemovac: what to know *(class only)*","text":"Both are closed-suction surgical drains that pull fluid from the wound to prevent hematoma and infection. JP (Jackson-Pratt) = small bulb. Hemovac = larger round, spring-loaded canister for more drainage. Suction only works when the device is compressed: empty it, squeeze the bulb or press the canister flat, then close the plug. Empty when half to two-thirds full (or per order) and record the amount, color, and consistency on I&O. Expected: bloody → serosanguineous → serous. Report a sudden increase, bright red blood, or foul or cloudy drainage. Keep it below the wound, secured to the gown, with no kinks; aseptic technique; assess the insertion site."},{"ch":"male","chTitle":"Male Reproductive and Breast Disorders","id":"male--more-from-your-first-study-guide","title":"More from your first study guide","text":"BPH drug classes: alpha blockers (\"-osin\") and 5-ARIs (\"-steride\"). 5-ARIs also ↓PSA and shrink the gland over 3–6 months. Anticholinergic: oxybutynin for overactive bladder. Screening talk starts at: 50 (average risk; DRE age 50 per ACS) · 45 (African American or first-degree relative diagnosed <65) · 40 (more than one first-degree relative at an early age). PSA ≥4 → further assessment (your notes: starts at 4 for watchful waiting); <2.5 retest every 2 years. Prostate post-op complications ★ (exam): impotence/ED (most expected after radical prostatectomy) · urinary incontinence · infection (fever, ↑pain, swelling, redness, drainage, N/V) · bleeding/hemorrhage and clots in urine ( bladder irrigation clears clots ) · retrograde ejaculation (TURP/open prostatectomy) · rectal complications (cryotherapy) · sexual dysfunction and emotional distress (nursing dx). Also standard after prostatectomy/TURP: continuous bladder irrigation keeps urine light pink, catheter care/traction, bladder spasms (antispasmodics), Kegel exercises for incontinence, early ambulation for DVT prevention. Prostate cancer: good 5-year survival; PSA is the \"preferred standard\" test. Brachytherapy: minimize contact and wear thicker PPE; no sex 2 weeks, then condoms. Chemo when hormone-refractory (docetaxel). Testicular: average age 33 (20–34 per Table 13.2). Cure rate 90–100% if early; 95% overall, 80% with mets. Chemo: cisplatin, etoposide, bleomycin (watch kidneys, lungs, hearing). Teach testicular self-exam in a warm shower. Male breast cancer meds: tamoxifen in men: ED, headaches, hot flashes. Neoadjuvant = before (shrink); adjuvant = after (insurance vs recurrence). Slide wording, Ch 66: testicular nursing diagnoses: ineffective health maintenance, risk for infection. Cryptorchidism = incomplete descent of one or both testes from the abdomen into the inguinal canal. Case: Scott Andrews, 65 (BPH) Difficulty urinating, pain with urination, occasional blood-tinged urine , urgency, nocturia 3–4 times a night , occasional incontinence. Plan: PSA, urinalysis, DRE. DRE result, PSA <2 ng/mL , normal urinalysis → best intervention is watchful waiting. If symptoms worsen: 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors or alpha-adrenergic blockers. Nurse teaches watchful waiting and the importance of follow-up. Answers: why BPH causes symptoms = obstruction of urine flow · why watchful waiting = minimal symptoms and enlargement · needs more teaching = \"Glad I can take my decongestants\" · Flomax = relaxes the prostate muscle · report pain, fever, N/V = urine stasis can cause a UTI."},{"ch":"male","chTitle":"Male Reproductive and Breast Disorders","id":"male--chapter-66-powerpoint-word-for-word","title":"Chapter 66 PowerPoint, word for word","text":"Your instructor's slides for this chapter, kept in slide order. Slide Slide 1 · What BPH is Epidemiology: 50% of men aged 51–60 · 90% of men over age 80 Pathophysiology: Enlargement of the prostate – size of a walnut [Image: Prostate] Side-by-side drawing: a normal prostate sits under the bladder with the urethra running freely through it; an enlarged prostate (BPH) bulges and squeezes the urethra, narrowing the channel urine flows through. Clinical Manifestations: Difficulty starting flow of urine · Weak urine stream · Multiple interruptions during urination · Dribbling once urination is complete · Urgency, frequency · Nocturia · Bladder outlet obstruction Slide Slide 2 · Finding it and the first-line approach Medical Management – Diagnosis: Digital rectal examination · Urinalysis · Prostate-specific antigen Medical Management – Treatment: Watchful waiting/active surveillance · Avoid tranquilizers and decongestants [Image: Treatment Options for Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia] Flowchart: patient with lower urinary tract symptoms → history, physical, DRE, urinalysis (± PSA) → AUA symptom score. Mild symptoms → watchful waiting. Moderate to severe → discuss treatment options (medications, minimally invasive therapy, or surgery). Obstructive complications (retention, recurrent UTI, stones, kidney damage) → surgery. Slide Slide 3 · Drugs & alternatives Medical Management – Medications: 5-alpha reductase inhibitors · Alpha-adrenergic blockers · Anticholinergic Slide Slide 4 · Procedures Surgical Management: TURP · Transurethral incision of prostate · Open prostatectomy · Laser surgery [Image: Transurethral Resection of the Prostate (TURP)] Before: the enlarged prostate narrows the urethra under the bladder and a resectoscope is passed up through the penis. After: the entire inner prostate is removed, leaving only the outer layer, so the channel is wide open and urine flows easily. Slide Slide 5 · Assess, act, teach, evaluate Assessment and analysis: Clinical manifestations are caused by obstruction of urine flow due to an enlarged prostate Nursing diagnoses: Disturbed sleep pattern · Risk for infection Assessments: Urinary symptoms · Temperature · Focused abdominal examination · Bladder scan · Urinalysis Actions: Check for PVR · Catheterization · Administer medications as ordered – 5 alpha reductase inhibitors to limit the production of DHT, a substance that encourages the prostate to hyperplasia; Alpha adrenergic blockers to relax smooth muscles Teaching: Watchful waiting – recognition of worsening symptoms · Decreased liquid intake in the evening · Medication therapy education · Follow-up · Surgical options · Post surgical care Evaluating care outcomes: Less urinary retention · Strong urine stream · Decreased nocturia Slide Slide 6 · Epidemiology, risk, pathophysiology, signs Epidemiology: One of the most commonly diagnosed cancers in men, globally · 5 year survival rate · Diagnosed at age 65 Risk Factors: Family history · Diet high in red meat (heterocyclic amines) · Consumption of calcium · High BMI Pathophysiology: Slow growing and asymptomatic until advanced · Metastasis to lymph nodes, bone, rectum, and bladder [Image] Normal prostate next to a prostate with cancer: the tumor sits in the outer edge of the gland, away from the urethra, so urine flow isn't blocked early. Clinical Manifestations: Trouble urinating · Weak stream of urine Slide Slide 7 · PSA, biopsy, gleason, TNM Medical Management – Diagnosis: Digital rectal exam – age 50 (ACS) · Prostate-specific antigen – gold standard · Biopsy [Image: Gleason Scoring System] Five panels of prostate tissue. Grade 1: small, uniform, well-formed glands that look nearly normal. Grades 2–4: glands become more irregular, crowded, and fused. Grade 5: sheets of cells with almost no gland structure (poorly differentiated). [Image: Stages of Prostate Cancer] Four drawings of the prostate under the bladder. T1: tiny tumor you can't feel. T2: tumor still inside the prostate. T3: tumor breaks through the capsule into surrounding tissue. T4: tumor invades neighboring organs (bladder, rectum). Slide Slide 8 · Radiation, freezing, hormones, chemo, surgery Medical Management – Treatment: Radiation – non surgical option · Brachytherapy – radioactive seeds · Cryotherapy – liquid nitrogen (freeze the prostate) · Ablative hormone therapy – used in men metastasized to lymph nodes – suppression of testosterone that promotes tumor · Chemotherapy – hormone refractory Surgical Management: Radical prostatectomy – prostate and seminal vesicles are removed – almost always results in impotence · (new) Nerve sparing prostatectomy – attempts to keep the nerves intact that control erection Slide Slide 9 Assessment and analysis: Clinical manifestations evident once disease is advanced related to obstruction of flow of urine and mass causing pain Nursing diagnoses: Risk for infection · Sexual dysfunction Assessments: Risk factors · PSA levels – 4 ng/ml requires further assessment · DRE results – large palpable prostate · Urinary symptoms Actions: Administer medications as ordered · Postoperative surgical care Teaching: Disease process · Brachytherapy · Chemotherapy/radiation · Signs of infection · Prevention Evaluating care outcomes: Normal sexual functioning · Normal urination · No incontinence Slide Slide 10 · The young man's cancer Epidemiology: Increased incidence over last 20 years · Due to environmental · Genetics · Increased risk in males with Down's syndrome Risk Factors: Cryptorchidism – greatest risk · incomplete descent of one or both testes from the abdomen into the inguinal canal resulting in absence of scrotum Pathophysiology: Begin in testicles · Spread through blood supply, tubes responsible for sperm transport, lymph system [Image] Cross-section of a testis inside the scrotum with a distinct mass growing within the testicle. Clinical Manifestations: Painless mass · Some patients have abdominal dull pain, dull ache · Feeling firm or heavy in the scrotum · The mass may also present with swelling and redness Diagnosis: Physical assessment · Ultrasonography · Laboratory results – alpha-fetoprotein and human chorionic gonadotropin Treatment: Removal of affected testicle for seminomas · Radiation · Chemotherapy Assessment and analysis: Clinical manifestations due to mass or lump in testes · Abdominal pain · Firm or fullness in the scrotum Nursing diagnoses: Ineffective health maintenance · Risk for infection Assessments: Physical assessment – lumps, redness · Vital signs – hypotension, tachycardia, increased temperature Actions: Wound care · Administer chemotherapeutic agents as ordered Teaching: Signs of infection · Chemotherapy · Retroperitoneal lymph node resection – possible nerve damage · Sperm storage – preserve potential fertility · Follow-up Evaluating care outcomes: Early diagnosis · Cure of disease · Successful family planning Slide Slide 11 · Rare, spreads easily Epidemiology: 100 times less common than female breast cancer · 2,360 cases in 2014 Risk Factors: Advanced age between 60 and 70 · Mutation BRCA2 gene or BRCA1 gene · Family history · Excessive alcohol consumption · Testicular disorders · Environmental factors · Hormonal imbalances Pathophysiology: Uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells of breast tissue · Clinical manifestation – Mass in breast tissue Diagnosis: Clinical breast examination · Ultrasonography · MRI Treatment: Surgical resection · Chemotherapy · Radiation · Targeted therapy Assessment and analysis: Clinical manifestations due to mass or lump in breast tissue – lump, swelling, dimpling, nipple discharge, inverted nipple, red warm and swollen breast Nursing diagnoses: Ineffective health maintenance · Risk for infection Assessments: Lump in breast · Dimpling or discharge from nipple · Nipple inversion · Redness · Postoperative assessment Actions: Administer medications as ordered · Change dressing · Empty drain at surgical site · Administer pain medication per order Teaching: Care of suture site · Signs and symptoms of infection · Breast self-examination · Follow-up appointment · Provide emotional support and resources Evaluating care outcomes: Good surgical site healing · Emotional healing Slide Slide 12 · Blunt, penetrating & degloving Epidemiology: 85% from blunt trauma – sports injuries, kick to the groin, MVA, straddling of an object, falls, injury (25–40 years old) · 15% from penetrating trauma Surgical Management: Primary closure using remaining scrotal skin Slide Slide 13 · Scott Andrews, 65, BPH Episode 1: Scott Andrews is a 65-year-old male patient who presents to his healthcare provider with a complaint of difficulty urinating. He complains of pain with urination and occasional blood-tinged urine. He states that he has an increased urgency to urinate and wakes up three to four times during the night to urinate. He also complains of occasional incontinence. Wrap-Up: After assessment of his symptoms and education, Scott agrees to a blood draw for PSA levels, a urinalysis, and a DRE. Based on the results of the DRE, a PSA of less than 2 ng/mL, and urinalysis within normal limits, Scott is told the best intervention at this time is \"watchful waiting.\" He is told that if his symptoms worsen, he may be prescribed medication therapy such as 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors or alpha-adrenergic blockers. The nurse provides patient education focusing on watchful waiting and the importance of future provider follow- up."},{"ch":"pain","chTitle":"Pain Management","id":"pain--pain-management","title":"Pain Management","text":"Pain is \"an unpleasant sensory and emotional experience associated with actual or potential tissue damage\" (IASP 2020), and it is whatever the patient says it is (McCaffery). More than two-thirds of cancer patients have moderate to severe pain."},{"ch":"pain","chTitle":"Pain Management","id":"pain--acute-vs-chronic","title":"Acute vs chronic","text":"Acute Chronic Time Sudden, short (hours to weeks), linked to tissue injury Lasts more than 3–6 months, may have no clear injury Purpose Protective Serves no biological purpose Feels like Sharp, easy to pinpoint Achy, dull, or burning, hard to localize. Resists treatment Examples Post-op, trauma Migraine, low back pain, neuropathy, fibromyalgia, phantom limb Gate control theory: the spinal cord \"opens or closes the gate\" to pain signals, and the brain shapes how pain is perceived. Peripheral and central sensitization amplify pain and can make it chronic (synaptic \"cellular memory\")."},{"ch":"pain","chTitle":"Pain Management","id":"pain--pain-by-origin","title":"Pain by origin","text":"Nociceptive: cutaneous (skin) and somatic (bone, joint, muscle). Visceral: hollow organs (heart, bladder, GI). Hard to localize, often referred. Autonomic signs: sweating, pallor, nausea, low HR and BP, syncope. Neuropathic: from nerve damage. Shooting, stinging, \"pins and needles\" with numb areas. Examples: postherpetic neuralgia, phantom limb, diabetic and chemo-induced neuropathy. The hardest type to treat. Older adults may show atypical pain: confusion, restlessness, poor appetite. Up to 30% have no pain with an MI."},{"ch":"pain","chTitle":"Pain Management","id":"pain--assessment-opqrst-aaa","title":"Assessment: OPQRST-AAA ★","text":"O Onset When did it start, sudden or gradual P Provocation What makes it worse (movement, turning) Q Quality Sharp, dull, burning, stabbing, crushing R Region / Radiation Point to it, does it spread S Severity 0–10 scale, effect on function T Time / duration How long, has it happened before AAA A ggravating and A lleviating factors, A ssociated symptoms (\"What makes the pain worse?\" assesses aggravating factors. ) The 0–10 scale measures intensity only , and scores of 4 or higher usually prompt intervention. Also assess function. Physical exam: palpation, ROM, neuro checks. Cognitively impaired patients: attempt self-report first, assume pain if a painful condition exists, establish a behavioral baseline (guarding, rubbing, rocking, grimacing), use PAINAD or the Checklist of Nonverbal Pain Indicators, give scheduled nonopioids, and avoid psychotropics (they blunt pain behaviors)."},{"ch":"pain","chTitle":"Pain Management","id":"pain--nonopioid-analgesics","title":"Nonopioid analgesics","text":"Acetaminophen Analgesic with few anti-inflammatory effects. Max 1,000 mg per dose and 3,000 mg in 24 hours. ⚠ Liver damage. Watch combination products (Percocet, cold medicines) and check labels. Alcohol and barbiturates increase hepatotoxicity. Monitor AST, ALT, and bilirubin. NSAIDs and aspirin Block COX and prostaglandins ( anti-inflammatory ). ⚠ COX-1 inhibition causes GI ulcers and bleeding , even short term. ⚠ Renal problems (caution in CKD and older adults). Avoid in the 3rd trimester. ⚠ Aspirin inhibits platelets , causing bleeding and thrombocytopenia. ⚠ OTC NSAIDs increase warfarin's effect (warfarin teaching). COX-2 selective (celecoxib): ⚠ MI and stroke risk (Vioxx was withdrawn). Older adults (Beers): avoid chronic NSAIDs (GI bleed risk, especially over 75 or with anticoagulants or steroids). Avoid NSAIDs in symptomatic heart failure. Ketorolac: bleeding risk with anticoagulants, renal impairment with ACE inhibitors."},{"ch":"pain","chTitle":"Pain Management","id":"pain--opioids","title":"Opioids","text":"Opioids For moderate to severe acute pain (post-op, trauma). Less effective for chronic pain and should not be started for it. For acute pain, prescribe short courses (less than 1 week). Work better combined with NSAIDs. Morphine is the gold standard. Hydromorphone is 5× morphine. Fentanyl is about 100× morphine (IV, or a transdermal patch for chronic cancer pain). Oxycodone (OxyContin) is highly addictive. ⚠ RESPIRATORY DEPRESSION is the most critical adverse effect (from mu receptors ). Monitor sedation and respirations, especially after IV doses and in the first 24 hours for opioid-naïve patients. ⚠ Constipation (mu receptors, ↓GI motility). Stool softeners and laxatives are almost always needed. More common in older adults. ⚠ Nausea and vomiting (aspiration risk post-op), urinary retention, pruritus, confusion and delirium in older adults (more common than respiratory depression), and paradoxical ↑pain in chronic use. Tolerance = needing a higher dose for the same effect. Addiction = compulsive use despite harm. They are not the same. Fewer than 5% of cancer patients become addicted. PCA: a low background dose plus patient-controlled boluses within preset limits."},{"ch":"pain","chTitle":"Pain Management","id":"pain--adjuvants-and-other-options","title":"Adjuvants and other options","text":"Corticosteroids: anti-inflammatory, used as adjuvants in cancer pain. Local anesthetics: lidocaine (onset 10 minutes, lasts 2 hours) and bupivacaine (more potent, CNS and cardiac toxicity). ⚠ Lidocaine with epinephrine is NOT used on fingers, toes, or the penis (necrosis). EMLA is applied 45–60 minutes before a needle stick. Neuropathic pain: gabapentin, pregabalin, TCAs (in older adults use nortriptyline or desipramine, and get an ECG), duloxetine (SNRI). Anticonvulsants (carbamazepine, phenytoin) can increase warfarin's effect. Ketamine (NMDA blocker) for resistant pain. Clonidine intrathecal or epidural. Baclofen. Rubefacients and capsaicin: not on radiated skin. WHO ladder (Ch 26): nonopioid → moderate opioid plus nonopioid → strong opioid plus nonopioid. Give around the clock for cancer pain. Nonpharmacologic: heat and cold, relaxation, massage, exercise, guided imagery, music, CBT, self-management education. Class note Class: slide 22 Know why we use opioids vs nonopioids. Nonopioids (acetaminophen, NSAIDs) for mild pain and anti-inflammation; opioids for moderate to severe pain; in cancer, use the WHO ladder and dose around the clock. Know your pain medications."},{"ch":"pain","chTitle":"Pain Management","id":"pain--more-from-your-first-study-guide","title":"More from your first study guide","text":"Why pain matters: most universal symptom; 1 of 3 ED chief complaints; 60% of patients report pain at primary care visits; costs $100 billion per year. Acute vs chronic (slide): acute alerts and prompts withdrawal, avoidance, predictable in course, intensity improves. Chronic: months–years, sustained or spontaneous, disrupts normal activities, unpredictable, inconsistently responsive to treatment. Chronic malignant (cancer) pain: rapidly dividing cells invade bone/tissue; <5% become addicted to opioid therapy. Breakthrough pain: bursts on top of controlled pain. Neuropathic: burning, shooting, tingling; worse at night (chemo neuropathy, phantom limb). Why two people feel the same injury differently: prior experience, expectations, anxiety, sociocultural factors, gender, genetics; older adults: mixed evidence, decrease in acute pain. Scales: numeric 0–10 · verbal best for older adults/limited vision · FACES for preverbal · PAINAD for cognitive impairment. Self-report = gold standard; score ≥4 requires a nursing response. Goal = improve comfort and function, not zero pain. Referred pain: heart → left chest, left shoulder/arm, jaw · liver and gallbladder → right shoulder/scapula, right upper abdomen · lungs and diaphragm → neck and shoulder · stomach and pancreas → epigastric (pancreas also mid-back) · small intestine, appendix, colon → around umbilicus/lower abdomen · kidneys, ureters, bladder → flank, groin, lower abdomen, low back · ovary → lower abdomen/pelvis. Medication table (slide 22: know why we use opioids vs nonopioids) Drug Key facts Nonopioids Mild–moderate pain (WHO step 1) and inflammatory pain. Kept at every step to potentiate opioids and lower opioid dose. No respiratory depression or addiction. Limits: GI bleed, kidney injury, bleeding (NSAIDs); liver (APAP) Acetaminophen Max 3,000 mg/24 h (1,000 mg/dose); liver toxicity; hidden APAP in Percocet, Vicodin, cold meds Aspirin Inhibits platelet aggregation → bleeding; ↑warfarin effect NSAIDs (ibuprofen, naproxen, ketorolac) COX-1 block → GI ulcers/bleeding; kidney injury; ↑bleeding with warfarin; can cause thrombocytopenia. Ketorolac max 5 days, 40 mg/24 h. Don't combine with steroids COX-2 (celecoxib) Spares stomach; slow onset (chronic OA); ↑MI/stroke risk Corticosteroids Anti-inflammatory; bone pain, cancer pain adjuvant. Hyperglycemia; taper Morphine Gold standard opioid Hydromorphone (Dilaudid) 5× morphine; good with kidney/liver impairment Fentanyl ~100× morphine; patch for long-term cancer pain (24–72 h to work) Oxycodone Potent, highly addictive Adjuvants Neuropathic: gabapentin, pregabalin, duloxetine, amitriptyline (Elavil); ketamine (Ketalar); lidocaine; clonidine Mu receptor = respiratory depression, miosis, euphoria, ↓GI motility. Opioids → start a bowel regimen (laxative + stool softener). Older adults: start low, go slow; avoid meperidine. Rubefacients: menthol ointments, cause local vasodilation, cool sensation. Local anesthetics topical or injected. WHO principles: analgesics at regular intervals · according to pain intensity · goal: functional quality of life · individualized dosing · evaluate response. Ladder: Step 1 mild: nonopioid (APAP, NSAIDs) ± adjuvant · Step 2 moderate: weak opioid (codeine, hydrocodone, oxycodone) + nonopioid ± adjuvant · Step 3 severe: strong opioid (morphine, fentanyl) + nonopioid ± adjuvant. Give at regular intervals, not \"on demand\"; oral first; PCA = basal + bolus with lockout. Heat/cold: cold for acute strains and back spasm (20–30 min); heat for back pain without spasm, cramps (5–15 min). Nonpharm: distraction (music, guided imagery, hypnosis, prayer), massage, positioning, TENS, relaxation, deep brain stimulation (also tx for PD tremors). Older adults: underdiagnosed, undertreated → depression, anxiety, impaired mobility, social isolation, falls, sleep disturbance, malnutrition. Cognitive impairment: self-report, cognition tests, behavior observation. Care planning: therapeutic partnership; assess pain and functional status; multidisciplinary team (PT, pharmacist, social worker); measure effectiveness (sleep length and quality)."},{"ch":"pain","chTitle":"Pain Management","id":"pain--chapter-11-powerpoint-word-for-word","title":"Chapter 11 PowerPoint, word for word","text":"Your instructor's slides for this chapter, kept in slide order. Slide Slide 1 · Why pain matters Most universal symptom · Significant public health problem 1 of 3 ED chief complaints is pain · 60% of patients report pain at primary care visits Costs $100 billion per year Slide Slide 2 · Gate control theory Two brain–spinal cord diagrams. Left: high-intensity pain goes up the ascending pathway, gate is open. Right: emotion, motivation, memory, and cognition send signals down the inhibitory descending pathway, gate is closed. Slide Slide 3 · Classifying pain by duration Acute pain – short duration, hours to weeks, sudden onset, alerts and prompts withdrawal, avoidance, predictable in course; intensity improves Chronic pain – long term, months–years, sustained or spontaneous, disrupts normal activities, unpredictable in course intensity, inconsistently responsive to both common and adjuvant pain management strategies Slide Slide 4 · Classifying pain by origin Nociceptive pain: cutaneous, somatic, visceral · Neuropathic pain Slide Slide 5 · How pain signals travel and become chronic Injury in the hand → peripheral nociceptor → ascending pathway in the spinal cord → brain perceives pain → descending pathway back down. Slide Slide 6 · Why two people feel the same injury differently Prior experience with pain · Expectations of pain · Anxiety Sociocultural determinants: values and expectations, access to healthcare resources, language barriers Gender: women have lower pain thresholds, rate noxious stimuli as more painful, have lower tolerance for intense pain; differences present after puberty; men and women respond differently to analgesia Genetics: pain threshold, pain tolerance, response to analgesics Pain and older adults: mixed evidence on prevalence and pattern of pain; decrease in acute pain Slide Slide 7 · The 0–10 scale and its cousins 0–10 scale · Measure of pain intensity Numeric rating scales · Verbal rating scales · Visual analog scales Figure: Simple Descriptive Scale, 0–10 Numeric Scale, Visual Analog Scale (VAS), and a pain thermometer Slide Slide 8 · OPQRST-AAA + physical exam OPQRST-AAA: Onset · Quality · Region and radiation · Severity · Time and duration · Aggravating/alleviating factors and associated clinical manifestations Physical exam: range of motion, neurological assessment, motor function Multidisciplinary team: physical therapist, pharmacist, social worker Slide Slide 9 · Partnership, care planning & nursing diagnoses Patient and nurse – the therapeutic partnership: assessment of pain, assessment of functional status, interviewing Care-planning process: data collection, evaluation of data, form diagnostic hypotheses Nursing diagnoses (full list below) Measuring effectiveness of care: review of laboratory values, structured questioning, length and quality of sleep Slide Slide 10 · NSAIDs, aspirin & acetaminophen Pharmacological options – nonopioid analgesics: NSAIDs Monitoring risks and benefits: prostaglandin synthesis – chemicals released that stimulate inflammation; COX-1 & COX-2 COX-1 – harmful to GI, disrupts protective lining · COX-2 – both anti-inflammatory & antipyretic Inhibit platelet aggregation · Thrombocytopenia – reduced platelet count Slide Slide 11 · Anti-inflammatory steroids Pharmacological options – corticosteroids: anti-inflammatory Slide Slide 12 · Numb the area, stay awake Local anesthetics: topically applied; injected into nerves and tissue Slide Slide 13 · Counterirritants and capsaicin Topical rubefacients: topically applied menthol-containing ointments; cause local vasodilation; produce cool sensation Slide Slide 14 · Receptors, potency & side effects Opioids: bind to opioid receptors in the brain; available via many routes Figure (continued_6): relative potency chart of opioids and NSAIDs compared with morphine, from most potent (top) to least potent (bottom) Slide Slide 15 · Adjuvants for tough pain Clonidine · Tricyclic antidepressants – Elavil · Glutamate receptor antagonist (principal excitatory neurotransmitter in CNS) – Ketalar · Ion-channel blockers – lidocaine Slide Slide 16 · Choosing the right drug and route Decision making for effective dose, route, and medication choice: best pain relief, fewest adverse effects Slide Slide 17 · WHO principles & three-step ladder WHO principles: analgesics given at regular intervals; administered according to pain intensity; goal is to improve functional quality of life; individualized dosing; attention to dose and response; evaluation of therapeutic response WHO three-step ladder (figure) Slide Slide 18 · Positioning, heat/cold, rom, massage Positioning · Heat and cold · Range of motion and exercise Slide Slide 19 · Refocusing attention Music therapy · Guided imagery · Hypnosis · Prayer Slide Slide 20 · TENS, DBS & expectancy Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation · Deep brain stimulation – lead is placed subcutaneously; also tx for PD tremors Slide Slide 21 · Older adults & cognitive impairment Older adults: underdiagnosed, undertreated, ineffective management → depression, anxiety, impaired mobility, social isolation, falls, sleep disturbance, reduced food and fluid intake, malnutrition Adults with cognitive or communication impairments: self-report, tests of cognition, observations of behavior, changes in routine behavior Slide Slides · Image-only slides (written out) [Image: Common areas of referred pain] Heart → left chest, left shoulder/arm, jaw · Liver & gallbladder → right shoulder/scapula, right upper abdomen · Lungs & diaphragm → neck and shoulder · Stomach & pancreas → epigastric (pancreas also mid-back) · Small intestine, appendix, colon → around the umbilicus/lower abdomen · Kidneys, ureters, bladder → flank, groin, lower abdomen, low back · Ovary → lower abdomen/pelvis."},{"ch":"cheat","chTitle":"Tell-Them-Apart Cheat Sheets","id":"cheat--tell-them-apart-cheat-sheets","title":"Tell-Them-Apart Cheat Sheets","text":"High-yield comparisons gathered from all the chapters."},{"ch":"cheat","chTitle":"Tell-Them-Apart Cheat Sheets","id":"cheat--tumor-markers-and-signature-labs","title":"Tumor markers and \"signature\" labs","text":"Marker / test Cancer / disorder Remember PSA Prostate (and BPH, prostatitis) ≥4 ng/mL needs workup. High sensitivity, low specificity. Can't tell BPH from cancer CA-125 Ovarian Normal in 50% of early cases. Also rises with endometriosis, fibroids, cirrhosis, diverticulitis. Not a screening test AFP + hCG Testicular Should fall after orchiectomy. If they don't, look for more tumor Bence Jones protein (urine), M protein Multiple myeloma Light chains are toxic to the kidney. CRAB criteria Reed–Sternberg cells Hodgkin's lymphoma Diagnosed by lymph node biopsy Philadelphia chromosome CML (and some B-ALL) Chromosome 9;22 swap in >90% of CML JAK2 mutation Polycythemia vera About 90%. ↑RBC, WBC, and platelets with a low EPO level ↓Ferritin, ↑TIBC, ↓MCV Iron deficiency anemia Microcytic and hypochromic ↑MMA (normal homocysteine) B₁₂ deficiency Macrocytic plus neuro signs ↑Homocysteine (normal MMA) Folate deficiency Macrocytic, no lasting neuro deficits ↑PT/PTT, ↓fibrinogen, ↑D-dimer DIC Clots everywhere, then bleeds everywhere ↑Eosinophils Allergy (type I) Normal 1–2%. Up to 12% with severe allergic rhinitis Heinz bodies, ↑retics G6PD deficiency Hemolysis"},{"ch":"cheat","chTitle":"Tell-Them-Apart Cheat Sheets","id":"cheat--must-know-numbers","title":"Must-know numbers","text":"Number Meaning ANC <1,500 Neutropenia (infection risk) ANC ≤500 Neutropenic precautions. Fever means IV antibiotics now Temp ≥100.4°F / 38°C Call the provider (chemo, neutropenia) Platelets <150,000 Thrombocytopenia <100,000 / <50,000 Start bleeding precautions / spontaneous bleeding possible, no sex <20,000 Spontaneous bleeding. Platelets usually transfused <5,000 Fatal CNS or GI bleed possible SpO₂ <90% Significant oxygenation problem. Goal ≥90% Hct <45% PV phlebotomy goal Ca >11 / >11.5 mg/dL Hypercalcemia (lymphoma / myeloma CRAB) INR 2–3 / 2.5–3.5 Warfarin goal / mechanical valve Acetaminophen 3,000 mg 24-hour max (1,000 mg per dose) Gleason 2–10 Two grades (1–5) added. Higher is worse RBC life 120 days Sickle cell RBC 15–20 days. Platelets 7–10 days"},{"ch":"cheat","chTitle":"Tell-Them-Apart Cheat Sheets","id":"cheat--precautions-side-by-side","title":"Precautions side by side","text":"Neutropenic (↓WBC) Bleeding (↓platelets) Hand hygiene, private room Soft toothbrush, no floss Avoid crowds, sick people, small kids, pets. Mask outside Electric razor, shoes on, clutter-free No fresh flowers or plants, no standing water Stool softener, don't blow your nose Wash produce. No raspberries or blackberries No IM injections. Minimal BP cuff pressure Daily temperature. Fever ≥100.4°F means call or go now Pressure or ice ≥5 minutes for cuts BOTH: no rectal temperatures, suppositories, or enemas"},{"ch":"cheat","chTitle":"Tell-Them-Apart Cheat Sheets","id":"cheat--painless-vs-painful-masses","title":"Painless vs painful masses","text":"Painless Breast cancer lump (typically), lymphoma nodes, testicular cancer, malignant lymph nodes (less tender than infected ones) Pain is the clue Myeloma (back and bone), leukemia (bone), sickle cell crisis, prostate cancer (late), spinal cord compression (back pain first)"},{"ch":"cheat","chTitle":"Tell-Them-Apart Cheat Sheets","id":"cheat--which-cancers-spread-where","title":"Which cancers spread where","text":"Cancer Common metastasis sites Breast Bone, lung, lymph nodes, liver, brain Lung Brain, bone, adrenal gland, other lung Prostate Bone, lymph nodes, rectum, bladder Ovarian Peritoneum, omentum, pleura Cervical Endometrium, vagina, pelvic wall Testicular Lymph nodes (back of the abdomen), lung, liver, bone, brain Uterine Lymph nodes, vagina, abdominal cavity, omentum"},{"ch":"cheat","chTitle":"Tell-Them-Apart Cheat Sheets","id":"cheat--lab-values-from-class-nur-291","title":"Lab values from class (NUR 291)","text":"Need to know lab numbers for exams (electrolytes, CBC). Electrolytes Electrolyte Normal Too low / too high Memory trick Sodium (Na⁺) 135–145 mEq/L <135 hyponatremia · >145 hypernatremia Commit to memory Potassium (K⁺) 3.5–5.0 mEq/L <3.5 hypokalemia · >5.0 hyperkalemia A bunch of bananas has 3–5, and you want them half ripe (.5): 3.5–5.0 Phosphorus (P) 2.5–4.5 mg/dL <2.5 hypophosphatemia · >4.5 hyperphosphatemia \"PHOR\" = 4, \"us\" = 2 (me + you); don't forget the .5 Calcium (Ca²⁺) 9–11 mg/dL <9 hypocalcemia · >11 hypercalcemia Call 911 Magnesium (Mg²⁺) 1.5–2.5 mg/dL <1.5 hypomagnesemia · >2.5 hypermagnesemia MAGnifying glass: 1.5–2.5, a little bigger than normal Chloride (Cl⁻) 95–105 mEq/L <95 hypochloremia · >105 hyperchloremia A chlorinated pool on a 95–105°F day Where these show up in this guide: Calcium: hypercalcemia from bone mets, lymphoma, and myeloma (CRAB >11.5), DiGeorge hypocalcemia (tetany), tumor lysis hypocalcemia. Potassium and phosphorus: tumor lysis syndrome raises both (peaked T waves, heart block). Sodium: SIADH from brain and lung cancer causes dilutional hyponatremia (confusion, seizures). Complete blood count (class) ★ Lab Memory hook Normal (class) WBC Wanna Buy a Car 5,000–10,000/mcL RBC Blood 4.5–5.5 million/mcL Hemoglobin Oxygen 12–18 g/dL Hematocrit Hemato-\"crisis\" 37–50% Platelets China plates 150K–400K/mcL Class note Class vs book Your class CBC sheet and the textbook (Table 33.7) use slightly different ranges. WBC: class 5,000–10,000 vs book 4,500–11,100 Platelets: class 150K–400K vs book 150K–450K Hgb: class 12–18 vs book F 11.7–15.5, M 14–17.3 Hct: class 37–50% vs book F 36–48%, M 42–52% RBC: class 4.5–5.5 million vs book F 4.01–5.51, M 4.51–6.01 Exam questions usually use values clearly outside both ranges, but ask your instructor which set the exam uses."},{"ch":"answers","chTitle":"Practice Question Answer Key","id":"answers--practice-question-answer-key","title":"Practice Question Answer Key","text":"The slides don't show answers, so these are worked out from your textbook content, each with the reason. Double-check any you're unsure of with your instructor. Click a question to reveal its answer."},{"ch":"answers","chTitle":"Practice Question Answer Key","id":"answers--chapter-13-cancer-overview","title":"Chapter 13 · Cancer overview","text":"Cancer is the ___ most common cause of death in the US. B. Second. Benign vs malignant cells? C. Malignant cells lack contact inhibition. Key lab for cancer with bone metastasis? B. Calcium (hypercalcemia). Most specific way to diagnose malignancy? D. Biopsy. Chemo before definitive surgery is? C. Neoadjuvant. Which statement shows chemo teaching worked? B. \"It is important that I monitor my temperature.\" Mrs. Jones, stage III? C. Large, locally advanced, possibly in local nodes. (Class notes said D, \"spread to lymph and blood systems.\" The book says C. Confirm with your instructor.) Oral hormonal therapy after mastectomy? B. Adjuvant. Call the provider immediately for? C. Fever (myelosuppression). Before first paclitaxel (SATA)? B, D (and C per class): emergency equipment ready (hypersensitivity), check IV blood return (irritant/vesicant), and ECG within 24 hours (class adds this; paclitaxel can affect heart rhythm). Go with B, C, D. Left chest wall radiation, which systems (SATA)? B, D, E (and C per class): integumentary (radiodermatitis), cardiac (late cardiac risk), respiratory (pneumonitis), plus musculoskeletal (shoulder stiffness and fibrosis). Go with B, C, D, E. Class PLQ 1: Regulated cell death that cancer cells lose is called? C. Apoptosis. Class PLQ 2: Stage III T3, N2, M1: which statement needs more teaching? A. \"I am relieved that the cancer is still local.\" M1 = metastasis present, so it is not local. Class PLQ 3: Leukemia patient with chemo neutropenia: include in the plan? C. Restricting visitors with communicable diseases."},{"ch":"answers","chTitle":"Practice Question Answer Key","id":"answers--chapter-26-lung-and-asthma","title":"Chapter 26 · Lung and asthma","text":"When to start palliative care in lung cancer? A. Upon diagnosis. Lung cancer plan of care (SATA)? A, B, D: education, frequent pain assessment, frequent oxygenation checks. Mr. Davis, most important part of management? B. Disease management through education. No wheezes and diminished breath sounds? C. Partially obstructed airway (not moving air). Emergency. Priority with a suspected partial obstruction? A. Airway management. Major part of his treatment program? A. Smoking cessation. Which statement needs more teaching? C. \"I can stop all my meds when my asthma is under control.\""},{"ch":"answers","chTitle":"Practice Question Answer Key","id":"answers--chapter-33-heme-assessment","title":"Chapter 33 · Heme assessment","text":"IDA manifestations (SATA)? D, E: ↑HR, ↑RR. Result that confirms IDA? A. Decreased transferrin saturation (\"always low\" in IDA). TIBC is ↑, ferritin ↓, MCV ↓ too. Compensatory finding in IDA? A. Increased respiratory rate. Mouth finding in IDA? C. Red, swollen, tender tongue (glossitis). Serum iron instructions? B. Drawn in the morning (no iron 24 hours before). Appropriate H&P action? C. Inspect oral mucosa and tongue (never deeply palpate the spleen). A differential tells you? B. The proportion of each WBC type. Priority goal with thrombocytopenia? B. Prevention of injury."},{"ch":"answers","chTitle":"Practice Question Answer Key","id":"answers--chapter-34-heme-disorders","title":"Chapter 34 · Heme disorders","text":"Greatest IDA risk? B. 15-year-old who is pregnant. Higher risk of secondary polycythemia? C. Smoking. Action for thrombocytopenia? A. Avoid IM injections. Result that raises infection risk in leukemia? B. ANC 400. Most essential for a lymphoma patient starting chemo? D. Neutropenic and bleeding precautions. Mr. Owens, highest leukemia risk? C. Oil refinery work (benzene). CBC result that correlates with leukemia? D. Platelets 97,000 (crowded marrow, thrombocytopenia). First priority after chemo (ANC 0.6)? D. Infection. Needs more bleeding-precaution teaching? B. \"I have an appointment to get my teeth cleaned.\" Go to the ED now (SATA)? A, B, C: temperature 102°F, chills, nosebleed >10 minutes. Class PLQ: What all leukemias have in common? A. All involve unregulated proliferation of white blood cells. Class PLQ: IDA menu that shows teaching worked? A. Roast beef, steamed spinach, tomato soup, and orange juice (heme iron + vitamin C)."},{"ch":"answers","chTitle":"Practice Question Answer Key","id":"answers--chapters-18-19-immune","title":"Chapters 18–19 · Immune","text":"Central lymphoid organs? D. Thymus and bone marrow. Needs more bone marrow biopsy teaching? C. \"Odds are I will die from this procedure.\" Most likely XLA? C. 7-month-old male with recurrent infections. Monitor in XLA? B. Recurrent respiratory infections. Prophylactic antibiotics in DiGeorge? B. Prevent life-threatening infections. Low IgG correlates with? C. HIGM syndrome (hyper-IgM). Severe anaphylaxis finding? A. Hypotension. How epinephrine helps? A. Dilates bronchioles, constricts vessels, improves cardiac contraction. Teaching priority after bee-sting anaphylaxis? C. EpiPen use. Mr. Smith (PCN anaphylaxis): nursing diagnosis? A. Risk for ineffective gas exchange. Mr. Smith: priority assessment? C. Respiratory status. Mr. Smith: priority action? C. Discontinue the IV penicillin. Mr. Smith: allergic reaction finding? D. Hypotension and tachycardia. Mr. Smith: what shows it is progressing? A. Circulatory failure. Class PLQ 1: Expected action of an immunoglobulin infusion? D. They enhance the recognition of antigens. Class PLQ 2: Humoral vs cell-mediated immunity? D. Humoral = B cells, in charge of antibodies; T cells directly destroy infected or abnormal cells. Class PLQ 3: Primary immune deficiency warning signs (SATA)? D, E, F: frequent, severe infections needing hospitalization · persistent lymphadenopathy · failure to thrive. Class PLQ 4: CATCH (DiGeorge) components (SATA)? D, E, F, G, H: cardiac defects · abnormal facies · thymic hypoplasia · cleft palate · hypocalcemia."},{"ch":"answers","chTitle":"Practice Question Answer Key","id":"answers--chapter-64-repro-assessment","title":"Chapter 64 · Repro assessment","text":"Main site of sexual pleasure? A. Clitoris. Luteal phase hormones? B. ↑Progesterone and estrogen. Negative feedback in spermatogenesis? D. Inhibin. Repro assessment includes (SATA)? A, D, E: history, physical, labs such as PSA."},{"ch":"answers","chTitle":"Practice Question Answer Key","id":"answers--chapter-65-female-cancers","title":"Chapter 65 · Female cancers","text":"Patient with LCIS: schedule which? C. Mammogram (no treatment, just surveillance). Priority follow-up call? C. Full and bloated, hard to eat (ovarian). 60-year-old most concerning complaint? D. Vaginal bleeding (uterine). Age 25, last Pap at 21, no primary HPV available? A. Breast exam, pelvic exam, and Pap smear (no HPV cotest before 30). After breast conservation surgery? A. Chemo, then radiation. Order to verify? B. Oral contraceptives (ER+ tumor). Most concerning wound finding? A. Bloody drainage saturating the dressing. Pain assessment includes (SATA)? A, B, D: pain scale, HR, BP. Radiation side effect to monitor? D. Redness, itching, irritation of the radiated skin. Neoadjuvant chemo? B. Before surgery to help complete excision. Question which order after mastectomy (receptor-negative)? A. Antiestrogen therapy."},{"ch":"answers","chTitle":"Practice Question Answer Key","id":"answers--chapter-66-male-disorders","title":"Chapter 66 · Male disorders","text":"True of BPH? B. It is not cancer. Expected BPH finding? C. Frequent nocturia. Why a catheter in BPH (SATA)? C, D: evaluate PVR, empty the bladder completely. Late-stage prostate cancer with no urinary trouble? A. Tumors grow at the edges (periphery) of the prostate. Best testicular cancer treatment? D. Remove the affected testicle. Groin injury in football? A. Blunt trauma. Scott: why BPH causes symptoms? B. Obstruction of urine flow. Scott: why watchful waiting? A. Minimal symptoms and enlargement. Scott: needs more teaching? C. \"Glad I can take my decongestants.\" Scott: Flomax? A. Relaxes the prostate muscle. Scott: report pain, fever, N/V? D. Urine stasis can cause a UTI. Class PLQ 1: Cancer client with fever, malaise, and a high WBC: what principle guides care? B. Infection is most likely. Class PLQ 2: Client with a stage IV tumor designation D. (Question text wasn't in your notes; stage IV = metastasized.) Class PLQ 3: (question text not in your notes) C, E"},{"ch":"answers","chTitle":"Practice Question Answer Key","id":"answers--chapter-11-pain","title":"Chapter 11 · Pain","text":"\"What makes the pain worse?\" assesses? D. Aggravating factors. Warfarin teaching? B. OTC NSAIDs increase the anticoagulant effect. Respiratory depression and ↓GI motility come from which receptor? C. Mu."},{"ch":"answers","chTitle":"Practice Question Answer Key","id":"answers--chapter-20-ms-logan-ra-case-study","title":"Chapter 20 · Ms. Logan (RA) case study","text":"Priority to follow up with the provider about? C. Oxycodone 5 mg q6h prn (opioids aren't recommended for chronic RA pain). Blank in your notes; worked out. Methotrexate statement showing teaching worked? C. \"I will use two effective means of birth control.\" (teratogenic) Nursing diagnosis for her plan of care? C. Risk of injury related to unstable gait. Monitor for after starting methotrexate? B. Elevated liver enzymes. Lifestyle changes (SATA)? C, E: ROM exercises for flexibility · aerobic exercise for weight control."},{"ch":"other","chTitle":"Other Exam Topics","id":"other--other-exam-topics","title":"Other Exam Topics","text":"Topics from your exam guide and purple notes that are not in your chapter PDFs. These are filled in from standard nursing content."},{"ch":"other","chTitle":"Other Exam Topics","id":"other--hospice-vs-palliative-care-2-questions","title":"Hospice vs. palliative care · 2 questions","text":"Palliative care Hospice care When Any stage , from diagnosis of a serious illness (Ch 26: starts on day 1) End of life: prognosis of ≤6 months if the disease runs its usual course Curative treatment? Yes , can continue alongside chemo, radiation, surgery No: patient stops curative treatment and chooses comfort care Goal Relieve symptoms (pain, dyspnea, N/V), improve quality of life for patient and family Comfort, dignity, peaceful death; family support including bereavement after death Who qualifies Anyone with serious illness, any age Terminal illness certified by physicians (Medicare hospice benefit) Where Hospital, clinic, home Usually home; also hospice facility, nursing home Remember All hospice is palliative, but not all palliative care is hospice. \"Palliative care is not the same as hospice\" (Ch 26). Patients can leave hospice to restart curative treatment. Signs death is near, and nursing care Signs: ↓ intake and urine output · mottled, cool extremities · Cheyne-Stokes breathing / apnea periods · noisy secretions (\"death rattle\") · ↓ LOC · hearing is often the last sense lost. Nursing: manage pain (around the clock; opioids/morphine for dyspnea), mouth care, repositioning, emotional and spiritual support, honor advance directives/DNR."},{"ch":"other","chTitle":"Other Exam Topics","id":"other--organ-donation-keep-organs-viable","title":"Organ donation: keep organs viable","text":"Ventilator: transplant needs viable organs, so after brain death the patient is kept on the ventilator (and hemodynamic support) to keep oxygen and blood flowing to the organs until recovery. Brain death = irreversible loss of all brain function (including brainstem), declared by a physician. The heart can keep beating with ventilator support. Maintain organ perfusion: BP/MAP support (fluids, vasopressors), oxygenation, normal temperature, urine output, electrolytes ( diabetes insipidus is common). Hospitals must notify the organ procurement organization (OPO) of every death or imminent death; trained OPO staff usually approach the family. Tissues (corneas, skin, bone, heart valves) can be donated after cardiac death; most solid organs need a perfused donor. Family pays no donation costs; open-casket funeral is still possible; most major religions support donation."},{"ch":"other","chTitle":"Other Exam Topics","id":"other--dosage-calculation-2-questions","title":"Dosage calculation · 2 questions","text":"Type Formula Example Basic (desired over have) (D ÷ H) × Q Order 500 mg; have 250 mg/5 mL → (500 ÷ 250) × 5 = 10 mL Weight-based mg/kg × kg (lb ÷ 2.2 = kg) 15 mg/kg for 44 lb → 20 kg × 15 = 300 mg IV pump rate mL ÷ hr = mL/hr 1,000 mL over 8 h = 125 mL/hr Gravity drip (mL × drop factor) ÷ minutes = gtt/min 1,000 mL over 8 h, 15 gtt/mL → (1,000 × 15) ÷ 480 = 31 gtt/min Infusion time Total mL ÷ mL/hr = hours 500 mL at 100 mL/hr = 5 h Concentration (heparin, units) Ordered units/hr ÷ units/mL = mL/hr 1,000 units/hr; 25,000 units/250 mL (100 units/mL) → 10 mL/hr Conversions: 1 kg = 2.2 lb · 1 g = 1,000 mg · 1 mg = 1,000 mcg · 1 tsp = 5 mL · 1 tbsp = 15 mL · 1 oz = 30 mL. Round per your program's rules (usually tenths for mL, whole numbers for gtt). Check units before you calculate. Practice: use the calculator on the Tools page to check your own dosage problems. Open the dosage calculator"}],"qa":[{"q":"Cancer is the ___ most common cause of death in the US.","a":"B. Second.","aHtml":"<strong>B. Second.</strong>","qHtml":"Cancer is the ___ most common cause of death in the US.","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Benign vs malignant cells?","a":"C. Malignant cells lack contact inhibition.","aHtml":"<strong>C.</strong> Malignant cells lack contact inhibition.","qHtml":"Benign vs malignant cells?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Key lab for cancer with bone metastasis?","a":"B. Calcium (hypercalcemia).","aHtml":"<strong>B. Calcium</strong> (hypercalcemia).","qHtml":"Key lab for cancer with bone metastasis?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Most specific way to diagnose malignancy?","a":"D. Biopsy.","aHtml":"<strong>D. Biopsy.</strong>","qHtml":"Most specific way to diagnose malignancy?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Chemo before definitive surgery is?","a":"C. Neoadjuvant.","aHtml":"<strong>C. Neoadjuvant.</strong>","qHtml":"Chemo before definitive surgery is?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Which statement shows chemo teaching worked?","a":"B. \"It is important that I monitor my temperature.\"","aHtml":"<strong>B.</strong> &quot;It is important that I monitor my temperature.&quot;","qHtml":"Which statement shows chemo teaching worked?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Mrs. Jones, stage III?","a":"C. Large, locally advanced, possibly in local nodes. (Class notes said D, \"spread to lymph and blood systems.\" The book says C. Confirm with your instructor.)","aHtml":"<strong>C.</strong> Large, locally advanced, possibly in local nodes. (Class notes said D, &quot;spread to lymph and blood systems.&quot; The book says C. Confirm with your instructor.)","qHtml":"Mrs. Jones, stage III?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Oral hormonal therapy after mastectomy?","a":"B. Adjuvant.","aHtml":"<strong>B. Adjuvant.</strong>","qHtml":"Oral hormonal therapy after mastectomy?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Call the provider immediately for?","a":"C. Fever (myelosuppression).","aHtml":"<strong>C. Fever</strong> (myelosuppression).","qHtml":"Call the provider immediately for?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Before first paclitaxel (SATA)?","a":"B, D (and C per class): emergency equipment ready (hypersensitivity), check IV blood return (irritant/vesicant), and ECG within 24 hours (class adds this; paclitaxel can affect heart rhythm). Go with B, C, D.","aHtml":"<strong>B, D</strong> (and <strong>C</strong> per class): emergency equipment ready (hypersensitivity), check IV blood return (irritant/vesicant), and ECG within 24 hours (class adds this; paclitaxel can affect heart rhythm). Go with B, C, D.","qHtml":"Before first paclitaxel (SATA)?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Left chest wall radiation, which systems (SATA)?","a":"B, D, E (and C per class): integumentary (radiodermatitis), cardiac (late cardiac risk), respiratory (pneumonitis), plus musculoskeletal (shoulder stiffness and fibrosis). Go with B, C, D, E.","aHtml":"<strong>B, D, E</strong> (and <strong>C</strong> per class): integumentary (radiodermatitis), cardiac (late cardiac risk), respiratory (pneumonitis), plus musculoskeletal (shoulder stiffness and fibrosis). Go with B, C, D, E.","qHtml":"Left chest wall radiation, which systems (SATA)?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Class PLQ 1: Regulated cell death that cancer cells lose is called?","a":"C. Apoptosis.","aHtml":"<strong>C. Apoptosis.</strong>","qHtml":"Class PLQ 1: Regulated cell death that cancer cells lose is called?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Class PLQ 2: Stage III T3, N2, M1: which statement needs more teaching?","a":"A. \"I am relieved that the cancer is still local.\" M1 = metastasis present, so it is not local.","aHtml":"<strong>A.</strong> &quot;I am relieved that the cancer is still local.&quot; M1 = metastasis present, so it is not local.","qHtml":"Class PLQ 2: Stage III T3, N2, M1: which statement needs more teaching?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Class PLQ 3: Leukemia patient with chemo neutropenia: include in the plan?","a":"C. Restricting visitors with communicable diseases.","aHtml":"<strong>C.</strong> Restricting visitors with communicable diseases.","qHtml":"Class PLQ 3: Leukemia patient with chemo neutropenia: include in the plan?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"When to start palliative care in lung cancer?","a":"A. Upon diagnosis.","aHtml":"<strong>A. Upon diagnosis.</strong>","qHtml":"When to start palliative care in lung cancer?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Lung cancer plan of care (SATA)?","a":"A, B, D: education, frequent pain assessment, frequent oxygenation checks.","aHtml":"<strong>A, B, D:</strong> education, frequent pain assessment, frequent oxygenation checks.","qHtml":"Lung cancer plan of care (SATA)?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Mr. Davis, most important part of management?","a":"B. Disease management through education.","aHtml":"<strong>B.</strong> Disease management through education.","qHtml":"Mr. Davis, most important part of management?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"No wheezes and diminished breath sounds?","a":"C. Partially obstructed airway (not moving air). Emergency.","aHtml":"<strong>C.</strong> Partially obstructed airway (not moving air). <strong>Emergency.</strong>","qHtml":"No wheezes and diminished breath sounds?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Priority with a suspected partial obstruction?","a":"A. Airway management.","aHtml":"<strong>A. Airway management.</strong>","qHtml":"Priority with a suspected partial obstruction?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Major part of his treatment program?","a":"A. Smoking cessation.","aHtml":"<strong>A. Smoking cessation.</strong>","qHtml":"Major part of his treatment program?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Which statement needs more teaching?","a":"C. \"I can stop all my meds when my asthma is under control.\"","aHtml":"<strong>C.</strong> &quot;I can stop all my meds when my asthma is under control.&quot;","qHtml":"Which statement needs more teaching?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"IDA manifestations (SATA)?","a":"D, E: ↑HR, ↑RR.","aHtml":"<strong>D, E:</strong> ↑HR, ↑RR.","qHtml":"IDA manifestations (SATA)?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Result that confirms IDA?","a":"A. Decreased transferrin saturation (\"always low\" in IDA). TIBC is ↑, ferritin ↓, MCV ↓ too.","aHtml":"<strong>A. Decreased transferrin saturation</strong> (&quot;always low&quot; in IDA). TIBC is ↑, ferritin ↓, MCV ↓ too.","qHtml":"Result that confirms IDA?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Compensatory finding in IDA?","a":"A. Increased respiratory rate.","aHtml":"<strong>A.</strong> Increased respiratory rate.","qHtml":"Compensatory finding in IDA?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Mouth finding in IDA?","a":"C. Red, swollen, tender tongue (glossitis).","aHtml":"<strong>C.</strong> Red, swollen, tender tongue (glossitis).","qHtml":"Mouth finding in IDA?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Serum iron instructions?","a":"B. Drawn in the morning (no iron 24 hours before).","aHtml":"<strong>B.</strong> Drawn in the morning (no iron 24 hours before).","qHtml":"Serum iron instructions?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Appropriate H&P action?","a":"C. Inspect oral mucosa and tongue (never deeply palpate the spleen).","aHtml":"<strong>C.</strong> Inspect oral mucosa and tongue (never deeply palpate the spleen).","qHtml":"Appropriate H&amp;P action?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"A differential tells you?","a":"B. The proportion of each WBC type.","aHtml":"<strong>B.</strong> The proportion of each WBC type.","qHtml":"A differential tells you?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Priority goal with thrombocytopenia?","a":"B. Prevention of injury.","aHtml":"<strong>B. Prevention of injury.</strong>","qHtml":"Priority goal with thrombocytopenia?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Greatest IDA risk?","a":"B. 15-year-old who is pregnant.","aHtml":"<strong>B.</strong> 15-year-old who is pregnant.","qHtml":"Greatest IDA risk?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Higher risk of secondary polycythemia?","a":"C. Smoking.","aHtml":"<strong>C. Smoking.</strong>","qHtml":"Higher risk of secondary polycythemia?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Action for thrombocytopenia?","a":"A. Avoid IM injections.","aHtml":"<strong>A. Avoid IM injections.</strong>","qHtml":"Action for thrombocytopenia?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Result that raises infection risk in leukemia?","a":"B. ANC 400.","aHtml":"<strong>B. ANC 400.</strong>","qHtml":"Result that raises infection risk in leukemia?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Most essential for a lymphoma patient starting chemo?","a":"D. Neutropenic and bleeding precautions.","aHtml":"<strong>D.</strong> Neutropenic and bleeding precautions.","qHtml":"Most essential for a lymphoma patient starting chemo?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Mr. Owens, highest leukemia risk?","a":"C. Oil refinery work (benzene).","aHtml":"<strong>C.</strong> Oil refinery work (benzene).","qHtml":"Mr. Owens, highest leukemia risk?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"CBC result that correlates with leukemia?","a":"D. Platelets 97,000 (crowded marrow, thrombocytopenia).","aHtml":"<strong>D.</strong> Platelets 97,000 (crowded marrow, thrombocytopenia).","qHtml":"CBC result that correlates with leukemia?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"First priority after chemo (ANC 0.6)?","a":"D. Infection.","aHtml":"<strong>D. Infection.</strong>","qHtml":"First priority after chemo (ANC 0.6)?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Needs more bleeding-precaution teaching?","a":"B. \"I have an appointment to get my teeth cleaned.\"","aHtml":"<strong>B.</strong> &quot;I have an appointment to get my teeth cleaned.&quot;","qHtml":"Needs more bleeding-precaution teaching?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Go to the ED now (SATA)?","a":"A, B, C: temperature 102°F, chills, nosebleed >10 minutes.","aHtml":"<strong>A, B, C:</strong> temperature 102°F, chills, nosebleed &gt;10 minutes.","qHtml":"Go to the ED now (SATA)?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Class PLQ: What all leukemias have in common?","a":"A. All involve unregulated proliferation of white blood cells.","aHtml":"<strong>A.</strong> All involve unregulated proliferation of white blood cells.","qHtml":"Class PLQ: What all leukemias have in common?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Class PLQ: IDA menu that shows teaching worked?","a":"A. Roast beef, steamed spinach, tomato soup, and orange juice (heme iron + vitamin C).","aHtml":"<strong>A.</strong> Roast beef, steamed spinach, tomato soup, and orange juice (heme iron + vitamin C).","qHtml":"Class PLQ: IDA menu that shows teaching worked?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Central lymphoid organs?","a":"D. Thymus and bone marrow.","aHtml":"<strong>D. Thymus and bone marrow.</strong>","qHtml":"Central lymphoid organs?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Needs more bone marrow biopsy teaching?","a":"C. \"Odds are I will die from this procedure.\"","aHtml":"<strong>C.</strong> &quot;Odds are I will die from this procedure.&quot;","qHtml":"Needs more bone marrow biopsy teaching?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Most likely XLA?","a":"C. 7-month-old male with recurrent infections.","aHtml":"<strong>C.</strong> 7-month-old male with recurrent infections.","qHtml":"Most likely XLA?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Monitor in XLA?","a":"B. Recurrent respiratory infections.","aHtml":"<strong>B.</strong> Recurrent respiratory infections.","qHtml":"Monitor in XLA?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Prophylactic antibiotics in DiGeorge?","a":"B. Prevent life-threatening infections.","aHtml":"<strong>B.</strong> Prevent life-threatening infections.","qHtml":"Prophylactic antibiotics in DiGeorge?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Low IgG correlates with?","a":"C. HIGM syndrome (hyper-IgM).","aHtml":"<strong>C. HIGM syndrome</strong> (hyper-IgM).","qHtml":"Low IgG correlates with?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Severe anaphylaxis finding?","a":"A. Hypotension.","aHtml":"<strong>A. Hypotension.</strong>","qHtml":"Severe anaphylaxis finding?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"How epinephrine helps?","a":"A. Dilates bronchioles, constricts vessels, improves cardiac contraction.","aHtml":"<strong>A.</strong> Dilates bronchioles, constricts vessels, improves cardiac contraction.","qHtml":"How epinephrine helps?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Teaching priority after bee-sting anaphylaxis?","a":"C. EpiPen use.","aHtml":"<strong>C. EpiPen use.</strong>","qHtml":"Teaching priority after bee-sting anaphylaxis?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Mr. Smith (PCN anaphylaxis): nursing diagnosis?","a":"A. Risk for ineffective gas exchange.","aHtml":"<strong>A.</strong> Risk for ineffective gas exchange.","qHtml":"Mr. Smith (PCN anaphylaxis): nursing diagnosis?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Mr. Smith: priority assessment?","a":"C. Respiratory status.","aHtml":"<strong>C. Respiratory status.</strong>","qHtml":"Mr. Smith: priority assessment?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Mr. Smith: priority action?","a":"C. Discontinue the IV penicillin.","aHtml":"<strong>C.</strong> Discontinue the IV penicillin.","qHtml":"Mr. Smith: priority action?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Mr. Smith: allergic reaction finding?","a":"D. Hypotension and tachycardia.","aHtml":"<strong>D.</strong> Hypotension and tachycardia.","qHtml":"Mr. Smith: allergic reaction finding?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Mr. Smith: what shows it is progressing?","a":"A. Circulatory failure.","aHtml":"<strong>A. Circulatory failure.</strong>","qHtml":"Mr. Smith: what shows it is progressing?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Class PLQ 1: Expected action of an immunoglobulin infusion?","a":"D. They enhance the recognition of antigens.","aHtml":"<strong>D.</strong> They enhance the recognition of antigens.","qHtml":"Class PLQ 1: Expected action of an immunoglobulin infusion?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Class PLQ 2: Humoral vs cell-mediated immunity?","a":"D. Humoral = B cells, in charge of antibodies; T cells directly destroy infected or abnormal cells.","aHtml":"<strong>D.</strong> Humoral = B cells, in charge of antibodies; T cells directly destroy infected or abnormal cells.","qHtml":"Class PLQ 2: Humoral vs cell-mediated immunity?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Class PLQ 3: Primary immune deficiency warning signs (SATA)?","a":"D, E, F: frequent, severe infections needing hospitalization · persistent lymphadenopathy · failure to thrive.","aHtml":"<strong>D, E, F:</strong> frequent, severe infections needing hospitalization · persistent lymphadenopathy · failure to thrive.","qHtml":"Class PLQ 3: Primary immune deficiency warning signs (SATA)?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Class PLQ 4: CATCH (DiGeorge) components (SATA)?","a":"D, E, F, G, H: cardiac defects · abnormal facies · thymic hypoplasia · cleft palate · hypocalcemia.","aHtml":"<strong>D, E, F, G, H:</strong> cardiac defects · abnormal facies · thymic hypoplasia · cleft palate · hypocalcemia.","qHtml":"Class PLQ 4: CATCH (DiGeorge) components (SATA)?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Main site of sexual pleasure?","a":"A. Clitoris.","aHtml":"<strong>A. Clitoris.</strong>","qHtml":"Main site of sexual pleasure?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Luteal phase hormones?","a":"B. ↑Progesterone and estrogen.","aHtml":"<strong>B.</strong> ↑Progesterone and estrogen.","qHtml":"Luteal phase hormones?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Negative feedback in spermatogenesis?","a":"D. Inhibin.","aHtml":"<strong>D. Inhibin.</strong>","qHtml":"Negative feedback in spermatogenesis?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Repro assessment includes (SATA)?","a":"A, D, E: history, physical, labs such as PSA.","aHtml":"<strong>A, D, E:</strong> history, physical, labs such as PSA.","qHtml":"Repro assessment includes (SATA)?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Patient with LCIS: schedule which?","a":"C. Mammogram (no treatment, just surveillance).","aHtml":"<strong>C. Mammogram</strong> (no treatment, just surveillance).","qHtml":"Patient with LCIS: schedule which?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Priority follow-up call?","a":"C. Full and bloated, hard to eat (ovarian).","aHtml":"<strong>C.</strong> Full and bloated, hard to eat (ovarian).","qHtml":"Priority follow-up call?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"60-year-old most concerning complaint?","a":"D. Vaginal bleeding (uterine).","aHtml":"<strong>D. Vaginal bleeding</strong> (uterine).","qHtml":"60-year-old most concerning complaint?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Age 25, last Pap at 21, no primary HPV available?","a":"A. Breast exam, pelvic exam, and Pap smear (no HPV cotest before 30).","aHtml":"<strong>A.</strong> Breast exam, pelvic exam, and Pap smear (no HPV cotest before 30).","qHtml":"Age 25, last Pap at 21, no primary HPV available?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"After breast conservation surgery?","a":"A. Chemo, then radiation.","aHtml":"<strong>A.</strong> Chemo, then radiation.","qHtml":"After breast conservation surgery?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Order to verify?","a":"B. Oral contraceptives (ER+ tumor).","aHtml":"<strong>B. Oral contraceptives</strong> (ER+ tumor).","qHtml":"Order to verify?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Most concerning wound finding?","a":"A. Bloody drainage saturating the dressing.","aHtml":"<strong>A.</strong> Bloody drainage saturating the dressing.","qHtml":"Most concerning wound finding?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Pain assessment includes (SATA)?","a":"A, B, D: pain scale, HR, BP.","aHtml":"<strong>A, B, D:</strong> pain scale, HR, BP.","qHtml":"Pain assessment includes (SATA)?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Radiation side effect to monitor?","a":"D. Redness, itching, irritation of the radiated skin.","aHtml":"<strong>D.</strong> Redness, itching, irritation of the radiated skin.","qHtml":"Radiation side effect to monitor?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Neoadjuvant chemo?","a":"B. Before surgery to help complete excision.","aHtml":"<strong>B.</strong> Before surgery to help complete excision.","qHtml":"Neoadjuvant chemo?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Question which order after mastectomy (receptor-negative)?","a":"A. Antiestrogen therapy.","aHtml":"<strong>A. Antiestrogen therapy.</strong>","qHtml":"Question which order after mastectomy (receptor-negative)?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"True of BPH?","a":"B. It is not cancer.","aHtml":"<strong>B.</strong> It is not cancer.","qHtml":"True of BPH?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Expected BPH finding?","a":"C. Frequent nocturia.","aHtml":"<strong>C.</strong> Frequent nocturia.","qHtml":"Expected BPH finding?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Why a catheter in BPH (SATA)?","a":"C, D: evaluate PVR, empty the bladder completely.","aHtml":"<strong>C, D:</strong> evaluate PVR, empty the bladder completely.","qHtml":"Why a catheter in BPH (SATA)?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Late-stage prostate cancer with no urinary trouble?","a":"A. Tumors grow at the edges (periphery) of the prostate.","aHtml":"<strong>A.</strong> Tumors grow at the edges (periphery) of the prostate.","qHtml":"Late-stage prostate cancer with no urinary trouble?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Best testicular cancer treatment?","a":"D. Remove the affected testicle.","aHtml":"<strong>D.</strong> Remove the affected testicle.","qHtml":"Best testicular cancer treatment?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Groin injury in football?","a":"A. Blunt trauma.","aHtml":"<strong>A. Blunt trauma.</strong>","qHtml":"Groin injury in football?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Scott: why BPH causes symptoms?","a":"B. Obstruction of urine flow.","aHtml":"<strong>B.</strong> Obstruction of urine flow.","qHtml":"Scott: why BPH causes symptoms?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Scott: why watchful waiting?","a":"A. Minimal symptoms and enlargement.","aHtml":"<strong>A.</strong> Minimal symptoms and enlargement.","qHtml":"Scott: why watchful waiting?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Scott: needs more teaching?","a":"C. \"Glad I can take my decongestants.\"","aHtml":"<strong>C.</strong> &quot;Glad I can take my decongestants.&quot;","qHtml":"Scott: needs more teaching?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Scott: Flomax?","a":"A. Relaxes the prostate muscle.","aHtml":"<strong>A. Relaxes the prostate muscle.</strong>","qHtml":"Scott: Flomax?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Scott: report pain, fever, N/V?","a":"D. Urine stasis can cause a UTI.","aHtml":"<strong>D.</strong> Urine stasis can cause a UTI.","qHtml":"Scott: report pain, fever, N/V?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Class PLQ 1: Cancer client with fever, malaise, and a high WBC: what principle guides care?","a":"B. Infection is most likely.","aHtml":"<strong>B.</strong> Infection is most likely.","qHtml":"Class PLQ 1: Cancer client with fever, malaise, and a high WBC: what principle guides care?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Class PLQ 2: Client with a stage IV tumor designation","a":"D. (Question text wasn't in your notes; stage IV = metastasized.)","aHtml":"<strong>D.</strong> (Question text wasn&#39;t in your notes; stage IV = metastasized.)","qHtml":"Class PLQ 2: Client with a stage IV tumor designation","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Class PLQ 3: (question text not in your notes)","a":"C, E","aHtml":"<strong>C, E</strong>","qHtml":"Class PLQ 3: (question text not in your notes)","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"\"What makes the pain worse?\" assesses?","a":"D. Aggravating factors.","aHtml":"<strong>D. Aggravating factors.</strong>","qHtml":"&quot;What makes the pain worse?&quot; assesses?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Warfarin teaching?","a":"B. OTC NSAIDs increase the anticoagulant effect.","aHtml":"<strong>B.</strong> OTC NSAIDs increase the anticoagulant effect.","qHtml":"Warfarin teaching?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Respiratory depression and ↓GI motility come from which receptor?","a":"C. Mu.","aHtml":"<strong>C. Mu.</strong>","qHtml":"Respiratory depression and ↓GI motility come from which receptor?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Priority to follow up with the provider about?","a":"C. Oxycodone 5 mg q6h prn (opioids aren't recommended for chronic RA pain). Blank in your notes; worked out.","aHtml":"<strong>C. Oxycodone 5 mg q6h prn</strong> (opioids aren&#39;t recommended for chronic RA pain). <em>Blank in your notes; worked out.</em>","qHtml":"Priority to follow up with the provider about?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Methotrexate statement showing teaching worked?","a":"C. \"I will use two effective means of birth control.\" (teratogenic)","aHtml":"<strong>C.</strong> &quot;I will use two effective means of birth control.&quot; (teratogenic)","qHtml":"Methotrexate statement showing teaching worked?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Nursing diagnosis for her plan of care?","a":"C. Risk of injury related to unstable gait.","aHtml":"<strong>C.</strong> Risk of injury related to unstable gait.","qHtml":"Nursing diagnosis for her plan of care?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Monitor for after starting methotrexate?","a":"B. Elevated liver enzymes.","aHtml":"<strong>B.</strong> Elevated liver enzymes.","qHtml":"Monitor for after starting methotrexate?","chapter":"answers"},{"q":"Lifestyle changes (SATA)?","a":"C, E: ROM exercises for flexibility · aerobic exercise for weight control.","aHtml":"<strong>C, E:</strong> ROM exercises for flexibility · aerobic exercise for weight control.","qHtml":"Lifestyle changes (SATA)?","chapter":"answers"}],"built":"2026-10-29T04:18:57.974Z"};
